window.GS_HISTORY = {"subject":"History","source":"Pinnacle SSC GS 6500+ TCS MCQs","sections":[{"name":"Ancient History","subtopics":[{"name":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","count":36},{"name":"Vedic Age","count":30},{"name":"Jainism","count":14},{"name":"Mauryan Dynasty","count":38},{"name":"Gupta Dynasty","count":24},{"name":"Vardhana Dynasty","count":9},{"name":"Chola Dynasty","count":18},{"name":"Miscellaneous","count":84}]},{"name":"Medieval History","subtopics":[{"name":"Foreign Invasions","count":9},{"name":"Delhi Sultanate","count":15},{"name":"Slave Dynasty","count":18},{"name":"Khilji Dynasty","count":11},{"name":"Tughlaq Dynasty","count":8},{"name":"Sayyid Dynasty","count":1},{"name":"Lodi Dynasty","count":5},{"name":"Mughal Period","count":9},{"name":"Babur","count":10},{"name":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","count":7},{"name":"Akbar","count":19},{"name":"Jahangir","count":6},{"name":"Shah Jahan","count":9},{"name":"Aurangzeb","count":7},{"name":"Sikh Guru","count":3},{"name":"Maratha Empire","count":4},{"name":"Vijaynagar Empire","count":15},{"name":"Wars and Treaties","count":26},{"name":"Miscellaneous","count":84}]},{"name":"Modern History","subtopics":[{"name":"The Revolt of 1857","count":8},{"name":"Governors and Viceroys","count":32},{"name":"British Acts and Policies","count":56},{"name":"Swadeshi Movements","count":7},{"name":"Gandhian Era","count":39},{"name":"Expansion of British Rule","count":9},{"name":"The Revolutionaries","count":40},{"name":"Struggle for Independence","count":23},{"name":"Socio Religious Reforms","count":85},{"name":"Its Sessions","count":29},{"name":"Muslim league","count":5},{"name":"Miscellaneous","count":72}]}],"questions":[{"id":0,"n":1,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Select the correct alternative of the basis of the given statements on the drainage system of the Harappan civilisation. Statement I: In most of the Harappan cities, the houses had open drainage systems outside. Statement II: In the Harappan civilisation, water and garbage used to drain out from the drains located outside the houses and go into the main underground drain.","o":{"a":"Only Statement I is true.","b":"Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","c":"Only Statement II is true.","d":"Neither Statement I nor Statement II is true."},"a":"c","s":"Only Statement II is true Drainage System of Harappan cities: There were covered drains on both sides of the streets. Streets with drains were laid out first and then houses built along them. If domestic wastewater had to flow into the street drains, every house needed to have at least one wall along a street.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":1,"n":2,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Select the correct alternative on the basis of the given statements on the cities of Harappan civilisation. Statement I: Most of the Harappan cities were divided into two parts: Higher Town and Lower Town. Statement II: The archaeologists describe the lower part of the towns as Citadel.","o":{"a":"Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","b":"Only Statement II is true.","c":"Only Statement I is true","d":"Neither Statement I nor Statement II is true."},"a":"c","s":"Only Statement I is true. The Harappan civilization was one of the oldest civilizations in the world. It flourished in the Indus River Valley region from around 2500 - 1500 BC. Harappan civilization: Excavated by - Daya Ram Sahni in 1921. Location - Situated on the bank of river Ravi in Punjab (Pakistan). Important Findings - Granaries, Bullock carts, Coffin burial, Mother goddess.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":2,"n":3,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Fire altars were found in which of the following groups of Harappan cities?","o":{"a":"Kalibangan and Lothal","b":"Banawali and Chanhudaro","c":"Mohenjo-Daro and Harappa","d":"Mohenjo-Daro and Dholavira"},"a":"a","s":"Kalibangan and Lothal Findings of different Indus valley civilization sites: Kalibangan - Lower fortified town, Boustrophedon style, Wooden drainage, Copper Ox, Evidence of earthquake, Wooden plough, Camel's bone. Lothal - Port Town, Evidence of Rice, Graveyard, Ivory scale, Copper dog. Mohenjo-Daro - Prepared Garments, Temple-like Palace, Pashupati seal, Statue of a dancing girl, The Great Bath, The Great Granary etc.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":3,"n":4,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Select the correct alternative on the basis of the given statements on the Great Bath of Harappan civilization. Statement I: The Great Bath was found in Mohenjo-Daro site of Harappan civilisation. Statement II: The Great Bath was lined with bricks, coated with plaster and made water-tight with a layer of natural tar.","o":{"a":"Neither Statement I nor Statement II is true.","b":"Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","c":"Only Statement I is true.","d":"Only Statement II is true."},"a":"b","s":"Mohenjo Daro is an archaeological site in the Sindh province of Pakistan. The meaning of Mohenjo Daro is The mound of the dead. It was discovered in 1922 by RD Banerjee. It was developed around 3000 BCE and it was the largest city of the ancient Indus Valley Civilization.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":4,"n":5,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Mohenjodaro site of Harappan civilization is situated on the bank of which river ?","o":{"a":"Ghaghara river","b":"Beas river","c":"Jhelum river","d":"Indus river"},"a":"d","s":"Indus river. List of Harappan civilisation and its location on River: Harappa (Ravi river), Lothal (Bhogava river), Kalibangan (Ghaggar River), Amri (Indus river), Chanhudaro (Indus river), Suktagendor (Dast river).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":5,"n":6,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following statements is true about the Great Bath of the Indus Valley Civilisation ?","o":{"a":"It was used for grain storage.","b":"It was used for farming.","c":"It was used as the water tank of the city.","d":"It was probably used for religious purpose."},"a":"c","s":"\" Great bath \" is a public water tank located in Mohenjo Daro (Pakistan). It was a large rectangular tank in a courtyard surrounded by a corridor on all four sides. There were two flights of steps on the north and south leading into the tank, which was made watertight by setting bricks on edge and using a mortar of gypsum. There were rooms on three sides, in one of which was a large well.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":6,"n":7,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following Indus Valley sites is now located in Pakistan ?","o":{"a":"Harappa","b":"Lothal","c":"Alamgirpur","d":"Kalibangan"},"a":"a","s":"Harappa. The Indus valley civilization (Bronze Age) is also called the Harappan culture. Other Important Sites : Harappa (river Ravi) - Excavated by Daya Ram Sahini in 1921, Mohenjodaro (Mound of Dead, river Indus) - Excavated by R.D Banerjee in 1922; Sutkagendor (Dast river, Balochistan); Chanhudaro (Pakistan, Indus river); Kalibangan (Rajasthan, Ghaggar river); Lothal (Gujarat, Bhogva river); Alamgirpur (Meerut, Hindon river).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":7,"n":8,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Iron age is so named because during this time iron mostly replaced _______ in implements and weapons, beginning in the Middle East and South-eastern Europe.","o":{"a":"wood","b":"brass","c":"bronze","d":"stone"},"a":"c","s":"Bronze. The Iron Age is the final period of the three-age division of the prehistory of humanity. It was introduced after the Stone Age (Paleolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic) and the Bronze Age.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":8,"n":9,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"In 1948, Ernest Mackay mentioned that in the Harappan city of Lothal, the drains for the drainage system were made of______________ bricks.","o":{"a":"red","b":"burnt","c":"mud","d":"sandstone"},"a":"b","s":"Burnt. Lothal is located near the Gulf of Cambay/ Gulf of Khambhat in Gujarat, on the bank of river Bhogavo, tributary of Sabarmati.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 4"},{"id":9,"n":10,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Pottery was first traceable to which period of ancient Indian history?","o":{"a":"Chalcolithic","b":"Palaeolithic","c":"Neolithic","d":"Mesolithic"},"a":"c","s":"Neolithic. Pottery has been discovered in the early villages of Mehrgarh. The Palaeolithic (the first and longest part of the Stone Age) was a period of prehistory when humans made stone tools. Mesolithic (microliths) was the period between the Paleolithic and Neolithic periods of the Stone Age.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 4"},{"id":10,"n":11,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"The prehistoric period in the early development of human beings is commonly known as the _________.","o":{"a":"Old stone age","b":"Mesolithic age","c":"Iron age","d":"New stone age"},"a":"a","s":"Old stone age. Old stone age also known as the Paleolithic Period. Tools - handaxes, choppers and cleavers. The earliest lower Palaeolithic site - Bori (Maharashtra). Mesolithic age - Sites - Gujarat (Langhnaj), Rajasthan (Bhilwara, Bagor), Uttar Pradesh (Damdama, Chopani Mando), Madhya Pradesh (Pachmarhi, Bhimbetka), Karnataka (Sanganakallu). New stone age also known as Neolithic age . Tools - Weapons made of bones - needles, arrowheads, etc. Sites - Burzahom (Kashmir), Chirand (Bihar), Mehrgarh (Balochistan).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":11,"n":12,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"The ancient city of Dholavira, is located in which state?","o":{"a":"Madhya Pradesh","b":"Rajasthan","c":"Karnataka","d":"Gujarat"},"a":"d","s":"Gujarat. Dholavira : It was discovered in 1968 by J.P. Joshi in Rann of Kachchh in Gujarat. It is situated on the banks of river Luni. It has a fortified citadel, and a lower town with walls made of sandstone or limestone. The first astronomical observatory of the Harappan Civilization is located in Dholavira.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":12,"n":13,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"In India, the practice of erecting megaliths began about __________ ago.","o":{"a":"3000 years","b":"2000 years","c":"500 years","d":"1000 years"},"a":"a","s":"3000 years. A megalith is a large stone that has been used to construct a pre - historic structure or monument. Type - Clairn Circles type of Megalith (grave/memorial) were the stone pieces set in a circular shape around the grave. Menhir (standing stone), Capstone (Single megaliths placed horizontally over burial chambers ) , Dolmen (A large capstone on two or more support stones creating a chamber). Megalith Sites - Nilaskal (Karnataka), Hanamsagar (Karnataka), Junapani (Maharashtra), Hire Benakal (Karnataka), etc.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":13,"n":14,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following ancient sites is NOT situated in India?","o":{"a":"Hunsgi","b":"Hallur","c":"Inamgaon","d":"Mehrgarh"},"a":"d","s":"Mehrgarh - It is a Neolithic Archeological Site, located on the Bolan river (bolan pass) in Balochistan (Pakistan), Discovered by - French Archaeologist Jean Francois Jarrige (1974). Findings: The earliest known center of agriculture in South Asia; First evidence of Cotton; Early Beadmakers; Shell Trade and Shell Working. Hunsgi - It is a Paleolithic Site, Located in Yadgir (Karnataka). Hallur - It belongs to the earliest Iron Age Site (Neolithic), Location - Bank of River Tungabhadra (Karnataka). Inamgaon - It is a post -Harappan agrarian village and archaeological site located in Maharashtra (situated on Ghod river).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":14,"n":15,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Mohenjodaro belongs to which of the following civilizations.","o":{"a":"Maya civilization","b":"Indus Valley civilization","c":"Mesopotamian civilization","d":"Egyptian civilization"},"a":"b","s":"Indus Valley civilization Mohenjo-Daro (Mound of the Dead Men) - Excavated by R. D Banerjee in 1922. Evidence - Great bath, Granary, Unicorn Seals (Most numbers of it in here), Bronze dancing girl statue, Pashupati Seals, Steatite statue of beard man. Important Sites in India : Rajasthan (Kalibangan), Gujarat (Lothal, Dholavira, Rangpur, Surkotada), Haryana (Banawali), Punjab (Ropar). In Pakistan - Harappa (on river Ravi), Mohenjodaro (on the bank of the Indus River in Sindh), and Chanhudaro (in Sindh).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":15,"n":16,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Cotton was probably grown at Mehrgarh from about ________.","o":{"a":"7000 years ago","b":"8000 years ago","c":"6000 years ago","d":"5000 years ago"},"a":"a","s":"7000 years ago. It is the oldest agricultural settlement in the Indian subcontinent.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":16,"n":17,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following Harappan town was located on Khadir Beyt in the Runn of Kutch and was divided into three parts?","o":{"a":"Sotka - koh","b":"Chanhudaro","c":"Surkotada","d":"Dholavira"},"a":"d","s":"Dholavira. Discovered in 1968 by archaeologist Jagat Pati Joshi. Fifth largest metropolis of Indus Valley Civilization, 40th Indian site on UNESCO's World Heritage list. Surkotada: Discovered in 1964-68 by Jagat Pati Joshi, located in northeast Bhuj in Gujarat. Chanhudaro: Discovered in 1931 by N. G. Majumdar, located in Sindh, Pakistan. Sotka koh - Discovered by George F. Dales, located on the Makran coast, near the city of Pasni, Balochistan (Pakistan).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":17,"n":18,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Bhimbetka, a noted site of the Palaeolithic period, is located in which state of India?","o":{"a":"Bihar","b":"Uttar Pradesh","c":"Rajasthan","d":"Madhya Pradesh"},"a":"d","s":"Madhya Pradesh. Bhimbetka Rock Paintings (UNESCO World Heritage Site, 2003) is located in the Vindhya ranges of Madhya Pradesh. It was discovered by V. S. Wakankar in 1957 . The paintings at Bhimbetka belong to the Upper Paleolithic, Mesolithic, Chalcolithic, early historic, and medieval periods. The complex is surrounded by the Ratapani Wildlife Sanctuary.","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":18,"n":19,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"In which of the following Indian states, Harappan cities have NOT been found?","o":{"a":"Uttarakhand","b":"Gujarat","c":"Rajasthan","d":"Haryana"},"a":"a","s":"Uttarakhand. Harappan sites - Gujarat (Lothal, Dholavira, Babar Kot, Bhagatrav, Desalpur, Gola Dhoro, Kaj, Kanjetar, Khirasara, Kotada Bhadli, Kuntasi, Loteshwar, etc), Rajasthan (Baror, Kalibangan, and Karanpura), Haryana (Balu, Rakhigarhi, Banawali, Bhirrana, Farmana, Hisar mound, Jogna Khera, Kunal, Mitathal, Tigrana, etc).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":19,"n":20,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"During which of the following period Ostriches were found in India?","o":{"a":"Mesolithic","b":"Chalcolithic","c":"Neolithic","d":"Palaeolithic"},"a":"d","s":"Palaeolithic age . Large quantities of ostrich egg shells found at Patne, Maharashtra.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":20,"n":21,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"In which of the following sites of jadeite stone was found?","o":{"a":"Mahagara","b":"Mehrgarh","c":"Hallur","d":"Daojali Hading"},"a":"d","s":"Daojali Hading - A neolithic site in Dima Hasao District of Assam. Situated near Brahmaputra valley close to routes leading into China and Myanmar. Artifacts found - Jadeite stone (must have been transported from China). 4 types of ceramic - cord-marked, incised, stamped, and plain fine red ware.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":21,"n":22,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"In which among the following periods Catal Huyuk was one of the most famous sites?","o":{"a":"Neolithic","b":"Mesolithic","c":"Palaeolithic","d":"Chalcolithic"},"a":"a","s":"Neolithic. Catal Huyuk site located near the modern city of Konya in south central Turkey. Features - substantial size and great longevity of the settlement, distinctive layout of back-to-back houses with roof access. Neolithic sites in India - Marakdola, Daojali Hading and Sarutaru in Assam.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":22,"n":23,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"According to the archaeologists, in Harappan cities the part to the west was smaller and higher, was known as ___________.","o":{"a":"citadel","b":"Olympus","c":"lower town","d":"colosseum"},"a":"a","s":"Citadel. Chanudaro was the only Harappan city without a Citadel. Important Excavations : Harappa (by Daya Ram Sahni in 1921), Chanhudaro (by NG Majumdar in 1931) and Surkotada (by JP Joshi in 1964).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":23,"n":24,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following metals was used to make weapons and tools in Harappan cities?","o":{"a":"Silver","b":"Gold","c":"Copper","d":"Iron"},"a":"c","s":"Copper was the first metal used in Indus Valley (Bronze Age civilization) to make tools, utensils, and other objects. Other metals used were lead, gold, bronze and silver .","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":24,"n":25,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"The Harappan brought Gold from present-day _______ in India.","o":{"a":"Telangana","b":"Tamil Nadu","c":"Karnataka","d":"Kerala"},"a":"c","s":"Karnataka. Gold ornaments are found at both Mohenjo-daro and Harappa. Harappa - Bullock cart, Granaries, Coffin burials (Only founded in Harappa), Terracotta figurines. Chanhudaro (only city without a citadel) - Bangle factory, Inkpot.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":25,"n":26,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following types of house remains were found in Mehrgarh site of Harappan civilization?","o":{"a":"Triangular or circular","b":"Rectangular or circular","c":"Square or rectangular","d":"Circular or square"},"a":"c","s":"Square or rectangle. Major findings at Mehrgarh are evidence of animal bones and the remains of houses. Mehrgarh was discovered in 1974 by Jean-Francois Jarrige. It is the first place where people started growing barley and wheat, reared the sheep and goat as it was one of the oldest villages.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":26,"n":27,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"The word India came from the Indus, called ______ in Sanskrit.","o":{"a":"Bhanuh","b":"Adya","c":"Sarvatr","d":"Sindhu"},"a":"d","s":"Sindhu (the local name of Indus). Other names of India: Bharat, Aryavarta, Hindustan, Tenjiku, and Jambudweep.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":27,"n":28,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"The duration of the Mesolithic period is from about ______ years ago to ____ years ago.","o":{"a":"14000, 10000","b":"17000, 12000","c":"12000, 10000","d":"10000, 7000"},"a":"c","s":"12000, 10000 . The Mesolithic Period (Middle Stone Age) is the earliest Holocene culture. Feature : Microliths, technological innovations (bow and arrow for hunting, querns, grinders, etc.), Excavated sites (Tilwara, Bagor, Tarsang, Morkhana, Lekhahia, Birbhanpur, etc.). Paleolithic age (old stone age): Period - (500,000 - 10,000 BCE), Neolithic age (new stone age): Period - (7000 - 1000 BCE).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":28,"n":29,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following were NOT a part of a citadel in Harappan architecture?","o":{"a":"Storehouses for grains","b":"Great Bath for important people","c":"Fire altars for religious ceremonies","d":"Residential buildings for masses"},"a":"d","s":"A citadel is the core fortified area of a town. Harappan Civilization (Indus Valley Civilization or Bronze Age civilization), lasted from 2600 -1900 BCE was divided into the citadel and lower town. Rakhigarhi (Haryana) is the largest Harappan site in India.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":29,"n":30,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"What was the shape of the Great Bath of Mohenjo Daro?","o":{"a":"Rectangular","b":"Square","c":"Elliptical","d":"Circular"},"a":"a","s":"Rectangular. Findings of Mohenjo-Daro: Prepared Garments, Temple-like Palace, Pashupati seal, Statue of a dancing girl, Ivory weight balance, The Great Bath, The Great Granary, and Priest-king statue.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":30,"n":31,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Most Harappan seals made of ____.","o":{"a":"Bricks","b":"Granite","c":"Steatite","d":"Brass"},"a":"c","s":"Steatite (a kind of soft stone Some seals were also made of terracotta, gold, agate, chert, ivory, and faience. Two important seals were the Pashupati seal and the Unicorn seal.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":31,"n":32,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"A celt is ______ from the Neolithic period.","o":{"a":"a tomb","b":"a house","c":"a tool","d":"an urn"},"a":"c","s":"a tool . The term Neolithic Period refers to the last stage of the Stone Age. Three different periods: Palaeolithic (Old Stone Age), Mesolithic (Middle Stone Age), and Neolithic (New Stone Age). The Neolithic period is significant for its megalithic architecture, the spread of agricultural practices, and the use of polished stone tools.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":32,"n":33,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"'Microliths' were ______ used by early humans.","o":{"a":"coins","b":"stone tools","c":"clothes","d":"earthen pots"},"a":"b","s":"Stone tools . The microliths were used in spear points and arrowheads. These tools were used to cut, pound, crush, and access new foods-including meat from large animals.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":33,"n":34,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following is a Palaeolithic site of archaeological importance in India?","o":{"a":"Chirand","b":"Burzahom","c":"Hunasagi","d":"Mehrgarh"},"a":"c","s":"Hunasagi is a palaeolithic site of archaeological importance in India. Some Old Stone Age stone-cutting equipment has been found and kept in the Hunasagi Gram Panchayat office.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":34,"n":35,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following Harappan sites is located in Afghanistan?","o":{"a":"Shortughai","b":"Balakot","c":"Nageswar","d":"Kalibangan"},"a":"a","s":"Shortughai . Shortugai was a trading post of Harappan times and it seems to be connected with lapis lazuli mines located in the surrounding area. It also might have connections with Tin trade (found in Afghanistan) and camel trade, along with other Afghan valuables.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":35,"n":36,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Prehistoric And Indus Valley","q":"Which of the following sites of Indus valley civilization is not on the bank of river Indus?","o":{"a":"Chanhudaro","b":"Mohenjodaro","c":"Ropar","d":"Kot-Diji"},"a":"c","s":"Ropar is situated on the banks of the Sutlej River. Features - It was identified by excavations such as the steatite seal and marks on charred clay. Copper implements, as well as terracotta beads and bangles, were discovered.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":36,"n":37,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Asikni, which was a part of Sapta-Sindhu, is the name of which of the following rivers in present times ?","o":{"a":"Jhelum","b":"Beas","c":"Chenab","d":"Ravi"},"a":"c","s":"Chenab. Ancient names of Indian Rivers - Vitastata (Jhelum), Purushni (Ravi), Shatudri (Satluj), Vipasha (Beas), Sadanira (Gandak), Drishdvati (Ghaghara).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":37,"n":38,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following Vedas depicts the life of the early Vedic people of India and has a collection of 1028 hymns divided into 10 mandalas?","o":{"a":"Samaveda","b":"Atharvaveda","c":"Yajurveda","d":"Rigveda"},"a":"d","s":"Rigveda. There are 1028 mantras in the Rigveda which include the unpromising Valakhilia hymns. They were composed in an archaic language (evolved into classical Sanskrit). The Rig Veda is the earliest of the four Vedas and one of the most important texts of the Hindu tradition. It is a large collection of hymns in praise of the gods, which are chanted in various rituals. Other Vedas:- Samaveda (The earliest reference for singing), Yajurveda (It is also called the book of prayers), Atharvaveda (The book of magic and charms).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":38,"n":39,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following Vedas is found in two recensions, black and white, and is full of rituals to be performed publicly or individually?","o":{"a":"Rigveda","b":"Atharvaveda","c":"Yajurveda","d":"Samaveda"},"a":"c","s":"Yajurveda: Notable Upanishads of Yajurveda - Brihadaranyaka, Isha, Taittriya, Katha, and Maithri. The Rigveda - It also includes the famous Gayatri mantra and the Purusha Sukta. Atharva Veda ('Book of magic spells') - Has 730 hymns (Suktas), and 20 books (Khandas).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":39,"n":40,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following Gods acquires a new epithet 'Lord of the Plough' in the later Vedic period?","o":{"a":"Varuna","b":"Brahma","c":"Vishnu","d":"Indra"},"a":"d","s":"Indra is the Vedic god of thunder, lightning, rain, and war. Rig Veda contains about 250 hymns in praise of Indra, and was also considered as chief god in it. Varuna is the Vedic god of the ocean, cosmic order, and law.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":40,"n":41,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"The Vedic Aryans lived in the area called Sapt-Sindhu, which means area drained by seven rivers. One of the rivers among the seven is Jhelum. What was its ancient name?","o":{"a":"Askini","b":"Parushni","c":"Vipash","d":"Vitasta"},"a":"d","s":"Vitasta. Sapta Sindhu rivers and their ancient/alternate names - Indus (Sindhu, Singi Khamban), Jhelum (Hydaspes), Chenab (Asikini, Chandrabhaga), Ravi (Parushni, Airavati), Beas (Vipasha, Hyphasis), Sutlej (Sutudri, Zarodros) and the Saraswati river. Punjab was known as 'Sapta Sindhu' in the Vedic literature.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":41,"n":42,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"A wealthy person in the early Vedic period was known as___________","o":{"a":"Duhitri","b":"Gaveshna","c":"Gomat","d":"Ravi"},"a":"c","s":"Gomat. Early Vedic Period (1500 - 1000 BC), also known as Rig Vedic Period. Later Vedic Period (1000 - 600 BC). Other Terminologies during Vedic Period: Rajan (Chief) was called Janasya Gopati or Gopa (Protector of Cows), Gavisthis or Gavenshana (Battle for Cows), Purohit (Chief priest and Minister), Senani (Leader of the Army), Samiti (General Assembly of people) and Sabha (Council of elders), Kula (Family), Kulapa (Head of the family), Visu (The group of villages commanded by 'Vishayapati'), Gana (Assembly).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":42,"n":43,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"In which of the following Vedas was Dasarajna war (the war of ten kings) mentioned?","o":{"a":"Yajurveda","b":"Samaveda","c":"Rigveda","d":"Atharvaveda"},"a":"c","s":"Rigveda (Book of Mantras) - The oldest of all Vedas (around 1500 BC); Has 10552 mantras, 1028 hymns and 10 mandalas (Mandalas 1 and 10 are the youngest and the longest books).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":43,"n":44,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following varnas primarily used to do cultivation, animal husbandry and trade during the later Vedic period?","o":{"a":"Kshatriya","b":"Brahmana","c":"Shudra","d":"Vaishya"},"a":"d","s":"Vaishya. Vedic Age (1500-600 BC): Vedas (Rig, Yajur, Sama and Atharva) were composed in this period. Four Varnas : Brahmans (priests and teachers of law), Kshatriyas (warriors, kings, administrators), Vaishyas (merchant-traders), Shudras (artisans and laborers).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":44,"n":45,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"The main (chief) social unit of the Aryans was______ .","o":{"a":"Rajan","b":"Parishad","c":"Gana","d":"Jana"},"a":"d","s":"Jana (Tribes) . Gana - Tribal or clan based assemblies, Parishad - A small body similar to Samitis/sabhas consisting exclusively of Brahmins, Rajan - A kind of Chief or King. Other terms used by Aryans : Ayas - Terms used for metals (copper/bronze), Kula - Family, Kulapas - Head of a family, Gramanis - Head of the village (Grama), Sangrihitri - Treasurer.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":45,"n":46,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the four Vedas contains a collection of magic spells and charms to fend off evil spirits and diseases?","o":{"a":"Rig Veda","b":"Sama Veda","c":"Yajur Veda","d":"Atharva Veda"},"a":"d","s":"Atharva Veda ('Book of magic spells') - Has 730 hymns/suktas, 5987 mantras, and 20 books (Khandas). Sama Veda ('Book of Chant') - The earliest reference for singing; 1875 verses. Yajur Veda ('Book of Rituals') - Has two types, Krishna (or black) and Shukla (or white). These four Vedas are also 'Samhitas' (a collection).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":46,"n":47,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"The Purva Mimansa school of philosophy was founded by ________ in ancient India. Graduate Level 28/06/2023 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Vyasa","b":"Kapila","c":"Patanjali","d":"Jaimini"},"a":"d","s":"Jaimini. Purva Mimansa ('Prior Study') also known as Dharma Mimamsa and Karma Mimamsa ('Study of Actions'). The six principal Hindu darshans are Samkhya, Yoga, Nyaya, Vaisheshika, Mimamsa, and Vedanta. Badarayana - Author of Brahma Sutra or Vedanta Sutra. Patanjali - Philosophy of Yoga sutra. Kapila - Philosophy of Sankhya. Vyasa - Author of the Mahabharata, Vedas and Puranas.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":47,"n":48,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following is the oldest text of ancient India? Higher Secondary 30/06/2023 (Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Mahabhashya","b":"Arthashastra","c":"Rig Veda","d":"Manu Smriti"},"a":"c","s":"Rig Veda. Indra is the chief deity. Gayatri mantra (dedicated to Savitri) taken from Rig-Veda. Mahabhashya - Patanjali. Arthashastra - Chanakya. In 2007 , 30 Rig Veda manuscripts dating from 1800 to 1500 BC were included in the UNESCO's Memory of the World Register.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":48,"n":49,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"India was named 'Bharat' after the name of the Bharat clan. In which of the following Vedas is this clan first mentioned ?","o":{"a":"Atharvaveda","b":"Yajurveda","c":"Samaveda","d":"Rigveda"},"a":"d","s":"Rigveda. There are 1028 Suktas in 10 Mandals (Chapters). Mandalas - 1st and 10th mandals are the newest mandals. Chaturvarna (Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra) is mentioned for the first time in Purusha Sukta of 10th Mandal.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 1"},{"id":49,"n":50,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Four ashrams were recognised by Brahmins around the time when Jainism and Buddhism were becoming popular. Which of the following was NOT a recognised ashrama?","o":{"a":"Parmarth","b":"Vanaprastha","c":"Brahmacharya","d":"Grihastha"},"a":"a","s":"Parmarth. Ashram (A stage of life): There were four stages in which the lives of people were divided - Brahmacharya, Grihastha, Vanaprastha, and Sanyasa. Men (Brahmin, Kshatriya and Vaishya) were allowed to follow the stages of the Ashram. Women were not allowed to study the Vedas, and they had to follow the ashramas chosen by their husbands.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":50,"n":51,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Sanskrit is a part of a family of languages known as ___________ .","o":{"a":"Indo-European","b":"Indo-Asian","c":"Indo-Tibetan","d":"Indo-Dravidian"},"a":"a","s":"Indo-European - It includes Sanskrit, Assamese, Gujarati, Hindi, Kashmiri, and Sindhi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":51,"n":52,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"How many Vedas are there in Indian literature of classical Hinduism?","o":{"a":"Three","b":"Four","c":"One","d":"Two"},"a":"b","s":"Four. Types of Vedas: Rigveda, Samaveda, Yajurveda, and Atharvaveda. Each Veda has four subdivisions - The Samhitas (mantras and benedictions), the Aranyakas (text on rituals, ceremonies, sacrifices and symbolic -sacrifices), the Brahmanas (commentaries on rituals, ceremonies and sacrifices), and the Upanishads (texts discussing meditation, philosophy and spiritual knowledge).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":52,"n":53,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"How many Vedangas are there in total ?","o":{"a":"Ten","b":"Six","c":"Five","d":"Two"},"a":"b","s":"Six Vedangas are - Shiksha (Phonetics), Kalpa (Ritual Canon), Vyakaran (Grammar), Nirukta (Explanation), Chhanda (Vedic meter) and Jyotisha (Astrology).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":53,"n":54,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"How many Mandalas are there in the Rigveda? Higher Secondary 02/08/2022 (Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Six","b":"Four","c":"Eight","d":"Ten"},"a":null,"s":"Ten mandals (books). Rig Veda is the oldest of the four Vedas. It is a collection of 10,600 verses and 1,028 hymns. The Famous Gayatri mantra is in Rig Veda Mandala 3. Each mandala consists of Suktas which are basically eulogies.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":54,"n":55,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"The language of the Aryan texts was: Higher Secondary 03/08/2022 (Shift - 4 )","o":{"a":"Ardh-Magadha Prakrit","b":"Prakrit","c":"Persian","d":"Sanskrit"},"a":"d","s":"Sanskrit was the first language spoken in India. Ardhamagadhi Prakrit was a Middle Indo-Aryan language. Prakrit is a group of vernacular Middle Indo-Aryan languages spoken in India between the third and seventh century BCE.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":55,"n":56,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"The iron implement site of Hastinapur was found in which present day state of India?","o":{"a":"Bihar","b":"Uttar Pradesh","c":"Haryana","d":"Madhya Pradesh"},"a":"b","s":"Uttar Pradesh . Excavations at Hastinapur were carried out in the early 1950s by B.B. Lal, Director General of the Archaeological Survey of India. Hastinapur is famous for many historical temples like Karna temple, Pandeshwar temple, Kailash Parvat. It was also known as the capital city of Pandavas and Kauravas at the time of Mahabharata.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":56,"n":57,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"As per the Rigvedic or Early Vedic Period (1500-1000 BC), who among the following was a River Goddess?","o":{"a":"Agni","b":"Sindhu","c":"Arika","d":"Usha"},"a":"b","s":"Sindhu . The seven rivers mentioned in the Rig-Veda - Sindhu (Indus), Vitasta (Vehit/Jhelum), Asikni (Chenab), Prusni/Eravati (Ravi) Vipasha (Beas), Sutudri (Sutlej) and Sarasvati (which dried up). Agni (fire-god of Hinduism), Usha (Goddess of Dawn), Arika (God of Beauty).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":57,"n":58,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"In the Rig Veda there is a hymn in the form of a dialogue between Sage Vishvamitra and two rivers that were worshipped as goddesses. Which are these rivers?","o":{"a":"Alakananda and Bhagirathi","b":"Ravi and Chenab","c":"Ganga and Yamuna","d":"Beas and Sutlej"},"a":"d","s":"Beas and Sutlej are two rivers that were worshipped as goddesses in the Rig Veda. Devprayag is the place where the rivers Alaknanda and Bhagirathi confluence. The most prominent river in the Rig Veda is Saraswati.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":58,"n":59,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following statements about the 'Gotra' practice in ancient India is true?","o":{"a":"Men and women were expected to marry within the same gotra.","b":"People belonging to the same gotra were regarded as descendants of the person after whom the gotra was named.","c":"Women retained their father's gotra after marriage.","d":"Each gotra was named after a famous king."},"a":"b","s":"Gotra originally referred to the seven lineage segments of the Brahmans (priests), who trace their derivation from seven ancient seers: Atri, Bharadvaja, Bhrigu, Gotama, Kashyapa, Vasishtha, and Vishvamitra.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":59,"n":60,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following varnas was responsible for protecting people and administering justice in ancient India as per rules laid down by the Dharmasutras and Dharmashastras?","o":{"a":"Vaishya","b":"Shudra","c":"Kshatriya","d":"Brahmana"},"a":"c","s":"Kshatriyas (warriors, kings, administrators), Brahmins (priests, teachers, intellectuals), Vaishyas (agriculturalists, traders, farmers) and Shudras (workers, labourers, artisans). Dharmasutras - It contains guidelines for individual and social behaviour, ethical norms, as well as personal, civil and criminal law.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":60,"n":61,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"Which of the following Vedas dealt with music?","o":{"a":"Sama Veda","b":"Rig Veda","c":"Yajur Veda","d":"Atharva Veda"},"a":"a","s":"Sama Veda ('Book Of Chant') - Vedic verses are provided with musical notation and are intended as an aid to the performance of sacred songs. Other Vedas - The Rig Veda ('Book Of Mantras'), The Yajur Veda ('Book Of Ritual'), The Atharva Veda ('Book Of Spell').","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":61,"n":62,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"According to the Puranas, Lord Vishnu took the shape of ______ in order to rescue the earth, which had sunk into the water.","o":{"a":"a lion","b":"a boar","c":"a tiger","d":"an elephant"},"a":"b","s":"A boar . Varaha (Boar), the third of the 10 incarnations (avatars) of the Hindu god Vishnu. According to Hindu iconography, the boar is a symbol of all aspects of sacrifice.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":62,"n":63,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"A person's Vedic approach and his relationship with society are determined by the four goals of life. Which of the following is not among these goals?","o":{"a":"sidhi","b":"artha","c":"moksha","d":"dharma"},"a":"a","s":"Sidhi is not the goal which determines vedic approach of a person and its relationship with the society. The four goals determining a person's approach are - Kama, Artha, Dharma and Moksha.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":63,"n":64,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"According to 'The Ramayana', Ratnakar was the other name of ______.","o":{"a":"Vishvamitra","b":"Gautam","c":"Durbasa","d":"Valmiki"},"a":"d","s":"Valmiki was born as Agni Sharma to a Brahmin named Pracheta (also known as Sumali) of Bhrigu gotra. According to legend he once met the great Sage Narada and had a discourse with him on his duties.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":64,"n":65,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"The main collections of Vedic hymns are called ______.","o":{"a":"Sutra","b":"Pad","c":"Samhita","d":"Mukh"},"a":"c","s":"Samhita . The Rigveda Samhita is the oldest known Vedic Sanskrit text. It is a collection of 1,028 Vedic Sanskrit hymns and 10,600 verses in all, organised into ten books (Sanskrit: mandalas). The hymns are dedicated to Rigvedic deities.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":65,"n":66,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vedic Age","q":"'Purusha Sukta' is a hymn from which Veda?","o":{"a":"Atharvaveda","b":"Yajurveda","c":"Rigveda","d":"Samaveda"},"a":"c","s":"Rigveda . The most commonly used portion of the Sukta contains 24 mantras or stanzas. Purusha Suktam is dedicated to Lord Vishnu, who is described as the great Purusha in the hymn. Purusha Suktam is one of the Pancha Suktam (5 Suktams) - Purusha Suktam, Vishnu Suktam, Sri Suktam, Bhu Suktam, and Nila Suktam.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":66,"n":67,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"The Jain temple at Pattadakal was built by ________.","o":{"a":"Chalukyas","b":"Cholas","c":"Rashtrakutas","d":"Hoysalas"},"a":"c","s":"Rashtrakuta . Other temples made by Rashtrakutas - The Kailasanatha Temple in Ellora, the Navalinga Temples in Pattadakal. Chalukya dynasty - Ravana Phadi Cave (Aihole), Durga Temple (Aihole), Lad Khan temple (Aihole). Chola dynasty - Brihadeeswara temple (Thanjavur), Vijayala - Cholesvara (Narta Malai), Rajarajeshwar (Tanjore), Balasuramanya (Kannanur).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":67,"n":68,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Who was the last tirthankara of the Jainas?","o":{"a":"Vardhamana Mahavira","b":"Chandraprabha","c":"Rishabhanatha","d":"Padmaprabha"},"a":"a","s":"Vardhamana Mahavira: He lived in the 6th century BCE and is considered to be the founder of modern Jainism. He belonged to the Jnatrika Clan and was connected to the royal family of Magadha. He delivered his first sermon at Vipulachla hill, Rajgriha. Chandraprabha was the 8th tirthankara. Padmaprabha was the 6th tirthankara. Parsva Natha was the 23th tirthankara.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":68,"n":69,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"At the age of _____ Mahavira left home and went to live in a forest.","o":{"a":"35","b":"27","c":"33","d":"30"},"a":"d","s":"30 . Vardhaman Mahavira (540 - 468 B.C.) was considered the last Tirthankara of Jainism. He attained highest spiritual knowledge at Jimbhikagrama village under a sal tree aged 42 , called as Kaivalya. He preached his teachings for 30 years and died at Pava (near Rajagriha) aged 72.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":69,"n":70,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Where did Lord Mahavira attain salvation?","o":{"a":"Sonagiri","b":"Pawapuri","c":"Shravanabelagola","d":"Mount Abu"},"a":"b","s":"Pawapuri . Pawapuri or Pawa is located in the Nalanda district in the Bihar state. It is also called Apapapuri, meaning 'the Sinless town'.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":70,"n":71,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Name the holy city recognized as the birthplace of the first and fourth Jain Tirthankaras.","o":{"a":"Varanasi","b":"Dwaraka","c":"Ayodhya","d":"Gaya"},"a":"c","s":"Ayodhya is recognized as the birthplace of the first (Rishabhdev) and fourth (Abhinandannath) Jain Tirthankaras.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":71,"n":72,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"According to Jain Philosophy, the term 'Jina' means _____.","o":{"a":"Iord","b":"free from fetters","c":"worthy","d":"the conqueror"},"a":"d","s":"the conqueror. Jainism - There were 24 Tirthankaras (great teachers who had attained all knowledge (Shurasena and Panchala), Bundelkhand region (Chedi), Rajouri, Hajra and North -West Frontier Province (Kamboja), Banks of Godavari (Asmaka or Assaka), Deoria and Uttar Pradesh (Malla).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":72,"n":98,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Iron mines in Jharkhand attributed to the rise of which of the following kingdoms in ancient India?","o":{"a":"Kuru","b":"Magadha","c":"Kashi","d":"Kushan"},"a":"b","s":"Magadha. Kuru (Meerut and Haryana) - Ruled by an Indo-Aryan clan in the iron age Vedic India (1200 - 900 BC) who ruled the regions between Saraswati and the Ganga river.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 1st shift"},{"id":73,"n":99,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Magadha Mahajanapada was surrounded by the rivers______.","o":{"a":"Ganga and Jhelum","b":"Ganga and Yamuna","c":"Ganga and Son","d":"Ganga and Ghaghara"},"a":"c","s":"Ganga and Son. Magadha became the most powerful among the mahajanapadas in 6th Century AD due to these rivers and fertility of land. Now Magadha is a part of Bihar region.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":74,"n":100,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"In ancient times, the area to the south of the Ganga was known as ______.","o":{"a":"Magadha","b":"Kosala","c":"Anga","d":"Matsya"},"a":"a","s":"Magadha . The earliest reference to the Magadha people occurs in the Atharvaveda, where they are found listed along with the Angas, Gandharis and Mujavats. Magadha (Capitals - Rajgriha and Pataliputra), Kosala (Sravasti), Anga (Champa) and Matsya (Viratnagar) were among the 16 Mahajanapadas.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":75,"n":101,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"________ in Bihar was the capital of Magadha for several years. Later the capital was shifted to Pataliputra (present-day Patna). Higher Secondary 01/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Lumbini","b":"Kalinga","c":"Mathura","d":"Rajagriha"},"a":"d","s":"Rajagriha. Magadha Empire was ruled by following dynasties: Haryanka Dynasty (Bimbisara), Shishunaga Dynasty (Shishunaga), and Nanda Dynasty (Mahapadma Nanda). Alexander invaded North-Western India in 326 BC during the reign of Dhana Nanda (last emperor of Nanda).","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":76,"n":102,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Rulers of Mahajanapadas in ancient India collected taxes on crops at the rate of _____ of what was produced.","o":{"a":"1/3rd","b":"1/5th","c":"1/6th","d":"1/4th"},"a":"c","s":"1/6th. Rulers of mahajanapadas in ancient India collected a tax called 'Bhaga' from the Farmers in their region.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":77,"n":103,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Which of the following pairs of 'Name of Ruler - Empire' is correctly matched? I. Mahapadma Nanda - Magadha Empire II. Ajatasatru - Maurya Empire","o":{"a":"Only II","b":"Neither I nor II","c":"Both I and II","d":"Only I"},"a":"d","s":"Only I. Mahapadma Nanda established the Nanda dynasty in Eastern Magadha. He established Pataliputra (Patna, Bihar) as his kingdom's capital. He is called the \"first historical emperor of India.\".","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":78,"n":104,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Ajatshatru, a ruler of the Haryanka Dynasty, was the son of _______.","o":{"a":"Naga-Dasak","b":"Udayin","c":"Anurudha","d":"Bimbisara"},"a":"d","s":"Bimbisara was the first ruler of Magadha from the Haryanka dynasty . Ajatashatru (492 to 460 BCE or early 4th century BCE) was a king of the Haryanka dynasty of Magadha in East India. He was the son of King Bimbisara and was a contemporary of both Mahavira (Nigantha Nataputta) and Gautama Buddha.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":79,"n":105,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Jainism","q":"Who among the following was the last ruler of the Nanda dynasty?","o":{"a":"Dhanananda","b":"Panduka","c":"Govishanaka","d":"Kaivarta"},"a":"a","s":"Dhana Nanda (died 321 BCE was the last ruler of the Nanda dynasty. He was the youngest of the eight brothers of the dynasty's founder Ugrasena. The Nanda dynasty ended with him in about 321 BCE when Chandragupta Maurya led the foundation of the Mauryan Dynasty.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":80,"n":106,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which of the following was a Ceylonese chronicle that acted as an important literary source of the Mauryan empire?","o":{"a":"Indica","b":"Mudrarakshas","c":"Mahavamsa","d":"Arthasastra"},"a":"c","s":"Mahavamsa - It was written by Mahanama in Pali language. Literary Sources of Mauryan Period: \"Arthashastra\" (Kautilya), \"Indica\" (Megasthenes). Buddhist Literature (Jatakas, Digha Nikaya and Sumangalavilasi) , Jain Literature (Hemchandra's Jaina Parishishta describes Chandragupta's life including his embrace of Jainism) etc .","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":81,"n":107,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was the last Mauryan ruler and was killed by his commander-in-chief?","o":{"a":"Dashratha","b":"Brihadratha","c":"Shatadhanvan","d":"Bindusara"},"a":"b","s":"Brihadratha. He was killed by Pushyamitra Shunga who founded the Shunga Dynasty. Mauryan Empire was founded by Chandragupta Maurya in 321 B.C by defeating Dhanananda of Nanda dynasty. Their capital was Patliputra. List of Mauryan Empire Rulers - Chandragupta, Bindusara, Ashoka, Dasharatha, Samprati, Shalishuka, Devavarman, Shatadhanvan, Brihadratha.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":82,"n":108,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who among the following Mauryan kings turned to Buddhism and its tenet of non-violence after the Kalinga war ?","o":{"a":"Ashoka","b":"Dasharatha","c":"Chandragupta Maurya","d":"Bindusara"},"a":"a","s":"Ashoka - He was the third Mauryan emperor. Ashoka's 13th Rock Edict describes the Kalinga war. He patronised the third Buddhist council in Pataliputra presided over by Moggaliputta Tissa. Chandragupta Maurya was a follower of Jainism. Bindusara was a follower of Ajivika religion.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":83,"n":109,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who among the following Mauryan kings fasted to death and died at Shravanabelagola?","o":{"a":"Bindusara","b":"Dasharatha","c":"Ashoka","d":"Chandragupta Maurya"},"a":"d","s":"Chandragupta Maurya: His Greek name was Sandrocottos. He was the founder of the Mauryan Empire. He occupied patliputra (Magadh Empire) in 322 B.C by defeating Dhanananda of Nanda Empire. During his tenure Megasthenese (Greek Historian) visited India. He used to follow Jainism. He was succeeded by Bindusara (Son of Chandragupta). Ashoka was the son and the successor of Bindusara. Dashratha was the Grandson of Ashoka.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":84,"n":110,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"The foundation of Sanchi Stupa was laid by which of the following Kings?","o":{"a":"Samudragupta","b":"Bimbisara","c":"Ashoka","d":"Chandragupta Maurya"},"a":"c","s":"Ashoka. Other Famous Stupas in India - Mahabodhi Stupa, Bodh Gaya (Bihar); Shanti Stupa, Leh (Ladakh); Dhamekh Stupa, Sarnath (Uttar Pradesh); Amaravati Stupa (Andhra Pradesh); Dro-dul Chorten Stupa (Sikkim).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":85,"n":111,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"What was the function of an officer called Samaharta?","o":{"a":"Reserve the state treasury","b":"Tax assessment","c":"Security assurance","d":"To correspond"},"a":"b","s":"Tax assessment. Officers during the Mauryan administration: Shulkadhyaksha (Officer-in-charge of royal income), Koshadhyaksha (Treasury officer), Lakshanadhyaksha (Coin minting), Rajjukas (Land measurer), Yukta (Subordinate revenue officer), Sannidata (Treasury head), Panyadhyaksha (Office of commerce department).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":86,"n":112,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Ashoka sent a mission to spread the principle of Dhamma led by his son, Mahendra and daughter, Sanghamitra to_________.","o":{"a":"Kalinga","b":"Ceylon","c":"Cambodia","d":"Thailand"},"a":"b","s":"Ceylon. They were appointed to carry out the duties of a Buddhist missionary. Ashoka the Great (268-232 BCE) was the third Mauryan emperor who embraced Buddhism and promoted the concept of dhamma (righteousness).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":87,"n":113,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who sent the Greek ambassador Megasthenes to the court of Chandragupta Maurya?","o":{"a":"Seleucus","b":"Tolmi","c":"Dionysus","d":"Sikandar"},"a":"a","s":"Seleucus I Nicator (founded the Seleucid Empire ) was a Macedonian Greek general, officer and successor of Alexander the Great. Chandragupta Maurya - Became a Jain monk and committed Sallekhana (Practice of fasting till death) at Shravanabelagola.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":88,"n":114,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was defeated by Mauryan emperor Chandragupta Maurya?","o":{"a":"Cassander","b":"Seleucus Nicator","c":"Antigones","d":"Ptolemy"},"a":"b","s":"Seleucus Nicator Megasthenes: He wrote the book \"Indica\". After Alexander's death his empire was divided between four of his generals: Cassander, Ptolemy, Antigonus, and Seleucus.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":89,"n":115,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Chandragupta Maurya overthrew the Nanda Dynasty of Magadha with the help of _________.","o":{"a":"Skandagupta","b":"Chandragupta II","c":"Vishnugupta","d":"Samudragupta"},"a":"c","s":"Vishnugupta (Kautilya Chandragupta Maurya - Founder of Maurya dynasty (321-185 BCE). The Nanda dynasty (343 BCE to 321 BCE) - founded by Mahapadma Nanda. Magadha was ruled by the Haryanka dynasty, the Shishunaga dynasty, the Nanda dynasty, the Mauryan dynasty, the Shunga dynasty and the Kanva dynasty . Samudragupta - Known as the \"Indian Napoleon\".","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":90,"n":116,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Emperor Ashoka conquered Kalinga after how many years of his coronation?","o":{"a":"5 years","b":"8 years","c":"11 years","d":"15 years"},"a":"b","s":"8 years. Ashoka fought the Kalinga War in 261 BC. King Ashoka (268 - 232 BC) was the third king of the Mauryan Empire best known for his renunciation of war, and development of the concept of dhamma (pious social conduct).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":91,"n":117,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"How many classes of Indian society are mentioned in Megasthenes book Indica?","o":{"a":"12","b":"10","c":"5","d":"7"},"a":"d","s":"7 . They were Brahmins (upper class), Philosophers, Farmers, Herders, Artisans, Military, Overseers, Councillors and Assessors. Other Foreign travelers and their books: Al -biruni (Kitab-ul-hind), Ibn Battuta (Rihla).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 3rd shift"},{"id":92,"n":118,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"According to Kautilya's Arthashastra, the Commanding Officer of the cavalry in the Mauryan administration was known as:","o":{"a":"Rathadhyaksha","b":"Hastyadhyaksha","c":"Ashwadhyaksha","d":"Patyadhyaksha"},"a":"c","s":"Ashwadhyaksha. Officers in Mauryan Empire: Mantrin (Chief Minister), Senapati (Commander-in -Chief), Yuvraj (Crown prince), Shulkadhyaksha (Officer in-charge of royal income), Prashasti (Prison head), Koshadhyaksha (Treasury officer), Vyabharika (Chief Judge), Dandapala (Head of Police), Annapala (Head of food grains department), Pradeshika (District administrator), Hastyadhyaksha (Chief Commander of Elephant Corps), Rathadhyaksha (Chief Commander of Chariot Corps) and Nayaka (City security officer).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 4th shift"},{"id":93,"n":119,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"The Mauryan pillar capital found at __________ is popularly known as the Lion Capital.","o":{"a":"Bhabru","b":"Bairat","c":"Sanchi","d":"Sarnath"},"a":"d","s":"Sarnath Lion Capital - Erected by Ashoka (Around 250 BCE) in commemoration of Dhammachakra pravartana or the first sermon of Buddha. The Lion Capital of Ashoka at Sarnath is the Indian National Emblem. The inscription Satyamev Jayate is taken from the Mundaka Upanishad.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":94,"n":120,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which of the following texts lays down minute details of the administrative and military organisation during the Mauryan empire? Higher Secondary 27/06/2023 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Brihat Samhita","b":"Nitisara","c":"Shulba Sutra","d":"Arthashastra"},"a":"d","s":"Arthashastra - It focused primarily on principles and practice of statecraft, Written in the Sanskrit language by Kautilya (Chanakya /Vishnugupta), who was the Prime Minister under Chandragupta Maurya's reign. Brihat Samhita (Written by Varahamira) - It is an exhaustive study on architecture, temples, planetary motions, eclipses, timekeeping, astrology and mathematics. Nitisara ( Written by Kamandaki) - Ancient Indian treatise on politics and statecraft. Shulba Sutra - It provides geometry relevant to the fire-altar building and is part of the Srauta rite.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":95,"n":121,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Nagarjuni Caves were donated to which of the following sects by Mauryans? Higher Secondary 28/06/2023 ( Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Ajivika","b":"Buddhism","c":"Lokayat","d":"Jainism"},"a":"a","s":"Ajivika - It was founded by Goshala Maskariputra. It is based on the philosophy that the affairs of the entire universe were ordered by a cosmic force called Niyati that determined all events, including an individual's fate. The seven caves were created by Mauryan time for the Ajivika Sect. Barabar Caves - Karna Chaupar, Sudama Cave, Lomas Rishi Cave, Vishwamitra (Visva Zopri) Cave. Nagaragunja Caves - Gopi Cave, Bahayak Cave and Vedantika Cave.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":96,"n":122,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"According to the Ashokan Inscription, which of the following was NOT a provincial center in the Magadha Empire?","o":{"a":"Tosali","b":"Ujjayini","c":"Taxila","d":"Indraprastha"},"a":"d","s":"Indraprastha. James Prinsep - First person to decipher Ashoka's edicts. These Ashoka's inscriptions are the first tangible evidence of Buddhism.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":97,"n":123,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"As per the contents mentioned in Asokan inscriptions, how many major political centres were there in the Mauryan empire?","o":{"a":"five","b":"six","c":"four","d":"three"},"a":"a","s":"Five. Major Rock Edicts of Ashoka: Rock Edict I - prohibits animal slaughter. Rock Edict II - describes Chola, Pandyas, Satyapur and Keralaputra Kingdom of South India. Rock Edict IV - Impact of Dhamma on society. Rock Edict VIII - Describes Asoka's first Dhamma Yatra to Bodhgaya and Bodhi Tree. Rock Edict XIII: Asoka's victory over Kalinga. The victory of Asoka's Dhamma over Greek Kings, Antiochus, Ptolemy, Antigonus, Magas, Alexander and Cholas, and Pandyas. This is the Largest Edict. It mentions Kamboj, Nabhaks, Bhoja, and Andhra.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":98,"n":124,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which Mauryan ruler was called Amitrochates by the Greeks?","o":{"a":"Samudragupta","b":"Kanishka","c":"Bindusara","d":"Chandragupta Ⅰ"},"a":"c","s":"Bindusara. He was the son of Chandragupta Maurya and was the second Mauryan emperor of India whose reign from 297 - 273 BCE . He was also known as Amitrochates (in Greek) or Amitraghata (in Sanskrit) meaning Slayer of all enemies.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":99,"n":125,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"There were several cities in the Mauryan empire. Which of the following cities was located on the southernmost side?","o":{"a":"Girnar","b":"Topra","c":"Brahmagiri","d":"Rupnath"},"a":"c","s":"Brahmagiri. The Maurya Empire: Founder - Chandragupta Maurya, Capital - Pataliputra (modern Patna). Three important rulers of the Mauryan Empire - Chandragupta, his son Bindusara, and Bindusara's son, Ashoka. Important animals on capital of Ashoka: Sarnath Pillar (Varanasi) - Four Lions, Sanchi Pillar (Madhya Pradesh) - Four lions, but it is broken, Rampurva Pillar (Bihar) - Single Bull, Sankissa Pillar (Farrukhabad, Uttar Pradesh) - Single Elephant, Lauriya Nandangarh Pillar (West Champaran Bihar) - Single Lion.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":100,"n":126,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which of the following places does not contain the major inscription of Ashoka ?","o":{"a":"Girnar","b":"Kandahar","c":"Sanchi","d":"Kalsi"},"a":"c","s":"Sanchi. It is a famous Buddhist site with several stupas and monasteries. Language used in Ashoka's inscriptions - Prakrit . Major inscriptions found at Allahabad, Kandahar, Kalinga, Sannati, Rummindei, Girnar, Shahbazgarhi and Mansehra. Ashoka's name is only used in four of these locations: Maski, Brahmagiri (Karnataka), Gujjara (Madhya Pradesh) and Nettur (Andhra Pradesh).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":101,"n":127,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"What was the purpose behind the Pillars of Ashoka?","o":{"a":"To spread bherighosha","b":"To spread mass conversion","c":"To spread sectarian faith","d":"To spread the dharma policies"},"a":"d","s":"To spread the dharma of policies. Pillars are inscribed with edicts by the 3rd Mauryan Emperor Ashoka the Great (c. 268 to 232 BCE). There are seven pillar edicts. The Seventh Pillar Edict mentions Dhamma Mahamattas and it is the longest pillar almost of all.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":102,"n":128,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which caves are considered as the oldest surviving rock-cut caves in India dating back to the Maurya period?","o":{"a":"Barabar and Nagarjuni caves","b":"Bagh Caves","c":"Elephanta Caves","d":"Ajanta and Ellora Caves"},"a":"a","s":"Barabar and Nagarjuni caves - Located in Jehanabad district of Bihar, Constructed by Emperor Ashoka for the benefit of Ajivika ascetics. They were cut from a single solid granite block.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":103,"n":129,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Somewhat before the time of the Mauryan empire, about _______ years ago, emperors in China began building the Great Wall.","o":{"a":"1200","b":"3500","c":"2400","d":"600"},"a":"c","s":"2400 . Emperor Qin Shi Huang ordered construction of the Great Wall around 221 B.C. It was meant to protect the northern frontier of the empire from pastoral people.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":104,"n":130,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Pushyamitra, who was the commander of Brihadratha, the last Mauryan emperor, killed the king and established a new dynasty. Which of the following was his dynasty?","o":{"a":"Shunga","b":"Kanva","c":"Satavahana","d":"Chedi"},"a":"a","s":"Shunga. Shunga Dynasty Founder - Pushyamitra Sunga. Ruled - From 185 BCE to 73 BCE. Last Ruler - Devabhuti. Kanva Dynasty Founder - Vasudeva Kanva. Ruled - From 73 BCE to 28 BCE. Last Ruler - Susarma. Satavahana Dynasty Founder - Simuka . Famous Ruler - Gautamiputra Satakarni.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":105,"n":131,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Ashoka appointed ______ to solve the social problems in his region.","o":{"a":"samaharta","b":"nyayadhish","c":"amatya","d":"dhamma mahamatta"},"a":"d","s":"Dhamma Mahamatta Tirthas: The highest category of officials in the administration. There were 18 Tirthas. Amatyas: High ranking officials almost like present-day secretaries. The important officials in the revenue department are Sannidhata: Chief treasury, Samaharta: collector general of revenue. Yuktas: Subordinate officers responsible for the Empire's revenue. Rajjukas: Officers in charge of land measurement and boundary-fixing.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":106,"n":132,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which of the following statements is correct? I. Most of Ashoka's inscriptions were written in Brahmi script. II. Many of Chanakya's ideas were written down in a book called the Arthashastra.","o":{"a":"Only II","b":"Only I","c":"Neither I nor II","d":"Both I and II"},"a":"d","s":"Both I and II . Ashoka's inscription was written in Brahmi Script. Brahmi Script was deciphered by James Prinsep.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":107,"n":133,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which of the following rulers did NOT belong to the Maurya dynasty?","o":{"a":"Bindusara","b":"Ashoka","c":"Chandragupta","d":"Bimbisara"},"a":"d","s":"Bimbisara is considered the main founder of the Haryanka dynasty. He had his capital at Girivraja/Rajagriha (Rajgir). He was a contemporary and a follower of the Buddha. He was the first king to have a standing army.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":108,"n":134,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was the first Mauryan ruler who tried to spread his message to the people through inscriptions?","o":{"a":"Chandragupta","b":"Ashoka","c":"Brihadratha","d":"Bindusara"},"a":"b","s":"Ashoka (Mauryan Emperor His inscriptions were carved on pillars, as well as on rock surfaces. James Prinsep, was the first person to decipher Ashoka's edicts. These Ashoka's inscriptions are the first tangible evidence of Buddhism. There are 14 major rock edicts, 15 minor rock edicts, and 7 pillar inscriptions.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":109,"n":135,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which of the following names was used for the Indian subcontinent by ancient travellers like Megasthenes, FaHien, Huen Tsang, Al Beruni, Ibn Batuta etc. for active contribution of Indian traders?","o":{"a":"Suvar\u0000a Bhoomi","b":"Suvar\u0000a Jagat","c":"Suvar\u0000a Bet","d":"Suvar\u0000a Desh"},"a":"a","s":"Suvar\u0000a Bhoomi (Indian Subcontinent), appears in many ancient Indian literary sources and Buddhist texts such as the Mahavamsa, Jataka tales, the Milinda Panha, and the Ramayana. The famous book on India: Megasthenes- 'Indica', Fahein- 'Fo - kwo - ki', Hiuen Tsang- 'Si-yu-ki', Al-Biruni- 'Kitab-ul-Hind', and Ibn Batuta- 'The Rihla'.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":110,"n":136,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which two kings fought in the Battle of Hydaspes?","o":{"a":"Chandragupta and Dhana Nanda","b":"Alexander and Porus","c":"Ashoka and Mahapadmanabha","d":"Mihirakula and Yasodharman"},"a":"b","s":"Alexander (Sikandar) and Porus fought in the Battle of Hydaspes. It was fought in the year 326 BC. It took place on the banks of the Jhelum River in the Punjab region of the Indian subcontinent.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":111,"n":137,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Ashokan Minor Rock Edicts are found in different parts of India. Which of the following is NOT a find spot of Ashokan Minor Rock Edicts in Karnataka?","o":{"a":"Brahmagiri","b":"Gavimath","c":"Rupnath","d":"Maski"},"a":"c","s":"Rupnath. Minor rock edicts of Ashoka - Kandahar (Afghanistan), Lampaka (Afghanistan), Bahapur (Delhi), Bairat (Rajasthan), Bhabru (Rajasthan), Gujarra (Madhya Pradesh), Rupnath (Madhya Pradesh), Panguraria (Madhya Pradesh), Sasaram (Bihar), Barabar Caves (Bihar), Rajula-Mandagiri, Andhra Pradesh, Suvarna Giri (Andhra Pradesh), Brahmagiri (Karnataka), Jatina- Rameshwara, Karnataka, Siddapur (Karnataka), Maski (Karnataka), Nittur (Karnataka), Udegolam (Karnataka), Palkigundu and Gavimath (Karnataka).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":112,"n":138,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"A type of court called 'Kantakasodhana' was prevalent in the ______ Empire.","o":{"a":"Kushana","b":"Mauryan","c":"Chola","d":"Rashtrakuta"},"a":"b","s":"Mauryan Empire. It literally means 'removal of the thorns' and was used in reference to criminal justice in ancient India. Kautilya also mentioned the cases that are subject to kantaka shodhana in his book Arthashastra.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":113,"n":139,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who among the following published a set of Ashokan inscriptions in 1877 ?","o":{"a":"DC Sircar","b":"MS Vats","c":"Alexander Cunningham","d":"Colin Mackenzie"},"a":"c","s":"Alexander Cunningham published a set of Ashokan inscriptions in the year 1877. He was the founder of the Archaeological Survey of India. In 1851, he explored the Buddhist monuments of Central India. He had conducted excavations at Sarnath, Sankasya and Sanchi.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":114,"n":140,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was the first scholar to decipher the Ashokan edicts?","o":{"a":"Alexander Cunningham","b":"James Prinsep","c":"James Taylor","d":"V Gordon Childe"},"a":"b","s":"James Prinsep was the first scholar to decipher the Ashokan edicts. Most of the dialects of Ashokan edicts were written in Prakrit language using Brahmi and Kharosthi scripts. Alexander Cunningham is also known as the father of Indian Archaeology.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":115,"n":141,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which was the first empire in ancient India to use elephants in its war on a large scale?","o":{"a":"Shunga","b":"Chola","c":"Kushans","d":"Magadha"},"a":"d","s":"Magadha . The Maurya Empire was a geographically extensive Iron Age historical power in South Asia based in Magadha, founded by Chandragupta Maurya in 322 BCE. King Porus used elephants against Alexander in the battle of hydaspes.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":116,"n":142,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"In Indian history, who were referred to as the 'atavika rajya'?","o":{"a":"South Indian kingdom","b":"Republican state","c":"Forest kingdom","d":"Mughal empire"},"a":"c","s":"Forest kingdom . Their early reference is found in the Mauryan period. During the age of Mahajanpadas, Jharkhand state was a part of Magadha and Anga. In the Mauryan period, this region was ruled by a number of states, which were collectively known as the Atavika (forest) states.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":117,"n":143,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Mauryan Dynasty","q":"Which of the following was NOT a type of sacrifice performed by kings in ancient India to establish their position ?","o":{"a":"Vajapeya","b":"Rajasuya","c":"Ashvamedha","d":"Muvendavelan"},"a":"d","s":"Muvendavelan was a prominent military officer for a king of the Chola Throne. Vajapeya was a Soma Yajna. Rajasuya (Imperial Sacrifice or the king's inauguration sacrifice) is a Srauta ritual of the Vedic religion. It is a consecration of a king. Ashvamedha is a horse sacrifice ritual followed by the Śrauta tradition of Vedic religion.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":118,"n":144,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Samudragupta's achievements are recorded in a long inscription called Prashasti by his court poet, ______________.","o":{"a":"Harisena","b":"Rudrasena","c":"Kalhan","d":"Kalidasa"},"a":"a","s":"Harisena. Samudragupta is the successor of Chandragupta I. He also assumed the titles of Kaviraj and Vikramanka.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":119,"n":145,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"The paintings in the Bagh caves belong to which of the following periods?","o":{"a":"Maukhari","b":"Maurya","c":"Chola","d":"Gupta"},"a":"d","s":"Gupta. Bagh Caves are located in Gwalior (Madhya Pradesh). These are a group of nine rock-cut monuments. These caves were founded by the Buddhist monk Dataka. Caves in India - Undavalli caves (Andhra Pradesh), Barabar Caves (Bihar), Badami Cave Temples (Karnataka), Udaygiri Caves (Madhya Pradesh), Ajanta Caves and Ellora Caves (Maharashtra).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":120,"n":146,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"The Vakataka dynasty was directly related to which Gupta emperor?","o":{"a":"Chandra Gupta I","b":"Shree Gupta","c":"Samudra Gupta","d":"Chandra Gupta II"},"a":"d","s":"Chandra Gupta II. Vakataka dynasty : Founded by Vindhyashakti in 250 CE. Ajanta caves (numbers ⅩⅥ, ⅩⅦ, ⅩⅨ) are the best examples of Vakataka excellence in the field of painting. Shree Gupta (240 to 280 CE) was founder of the Gupta dynasty. Chandra Gupta I (319 to 335 CE) assumed the title of 'Maharajadhiraja. Samudra Gupta (\"Napolean of India\" title given by VA Smith, 335 to 375 CE) - He was given the title of Kaviraj.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":121,"n":147,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"In which period is the first inscriptional example of the practice of Sati found?","o":{"a":"Maukhari","b":"Vardhana","c":"Satavahana","d":"Gupta"},"a":"d","s":"Gupta . Practice of Sati has been found in the Eran Pillar Inscription of Bhanugupta, Madhya Pradesh. Sati Movement : Raja Ram Mohan Roy described 'Sati' as 'murder' according to every shastra. By his efforts, Bengal Sati Regulation Act was passed to criminalize the practice of Sati in 1829.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":122,"n":148,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"The famous source of the Gupta empire, the Prayaga Prashasti is also known as the ________. Graduate Level 27/06/2023 ( Shift - 4)","o":{"a":"Allahabad pillar inscription","b":"Ahmedabad pillar inscription","c":"Bithur pillar inscription","d":"Lucknow pillar inscription"},"a":"a","s":"Allahabad pillar inscription composed in Sanskrit by Harishena, the court poet of Samudragupta.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":123,"n":149,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Who is given the credit for starting the Gupta era?","o":{"a":"Samudragupta","b":"Chandragupta II","c":"Kumaragupta","d":"Chandragupta I"},"a":"d","s":"Chandragupta I (320 - 335 CE) . Sri Gupta (late 3rd century CE) - Founded the Gupta dynasty. Skandagupta (455-467 CE) - Considered the last of the great Gupta Emperors.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 2"},{"id":124,"n":150,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Amar singh was one of the luminaries in the court of ________.","o":{"a":"Chandragupta II","b":"Kumaragupta","c":"Chandragupta I","d":"Samudragupta"},"a":"a","s":"Chandragupta II (380 - 415 CE). He was the third ruler of the Gupta Dynasty, Son of Samudragupta. He defeated the Sakas to annex Gujarat and Saurashtra and made Ujjain his second capital. First Gupta ruler to issue Silver coins known as 'Rupaka'.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 3"},{"id":125,"n":151,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Who was the court poet of Harshavardhana?","o":{"a":"Hema Saraswati","b":"Parmanand","c":"Banabhatta","d":"Sri Ponna"},"a":"c","s":"Banabhatta - His teacher's name was Bhatsu or Bharvu. Banabhatta's three works are famous - \"Harshacharita\", \"Kadambari\" and \"Chandishtak\". Harshvardhan was the ruler of the Pushyabhuti dynasty of Thaneshwar.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":126,"n":152,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Chandragupta, was the first ruler of the Gupta dynasty to adopt the grand title of .","o":{"a":"dharamraja","b":"maharaja-dhiraja","c":"maharaja","d":"devanampriya"},"a":"b","s":"Maharaja-dhiraja. Gupta Dynasty : Founder - Sri Gupta (Maharaja). Chandragupta - 1 married the Lichchhavi Princess Kumaradevi. Fahien visited India during the reign of Chandragupta II. Kumaragupta laid the foundation of Nalanda University.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":127,"n":153,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Who among the following Navratnas from Chandragupta II court wrote 'Mantrashastra' ?","o":{"a":"Kalidasa","b":"Ghatakarapara","c":"Vetala Bhatta","d":"Amarasimha"},"a":"c","s":"Vetala Bhatta. Nine jewels (Navratna) of Chandragupta II - Amarasimha, Dhanvantari, Harisena, Kalidasa, Kshapanaka, Shanku, Varahamihira, Varuchi, Vetala- Bhatta. The great Sanskrit poet and playwright Kalidasa was the most notable of nine jewels.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":128,"n":154,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"The reign of Kumaragupta I the son and successor of _____ was one of peace and relative inactivity.","o":{"a":"Chandragupta I","b":"Skandagupta","c":"Chandragupta II","d":"Samudragupta"},"a":"c","s":"Chandragupta II ( successor of Samudragupta ). He adopted the title 'Vikramaditya'. Kumaragupta I: He was also known as Shakraditya and Mahendraditya. He founded Nalanda Mahavihara in 5th century CE. His successor - Skandagupta.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":129,"n":155,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Which king was shown on his coins as a Veena player?","o":{"a":"Ashoka","b":"Chandragupta Maurya","c":"Samudragupta","d":"Chandragupta I"},"a":"c","s":"Samudragupta - He was a ruler of the Gupta dynasty. On several of his coins, he was depicted as seated on a sofa and playing a Veena. He is known as the Napoleon of India. He was given the title of Kaviraj.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":130,"n":156,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"After the victory over Sakas, who among the following adopted the title of 'Vikramaditya\"?","o":{"a":"Chandragupta I","b":"Chandragupta II","c":"Skandagupta","d":"Kumaragupta I"},"a":"b","s":"Chandragupta II - He was the son of Samudragupta and Datta Devi. He ruled the Gupta Empire from 380 to 415 AD during the Golden Age of India. He established the Mehrauli iron pillar. Gupta Empire (320 - 550 CE) - It stretched across northern, central and parts of southern India.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":131,"n":157,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Who among the following succeeded Ghatotkacha, the Gupta ruler?","o":{"a":"Kumaragupta I","b":"Chandragupta I","c":"Chandragupta II","d":"Skandagupta"},"a":"b","s":"Chandragupta I. Founder - Sri Gupta, Next Successors - Ghatotkacha, Chandragupta I, Samudragupta, Chandragupta II, Kumaragupta I (founded Nalanda University).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":132,"n":158,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Ruler of which dynasty built the famous Iron pillar in Delhi?","o":{"a":"Gupta","b":"Pallava","c":"Maurya","d":"Pushyabhuti"},"a":"a","s":"Gupta dynasty. In Mehrauli (Delhi) an iron pillar was constructed by the famous king Chandragupta II, India, notable for the rust-resistant composition of the metal used in its construction. The inscription on the pillar is in Sanskrit, about Gupta emperor Chandragupta II.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":133,"n":159,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Which of the following offices was NOT held by the poet Harishena?","o":{"a":"Sandhi-vigrahika","b":"Maha-danda-nayaka","c":"Nagara-shreshthi","d":"Kumar-amatya"},"a":"c","s":"Nagara-shreshthi (Chief banker or merchant). Offices held by Poet Harishena: Maha-danda-Nayaka (Chief Judicial Officer), Kumar-amatya (Important minister) and a sandhi - vigrahika (Minister of War and Peace).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":134,"n":160,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Fa Xian began his journey back home from which of the following Indian state?","o":{"a":"Bihar","b":"Maharashtra","c":"Bengal","d":"Odisha"},"a":"c","s":"Bengal. Fa-Xian (Chinese traveller) started his journey back home from Bengal by boarding a merchant ship. He traveled from China to India on foot to acquire Buddhist texts. He came to India, under the reign of Chandragupta II, and arrived at Pataliputra from the northwest.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":135,"n":161,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Xuan Zang and other pilgrims spent time studying in Nalanda, the most famous Buddhist monastery, located in which of the following Indian states?","o":{"a":"Odisha","b":"Bengal","c":"Bihar","d":"Sikkim"},"a":"c","s":"Bihar. Kumaragupta (Shakraditya) of the Gupta dynasty founded Nalanda University in modern Bihar in the early 5th century. Bakhtiyar Khilji (general of Qutbuddin Aibak) was demolished in the 12 th century (1193 AD). Nalanda: UNESCO World Heritage Site, Institute of National Importance, Famous scholars (Nagarjuna (Madhyamika Sunyavada) and Aryabhatta), 3 buildings (Ratnasagar, Ratna Dadhi, and Ratna Ranjak), Visitors (Hiuen Tsang, I-Tsing).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":136,"n":162,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Samudragupta's mother belonged to which of the following gana?","o":{"a":"Koliya","b":"Lichchhavi","c":"Sakya","d":"Vajji"},"a":"b","s":"Licchavi. Samudragupta (335 -375 CE, Kaviraja) was the son of Chandragupta I and Queen Kumaradevi (Licchavi clan). He is called 'Napoleon of India' by V.A. Smith.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":137,"n":163,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Which of the following titles was held by Samudragupta, a ruler of the Gupta Dynasty?","o":{"a":"Jahan Panah","b":"Sahib-e-Qiran","c":"Kaviraja","d":"Mahendraditya"},"a":"c","s":"Kaviraja . Samudragupta: Ruled under Gupta dynasty from 335 /336 CE to 375 CE; Called 'Napoleon of India' by V.A. Smith and Kaviraja (king among poets); His campaigns are mentioned in the Eran inscription (Madhya Pradesh).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":138,"n":164,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"In the context of Gupta administration, the term 'vithi' referred to a/an:","o":{"a":"elephantry unit","b":"administrative unit","c":"personal guard of the king","d":"soldier"},"a":"b","s":"Administrative unit. In Gupta's period, there was a hierarchy of administrative divisions from top to bottom. The empire was called by various names such as Rajya, Rashtra, Desha, Mandala, Prithvi, and Avani. It was divided into provinces, which were further divided into Vishayas. A Vishayapati administered the Vishaya. A part of the Vishaya was called Vithi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":139,"n":165,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Who among the following Gupta rulers married a girl from the Lichchhavi clan?","o":{"a":"Samudragupta","b":"Sri Gupta","c":"Chandragupta-I","d":"Ramagupta"},"a":"c","s":"Chandragupta-I was a Gupta ruler and married a girl from the Lichchhavi clan. Chandragupta married a girl named Kumaradevi who belonged to the Lichchavi clan. The Lichchhavi clan was headquartered at Vaishali in present-day Bihar during the time of Gautama Buddha.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":140,"n":166,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"Prabhavati Gupta, the Queen Regent of the Vakataka empire, was the daughter of Kuberanaga and ______.","o":{"a":"Skandagupta","b":"Chandragupta I","c":"Chandragupta II","d":"Kumaragupta"},"a":"c","s":"Chandragupta II. Prabhavati Gupta was a Gupta princess and Vakataka queen who was the consort of Maharaja Rudrasena II. She ruled the Vakataka kingdom as regent from about 390 to 410.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":141,"n":167,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Gupta Dynasty","q":"The Gupta imposed a fine called _____ which was a plough tax paid by every cultivator owing a plough.","o":{"a":"Hiranya","b":"Kara","c":"Sulka","d":"Halvakara"},"a":"d","s":"Halvakara was a kind of tax for those who owned a plough during the Gupta administration.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":142,"n":168,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"According to which of the following foreign travellers did Dhruvasena II attend Harsha's assembly at Prayag (Allahabad)?","o":{"a":"Marco Polo","b":"Hiuen Tsang","c":"Fa-Hien","d":"I-tsing"},"a":"b","s":"Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang) - Chinese Buddhist monk who travelled from China to India to study Buddhism and obtain Buddhist scriptures during the reign of King Harsha Vardhan. Assembly of Prayag (Maha Moksha Parishad) - Harsha Vardhan gave all his belongings in charity for the welfare of the public.","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":143,"n":169,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"Who sent an envoy to the Chinese court in 641 AD?","o":{"a":"Narsimhavarman I","b":"Harsha Vardhan","c":"Narsimhavarman II","d":"Pulakeshin I"},"a":"b","s":"Harsha Vardhan. In 641 AD Harshavardhan sent an envoy to Taizong (Tai-Tsung), the Tang emperor of China and in return, the Chinese ruler sent three embassies to Harsha court. Hiuen-Tsang, a Chinese pilgrim who visited Harsha's court, wrote a book 'Si-Yu-Ki' (the world of the west) after going back to China. Hiuen-Tsang in his book also praised two other kings than Harsha Vardhan Narsimha Varman of the Pallava dynasty and Pulakesin II of the Chalukya dynasty.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":144,"n":170,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"After about 75 years of Harshavardhana's death ______ rose to power in Kannauj.","o":{"a":"Yashovarman","b":"Yadagiri","c":"Mahendravarman","d":"Pulakeshin II"},"a":"a","s":"Yashovarman. He was the founder of the Varman dynasty of Kanauj and surrounding areas during the 8th and the 9th centuries. Mahendravarman (600 AD - 630 AD) was a ruler of the Pallava dynasty. Pulakeshin II (610 - 642 AD) was a ruler of the Chalukya dynasty and was a contemporary of Harshavardhana.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":145,"n":171,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"What was the period of Harshavardhana's reign?","o":{"a":"647 CE to 700 CE","b":"570 CE to 610 CE","c":"606 CE to 647 CE","d":"545 CE to 570 CE"},"a":"c","s":"606 CE to 647 CE Harshavardhana - Last ruler of the Vardhana Empire (Pushyabhuti dynasty); Undisputed ruler of Thaneshwar (modern -day Haryana). Empire covers present day states of Odisha, Bengal, Punjab and the whole of Indo-Gangetic plain. Chinese monk and traveler Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang) spent eight years in his empire. He was author of three Sanskrit plays - Nagananda, Ratnavali, Priyadarshika. His Court Poet - Banabhatta.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":146,"n":172,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding king Harshavardhana?","o":{"a":"Harshavardhana ruled nearly about 1400 years ago","b":"Xuan Zang spent a lot of time at Harsha's court","c":"Harshavardhana's court poet was Harishena","d":"Harshacharita is a biography written on Harshavardhana"},"a":"c","s":"Harishena - He was the court poet of Samudragupta (Gupta dynasty).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":147,"n":173,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"Who among the following defeated Harshavardhana when he invaded the Chalukya kingdom in the Deccan?","o":{"a":"Mangalesha","b":"Pulakesin II","c":"Vikramaditya I","d":"Kirtivarman I"},"a":"b","s":"Pulakesin II defeated Harshavardhana when he invaded the Chalukya kingdom in the Deccan on the banks of Narmada River. Mangalesha was a king of the Chalukya dynasty of Vatapi in Karnataka. Vikramaditya I was the third son and followed his father, Pulakeshin II on to the Chalukya throne. Kirttivarman I was a ruler of the Chalukya dynasty of Vatapi (present-day Badami) in India.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":148,"n":174,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"King Shashanka, against whom Harshvardhana declared war, was a ruler of the ______ kingdom.","o":{"a":"Kanyakubja","b":"Junagarh","c":"Magadha","d":"Gauda"},"a":"d","s":"Gauda . Karnasubarna was the capital of Gauda Kingdom during the reign of Shashanka, the first important king of ancient Bengal who ruled in the 7th century.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":149,"n":175,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"Pushyabhuti, who ruled from Thaneswar, was the founder of ______ dynasty.","o":{"a":"Vardhana","b":"Chera","c":"Pandya","d":"Chalukya"},"a":"a","s":"Vardhana dynasty. Prabhakar Vardhana was the first notable king of the Vardhana Dynasty. The dynasty reached its glory under the last king of this dynasty, Harshavardhana .","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":150,"n":176,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Vardhana Dynasty","q":"King Harshavardhan ascended the throne of Thaneshwar and Kannauj on the death of his brother, ______.","o":{"a":"Indravardhan","b":"Suryavardhan","c":"Rajyavardhana","d":"Chandravardhan"},"a":"c","s":"Rajyavardhana. He was the eldest son of Prabhakaravardhana and a member of the Pushyabhuti Dynasty. He ascended the throne after his father's death and was succeeded by his younger brother Harsha.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":151,"n":177,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"The Brihadeswara Temple at Tanjavur built by:","o":{"a":"Rajaraja II","b":"Samudragupta","c":"Rajendra Chola","d":"Rajaraja I"},"a":"d","s":"Rajaraja I: The greatest ruler of the Chola Dynasty (Founder - Vijayalaya). Other temples made by the Chola Rulers: Rajaraja I - Tiruvalisvaram temple (Brahmadesam); Aditya I - Balasuramanya temple (Kannanur); Rajendra Chola - Gangaikondachola -puram temple; Kulothunga III - Kampahareswarartemple (tribhuvanam).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":152,"n":178,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Who founded the Chola empire?","o":{"a":"Dantidurga","b":"Singhavishnu","c":"Upendra Rai","d":"Vijayalaya"},"a":"d","s":"Vijayalaya. Chola Dynasty: Vijayalaya - Given the title Parakesarivarman. Other South Indian Dynasties: Chera Dynasty: Founder - Uthiyan Cheralathan (Perum Chorru). Pandya Dynasty - Kadungon. Vijayanagara Empire - Harihara I and Bukka Raya . Satavahana Dynasty - Simuka. Hoysala Dynasty - Sala / Nrip kaam dwitiya .","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":153,"n":179,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"In the 11th century, the army of which of the following Cholas reached upto Ganga to invade Bengal?","o":{"a":"Rajendra I","b":"Rajaraja I","c":"Vijayalaya","d":"Rajadhiraj I"},"a":"a","s":"Rajendra I (1014 and 1044 CE) - He was also known as Gangaikonda Chola. He led a successful military expedition to the Ganges, bringing Ganges water to his new capital in the Kaveri Delta, Gangaikonda cholapuram. He defeated Mahipala, the Pala king of Bengal and Bihar.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":154,"n":180,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Who among the following rulers invaded Malaya to liberate their trade with China?","o":{"a":"Pallava","b":"Chola","c":"Chalukya","d":"Rashtrakuta"},"a":"b","s":"Chola dynasty . Founder - Vijayalaya, capital - Tanjore.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":155,"n":181,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Sabha and Ur, two kinds of village assemblies are referred to in which of the following dynasties?","o":{"a":"Chola","b":"Rashtrakuta","c":"Gurjara Pratihara","d":"Chalukya"},"a":"a","s":"Chola. Ur - Assembly of common villagers. Sabha - Assembly in the villages which were inhabited predominantly by the Brahmanas. Nagaram - Assembly of merchants, traders and artisans. The Chola dynasty was founded by Vijayalaya Chola. Rajendra Chola III - the last ruler. Capital - Thanjavur (Tanjore).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":156,"n":182,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"\"Brahmadeya\" , a type of land during the Chola Period was the ________.","o":{"a":"land gifted to Brahmans","b":"land of non-Brahmana peasant proprietors","c":"land gifted to temples","d":"land of royal person"},"a":"a","s":"Land gifted to Brahmans There were five types of 'land gifts' that Chola kings gave to their people on the basis of Chola inscriptions : Vellanvagai - For non-Brahmana, peasant proprietors. Shalabhoga - For the maintenance of a school. Devadana or Tirunamattukkani - Land gifted to temples. Pallichchhandam - Land donated to Jaina institutions.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":157,"n":183,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"The Airavatesvara Temple in Tamil Nadu was built by _________ .","o":{"a":"Rajaraja Chola II","b":"Rajaraja Chola I","c":"Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh II","d":"Maharaja Pratap Singh"},"a":"a","s":"Rajaraja Chola II Airavatesvara Temple is a Hindu temple built in the Dravidian style of architecture. Dedicated to - Lord Shiva. Rajaraja Chola I - Built Brihadeeswarar Temple in Thanjavur. Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh II (Founder of Jaipur)..","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":158,"n":184,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"The term \"Vetti\" in Chola empire is related to ___________","o":{"a":"part payments","b":"army","c":"forced labour","d":"gold coins"},"a":"c","s":"Forced labour. Cholas (8th - 12th century AD) - A southern region ruling dynasty began when they defeated the Pallavas to come into power. The empire was founded by Vijalaya. Some types of taxes in their empire: Kadmai or Irai - Tax on land; Pattam and ayam - Taxes imposed on non-agricultural professions like artisans and merchants; Nirvilai or nirkkirai - Water tax; Ulku and sungam - Toll tax; Tattar-pattam - A tax levied on gold -smiths; Sangam - Export and import taxes; Kannala Kanam - Marriage tax; Poorvi - War tax.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":159,"n":185,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Rajaraja I, considered the most powerful Chola ruler, became king in ______.","o":{"a":"999","b":"979","c":"971","d":"985"},"a":"d","s":"985. Rajaraja Chola I (985-1014 AD) is the greatest Chola ruler. He was known as \"Rajaraja the Great''. Other names - Arunmozhivarman and Ponniyin Selvan. He built Rajarajeshwara temple (Brihadeshwara) in Thanjavur, Tamil Nadu. He conquered Sri Lanka.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":160,"n":186,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Which of the following towns was built by the ruler Vijayalaya in Kaveri delta?","o":{"a":"Tiruchirappalli","b":"Tiruppur","c":"Madurai","d":"Thanjavur"},"a":"d","s":"Thanjavur is an important agricultural centre located in the Kaveri Delta (Tamil Nadu), built by Vijayalaya who belonged to the Chola family of Uraiyur. Tiruppur (Located on the banks of Noyyal River in Tamil Nadu, built during the reign of Cholas and Pandyas), Madurai (Located on the river of Vaigai), Tiruchirappalli (located in Kaveri delta in Tamil Nadu).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":161,"n":187,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Sultan Mahmud of Ghazni was a contemporary of which Chola king?","o":{"a":"Rajaraja I","b":"Rajendra II","c":"Rajadhiraja","d":"Rajendra I"},"a":"d","s":"Rajendra I who ruled from 1014 to 1044 CE. Ghazni invaded India seventeen times. In 1018 he plundered the holy city of Mathura and also attacked Kannauj.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":162,"n":188,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"In the early eleventh century, the Chola king Rajendra I built a Shiva temple and filled it with a Sun-pedestal seized from the ______.","o":{"a":"Palas","b":"Vakatakas","c":"Chalukyas","d":"Kalingas"},"a":"c","s":"Chalukyas. Chola king Rajendra-I (reigned between 1014 and 1044 CE) built a Shiva temple in his capital, Gangaikondacholapuram (north Tamil Nadu) and filled it with prized statues seized from defeated rulers such as Sun-pedestal (Chalukyas), a Ganesha statue and several statues of Durga, a Nandi statue, an image of Bhairava and Bhairavi (Kalingas of Orissa), and a Kali statue (Palas of Bengal).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":163,"n":189,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Who among the following belonged to the ancient chiefly family of the Cholas from Uraiyur, captured the Kaveri delta from the Muttaraiyar in the middle of the ninth century ?","o":{"a":"Gandaraditya","b":"Vijayalaya","c":"Parantaka I","d":"Aditya I"},"a":"b","s":"Vijayalaya founded the Chola Empire and captured Thanjavur (Capital of Chola Empire) in 850 CE. He constructed a temple for the goddess Nishumbhasudini in Thanjavur. Aditya I succeeded him in 871 CE. Parantaka I was successor of Aditya I. He was known as the destroyer of Madurai (the capital city of the Pandyas). Gandaraditya succeeded Parantaka I","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":164,"n":190,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Which of the following towns was grown as a temple town during the Chola dynasty?","o":{"a":"Bhillasvamin","b":"Ajmer","c":"Ahmedabad","d":"Ahmednagar"},"a":"a","s":"Bhillasvamin. Famous Chola dynasty temples: Moovar Koil (Kodumbalur), Koranganatha (Musiri), Valisvara Temple, Kampaheswarar Temple (Thirubuvanam). All temples mentioned above are in Tamil Nadu.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":165,"n":191,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Which of the following territories to the south and north were made part of the Chola kingdom by the successors of the ruler Vijayalaya?","o":{"a":"Pallava and Chera","b":"Pandyan and Pallava","c":"Rashtrakuta and Chera","d":"Vakataka and Satavahana"},"a":"b","s":"Pandaya and Pallava. The Pallava dynasty (275 CE to 897 CE, Capital - Kanchipuram) ruled in Deccan (Tondaimandalam). Pandya (Capital - Madurai). The Cheras (Capital - Vanji), Greatest ruler of Cheras - Senguttuvan, also known as Red Chera.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":166,"n":192,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Which dynasty bronze statues were made using the \"lost wax\" technique?","o":{"a":"Chola","b":"Pandya","c":"Chalukya","d":"Pala"},"a":"a","s":"Chola bronzes are created using the lost wax technique (Cire Perdue). Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin. The lost wax technique is a method of metal casting in which molten metal is poured into a mold that has been created by means of a wax model. Examples: Nataraja Bronze Casting, Shiva and Parvati (Sivagami, 1012 CE), etc.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":167,"n":193,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Puhar or Kaveripattinam, was the port of which of the following dynasty?","o":{"a":"Cheras","b":"Cholas","c":"Pandyas","d":"Vakatakas"},"a":"b","s":"Cholas (8th-12th century AD was founded by Vijayalaya in the 8 th century. Rajaraja Chola (greatest king of the Chola Empire) reigned between 985 and 1014 C.E.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":168,"n":194,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Chola Dynasty","q":"Rajendra I was the son of which of the following Chola kings? Higher Secondary 02/08/2022 ( Shift - 2 )","o":{"a":"Virarajendra","b":"Rajaraja I","c":"Vijayalaya","d":"Gandaraditya"},"a":"b","s":"Rajaraja I (ruler of Imperial Chola) . Rajendra I (1012-1044 A.D) reasserted the Chola authority over the Chera and Pandya countries. He defeated Jayasimha II, the Western Chalukya king. He founded the city of Gangaikondacholapuram and constructed the famous Rajesvaram temple in that city.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":169,"n":195,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the author of \"Mahabhashya\"?","o":{"a":"Bana","b":"Panini","c":"Patanjali","d":"Kautilya"},"a":"c","s":"Patanjali. \" The Yoga Sutra\" was also written by Patanjali. Authors and their books: Kalhana - \"Rajatarangini\". Vatsayana - \"Kamasutra\". Vishnu Sharma - \"Panchatantra\". Kautilya - \"Arthasastra\". Panini - \"Ashtadhyayi\". Megasthenes - \"Indica\". Jayadeva - \"Gitagovinda\". Sri Harsha - \"Naishadha Charita\". Banabhatta - \"Harshacharita\", \"Kadambari\".","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":170,"n":196,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following dynasties succeeded the Palas of Bengal ?","o":{"a":"Senas","b":"Devas","c":"Maukharis","d":"Gaudas"},"a":"a","s":"Senas Dynasty: A hindu dynasty that ruled over eastern Bengal and western Bihar for about two centuries. Important Rulers - Vijaya Sena, Ballal Sena, Lakshmana Sena. Deva dynasty - Ruled over eastern Bengal after the Sena dynasty. Maukharis dynasty - A post-Gupta dynasty that ruled over the vast plains of Ganga-Yamuna for over six generations.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":171,"n":197,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who had Sialkot as his capital ?","o":{"a":"Menander","b":"Demetrios","c":"Eukratides","d":"Pushyamitra"},"a":"a","s":"Menander: A Greek king who ruled over the Indo-Greek kingdom. Demetrios (Greek king): His capital was Takshashila. Eucratides: Expanded the Indo-Greek territories into Bactria and the northern regions of India. Pushyamitra Sunga - An Indian king who ruled over the Sunga dynasty. His capital was Pataliputra.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":172,"n":198,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"To which of the following dynasties were the Vatapi Chalukyas tributary princes?","o":{"a":"Kalyani Chalukyas","b":"Cholas","c":"Pallavas","d":"Kadambas"},"a":"d","s":"Kadamba dynasty (Founder - Mayurasharma) was an ancient Indian dynasty that ruled over parts of South India, particularly the present-day Karnataka region, from the 4th to the 6th centuries CE. They are origi nally the tributary princes under the Kadambas of Banavasi who ruled the Kanara coast from about the 4th century AD.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":173,"n":199,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following pairs of locations are famous for Buddhist railings and gateways?","o":{"a":"Bharhut and Amravati","b":"Patna and Rajgir","c":"Parasnath and Ajanta","d":"Sarnath and Kushinagar"},"a":"a","s":"Bharhut and Amravati Bharhut Stupa is located in Satna, Madhya Pradesh. It was discovered in a ruined state by Alexander Cunningham and his team. The discovered railing and gateway are now on display at the Indian Museum (Kolkata). Amravati Stupa was discovered by Colin Mackenzie (1797). It has a circular altar, which depicts Lord Buddha in his human form atop an elephant. The high platforms of the stupa are spread in four directions.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":174,"n":200,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Junagadh inscription of Kathiawar is related to which of the following kings ?","o":{"a":"Kanishka","b":"Gautamiputra Satakarni","c":"Narasimhavarman","d":"Rudradaman I"},"a":"d","s":"Rudradaman I. Junagadh rock inscription (Girnar rock inscription) is written in the Sanskrit language. These rocks contain inscriptions Rudradaman I and Skandgupta. Inscription and Belongs to King : The Rabatak inscription (Afghanistan) - Kanishka. The Nasik prasasti inscription - Gautamiputra Satakarni. Aihole Inscription - Pulakeshin (II). Gwalior Inscription - Bhoja. Allahabad Inscription - Samudragupta.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":175,"n":201,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following is the oldest dated fort in India ?","o":{"a":"Kangra Fort","b":"Mehrangarh Fort","c":"Srirangapatna Fort","d":"Golconda Fort"},"a":"a","s":"Kangra Fort (Nagarkot Fort This fort was built by Susharm Chand (Katoch Dynasty). Mohammed Ghazni in 1009 A.D plundered the Shahi treasury at kangra, Himachal Pradesh. The Katoch dynasty traces its origins to the ancient Trigarta Kingdom, mentioned in the Mahabharata epic. Forts in India: Golconda Fort - Hyderabad, Mehrangarh Fort - Jodhpur (Rajasthan), Srirangapatna Fort - Karnataka, Panhala Fort - Maharashtra, Daulatabad Fort - Aurangabad (Maharashtra).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":176,"n":202,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Brihat Samhita was the treatise of which of the following subjects ?","o":{"a":"Botany","b":"Astronomy","c":"Geography","d":"Natural History"},"a":"b","s":"Astronomy. Brihat Samhita - It is the noted work of Varahamihira. It is an important encyclopedic text in Sanskrit. This text exists in many Indian scripts and is preserved in Hindu, Jain, and Buddhist temples and monasteries. Other Samhitas - Jaya Samhita (Mahabharat), Charak Samhita (Medicinal Science), Sushruta Samhita (Book on Surgeries and related science).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":177,"n":203,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Shore temple of Mamallapuram was built by:","o":{"a":"Cholas","b":"Chalukyas","c":"Pallavas","d":"Nagas"},"a":"c","s":"Pallavas (Narasimhavarman II). The famous Shore Temple is located at Mahabalipuram or Mamallapuram (Tamil Nadu), located along southeastern India's Coromandel Coast. It was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1984. Pallavas Dynasty (275 - 897 CE) - Founder - Simhavishnu, Capital - Kanchipuram. Famous rulers of Pallavas dynasty - Sivaskandavarman, Mahendravarman.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":178,"n":204,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which ruler of the Chahamana dynasty made Ajmer his capital?","o":{"a":"Prithviraj III","b":"Prithviraj II","c":"Arnoraj","d":"Ajayaraja"},"a":"d","s":"Ajayaraja. Chahamanas or Chauhans dynasty : Chauhans of Delhi and Ajmer, also known as the Chahamanas of Shakambhari, ruled over part of modern-day Rajasthan. Important Kings : Vasudeva, Ajayaraja, Arnoraja, Vigraharaja IV, Prithviraj Chauhan III, and Hariraja.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":179,"n":205,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Prithviraj Raso, an epic poem about the life of the 12th century Indian king, Prithviraj Chauhan, was written by:","o":{"a":"Chand Bardai","b":"Banabhatta","c":"Bharavi","d":"Ashvaghosa"},"a":"a","s":"Chand Bardai. Indian Epics and writers - Mahabharata (Sage Ved Vyasa), Ramayana (Valmiki), Raghuvamsa (Kalidasa), Buddhacharita (Asvogosha), Silapadikaram (Ilango Adigal in Tamil), Ramcharitmanas (Tulsidas in Awadhi), Kumarasambava (Kalidasa in Sanskrit). Banabhatta wrote Harshacharita and Bharavi wrote Kiratarjuniya.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":180,"n":206,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Dhruvasena II is related to which of the following ruling dynasties?","o":{"a":"Vakataka","b":"Maitraka","c":"Pushyabhuti","d":"Maukhari"},"a":"b","s":"Maitraka dynasty, which ruled over Gujarat and Saurashtra from the 5th to the 8th centuries CE. Vakataka dynasty : Founded by Vindhyashakti. Ajanta caves (numbers ⅩⅥ, ⅩⅦ, ⅩⅨ) are the best examples of Vakataka excellence in the field of painting. Other dynasties and their founders - Vardhana dynasty (Pushyabhuti), Reddi kingdom (Prolaya Vema Reddi).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":181,"n":207,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The 'Tripartite struggle' was between Gurjara Pratiharas in North India, Palas in Eastern India and ________ in South India.","o":{"a":"Cheras","b":"Cholas","c":"Marathas","d":"Rashtrakutas"},"a":"d","s":"Rashtrakutas. The Tripartite Struggle, also known as The Kannauj Triangle Wars, for control of northern India took place in the ninth century in which Pratiharas emerged victorious. Rashtrakutas: Founder - Dantidurg, Capital - Malkhed. Gurjara Pratiharas: Founder - Nagabhatta-I , Capital - Kannauj. Palas - Founder - Gopala.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":182,"n":208,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Siddheshwar Mahadev Temple in Bengal depicts the architecture of which dynasty.?","o":{"a":"Pala Dynasty","b":"Hussain Shahi dynasty","c":"Chalukya dynasty","d":"Shunga Dynasty"},"a":"a","s":"Pala Dynasty. Temples and their associated Dynasty: Khajuraho Temple (Madhya Pradesh) - Bundelas or Chandelas; Kailash Temple (Maharashtra) - Rashtrakutas; Thousand Pillared Temple, Ramappa Temple (Telangana) - Kakatiyas; Brihadeeswara Temple (Tamil Nadu) - Cholas; Kailashnath Temple, Shore Temple (Tamil Nadu) - Pallavas; Dilwara Temple (Rajasthan) - Solankies; Virupaksha Temple (Karnataka) - Chalukyas; Sun Temple (Gujarat) - Solankis.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":183,"n":209,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Malavikagnimitra, a drama of the 5th century CE, was written by","o":{"a":"Kalidasa","b":"Harisena","c":"Bhavabhuti","d":"Pingala"},"a":"a","s":"Kalidasa. He was a court poet of Chandragupta-II (Vikramaditya). Other Books: Meghaduta, Kumarasambhava, Raghuva\u0000sa, and Vikramorvasiya. Harisena - Court poet of Samudragupta and composed Prayag pillar inscription in Sanskrit. Bhavabhuti - A great playwright, best known for his play Uttara-Ramacharitam (latter part of Rama's life). Pingala - Ancient Indian poet and mathematician, and the author of the Chanda\u0000sastra.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":184,"n":210,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Ravana Phadi cave and Durga Temple at Aihole portray the architectural style of which dynasty?","o":{"a":"Vijayanagara","b":"Chalukya","c":"Hoysala","d":"Pallava"},"a":"b","s":"Chalukya dynasty : Established by King Pulakesin I . Famous architecture: Chalukya - Meguti Jain Temple (Aihole), Pattadakal Temples (Bagalkot), Virupaksha Temple (Vijayanagara). Hoysala - Chennakesava Temple (Belur), Hoysaleswara Temple (Halebidu), Kesava Temple (Somanathapura). Pallava - Ratha temples or seven pagodas (Mahabalipuram), Kailasanathar Temple (Kanchipuram), Shore Temple (Mahabalipuram).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":185,"n":211,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Consider the following statements regarding the Indo Greeks. 1. The most celebrated Indo-Greek ruler was Menander. 2. Menander has been identified with King Milinda mentioned in the famous Buddhist text Milindapanha. 3. Milindapanho contains philosophical questions that Milinda asked Amrapali. 4. Impressed by the answers, King Milinda accepted Buddhism as his religion. Which among the above statements are correct.","o":{"a":"Only 3, 2 and 4","b":"Only 1, 2 and 4","c":"Only 1, 3 and 4","d":"Only 1, 2 and 3"},"a":"b","s":"Only 1, 2 and 4. Nageshwar (Buddhist monk) gave the answer to questions related to Buddhism in Milind Panhho. Milinda Panho (The questions of Menander is a book) describes the dialogue between Nageshwar (Nagasena) and Milinda (king of Bactria). The book was written by Nageshwar in the Pali language originally.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":186,"n":212,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The star-shaped temples were built during the reign of:","o":{"a":"Hoysala","b":"Chalukya","c":"Badami","d":"Cholas"},"a":"a","s":"Hoysala. Hoysala Architecture Temples: Aralaguppe is the temple dedicated to Lord Channakeshava (Vishnu). The temple was built during the rule of the Hoysala Empire King Vira Someshwara. There are five features that can be seen in their temples - Star-shaped sanctum sanctorum, jagati, Decorative panels, Shikara and Pillars.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":187,"n":213,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following was the capital of the Hindu Shahi Kingdom?","o":{"a":"Srinagar","b":"Lahore","c":"Waihind","d":"Multan"},"a":"c","s":"Waihind. Hindu Shahi kingdom was founded by Kallar. It ruled over Kabul and the old province of Gandhara from the decline of the Kushan empire. This kingdom was known as the Kabul Shahi dynasty. Ancient Dynasties with their Capital: Haryanka Dynasty - Rajgriha/Pataliputra, Shishunaga Dynasty - Rajgir, Nanda - Pataliputra, Satavahana - Pratishthana/Amaravati.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":188,"n":214,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Gahadavalas ruled over which region of India ?","o":{"a":"Ajmer","b":"Malwa","c":"Kanauj","d":"Patan"},"a":"c","s":"Kanauj. Gahadavalas or Rathors of Kanauj (1090-1194 AD): Founder - Chandradeva. After the failure of the Pratiharas, the Gahadavalas established themselves on the throne of Kanauj. Jai Chand - The last great ruler of this dynasty. He was killed in the Battle of Chandawar in 1194 AD by Mohammad Ghori.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":189,"n":215,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following ancient texts is a source to study Bharatanatyam?","o":{"a":"Abhinaya Darpana","b":"Abhigyan Shakuntalam","c":"Ashtadhyayi","d":"Arthashastra"},"a":"a","s":"Abhinaya Darpan Bharatnatyam (classical dance from Tamil Nadu). Natyashashtra (written in Sanskrit) by Bharata Muni and Abhinay Darpan by Nandikeshvara are considered to be the original sources of Bharatanatyam.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":190,"n":216,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Alvars were the devotees of which South Indian cult?","o":{"a":"Shaktism","b":"Saivism","c":"Jainism","d":"Vaishnavism"},"a":"d","s":"Vaishnavism. Nayanars and Alvars saints belonged to South India. Some famous Vaishnava devotees : Ramanuja, Madhvacharya, Chaitanya Mahaprabhu, Nimbarkacharya. Jainism - Devotees of Jainism are called Jains such as Rishabhadeva, Parshvanath, Bhadrabahu. Shaktism - Devotees of Shaktism are called Shaktas. Famous Shaktas: Adi Shankaracharya, Sarada Devi, Paramahansa Yogananda.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":191,"n":217,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The book 'Rajatarangini' is about the history of which state written in 12 th century CE?","o":{"a":"Bengal","b":"Kashmir","c":"Sindh","d":"Assam"},"a":"b","s":"Kashmir. Ancient Texts and Authors: Rajatarangini (River of Kings) - Kalhana, Harsha Charita - Bana Bhatta, Akbar-Nama - Abul Fazl, Prithviraj Raso - Chand Bardai, Humayun Nama - Gulbadan Begum, Meghdoot - Kalidasa, Arthashastra - Kautilya, Geet Govinda - Jaya Deva, Ratnavali - Harshavardhana.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":192,"n":218,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In the middle of which century did the Arab traveller Sulaiman visit India?","o":{"a":"15 th","b":"9 th","c":"11 th","d":"6 th"},"a":"b","s":"9th. Travellers Who Visited India: Hiuen Tsang (China) - During the reign of Harshavardhana. Al Biruni {Khwarizm (present-day Uzbekistan)} - In 11th century. During the reign of Sultan of Mahmood Ghaznavi. Ibn Battuta (Morocco) - During the reign of Muhammad Bin Tughlaq, Marco Polo (Italy) - During the reign of Pandya Kingdom. Megasthenes (Greece) - During the reign of Chandragupta Maurya. Abdur Razzaq (Persia) - During the reign of Deva Raya II (Sangama dynasty). Fa Hien (China) - During the reign of Chandragupta II. Al Masudi (Arab) - In 10th century AD.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":193,"n":219,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The rulers of which of the following Rajput dynasties patronised Jainism and one of them is reported to have accepted Jainism under the influence of a Jain scholar Hemachandra?","o":{"a":"Pratihara","b":"Parmara","c":"Chalukya","d":"Chahman"},"a":"c","s":"Chalukya . The Chalukya Dynasty was founded by Pulakesin I. Capital: Badami (Vatapi). Pratihara dynasty - Nagabhatta I was the founder. The Pratiharas who ruled over Kannauj for a longtime are also called Gurjara- Pratiharas. Parmar dynasty - Founded by Upendra or Krishnaraja. Capital: Ujjain, Later - Dharan Nagari (Dhar).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":194,"n":220,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following universities was founded by Pala rulers ?","o":{"a":"Vikramasila","b":"Nalanda","c":"Valabhi","d":"Taxila"},"a":"a","s":"Vikramasila : Location - Bihar. Founded - 8th-9th Century. Events - Destroyed by Muhammad bin Bakhtiyar Khalji (1193). Nalanda University : Founded by - Gupta Dynasty in the 4th - 5th century. Taxila University - An ancient Indian university in Taxila city, which is now in north-western Pakistan. Valabi University (Gujarat) : Established by - The Maitrak King, Bhattarka.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":195,"n":221,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Alha and Udal, who gave stiff resistance to the enemy, belonged to which of the following Rajput States?","o":{"a":"Parmar","b":"Kalchuri","c":"Chandella","d":"Gahadavala"},"a":"c","s":"Chandella (Founder - Nannuk) . They were originally vassals of the Gurjara - Pratiharas. The Chandelas initially ruled as feudatories of the Gurjara-Pratiharas of Kanyakubja (Kannauj). Original capital - Khajuraho. The last King of this dynasty - Paradidev (Parmadi). His generals - Alha and Udal. Battle of Mahoba: Fought between Paradidev and Prithviraj Chauhan-III in 1182 AD.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":196,"n":222,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Pala Dynasty rulers were primarily patrons of which religion?","o":{"a":"Buddhist","b":"Jain","c":"Hindu","d":"Islam"},"a":"a","s":"Buddhist. Pala Dynasty - Founded by Gopala, in 750 AD. They were staunch supporters of Mahayana Buddhism. They were in a tripartite struggle between the Palas, the Pratiharas and the Rashtrakutas for the control of Kanauj and North India. Dharmapala (770 - 810 AD) captured Kanauj and conducted a grand darbar. He took the titles of Parama Bhattaraka, Paramesvara and Maharajadhiraja.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":197,"n":223,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the author of the play 'Mrichchhakatika'?","o":{"a":"Kalidasa","b":"Amarasimha","c":"Shudraka","d":"Bhasa"},"a":"c","s":"Shudraka . Mrichchhakatika play was translated into English by Arthur W. Ryder in 1905 as \"The Little Clay Cart\". Other Sanskrit plays of Shudraka: \"Vinavasavadatta\", \"Padmaprabhritaka\", \"a bhana\" (short one -act monologue).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":198,"n":224,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Ashtadhyayi of Panini is a renowned work on ______.","o":{"a":"Mauryan Polity","b":"Gupta Administration","c":"Medicine","d":"Sanskrit Grammar"},"a":"d","s":"Sanskrit Grammar. Ancient Authors and books : (Kautilya) Chanakya's work - \"Arthashastra\" (on statecraft, political science, economic policy and military strategy). Maharshi Charak - \"Charaka Samhita\" (a famous text of ayurveda) - Ayurveda. \"Sushruta Samhita\" - A comprehensive textbook on medicine and surgery.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":199,"n":225,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the most famous Shaka ruler, who got the Sudarshana Lake in Kathiawar renovated?","o":{"a":"Rudradaman I","b":"Maues","c":"Rudrasimha III","d":"Chashtana"},"a":"a","s":"Rudradaman I (130 - 150 AD) . Sudarshan Lake was constructed by the order of Chandragupta Maurya by the Governor 'Pushyagupta Vaishya' appointed in his Girnar. Rudradaman I issued the first long inscription in chaste Sanskrit. Five branches of the Sakas in the Indian subcontinent: Afghanistan, Punjab with Taxila as capital, Mathura, Western India, Upper Deccan with Ujjain as their capital.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":200,"n":226,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the last king of the Shunga dynasty?","o":{"a":"Pushya mitra","b":"Bhavbhuti","c":"Shashanka","d":"Devabhuti"},"a":"d","s":"Devabhuti. He was assassinated by his minister (Vasudeva Kanva). Other Kings of Shunga Dynasty: Pushyamitra Shunga (Founder of Shunga Dynasty), Agnimitra, Vasujyeshtha, Vasumitra, Bhagabhadra. Shashanka: The first king of ancient Bengal, governed the Gauda Kingdom from 600 to 625 AD. Bhavabhuti: A famous Sanskrit playwright who wrote famous plays like Mahaviracharita, Malatimadhava etc.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":201,"n":227,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Of which of the following Pratihara kings was Rajashekhara, the famous poet and dramatist, the court poet?","o":{"a":"Rajapala","b":"Devapala","c":"Ramabhadra","d":"Mahendrapala"},"a":"d","s":"Mahendrapala (885-910 AD Works of Rajshekhara: Viddhashalabhanjika, Balabharata, Karpuramanjari, Balaramayana, Kavyamimamsa. Gurjara Pratihara dynasty (8th to 11th century): Founder - Harichandra. Mihira Bhoja (836-885 AD) - Most powerful Partihara king. Yashpala (1024- 1036) - last ruler of the Gurjara Pratihara dynasty.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 2nd shift"},{"id":202,"n":228,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who were the first to make land grants to Brahmins? Graduate Level 30/06/2023 ( Shift - 1)","o":{"a":"Sakas","b":"Satavahanas","c":"Guptas","d":"Kushans"},"a":"b","s":"Satavahanas (1st Century BC to 3rd Century AD) : Simuka was the founder. Sakas (2nd century BCE to the 4th century CE) - The most famous Shaka ruler in India was Rudradaman (130 A.D to 150 A.D.), came to an end with the defeat of the last Shaka ruler, Rudrasimha III by Chandragupta-II (Gupta dynasty). Kushans (First - Third Centuries) - Founded by Kujula Kadphises.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":203,"n":229,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"During the reign of which of the following Pallavas, Hiuen Tsang visited the Pallava capital Kanchi?","o":{"a":"Narasimhavarman I","b":"Narasimhavarman II","c":"Mahendravarman II","d":"Mahendravarman I"},"a":"a","s":"Narasimhavarman I. He was also known as Mahamalla or Mamalla, which means \"great wrestler\". He took the title Vatapikonda (the conqueror of Vatapi).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":204,"n":230,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The most important ruler of the Satavahanas was _______.","o":{"a":"Pulumavi IV","b":"Gautamiputra Shri Satakarni","c":"Simuka","d":"Vasistha Putra Pulumavi"},"a":"b","s":"Gautamiputra Shri Satakarni In the 2nd century BCE, Satavahana empire emerged in Deccan (Dakshinapatha). The Nasik inscription of Gautami Balashri mentioned the shri Satakarnis rule. He is described as the destroyer of the Sakas, Pahlavas and Yavanas.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":205,"n":231,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Rampurva bull that was part of a Mauryan pillar found in Rampurwa, Bihar is now placed at _______.","o":{"a":"Pradhan Mantri Bhavan","b":"Sansad","c":"Rajpath","d":"Rashtrapati Bhawan"},"a":"d","s":"Rashtrapati Bhawan. The Pillars of Ashoka are the most famous examples of Mauryan art. The Rashtrapati Bhavan houses the imposing 3rd century BC Ashoka pillar of sandstone known as the Rampurva bull. It got its name from the place of its discovery, Rampurwa in Bihar.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":206,"n":232,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the last Pallava king?","o":{"a":"Mahendravarman - I","b":"Aparajitavarman","c":"Narasimhavarman - I","d":"Simhavishnu"},"a":"b","s":"Aparajitavarman. Pallava dynasty (275 CE to 897 CE) - Also known as Tondaimandalam. Founder - Simha Vishnu (Avanisimha). Mahendravarman - He defeated the Kalabhras and re-established the Pallava kingdom. Narashimhavarman-1 - Son of Mahendravarman.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":207,"n":233,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Kandariya Mahadeva Temple was constructed in 999 A.D. by the king Dhangadeva of ______ dynasty.","o":{"a":"Chandela","b":"Pala","c":"Pallava","d":"Chola"},"a":"a","s":"Chandela. Kandariya Mahadeva temple - built at Khajuraho (Madhya Pradesh), made up of sandstone. Its first mention is found in 'al Biruni' And 'Ibn Battuta' work. It follows the Nagara Style of architecture and was recognized by UNESCO as a world heritage site in 1986. Other temples built by Chandela dynasty (Founder - Nanuka): Devi Jagdamba Temple, Chitragupta Temple, Laxmana Temple.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":208,"n":234,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In the mid-eighth century, Dantidurga, a Rashtrakuta chief, overthrew his Chalukya overlord and performed a ritual literally called a ___________.","o":{"a":"Prarthna sabha","b":"Golden womb","c":"Horse ride","d":"Subsidiary alliance"},"a":"b","s":"Golden womb (Hiranyagarbha). This ritual was performed with the help of Brahmanas, it was thought to lead to the \"rebirth\" of the sacrificer as a Kshatriya, even if he was not one by birth. The first known ruler of the dynasty was Dantidurga who conquered Badami in 752 A.D., defeating the Chalukyas.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":209,"n":235,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The emblem of the Chera Dynasty that controlled the central and northern parts of Kerala and the Kongu region of Tamil Nadu was _________ .","o":{"a":"birds","b":"fish","c":"bow and arrow gan","d":"tiger"},"a":"c","s":"Bow and Arrow Gan. The symbol also appears on the coins the dynasty minted. The dynasty controlled the central and northern parts of Kerala and the Kongu region of Tamil Nadu. Their capital was Vanji, Tamil Nadu.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":210,"n":236,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following is NOT part of six schools of Indian Philosophy?","o":{"a":"Samkhya","b":"Yoga","c":"Nyaya","d":"Dravida"},"a":"d","s":"Dravida. Samkhya: Founded by - Kapila (Book - Samkhya sutra) : It holds that reality is constituted of two principles of one female (Prakriti) and the other male (Purusha). Yoga - Union of the two principal entities. Nyaya: A technique of logical thinking. Author of the Nyaya Sutras - Gautama.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":211,"n":237,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which among the following rulers adopted the title devaputra?","o":{"a":"Hoyasala","b":"Kushana","c":"Chola","d":"Saka"},"a":"b","s":"Kushana. Kanishka (Kushana Dynasty) minted the gold and copper coins in which he named himself as 'Devaputra'. Scholars in Kanishka's court - Vasumitra, Asvaghosa (Court poet), Nagarjun, Charaka, Sushruta and Parsva. Other rulers of the Kushana dynasty: Kujula Kadphises (Founder), Vima Kadphises, etc.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":212,"n":238,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Amaravati School of art was developed under the patronage of __________ .","o":{"a":"Cholas","b":"Cheras","c":"Satavahanas","d":"Palas"},"a":"c","s":"Satavahans. Different School of arts: Gandhara School of Art (Kushana) - It developed in the North West Frontier, in the modern-day Kandahar region. It is also known as Indo-Greek art because of the heavy influence of Greek or Hellenistic sculpture. Mathura School of Art (Kushana) - It developed around Mathura.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":213,"n":239,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was a famous ruler of the Pandya dynasty of South India?","o":{"a":"Nedunjeliyan II","b":"Karikala","c":"Ashoka","d":"Nizam Shah"},"a":"a","s":"Nedunjeliyan II. He was made 'Pandya supreme in the south'. He defeated a confederacy of the Cholas and Cheras at Talaiyalanganam near Tiruvarur. His deeds have been described in the Maduraikkanci. Pandya Empire - Established by King Kadungon in the 6th century AD. Capital - Korkai, Madurai.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":214,"n":240,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Around 2000 years ago _________ became the second capital of the Kushanas.","o":{"a":"Patliputra","b":"Madurai","c":"Vaishali","d":"Mathura"},"a":"d","s":"Mathura . Kushan Dynasty (First-Third Centuries): Rulers - Kujula Kadphises (Founder). Vima Kadphises - First ruler to introduce pure gold coins in India. Kanishka - He administered the territory from two capitals: Purushapura (now Peshawar in northern Pakistan) and Mathura, in northern India. Vasudeva I - Last Kushana Ruler.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":215,"n":241,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Sangam poems mention a Tamil word 'Muvendar' which means ________.","o":{"a":"Three taxes","b":"Three ports","c":"Three chiefs","d":"Three horses"},"a":"c","s":"Three chiefs. It is a reference to the three ruling families of Tamil Nadu during the Sangam period: the Cholas, the Cheras, and the Pandyas.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":216,"n":242,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The kingdoms of Chola, Chera and Pandyas belong to which period in ancient India?","o":{"a":"Gupta Period","b":"Sangam Period","c":"Mauryan Period","d":"Mahajanapada period"},"a":"b","s":"Sangam Period. Three dynasties ruled during the Sangam Age. Cheras: Modern-Day City (Kerala), Ancient Capital (Vanji), Important Ruler (Cheran Senguttuvan), Important Ports (Musiri, Tondi), Emblem (Bow and Arrow). Cholas: Modern-Day City (Tamil Nadu), Ancient Capital (Uraiyur, Puhar), Important Ruler (Karikala), Important Ports. (Kaveripattinam), Emblem (Tiger). Pandyas: Modern-Day City (Tamil Nadu), Ancient Capital (Madurai), Important Ruler (Nedunchezhiyan), Important Ports {Muziris (Muchiri), Korkai, Kaveri}, Emblem (Carp).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":217,"n":243,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The term 'Kammakaras' in ancient times is related to ________.","o":{"a":"Landless agricultural labourers","b":"Craftsmen","c":"Tax collecting officer","d":"Goldsmith"},"a":"a","s":"Landless agricultural labourers . Terminologies related in ancient times - Shrenis: In ancient times, crafts persons and merchants formed associations. Samaharta and Sanidata - Tax collecting officers in ancient times. Sonalika is a Sanskrit word that refers to a goldsmith. Grihapatis- independent farmers. Dasa Karmakara - Men and women such as the dasa karmakara, who did not own land, and had to earn a living working on the fields owned by others. An agrahara was land granted to a Brahmana.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":218,"n":244,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which famous ruler was known as the \"Second Ashoka\" ?","o":{"a":"Chandragupta II","b":"Rudradaman I","c":"Milinda","d":"Kanishka"},"a":"d","s":"Kanishka. He was from the Kushan Dynasty reign from 127 - 151 AD. He was famous for his military, political, and spiritual achievements.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":219,"n":245,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following statements is correct with respect to the Sangam literature? I. These texts were supposed to be composed and compiled in assemblies of poets. II. The Sangam literature compiled in assemblies of poets were held in the city of Madurai.","o":{"a":"Neither I nor II","b":"Both I and II","c":"Only I","d":"Only II"},"a":"b","s":"Both I and II. Siddhar Agastyar presided over the first Tamil Sangam at Madurai. Sangam literature (the poetry of the noble ones, ancient Tamil literature) is the earliest known literature of South India. Tolkappiyam, Ettutogai, Pattuppattu, Pathinenkilkanakku, and the two epics (Silappathikaram and Manimegalai) comprise the Sangam literature. Sangam Literature: 2 categories (Aham and Puram), Tinai (seven minor genres), Ainkurunuru (collection of 500 short poems).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":220,"n":246,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following gave up his traditional professions and took to arms, successfully established a kingdom in Karnataka?","o":{"a":"Vasudeva Kanva","b":"Gurjara-Pratihara Harichandra","c":"Pushyamitra Shunga","d":"Kadamba Mayurasharman"},"a":"d","s":"Kadamba Mayurasharman was a Brahmana. The Kadamba Mayurasharman (345-365 C.E.) was the founder of the Kadamba Kingdom of Banavasi (Karnataka). Kadimi Chettu (story of Mayur Sharma) was written by Visawanatha Satyanarayana {Padma Bhushan (1970), Jnanpith Award (1971)}.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":221,"n":247,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following pairs of 'Dynasty - Ruled region' is correctly matched? I. Shakas - North west and north India II. Vakatakas - Central and western India","o":{"a":"Neither I nor II","b":"Only I","c":"Only II","d":"Both I and II"},"a":"d","s":"Both I and II. The Sakas (first shaka king, Maues or Moga) ruled over the north-west frontier, and north India (Punjab, Sindh, Kashmir, western Uttar Pradesh, Saurashtra, Kathiawar, Rajputana, Malwa, and the north Konkan belt of Maharashtra). Vakatakas (founder - Vindhyashakti) ruled over Central - Western India (southernmost points of Malwa and Gujarat in the north to the Tungabhadra River in the south, and from the Arabian Sea in the west to the edges of Chhattisgarh in the east).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":222,"n":248,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Merchants and migrants first brought the teachings of the holy Quran to India in the_______century.","o":{"a":"fourth","b":"seventh","c":"ninth","d":"sixth"},"a":"b","s":"seventh . Different religions and their holy books: Islam (Quran and Sunnah), Sikhism (Guru Granth Sahib), Jainism (Jain Agamas), Buddhist (Tipitaka), Hinduism (Bhagwat Gita, Ved, Upnishad), Christianity (Bible), Rastafari (Bible, Kebra Nagast, The Promise Key, Holy Piby), Shinto (Kojiki, Nihon Shoki, Fudoki), Zoroastrianism (Zend Avesta), etc.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":223,"n":249,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following is NOT associated with Aryabhata?","o":{"a":"Stated that day and night were caused by the rotation of the earth on its axis.","b":"Developed a scientific explanation for eclipses","c":"Found a way of calculating the circumference of a circle","d":"Spoke about elaborate surgical procedures"},"a":"d","s":"Sushruta {the father of surgery} spoke about elaborate surgical procedures in his book 'Sushruta Samhita'. Aryabhata invents: Mathematics {place value system and zero, Approximation of π, Trigonometry, Indeterminate equations, Algebra}, Astronomy {Motions of the solar system, Eclipses, Sidereal periods, Heliocentrism}.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":224,"n":250,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Ravikirti was the court poet of which of the following dynasty rulers ?","o":{"a":"Cholas","b":"Cheras","c":"Pallavas","d":"Chalukyas"},"a":"d","s":"Chalukya dynasty. The Jain poet Ravikirti was the court poet of 'Pulakeshin II' (610 to 642 CE), the first sovereign ruler of the Chalukya dynasty of Vatapi (modern Badami). Ravikirti's Aihole inscription (a nineteen-line Sanskrit inscription), in honour of his patron king, is found in the Meguti Jain temple at Aihole, Karnataka. Chalukya dynasty (between 6th and 12th centuries). Pallava dynasty (275 CE to 897 CE). Chera dynasty (in the Sangam period and before). Chola dynasty (300 BCE to 1279 CE).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":225,"n":251,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Durga temple at Aihole, built about ______ years ago.","o":{"a":"1700","b":"2000","c":"1100","d":"1400"},"a":"d","s":"1400 . Durga temple, Aihole, Karnataka - dedicated to Surya but named Durga because a durg or fortified lookout was constructed on top of it after the 13th-century during the wars between Hindu kingdoms and Islamic Sultanates. Construction - late 7th - early 8th century (during Chalukya dynasty) 1400 years ago.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":226,"n":252,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"According to the Brihat Samhita, what do you call the process of making scents, mouth perfumes and bath powders?","o":{"a":"Jatuka","b":"Gandhayukli","c":"Kamplcica","d":"Pattanga"},"a":"b","s":"Gandhayukli . Varahmihir's Brihat Samhita is a sort of encyclopedia, which was composed in the sixth century CE. It informs about the preparation of glutinous material to be applied on walls and roofs of houses and temples. It also gives references to perfumes and cosmetics. A number of classical texts, like Atharvaveda (1000 BCE) mention some dye stuff, the material used were turmeric, madder, sunflower, orpiment, cochineal and lac.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":227,"n":253,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following book was written by Aryabhata?","o":{"a":"Dhatupatha","b":"Aryabhatiyam","c":"Natya Shastra","d":"Romaka Siddhanta"},"a":"b","s":"Aryabhatiyam . Books written by Aryabhata: 'Dashgeetika', 'Arya Siddhanta', 'Rishab's Good Theory of Indian', etc. Aryabhata (a great mathematician-astronomer) contributions towards mathematics include algebra, approximation of pi, place value system and zero, trigonometry, etc. His contributions towards astronomy include Solar system motion, Sidereal periods, Eclipses, Heliocentrism, etc. Dhatupatha (Panini), Natya Shastra (Bharata), and Romaka Siddhanta (Varahmihira).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":228,"n":254,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the founder of the Kanva dynasty?","o":{"a":"Narayana","b":"Susharman","c":"Vasudeva","d":"Devabhuti"},"a":"c","s":"Vasudeva Kanva (73-63 BCE founded the Kanva dynasty (72-28 BCE, Kanvayanas). Rulers of the Kanva dynasty - Bhumimitra (64-50 BCE), Narayana (50-38 BCE), Susarman (38-28 BCE). Capital: Patliputra . Devabhuti - the last ruler of the Sunga Dynasty.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":229,"n":255,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The rulers of which of the following dynasties built the Khajuraho temples between 950 to 1050 A.D in central India?","o":{"a":"Gurjara-Pratihara Dynasty","b":"Rashtrakuta Dynasty","c":"Pala Dynasty","d":"Chandela Dynasty"},"a":"d","s":"Chandela Dynasty. The Khajuraho Group of Monuments are a group of Hindu and Jain temples in Chhatarpur district, Madhya Pradesh, Known for its Erotic Sculpture. The Pratiharas, also known as the Gurjara -Pratiharag (Founder - Harichandra). The most well-known king of this dynasty was Bhoja or Mihira Bhoja.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":230,"n":256,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which of the following languages did Amoghavarsha write Kavirajamarga?","o":{"a":"Tamil","b":"Sanskrit","c":"Kannada","d":"Telugu"},"a":"c","s":"Kannada. Amoghavarsha (Rashtrakuta ruler) also wrote 'Prasnottara Ratna Malika' (Sanskrit). He patronized many Indian scholars including mathematician Mahaviracharya , who wrote Ganita -sara- samgraha, Jinasena, Virasena, Shakatayan, and Sri Vijaya (a Kannada language theorist). He held titles such as Nrupathunga, Atishadhavala, Veeranarayana, Rattamarthanda , and Srivallabha during his reign. Historian Panchamukhi called him the 'Ashoka of South'.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":231,"n":257,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which king of the Pala dynasty defeated Indrayudh and made Chakrayudha as the ruler of Kannauj? Graduate Level 04/08/2022 (Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Dharmapala","b":"Mahendrapala","c":"Devapala","d":"Gopala"},"a":"a","s":"Dharampala (770-810 founded the Vikramshila University (Bhagalpur, Bihar) and built Somapuri monastery, Bihar. In 750 AD, Gopala founded the Pala dynasty. Capital: Muddagiri/ Munger (Bihar). Pala period (golden period in history of Bengal). Devapala (810-850), his Court poet was Buddhist scholar Vajradatta (author of Lokesvarasataka).","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":232,"n":258,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Manuscripts were usually written on:","o":{"a":"marbles","b":"palm leaf","c":"tulsi leaves","d":"iron"},"a":"b","s":"palm leaf. A manuscript is a handwritten composition on paper, bark, cloth, metal, palm leaf, or any other material dating back at least seventy-five years. The National Mission for Manuscripts was launched in February 2003 under the Ministry of Tourism and Culture.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":233,"n":259,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Mukteshwara Temple is a 10th-century Hindu temple dedicated to Shiva, located in _______.","o":{"a":"Assam","b":"Odisha","c":"Maharashtra","d":"Gujarat"},"a":"b","s":"Odisha ( Bhubaneswar Ancient temples - Virupaksha Temple, Hampi (Built by Lakkan Dandesha, a chieftain under the ruler Deva Raya II), Ellora Kailashanatha Temple (Krishna I of Rashtrakuta), Sun Temple Konark (Eastern Ganga King Narsimhadeva-1).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":234,"n":260,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following is NOT a Buddhist architectural marvel of India ?","o":{"a":"Pataleshwar Caves","b":"Sanchi Stupa","c":"Bharhut Stupa","d":"Ajanta Caves"},"a":"a","s":"Pataleshwar Caves (Pune) is dedicated to Shiva, it was a monumental monolithic excavation with a notable circular Nandi mandapam and a large pillared mandapam. The Cave Temple is an 8th-century rock-cut shrine built by the Rashtrakuta Dynasty.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":235,"n":261,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Queen Rudramadevi belonged to which dynasty of India?","o":{"a":"Chola","b":"Kakatiya","c":"Chalukya","d":"Chera"},"a":"b","s":"Kakatiya . Marco Polo mentioned her in his writings. Rudradeva I was the first independent ruler of this kingdom of Warangal. Their capital was Orugallu, now known as Warangal. In 1323 Prince Jaunakhan (Muhammad bin Tughlaq) defeated Prataparudra Deva (the last ruler of this kingdom) and annexed their kingdom","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":236,"n":262,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"______ was an important port city in ancient India.","o":{"a":"Tamralipti","b":"Shravasti","c":"Ahichhatra","d":"Champa"},"a":"a","s":"Tamralipti was a port city and capital of Suhma Kingdom in ancient Bengal, located on the coast of the Bay of Bengal. Shravasti was the capital of Kosala kingdom in ancient India and the place where the Buddha lived most after his enlightenment. Ahichhatra near the modern Ramnagar village in Aonla tehsil, Bareilly district in Uttar Pradesh. Champa, city of ancient India, the capital of the kingdom of Anga.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":237,"n":263,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following was a fishing colony that was used as a port for trading with the Romans and the Greco-Romans in ancient India?","o":{"a":"Lothal","b":"Badami","c":"Arikamedu","d":"Tulapurushandana"},"a":"c","s":"Arikamedu . Lothal is a port town located along the Bhogava river in Gujarat. Badami is located in a ravine at the foot of a rugged, red sandstone outcrop that surrounds Agastya lake (Karnataka). The King's Balance, also known as Tulapurushandana, is located southwest of Vittala temple(Telangana).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":238,"n":264,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Mesopotamians wrote on tablets made of:","o":{"a":"clay","b":"sandstone","c":"limestone","d":"slate"},"a":"a","s":"Clay . It is a sedimentary rock Sandstones are siliciclastic sedimentary rocks. Limestone is a sedimentary rock made of calcium carbonate (CaCO 3 ), usually in the form of calcite or aragonite. Slate, fine-grained, clayey metamorphic rock.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":239,"n":265,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which place had a water harvesting system channeling the flood water of the Ganga river in the first century B.C ? SSC MTS 06/10/21 (Evening)","o":{"a":"Kanpur","b":"Gorakhpur","c":"Mirzapur","d":"Sringaverapura"},"a":"d","s":"Sringaverapura (near Allahabad in Uttar Pradesh). Water harvesting in India - Dholavira in Harappan civilization, Bhopal Lake (built by the Paramara Raja Bhoj in 11th century), Iltutmish constructed a tank in Hauz Khas for supplying water to the Siri Fort (14th century).","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":240,"n":266,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which state of India was ruled by the Ningthouja dynasty?","o":{"a":"Manipur","b":"Haryana","c":"Gujarat","d":"Assam"},"a":"a","s":"Manipur . It has 125 extended families. It was apparently founded by King Nongda Lairen Pakhangba in 33 CE.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":241,"n":267,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Caves of Elephanta are predominantly dedicated to which of the following deities?","o":{"a":"Lord Shiva","b":"Lord Krishna","c":"Lord Rama","d":"Lord Ganesha"},"a":"a","s":"Lord Shiva. The caves of Elephanta were built by the Rashtrakuta rulers. They are on Elephanta Island, or Gharapuri, in Mumbai Harbour. There are about 34 caves. These are declared a World Heritage Site by UNESCO in 1987.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":242,"n":268,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which of the following temples will you find Gopurams?","o":{"a":"Shiva Temple, Chidambaram","b":"Bhabatarini Temple, Dakshineswar","c":"Golden Temple, Amritsar","d":"Dilwara Temple, Mount Abu"},"a":"a","s":"Shiva Temple, Chidambaram A gopuram or gopura is a monumental tower, usually ornate, at the entrance of any temple, especially in Southern India. They are a prominent feature of koils, Hindu temples built in the Dravidian style. They are topped by the kalasam, a bulbous stone finial. Nataraja temple in Chidambaram, Tamil Nadu is one of the famous examples of Gopuram.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":243,"n":269,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following wrote the basic text of Vaisheshika philosophy?","o":{"a":"Kanada","b":"Shankaracharya","c":"Patanjali","d":"Jaimini"},"a":"a","s":"Kanada . It is an ancient Sanskrit text at the foundation of the Vaisheshika school of Hindu philosophy. Vaisheshika is one of the six classical schools of Indian philosophy, often referred to as \"Darshanas.\"","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":244,"n":270,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The story of Maynamati and Gopichandra is a part of which of the following literary works?","o":{"a":"Nath literature","b":"Chandimangal Kavya","c":"Manasamangal Kavya","d":"Sanskrit epics"},"a":"a","s":"Nath literature. It includes stories concerning the worship of Dharma Thakur, and fairy tales, folk tales and ballads.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":245,"n":271,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Yashovigraha, Mahichandra and Chandradeva were the first three rulers of ______ dynasty.","o":{"a":"Maratha","b":"Chauhans","c":"Gahadavala","d":"Wadiyar"},"a":"c","s":"Gahadavala . Yashovigraha was the founder of the Gahadavala dynasty. The majority of the Gahadavala epigraphic records were discovered in Uttar Pradesh.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":246,"n":272,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was NOT a ruler of the Kushana dynasty?","o":{"a":"Nahapana","b":"Vasishka","c":"Vasudeva","d":"Huvishka"},"a":"a","s":"Nahapana was an important ruler of the Western Kshatrapas, a descendant of the Indo-Scythians, in northwestern India, who ruled during the 1st or 2nd century CE.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":247,"n":273,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Identify the Indian state which was known as 'Pragjyotisha' during the epic period.","o":{"a":"Assam","b":"Odisha","c":"Kerala","d":"Bihar"},"a":"a","s":"Assam was known as Pragjyotisha or the place of eastern astronomy during the epic period and later named Kamrupa.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":248,"n":274,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which of the following caves is the painting named 'Padmapani Bodhisattva' found?","o":{"a":"Ajanta Cave No. 4","b":"Ajanta Cave No. 2","c":"Ajanta Cave No. 3","d":"Ajanta Cave No. 1"},"a":"d","s":"Ajanta Cave No. 1. The iconic Ajanta paintings Padmapani and Vajrapani glorify the walls of the Cave 1 of Ajanta. Cave 1 of Ajanta was built in the second phase of excavation under the patronage of King Harisena of the Vakataka dynasty.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":249,"n":275,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Bhaskaravarman of the Varman dynasty ruled in the ______ region.","o":{"a":"Kamarupa","b":"Ujjain","c":"Magadha","d":"Vaishali"},"a":"a","s":"Kamarupa (Present day Assam). Varman dynasty (350-655 CE)- Rulers - Pushyavarman (Founder, contemporary of Samudragupta), Bhutivarman, Bhaskaravarman.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":250,"n":276,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The famous caves of Udayagiri and Khandagiri are located in :","o":{"a":"Tripura","b":"Uttarakhand","c":"Odisha","d":"Chhattisgarh"},"a":"c","s":"Odisha. Udayagiri-Khandagiri: Ancient caves near Bhubaneswar. Referred to as \"Kumari Parvat\" in the Hathigumpha inscription and Built by King Kharvela. 18 caves in Udayagiri, 15 in Khandagiri.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":251,"n":277,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Kolathunadu, Valluvanad and Thekkumkoor were ancient small-time kingdoms in which state of India?","o":{"a":"Gujarat","b":"Karnataka","c":"Kerala","d":"Bihar"},"a":"c","s":"Kerala. Kolathunadu - land lying between Perumba river in the north and Putupattanam river in the south. Valluvanad (Founder - Ilayi Raja) - Region between Meenachil and Pamban rivers. Thekkumkoor - The region between the Nila river and Panthaloor Hills.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":252,"n":278,"sec":"Ancient History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"When did the Vikrama Era begin?","o":{"a":"47 BC","b":"55 BC","c":"57 BC","d":"50 BC"},"a":"c","s":"57 BC. The Vikram Samvat calendar starts half a century before the Gregorian calendar and works on an Indian calendar cycle. The first day of the Vikram Samvat calendar is celebrated after Diwali in the states of Gujarat and Maharashtra.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":253,"n":279,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"In which year Timur's invasion of Delhi took place?","o":{"a":"1398","b":"1221","c":"1492","d":"1526"},"a":"a","s":"1398. Timur: They invaded India during the reign of Nasir-ud-Din Mahmud Shah Tughluq . He nominated Khizr Khan (Sayyid Dynasty - 1414 to 1451) to rule Punjab. Tughlaq Dynasty (1320 - 1414): Rulers - Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq (founder), Muhammad bin Tughlaq, Firoz shah Tughlaq, and Muhammad Shah.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":254,"n":280,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"Which of the following was the last invasion by Mahmud Ghazni outside Punjab?","o":{"a":"Somnath","b":"Mathura","c":"Kalinjar","d":"Kanauj"},"a":"a","s":"Somnath Temple (attacked in 1025 AD). India was first invaded by Mahmud of Ghazni in 1001 AD and he defeated the army of Raja Jayapala. Foreign Invaders in India: Mohammed Bin Qasim (712 AD), Alexander's Invasion (326 BC), Invasion of Nadir Shah (1739), Mahmud Ghazni Attacked India 17 times.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":255,"n":281,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"Between who among the following did the Battle of Chandawar take place?","o":{"a":"Jaychand and Mahmood Gaznavi","b":"Jaychand and Muhammad Ghori","c":"Prithviraj III and Mahmood Gaznavi","d":"Prithviraj III and Muhammad Ghori"},"a":"b","s":"Jaychand and Muhammad Ghori. Battle of Chandawar (1194) : Fought at - Chandawar (modern Firozabad). Jaychand (Gahadavala dynasty) was defeated and killed.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":256,"n":282,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"The famous Arabic scholar Al Biruni came into contact with India through _____ in the eleventh century A.D.","o":{"a":"Abdullah Shah Ghazi","b":"Mahmud Ghaznavi","c":"Al-Walid I","d":"Muhammad Bin Qasim"},"a":"b","s":"Mahmud Ghaznavi (998- 1030 AD) attacked India 17 times between 1000 to 1027 AD. His Important Invasions in India : In 1018, he plundered the holy city of Mathura and also attacked Kanauj (ruler - Rajyapala); In 1025 he attacked Somnath. Al-Biruni's Books : Kitab-ul-Hind and Tahkik-e- Hind.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":257,"n":283,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"When did the Mongol ruler Genghis Khan die?","o":{"a":"1219","b":"1210","c":"1235","d":"1227"},"a":"d","s":"1227. Genghis Khan (1162 - 1227 ) was the founder and first Great Khan of the Mongol Empire. He invaded India during the reign of Iltumish (3rd ruler of Mamluk dynasty). The Mongol Empire launched several invasions into the Indian subcontinent from 1221 to 1327.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":258,"n":284,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"Who among the following was the best-known Chahamana ruler?","o":{"a":"Prithviraja III","b":"Vigraharaja II","c":"Prithviraja l","d":"Vasudeva"},"a":"a","s":"Prithviraj III (1168-1192). He defeated Sultan Muhammad Ghori in 1191 (first Battle of Tarain), But he was defeated by Ghori in 1192 (second Battle of Tarain). Vigraharaja II {(971-998), Chahamana dynasty}. Prithviraj I {(1090 -1110), Chahamana dynasty}. Vasudeva {(73 BCE to 28 BCE), founder of Kanvas dynasty}.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":259,"n":285,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"In which year did the Mongols under Genghis Khan invade Transoxiana in North-East Iran?","o":{"a":"1219","b":"1208","c":"1213","d":"1205"},"a":"a","s":"1219. The Mongol conquest of Persia comprised three Mongol campaigns against Islamic states in the Middle East and Central Asia between 1219 and 1256. The Mongols had invaded the Delhi Sultanate a number of times, including in 1241, 1245, 1257, and 1285.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":260,"n":286,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"Zoroaster was a/an ______ prophet.","o":{"a":"Indian","b":"Chinese","c":"British","d":"Iranian"},"a":"d","s":"Iranian . Avesta, also called Zend-avesta, sacred book of Zoroastrianism containing its cosmogony, law, and liturgy, the teachings of the prophet Zoroaster (Zarathushtra).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":261,"n":287,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Foreign Invasions","q":"Who among the following built the first European fort in India in the year 1503 ?","o":{"a":"Dutch","b":"British","c":"French","d":"Portuguese"},"a":"d","s":"Portuguese . Fort Emmanuel was built in 1503 by the Portuguese. Situated at Fort Kochi, it was once a symbol of the alliance between the ruler of Kochi and the monarch of Portugal.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":262,"n":288,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"The office of Diwan-i-Insha dealt with which affair in administration?","o":{"a":"Military","b":"Religious affairs","c":"Finance","d":"State correspondence"},"a":"d","s":"State correspondence. Delhi Sultanate Administration : Diwan -i-Wizarat (Department of Revenue and Finance), Diwan-i-Arz (Department of Military), Diwan-i-Risalat (Department of Religious affairs), Diwan-i-bandagan (Department of slaves), Diwan-i-Khairat (Charity department), Diwan-i-mustakhraj (to realise arrears), Diwan- i-kohi (Department of agriculture).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":263,"n":289,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Who was the Sultan of Delhi at the time of the establishment of Vijayanagara Empire?","o":{"a":"Nasiruddin Mahmud","b":"Iltutmish","c":"Mohammad bin Tughlaq","d":"Alauddin Khilji"},"a":"c","s":"Mohammad bin Tughlaq Vijaynagar Empire: Established - 1336 by Harihara and Bukka. Capital - Hampi. The Empire stretched from Krishna river in the north to extreme south of the peninsula. Dynasties with first and last rulers - Sangama Dynasty (Harihara I, Rajasekhara), Saluva Dynasty (Saluva Narasimha, Sadasiva), Tuluva Dynasty (Rama, Venkata), Aravidu Dynasty (Tirumala Raya, Sriranga III).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":264,"n":290,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Which of the following systems of the Delhi sultanate had a influence on the Bahmani and Vijayanagar kingdoms?","o":{"a":"Walis","b":"Chahalgani","c":"Bitikchi","d":"Iqtadari"},"a":"d","s":"Iqtadari System:- Introduced by Shamsuddin IIltutmish (3rd Sultan of Delhi). Provision - Under this system, the land of the empire was divided into several large and small tracts called Iqta and assigned these Iqtas to his soldiers, officers and nobles. Administrative Terms used in Sultanate Period : Dabir (Secretary), Faujdar (Commander of army), Sipahsalar (Commander), Amil (Revenue officer), Diwan (Office: the central secretariat).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":265,"n":291,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Who among the following was the head of the Diwan-i-Insha department under the Delhi sultanate?","o":{"a":"Dabir-i-Khas","b":"Barid-i-Mumalik","c":"Amir-i-Dad","d":"Wakil-i-Dar"},"a":"a","s":"Dabir-i-Khas Diwan -i-Bandagan - A slave department. Diwan-i-kohi - Agricultural department. Diwan-i-Mustakhraj - Arrears department. Barid-i-Mumalik - Head of the information and intelligence department. Amir-i-Dad - The law officer. Wakil-i-Dar - Controller of the royal household.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":266,"n":292,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"The Tomb of Razia Sultan in the state of Haryana is made of which building material? Graduate Level 28/06/2023 ( Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Red sandstone","b":"Granite","c":"Marble","d":"Baked bricks"},"a":"d","s":"Baked bricks. Raziya sultan (1236 - 1240): Her tomb is situated in Kaithal (Haryana). She is the only female ruler ever to rule in the Delhi sultanate. Monuments built by Red sandstone: Qutub Minar, Red Fort and Humayun's Tomb. Taj mahal (white marble) - Tomb of Mumtaz Mahal.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":267,"n":293,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"During the Sultanate of Delhi, the term \"Ulema\" was associated with ______.","o":{"a":"Learned theologians and Jurists","b":"Commander-in-chief","c":"Postal officer","d":"Village Revenue officer"},"a":"a","s":"Learned theologians and Jurists. Officers in Delhi Sultanate - State Level: Amil (Revenue Officer), Barid (Intelligence agents), Khwaja (Accounts Officer), Kotwal (Head of city law and order), Muhatasib (Head of municipality law and order), Muftis (Expounder of law).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":268,"n":294,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Who among the following was the daughter of Iltumish, a ruler of Slave Mamluk dynasty?","o":{"a":"Kubra Noorzai","b":"Harkha","c":"Razia Sultan","d":"Shafiqa Ziayee"},"a":"c","s":"Razia Sultan. In 1236, Razia Sultan (Mamluk dynasty), succeeded her father to the Sultanate of Delhi. She sent an expedition against Ranthambore to control the Rajputs. Mamluk dynasty (1206 to 1290) - Founded by Qutbuddin Aibak (Lakh Baksh). He was succeeded by Iltutmish who was his son-in-law.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":269,"n":295,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Under the Delhi Sultanate, a religious tax ________ was imposed on the wealthy and rich Muslims in India.","o":{"a":"Firuz","b":"Mamluk","c":"Zakat","d":"Kismat"},"a":"c","s":"Zakat. Zakat tax was abolished by Sikander lodi. Firoz Shah Tughlaq introduced four types of taxes based on the Quran. They were Kharaj (Land tax), Khams (Tax on booty captured in war), Jizya (Imposed on non muslim), and Zakat.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":270,"n":296,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"In which year was Razia removed from the throne of the Delhi Sultanate?","o":{"a":"1244","b":"1240","c":"1238","d":"1236"},"a":"b","s":"1240. Razia Sultana ( 1236 -1240 ) married Malik Ikhtiar-ud-Altunia (the governor of Bhatinda under the rule of the Delhi Sultanate under Mamluk dynasty). She was killed on 15 October 1240 at Kaithal (Haryana). Tomb - Kaithal (Haryana).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":271,"n":297,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Who composed Tabaqat-i-Nasiri in the Delhi Sultanate period?","o":{"a":"Hasan Nizami","b":"Amir Khusrau","c":"Minhaj-us-Siraj","d":"Ziauddin Barani"},"a":"c","s":"\"Tabaqat-i-Nasiri'' (1260) was written by Minhaj-i-Siraj in Persian language. Hasan Nizami (Persian language poet) wrote Tajul-Maasir ( the first official history of the Delhi Sultanate). Sufi poet Amir Khusro was the court poet of Alauddin Khilji. His famous works: Tughluq Nama, Wast-ul-Hayat, Nuh Sipihr, Ashiqa, Khamsa, Baqia-Naqia. Ziauddin Barani (Muslim political thinker of Delhi Sultanate) wrote Tarikh-i-Firozshahi and Fatwa-i-Jahandari.","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":272,"n":298,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Zafar khan was a famous General of which of the following rulers of the Delhi Sultanate?","o":{"a":"Alauddin Khilji","b":"Iltutmish","c":"Muhammad bin Tughluq","d":"Balban"},"a":"a","s":"Alauddin Khilji's Generals : Hizabruddin (Zafar Khan), Almas Beg (Ulugh Khan), Malik Kafur (Taj al-Din Izz al-Dawla), Nusrat Khan (Malik Nusrat Jalesari). Muhammad bin Tughlaq (Jauna Khan): Introduced token currency .","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":273,"n":299,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Which of the following combinations of 'Ruler - Predecessor' is correct with respect to the Delhi Sultanate?","o":{"a":"Alauddin Khilji - Jalaluddin Khilji","b":"Jalaluddin Khilji - Alauddin Khilji","c":"Muhammad Tughlaq - Firuz Shah Tughluq","d":"Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq - Muhammad Tughluq"},"a":"a","s":"Rulers of Khilji dynasty - Jalaluddin Khalji (founder) - Alauddin Khalji - Qutb ud-Din Mubarak. Rulers of Tughlaq dynasty - Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq (founder) - Muhammad bin Tughlaq - Firoz Shah Tughlaq .","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":274,"n":300,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Who among the following rulers ruled before Prithviraj Chauhan over Delhi?","o":{"a":"Ghiyasuddin Balban","b":"Ananga Pala","c":"Jalaluddin Khalji","d":"Qutbuddin Aibak"},"a":"b","s":"Ananga Pala Tomar - founded Delhi in 1052. An inscription of Vikram Samvat 1383 in the Delhi Museum confirms the founding of Delhi by the Tomars.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":275,"n":301,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Who was the author of Tarikh - i - Mubarak Shahi which is a Persian language chronicle of the Delhi Sultanate?","o":{"a":"Jawahar-fi-Jawahir","b":"Khazain-ul-Futuh","c":"Tarikh-i-Firoz Shahi","d":"Yahya bin Ahmad Sirhindi"},"a":"d","s":"Yahya bin Ahmad Sirhindi Amir Khusrau (Books - 'Tughlaq Nama', 'Nuh Sipihr', 'Khazain ul Futuh' ).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":276,"n":302,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Delhi Sultanate","q":"Who was the ruler of Delhi sultanate when Ibn Batuta came to India?","o":{"a":"Alauddin Khilji","b":"Firuz Shah Tughlaq","c":"Muhammad Bin Tughlaq","d":"Ibrahim Lodi"},"a":"c","s":"Muhammad Bin Tughlaq was the ruler of the Delhi sultanate when Ibn Batuta came to India. Muhammad bin Tughluq was the Sultan of Delhi from 1325 to 1351 from the Tughlaq Dynasty.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":277,"n":303,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Mameluke style of architecture belongs to which dynasty?","o":{"a":"Lodi dynasty","b":"Slave dynasty","c":"Khilji dynasty","d":"Tughlaq dynasty"},"a":"b","s":"Slave dynasty (1206 - 1290) - It is also known as the Mamluk Dynasty. Established by - Qutbuddin Aibak. Capitals - Lahore (1206 - 1210). Famous buildings built by rulers of the slave dynasty: Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque, Qutub Minar, Adhai Din ka Jhonpra, Tomb of Nasir-ud-Din Mohammed, Tomb of Shams-ud-Din Iltutmish.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":278,"n":304,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"The Delhi sultan, who succumbed to injuries as a result of his fall from horse, was __________ .","o":{"a":"Qutubuddin Aibak","b":"Behram Shah","c":"Balban","d":"Iltutmish"},"a":"a","s":"Qutubuddin Aibak (Known as Lakh Baksh). He was the slave of Muhammad Ghori and was the first sultan of Delhi Sultanate. He established the slave dynasty (Mamluk dynasty). Famous Ruler of Slave Dynasty (1206 AD-1290 AD): Qutbuddin Aibak (1206-1210), Shamsuddin Iltutmish (1210-1236), Raziya Sultan (1236-1240), Balban (1266-1287).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":279,"n":305,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"The Tomb of Iltutmish was built in which year?","o":{"a":"1458 AD","b":"1388 AD","c":"1235 AD","d":"1199 AD"},"a":"c","s":"1235 AD. Iltutmish (Reign 1210 - 1236 AD) introduced silver coin (Tanka) and copper coin (Jital), and completed the construction of Qutub Minar. He also constructed a large reservoir Hauz-i-Sultani just outside Dehli-i-Kuhna.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":280,"n":306,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Ghiyasuddin Balban introduced the customs of 'Sijda' and 'Paibos' as normal forms of salutations to the king as a part of _______etiquette.","o":{"a":"Army","b":"Family","c":"Religious","d":"Court"},"a":"d","s":"Court","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":281,"n":307,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Who started the practice of celebrating Nauroz during the Delhi Sultanate period?","o":{"a":"Sikandar Sur","b":"Shamsuddin Iltutmish","c":"Ghiyasuddin Balban","d":"Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq"},"a":"c","s":"Ghiyasuddin Balban. Nowruz is the Iranian New Year, also known as the Persian New Year. Ghiyasuddin Balban (1266-1287) was the first Sultan of Delhi to introduce the practice of 'Sijda' (prostration) and Paibos (kissing the feet of monarch) as the normal form of Salutation of the king.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":282,"n":308,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Balban served as naib to which of the following sultans of Delhi?","o":{"a":"Iltutmish","b":"Razia","c":"Nasiruddin Mahmud","d":"Qutb ud-Din Aibak"},"a":"c","s":"Nasiruddin Mahmud (1246-1266 AD) - Eighth ruler of Slave dynasty (Mamluk dynasty). Minhaj -i-Siraj was his court historian. Balban (1266-1287 AD) : He pursued the policy of 'blood and iron'. He established the department of military (Diwan-i-Ariz). Razia Sultan (1236-1240 AD) : First muslim woman to rule over Delhi sultanate.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":283,"n":309,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Iltutmish introduced a copper coin called _________ in place of the silver coin.","o":{"a":"Rupak","b":"Rupee","c":"Jittal","d":"Tanka"},"a":"c","s":"Jittal. Shamsuddin Iltutmish was the third Sultan of the Delhi Sultanate. He introduced the Chahalgani system. He coined tanka (Silver) and Jittal (Copper).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":284,"n":310,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Who among the following revolted against Balban, Sultan of Delhi, and declared himself as an independent ruler of Bengal in 1279?","o":{"a":"Muhammad Khan","b":"Tughril Beg","c":"Bughra Khan","d":"Nasir-ud-din Mahmud"},"a":"b","s":"Tughril Beg . Balban (Mamluk dynasty or Slave Dynasty) - introduced the practice of Sijda (prostration) and paibos (kissing the feet of monarch). Ghiyas-ud-Din Balban appointed his own son Bughra khan as governor of Bengal. Nasir-ud-Din Mahmud Shah Tughluq also known as Nasiruddin, was the last sultan of the Tughlaq dynasty.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":285,"n":311,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Who occupied Badaun (A.D. 1197-98) and appointed Iltutmish as its first Muslim Governor?","o":{"a":"Prithviraj Chauhan","b":"Maharana Pratap","c":"Muhammad Ghori","d":"Qutbuddin Aibak"},"a":"d","s":"Qutbuddin Aibak {1206- 1210 ( First ruler of Slave Dynasty /Mamluk Dynasty )}: He established the first Muslim dynasty and was also known as Lakh Baksh . He died in Lahore, Pakistan after falling from a horse ride.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":286,"n":312,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Qutub Minar is an example of ______ architecture.","o":{"a":"Indo-Islamic","b":"Dravidian","c":"Sufi","d":"Mauryan"},"a":"a","s":"Indo - Islamic. Indo-Islamic architecture is the architecture of the Indian subcontinent produced for Islamic patrons and purposes. Famous Examples - City of Mandu (Madhya Pradesh), Taj Mahal (Agra), Gol Gumbaz (Karnataka), Jama Masjid (Delhi) etc. Dravidian architecture Examples - Brihadeshwara temple, Annamalaiyar Temple, Meenakshi temple, Airavatesvara temple etc.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":287,"n":313,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Who had assumed the title of Balban?","o":{"a":"Kabir Khan","b":"Farid Khan","c":"Ulugh Khan","d":"Ayaz Khan"},"a":"c","s":"Ulugh Khan","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":288,"n":314,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Minhaj-i-Siraj was a chronicler during the rule of ______.","o":{"a":"Sultan Iltutmish","b":"Qutbuddin Aibak","c":"Ghiyasuddin Balban","d":"Alauddin Khalji"},"a":"a","s":"Iltutmish (ruler of Slave dynasty). Minhaj-i-Siraj was a 13th-century Persian historian. He worked as a teacher in a madrasa under Nasiruddin Qubacha. During Razia Sultan's reign, he was appointed as principal of Madrasa in Delhi. During the reign of Muiz ud-Din Bahram, he was appointed to the post of Chief Qazi. During the reign of Nasiruddin Mahmud, he was made a court poet. He dedicated his work 'Tabaqat-e-Nasiri' to Nasiruddin Mahmud. He died in Delhi, during the reign of Balban.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":289,"n":315,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Quwwat al-Islam mosque and minaret, built during the last decade of the ______ century.","o":{"a":"eleventh","b":"thirteenth","c":"fourteenth","d":"twelfth"},"a":"d","s":"Twelfth. Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque (1192-1198) built by Qutubuddin Aibak. Other Monuments of Qutub complex - Alai Darwaza (by Ala-ud-din Khalji in 1311 AD). Tomb of Iltutmish (1235). Tomb of Imam Zamin (1537).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":290,"n":316,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"What is the approximate height (in metres) of the Qutub Minar situated in Delhi ?","o":{"a":"75","b":"77","c":"73","d":"71"},"a":"c","s":"73 (237.8 ft). Qutub Minar has five storeys. It was started in 1199 by Qutb ud-Din Aibak, completed in 1220 by his son-in-law Iltutmish. UNESCO (1993). Other Important Minars - Charminar, Hyderabad (1591) by Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah, height is 48.7 m (160 ft). Chand Minar - height 64 m (210 ft) built by Ala-ud-din Bahmani in Daultabad, Maharashtra.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":291,"n":317,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was the Wazir of Sultan Iltutmish? Graduate Level 05/08/2022 ( Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Sai'd Fakhr-I-Mudabbir","b":"Malik Naik","c":"Nizam ul-Mulk Junaidi","d":"Nusrat Khan"},"a":"c","s":"Nizam ul-Mulk Junaidi Iltutmish is known as the real founder of Delhi Sultanate who occupied the throne in 1210 AD after KIlling Aram Bakshsh. He shifted his capital from Lahore to Delhi.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":292,"n":318,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Ghiyasuddin Balban, ruler of the Slave dynasty, took up the title of _______.","o":{"a":"Nur-al-Din (light of the faith)","b":"NurMahal (light of the palace)","c":"Zil-i-Ilahi (shadow of God)","d":"JahanPanah (protector of the world)"},"a":"c","s":"Zil-i-Ilahi (shadow of God Ghiyas ud din Balban - Abolished Chahalghani (Council of 40 nobles), Introduced Persian customs as sizdah (prostration), paibos (kissing feet of sultan), established Diwan-i-Ariz (defence department).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":293,"n":319,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"Which of the following is the correct chronological order of early Turkish rulers who ruled over Delhi from the year 1206 to 1290?","o":{"a":"Qutubuddin Aybak, Shamsuddin Iltutmish, Raziyaa","b":"Raziyya, Shamsuddin Illtutmish, Qutubuddin Aybak","c":"Qutubuddin Aybak, Raziyya, Shamsuddin Illtutmish","d":"Shamsuddin Illutmish, Raziyya, Qutubuddin Aybak"},"a":"a","s":"Qutubuddin Aibak, Shamsuddin Illtutmish, Raziyaa. Chronological order of the rulers is Qutb ud-Din Aibak (founder) → Aram Shah → Iltutmish → Rukn-ud-din Feroze → Razia al-Din → Muiz-ud-din Bahram → Ala-ud-din Masud → Nasiruddin Mahmud → Ghiyas-ud-din Balban → Muiz-ud-din Muhammad Qaiqabad.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":294,"n":320,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Slave Dynasty","q":"In which of the following forts was Razia Sultan imprisoned by Malik Ikhtiyar-ud-din Altunia?","o":{"a":"Qila Mubarak in Bathinda","b":"Jaigarh fort in Jaipur","c":"Golconda fort in Golconda","d":"Mehrangarh fort in Jodhpur"},"a":"a","s":"Qila Mubarak in Bathinda, built by Raja Dab. Jaigarh fort in Jaipur was built in 1726 AD by Jai Singh II. Golconda fort in Golconda was built by Qutb Shahi dynasty. Mehrangarh fort in Jodhpur was built by Rao Jodha.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":295,"n":321,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Who among the following Sultans framed a series of regulations to weaken the power of nobles?","o":{"a":"Muhammad bin Tughluq","b":"Balban","c":"Alauddin Khilji","d":"Iltutmish"},"a":"c","s":"Alauddin Khilji (Khilji Dynasty 1290-1320): He made nobles pay land tax for their holdings. This curbed the excess of wealth owned by nobles. The nobles were also ordered not to have social gatherings or intermarriages without his permission. The group of nobles, called \"The Forty\".","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":296,"n":322,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Who among the following classical poets of the Sultanate period in India popularized Qawwali, a form of music which derived its name from the Arabic word 'qaul', meaning 'to speak'?","o":{"a":"Jalal al-Din Muhammad Rumi","b":"Amir Khusrau","c":"Nizam al-Din Awliya","d":"Shams al-Din Muhammad Hafiz"},"a":"b","s":"Amir Khusrau - He was also known as Tuti-e-Hind (Parrot of India). He was the court musician of Alauddin Khilji. He also lived in courts of different rulers such as Balban, Muhammad, Cakubad, Jalaluddin Khilji, Mubarak Shah Khaliji, and Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq . He was also a disciple of Nizammuddin Auliya.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":297,"n":323,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Which of the following Sultans of Delhi was appointed as Amir-i-Tuzuk (Master of Ceremonies) before becoming the Sultan?","o":{"a":"Alauddin Khalji","b":"Balban","c":"Iltutmish","d":"Muhammad Bin Tughlaq"},"a":"a","s":"Alauddin Khalji (1296 - 1316). He was the second ruler of Khilji dynasty after Jalaluddin Khilji (Founder - 'Khalji Dynasty'). He was also appointed Arizi-i-Mumalik (Minister of War) during the reign of Jalaluddin Khilji and laid the foundation of his capital Siri in 1303 A.D.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":298,"n":324,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Who was the first to mention the incident of Jauhar in Persian ?","o":{"a":"Ibn Batutah","b":"Amir Khusrau","c":"Ziauddin Barani","d":"Hasan Nizami"},"a":"b","s":"Amir Khusrau. 'Jauhar' - The collective self-immolation by women in order to escape capture and forced into slavery by invaders in face of imminent defeat. In 1303, Alauddin Khilji laid the size of Chittor.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":299,"n":325,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was the slave-general Alauddin Khilji, who led his army in the battle against Ramachandra of Devagiri?","o":{"a":"Nusrat Khan","b":"Malik Kafur","c":"Ulugh Khan","d":"Zafar Khan"},"a":"b","s":"Malik Kafur - Also known as 'Hazar Dinari' Was a slave of the Delhi Sultanate ruler Ala-ud-din Khilji (1296-1316). Alauddin Khilji - second sultan of Khilji dynasty. Important Military Campaigns - Gujarat (1299), Ranthambore (1301), Chittor (1303), Malwa (1305) and Jalore (1311). Administrative Reforms - implemented the system of \"dagh Pratha ,\" branding horses, and \"Huliya Pratha (a system of ranks)\" keeping records of soldiers.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":300,"n":326,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"During Alauddin Khilji's reign, the cloth market was known as______.","o":{"a":"Sarai-i-Adl","b":"Mandi","c":"Munhiyans","d":"Shahna-i-Mandi"},"a":"a","s":"Sarai-i-Adl (Place of justice Alauddin Khilji, under his market policy, built three markets of foodgrain, precious clothes and slave cattle near Kushke Sabz (Hara Rajmahal) within the Badaun gate in Delhi. These were government - aided markets.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":301,"n":327,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Who was the first Sultan of the Delhi Sultanate to start military expeditions into southern India?","o":{"a":"Ghiyasuddin Balban","b":"Alauddin Khalji","c":"Muhammad Tughluq","d":"Shamsuddin Iltutmish"},"a":"b","s":"Alauddin Khalji (1296-1316):- He was the second ruler of the Khilji dynasty of the Delhi Sultanate. Battles related to Alauddin Khilji : Gujarat Vijay : (1299) Karna rescued himself and his family to Devagiri. Ranthambore Vijay : (1301) Chauhan King Hammir Dev defeated Sultan Alauddin Khilji, under Nusrat Khan. Chittor Vijay : (1303) Alauddin Khalji defeated Raja Ratan singh.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 1"},{"id":302,"n":328,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was the founder of the Khilji Dynasty?","o":{"a":"Jalal-ud-din Firoz Khilji","b":"Alauddin Khilji","c":"Nasiruddin Khusrau Shah","d":"Qutbuddin Mubarak Shah"},"a":"a","s":"Jalal-ud-din Firoz Khilji. He ascended to the throne in 1290 AD after deposing Qutbuddin Mubarak Shah, the last ruler of the Slave Dynasty.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":303,"n":329,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Which monument of Khilji dynasty in Delhi has the utilization of red sandstone?","o":{"a":"Moti Masjid","b":"Victoria memorial","c":"Charminar","d":"Alai Darwaza"},"a":"d","s":"Alai Darwaza. Built by Allaudin Khilji the Sultan of Delhi in 1311 AD. Moti Masjid - Aurangzeb (Delhi). Victoria memorial - George V (Kolkata). Charminar - Mohammed Quli Qutub Shah (Hyderabad).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":304,"n":330,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"Which officer under Alauddin Khilji was required to maintain a register of merchants to ensure an adequate supply of goods?","o":{"a":"Nazir","b":"Rais Parwana","c":"Muhtasib","d":"Shahna-i-Mandi"},"a":"d","s":"Shahna-i-Mandi. Alauddin set up three markets at Delhi - First for food-grains, the second for cloth, sugar, ghee, oil, dry fruits and the third for horses, slaves and cattle. Diwan- e - Riyasat - Head of Market. The prices of all varieties of grains were fixed by the first regulation or Zabita. Munhiyans - Secret agents under Allauddin. Parwana Rais - Permit officer under Allauddin.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":305,"n":331,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Khilji Dynasty","q":"How many types of taxes were in the reign of the ruler Alauddin Khalji?","o":{"a":"3","b":"5","c":"6","d":"2"},"a":"a","s":"3 types of taxes: Jaziya (religious tax on Hindu community), Ghari tax (tax on Houses), and Chari tax (tax on cattles). He resisted the Mongol invasion more than 12 times. He followed the Divine Right Theory of Kingship. He was the first to bring the standing army system. He died in 1316 A.D.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":306,"n":332,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Who among the following was the successor of Muhammad-Bin Tughlaq to the throne?","o":{"a":"Ghiyath-ud-din Tughlaq Shah II","b":"Abu Bakr Shah Tughlaq","c":"Nais-ud-Din Mahmud Shah Tughlaq","d":"Firuz Shah Tughlaq"},"a":"d","s":"Firuz Shah Tughlaq (1351 - 1388). He was known as the father of the irrigation approach in India. Diwan -e-Khairat which was built for collecting money for charitable work. He imposed Jizya upon the Brahmins and made it a separate tax. Tughlaq Dynasty (1320 A.D - 1414 A.D) Founder - Ghiyas-ud-din Tughluq (Ghazi Malik).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":307,"n":333,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Who amongst the following raised the banner of revolt against Nasiruddin Khusrau?","o":{"a":"Nasiruddin Tughlaq","b":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq","c":"Feroz Shah Tughlaq","d":"Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq"},"a":"d","s":"Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq or Ghazi malik (1320-1325 AD) - He was the governor of Punjab during the reign of Allauddin khilji. He was the founder of the Tughlaq dynasty who fought against Mongols, and started Tughlaqabad Fort construction.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":308,"n":334,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Who among the following appointed Aziz Khummar a wine distiller to high administrative post?","o":{"a":"Alauddin Khalji","b":"Muhammad Tughluq","c":"Jalaluddin Khalji","d":"Firuz Shah Tughluq"},"a":"b","s":"Muhammad bin Tughluq (1324 -1351) - He was the eldest son of Ghiyasuddin Tughluq (founder of the Tughlaq dynasty). He appointed Firuz Hajjam - a barber, Mank a Tabbakh - a cook, and two gardeners - Ladha and Pira, to high administrative posts. He also decided to transfer his capital from Delhi to Devagiri (Daulatabad).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":309,"n":335,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Ziyauddin Barani wrote his chronicle first in 1356 and another version _____ years later.","o":{"a":"three","b":"eight","c":"two","d":"five"},"a":"c","s":"Two. Ziauddin Barani (1285-1358 CE) wrote his chronicle first in 1356 and wrote another version two years later. He was a Muslim political thinker of the Delhi Sultanate during Muhammad bin Tughlaq and Firuz Shah's reign. Other Books: Tarikh-i-Firoz Shahi and Fatwa-i-Jahandari, Sana-i- Muhammadi, Tarikh-i-Barmaki, etc.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":310,"n":336,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Who among the following Moroccan travellers came to India during the reign of Muhammad bin Tughlaq?","o":{"a":"Abd-al-Razzaq","b":"Al Masudi","c":"Suleiman al-Tajiri","d":"Ibn Battuta"},"a":"d","s":"Ibn Battuta (Moroccan traveler) came to India during the reign of Muhammad bin Tughlaq in 1333-1347. Book: Rihla. Abd-al-Razzaq visited Vijaynagar Kingdom during the reign of Deva Raya II (Sangama dynasty). Al-Masudi (Herodotus of the Arabs) visited India around 10 th century AD. Suleiman al-Tajiri visited India around the 9th century during the Pala empire.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":311,"n":337,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Which of the following is the correct chronological order of the rulers of the Tughlaq dynasty who ruled over Delhi from the year 1320 to 1414?","o":{"a":"Ghiyasuddin Tughluq, Firuz Shah Tughluq, Muhammad Tughluq","b":"Firuz Shah Tughluq, Muhammad Tughluq, Ghiyasuddin Tughluq","c":"Ghiyasuddin Tughluq, Muhammad Tughluq, Firuz Shah Tughluq","d":"Muhammad Tughluq, Firuz Shah Tughluq, Ghiyasuddin Tughluq"},"a":"c","s":"1320-1324 (Ghiyas-ud-din Tughlaq; Founder of Tughlaq dynasty) - 1324-1351 (Muhammad ibn Tughluq) - 1351-1388 (Firuz Shah Tughlaq) - (Muhammad Shah).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":312,"n":338,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Who among the following founded the city of Tughlaqabad?","o":{"a":"Ala-ud-din Khilji","b":"Ghiyas ud-din Tughluq","c":"Firoz Shah Tughlaq","d":"Muhammad bin Tughluq"},"a":"b","s":"Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq (Ghazi Malik). He founded the Tughlaq dynasty in 1320. He replaced men with horses in the courier system. He started Dawk chowkies. His son Jaunakhan ascended the throne and took the title Muhammad bin Tughlaq .","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":313,"n":339,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Tughlaq Dynasty","q":"Which Sultan established a separate department (Diwan-i-Amir Kohi) to take care of agriculture?","o":{"a":"Khizr Khan","b":"Muhammad Bin Tughlaq","c":"Tughril Beg","d":"Jalal-ud-din Khalji"},"a":"b","s":"Muhammad Bin Tughlaq established a separate department (Diwan-i-Amir Kohi) to take care of agriculture. This department was headed by officer Amir-e-Kohi with the aim to improve the condition of cultivation in doab.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":314,"n":340,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Sayyid Dynasty","q":"Who was the first ruler of the Sayyid Dynasty?","o":{"a":"Khizr Khan","b":"Alauddin Alam Shah","c":"Muhammad Shah","d":"Mubarak Khan"},"a":"a","s":"Khizr Khan (In 1414 A.D) - He captured the Delhi sultanate from the Nasir-ud-Din Mahmud Shah Tughluq (Tughlaq dynasty) and founded the Sayyid Dynasty. He was succeeded by Mubarak Shah ( first Sultan ruler to appoint Hindu nobles in the court of Delhi) and constructed \"Mubarakbad\" City. Ala-ud-din Shah - Last ruler of Sayyid dynasty. Rulers- Khizr Khan Sayyid → Mubarak Shah →Muhammad Shah → Ala-ud-Din Shah.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":315,"n":341,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Lodi Dynasty","q":"Which of the following mosques/masjids was built during the reign of Lodi dynasty of the Delhi Sultanate?","o":{"a":"Begumpuri mosque, Delhi","b":"Quwwat al-Islam, Delhi","c":"Lal Masjid, Delhi","d":"Moth ki Masjid, Delhi"},"a":"d","s":"Moth ki Masjid, Delhi. It was built in 1505 by Wazir Miya Bhoiya. Begumpuri mosque (Delhi) - It was constructed by Muhammad Tughluq . Quwwat al-Islam (Delhi) - It was constructed by Qutub-Ud-din Aibak. Lal Masjid (Delhi) - It was built by the Mughal emperor during Akbar's regime.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":316,"n":342,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Lodi Dynasty","q":"\"Double Dome\" feature of Indo-Islamic architecture was introduced by which dynasty of Delhi Sultanate?","o":{"a":"Slave Dynasty","b":"Khilji Dynasty","c":"Tughlaq Dynasty","d":"Lodi Dynasty"},"a":"d","s":"Lodi Dynasty ( 1451-1526 ). It was the fifth and final dynasty of the Delhi Sultanate and was founded by Bahlul Khan Lodi when he replaced the Sayyid dynasty. Double dome - built of two layers. There is one layer inside which provides a ceiling to the interior of the building. The other layer is the outer one which crowns the buildings. Example - Humayun's Tomb.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":317,"n":343,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Lodi Dynasty","q":"Who was the last sultan of the Lodi Dynasty?","o":{"a":"Khizr Khan","b":"Sikander Lodi","c":"Ibrahim Lodi","d":"Bahlol Lodi"},"a":"c","s":"Ibrahim Khan Lodi (1517-1526 AD) was the last Sultan of the Lodi Dynasty (1451 to 1526). The first battle of Panipat (1526 AD) - Babar defeated and killed Ibrahim Lodi. The tomb of Ibrahim Lodi is in Panipat (Haryana).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":318,"n":344,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Lodi Dynasty","q":"Bahlol Lodi was succeeded by his son __________under the title of Sultan Sikandar Shah in 1489. Graduate Level 03/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Nizam Khan","b":"Khizr Khan","c":"Bahlul Shah","d":"Sher Shah"},"a":"a","s":"Nizam Khan {assumed the title Sikandar Shah,founded Agra in 1504, relocated the capital from Delhi to Agra, pen name Gulrukh} . Bahlul Lodi {founder of Lodi Dynasty, Conquered Gwalior, Samthal, Sakit and Mewat, died in 1489 A.D}.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":319,"n":345,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Lodi Dynasty","q":"The _____ sultanate is ruled by the Sharqi dynasty.","o":{"a":"Agra","b":"Delhi","c":"Jaunpur","d":"Bharatpur"},"a":"c","s":"The Jaunpur sultanate was ruled by the sharqi dynasty. It was founded by Khwajah-i-Jahan Malik Sarwar in 1394. In 1479, Sultan Hussain Khan was defeated by the forces of Afghan chieftain Bahlul Lodhi and this dynasty came to an end.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":320,"n":346,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"Who among the following was given the charge of looking after the imperial household during the Mughal administration?","o":{"a":"Mir Saman","b":"Mir Bahri","c":"Mir Bakshi","d":"'Mir-e-aatish'"},"a":"a","s":"Mir Saman. Mir Bahri (mir-i-bahr) - Responsible for the maintenance of the river and seaports. Mir Bakshi - Headed military department, nobility, information and intelligence agencies. Mir-e-aatish - Head of the ordinance department.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":321,"n":347,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"Name the French jeweler who traveled to India at least six times during the Mughal period.","o":{"a":"Antonio Monserrate","b":"Jean-Baptiste Tavernier","c":"Seydi Ali Reis","d":"Peter Mundy"},"a":"b","s":"Jean-Baptiste Tavernier. He Came to India during the reign of Shah Jahan. Antonio Monserrate : A Spanish missionary, came during the reign of Akbar. Seydi Ali Reis : He was a Turkish traveler who visited India in the 16th century. Peter Mundy : A European philosopher and foreign traveler came to India during the reign of Shah Jahan.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":322,"n":348,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"Who built the Pathar Ki Masjid in Patna, Bihar?","o":{"a":"Feroz shah Tughlaq","b":"Parwez Shah","c":"Aurangzeb","d":"Alauddin Khilji"},"a":"b","s":"Parwez Shah (Son of Mughal emperor Jahangir) built \"Pathar Ki Masjid\" on the banks of River Ganga, Patna (Bihar) in 1621.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":323,"n":349,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"On 12 August ________ the Mughal emperor appointed the East India Company as the Diwan of Bengal.","o":{"a":"1765","b":"1768","c":"1772","d":"1760"},"a":"a","s":"On 12 August 1765, the Mughal emperor (Shah Alam II) appointed the East India Company as the Diwan of Bengal.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":324,"n":350,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"_______ in Gujarat was the emporium of western trade during the Mughal Period.","o":{"a":"Navasari","b":"Narmada","c":"Porbandar","d":"Surat"},"a":"d","s":"Surat. It grew famous for all types of textiles, particularly cotton textiles.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":325,"n":351,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"The Mughal court chronicles are written in _____","o":{"a":"Arabic","b":"Persian","c":"Turkish","d":"Urdu"},"a":"b","s":"Persian . Mughal chronicles such as the Akbar Nama were written in Persian . Some of the famous Books during Mughal Reign are Humayun Nama was written by Gulbadan Begum, Tuzuk E Babari - Babur, Akbar Nama- Abul Fazl, Tarikh E Akbari - Abul Fazl, Tahakik E Hind- Albiruni, Laila Majnu- Amir Khasuru, Shahnamah- Firdousi etc","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":326,"n":352,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"With reference to the Land Revenue System of Mughal Empire, the term ' was the amount assessed.","o":{"a":"Banjar","b":"Polaj","c":"Hasil","d":"Jama"},"a":"d","s":"Jama. The arrangements for collection of land revenue consisted of two stages : first assessment and then actual collection. The Jama was the amount assessed whereas the Hasil was the amount received or collected. Banjar was the worst kind of land that was left out of cultivation for five years or upwards.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":327,"n":353,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"Which Mughal prince translated the Upanishads into Persian in 1657?","o":{"a":"Sultan Luftallah","b":"Dara Shikoh","c":"Murad Mirza","d":"Shah Suja"},"a":"b","s":"Dara Shikoh . He was the eldest son and heir-apparent of the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan. He completed the translation of fifty upanishads from their original Sanskrit to Persian in 1657 so that they could be studied by muslim scholars.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":328,"n":354,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Mughal Period","q":"Who was the Mughal Emperor at the time of Battle of Plassey?","o":{"a":"Alamgir II","b":"Siraj- Ud- Daulah","c":"Mir Qasim","d":"Shah Alam II"},"a":"a","s":"Aziz-ud-din Alamgir II Alamgir II (Reign - 1754-1759) was the fifteenth Mughal Emperor.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":329,"n":355,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"Who made the use of Gunpowder common in North India to defeat enemies?","o":{"a":"Ibrahim Lodi","b":"Babur","c":"Rana Sanga","d":"Daulat Khan Lodi"},"a":"b","s":"Babur ( 1526-1530 ): The first Mughal emperor, succeeded to the throne of Ferghana in 1494, when he was only 12 years old. His battles: First Battle of Panipat (1526) - Defeated Ibrahim Lodi and his Afghan supporters at Panipat. Battle of Khanwa (1527) - Defeated Rana Sanga, Rajput rulers and allies at Khanua. Battle of Chanderi (1528) - Defeated the Rajputs at Chanderi. Battle of Ghaghra (1529) - Fought between Babar and Afghans.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":330,"n":356,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"Who was the eldest of four sons of Babur?","o":{"a":"Askari","b":"Humayun","c":"Hindal","d":"Kamran"},"a":"b","s":"Humayun - The second ruler of the mughal empire. The Afghan soldier Sher Shah Suri defeated him at the war in Chausa 1539 and Kannauj in 1540. Humayun was the first person who developed a citadel in Delhi, named as \"Din Panah\".","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":331,"n":357,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"The first battle of Panipat took place between Babur and _________.","o":{"a":"Medini Rai","b":"Sikandar Lodi","c":"Ibrahim Lodi","d":"Rana Sanga"},"a":"c","s":"Ibrahim Lodi . The First Battle of Panipat was fought in 1526. Second Battle of Panipat (1556): Hemu Vikramaditya and Akbar . Third Battle of Panipat (1761): Marathas and Ahmad Shah Abdali.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":332,"n":358,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"Babur defeated Mahmud Lodi and Sultan Nusrat Shah thus establishing Mughal rule in India in the Battle of __________ in 1529.","o":{"a":"Chausa","b":"Khanwa","c":"Ghagra","d":"Kannauj"},"a":"c","s":"Ghagra (Babur vs Sultan mahmud Lodhi) - Babur defeated Sultan mahmud Lodhi. Battle of Chausa (1539) - Fought between Humayun and Sher Shah Suri. Battle of Kannauj (1540) - Fought between Sher Shah Suri and Humayun.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":333,"n":359,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"Who among the following Mughal Emperors had assumed the title of Padshah?","o":{"a":"Jahangir","b":"Babur","c":"Humayun","d":"Akbar"},"a":"b","s":"Babur. He was the first Timurid ruler who, after his conquest of Kabul (1507), assumed the title of Padshah which means \"king of kings\" or \"emperor in persian\".","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":334,"n":360,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"After years of wandering Babur seized Kabul in_________.","o":{"a":"1501","b":"1504","c":"1503","d":"1502"},"a":"b","s":"1504. Babur - Founder of the Mughal Empire in India. Other Battles : First Battle of Panipat (1526) - Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi, Battle of Chanderi (1528) - Babur defeated Madiniray.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":335,"n":361,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"The famous monument, Aram Bagh, was built by Babur. It is located in __________.","o":{"a":"Delhi","b":"Uttar Pradesh","c":"Madhya Pradesh","d":"Bihar"},"a":"b","s":"Uttar Pradesh . Aram Bagh - Built by Babur in 1528 in Agra. Babur was the founder of the Mughal Empire. Monuments Built by Babur: The avenue garden in Kabul, the Panipat Mosque and the Kabuli Bagh Mosque.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":336,"n":362,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"Where was Babur buried for the first time in 1530 ? Graduate Level 02/08/2022 ( Shift - 3 )","o":{"a":"Kabul","b":"Agra","c":"Delhi","d":"Fargana"},"a":"b","s":"Agra. On December 26, 1530, Babur died at Agra. His body was first laid at Arambagh in Agra but was later taken to Kabul, where it was buried.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":337,"n":363,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"Who among the following took the title of 'Ghazi' after defeating Rana Sanga in the Battle of Khanwa in 1527?","o":{"a":"Babur","b":"Daulat Khan Lodhi","c":"Humayun","d":"Mahmud Shah I"},"a":"a","s":"Babur. Battle of Khanwa (1527) - Babur defeated Rana Sanga of Mewar and his allies and gained the title Ghazi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":338,"n":364,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Babur","q":"'Memoirs of Babur' or 'Baburnama', also known as 'Tuzk-e Babri', was written by:","o":{"a":"Faizi","b":"Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan","c":"Babur","d":"Talib Amah"},"a":"c","s":"Babur (Reign 1526-1530) - Original name - Zahiruddin Muhammad, Founder of the Mughal Empire.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":339,"n":365,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","q":"Which of the following cities was built by Humayun in Delhi?","o":{"a":"Akbarabad","b":"Dinpanah","c":"Muhammadabad","d":"Siri"},"a":"b","s":"Dinpanah. Humayun's Contribution to Architecture: Humayun's Tomb (UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1993) - It was built by his widow Biga Begum (Hajji Begum), Purana Quila (Delhi) - Built as a part of the new city Dinpannah. Akbarabad (Agra) - Sikandar Lodi founded it in 1504. Siri Fort - It was built by Alauddin Khilji. Muhammadabad - Mahmud Begarha (ruler of Gujarat) captured the fort of Champaner which controlled Malwa and Khandesh and constructed a new town called Muhammadabad.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":340,"n":366,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","q":"In which State of India is Sher Shah's Tomb located?","o":{"a":"Bihar","b":"Uttar Pradesh","c":"Punjab","d":"Bengal"},"a":"a","s":"Bihar (Sasaram) . Tombs of Mughal Emperors - Gardens of Babur (Kabul, Afghanistan), Humayun's Tomb (New Delhi), Akbar's Tomb (Sikandra, Uttar Pradesh), Tomb of Jahangir (Lahore, Pakistan), The Taj Mahal (Agra), Tomb of Aurangzeb (Ahmednagar).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":341,"n":367,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","q":"Who among the following medieval rulers focused his attention on the construction of sarais (shelter) for the convenience of travellers ?","o":{"a":"Babur","b":"Sher Shah Suri","c":"Akbar","d":"Humayun"},"a":"b","s":"Sher Shah Suri (1540-1545) : Administrative reforms - Introduction of Rupiya, Built Grand-Trunk Road, Staged Dak-Chowkis in Sarais.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":342,"n":368,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","q":"Which of the following rulers is credited with the construction of Purana Quila in Delhi? Higher Secondary 28/06/2023 ( Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Babar and Akbar","b":"Qutub ud din and Iltutmish","c":"ShahJahan and Jahangir","d":"Humayun and Sher Shah"},"a":"d","s":"Humayun and Sher Shah Sher Shah Suri was the founder of the Suri dynasty. He defeated Humayun in 1539 and 1540 in the battle of Chausa and Kannauj respectively. The construction of Purana Qila was started in the reign of Humayunas a part of his new city of Dinpanah in the 16th century and completed by Sher Shah Suri ('The Lion King').","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":343,"n":369,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","q":"The historical Sher-Shah Suri Marg runs between ___________.","o":{"a":"Kanpur and Kanyakumari","b":"Delhi and Manali","c":"Delhi and Amritsar","d":"Lucknow and Kolkata"},"a":"c","s":"Delhi and Amritsar. The Grand Trunk Road (formerly known as Uttarapath, Sarak-e-Azam and Badshahi Sarak) - Part of National Highway (12, 27, 19, 44, 3) in India.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":344,"n":370,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","q":"Humayun's Tomb situated in Delhi is built by ______ .","o":{"a":"Gulbadan Begum","b":"Biga Begum","c":"Hamida Begum","d":"Maham Begum"},"a":"b","s":"Biga Begum . The architect was Mirak Mirza Ghiyath.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":345,"n":371,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Humayun and Sher Shah Suri","q":"Mirza Kamran was the brother of which of the following Mughal emperors?","o":{"a":"Humayun","b":"Akbar","c":"Jahangir","d":"Shah Jahan"},"a":"a","s":"Humayun and son of Babur and Gulrukh Begum. He came to India in 1538.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":346,"n":372,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Tansen, the famous musician, often sang in the court of which ruler?","o":{"a":"Akbar","b":"Sher Shah Suri","c":"Feroz Shah Tughlaq","d":"Allaudin Khilji"},"a":"a","s":"Akbar was the third Mughal emperor, and he ruled over India from 1556 to 1605. Tansen was born into a Hindu Gaur Brahmin family in Behat, near Gwalior, India. Ramtanu was his birth name and was a disciple of Swami Haridas in earlier days and later on, he learned music from Hazrat Muhammad Ghaus. Navratnas of Akbar - Abul Fazl, Faizi, Tansen, Birbal,Todar Mal, Raja Man Singh, Abdul Rahim Khan-I-Khana, Fakir Aziao-Din, Mullah Do Piaza.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":347,"n":373,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"When was Khandesh merged into the Mughal empire by Akbar ?","o":{"a":"1595","b":"1601","c":"1597","d":"1605"},"a":"b","s":"1601. Khandesh was situated in the Tapti valley, Maharashtra. It was founded by Malik Raja Faruqi. Major military campaigns by Akbar - In 1568 the Sisodiya capital of Chittor was seized and in 1569 Ranthambore. 1585 -1605 - expansion of Akbar's empire.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":348,"n":374,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Who introduced the Mansabdari system into the administration under the Mughals?","o":{"a":"Akbar","b":"Shah Jahan","c":"Aurangzeb","d":"Jahangir"},"a":"a","s":"Akbar . The Mansabdari system was a grading system used by the Mughal rulers to fix the rank and salary of a Mansabdar. His rank was determined by a numerical value called 'Zat' . Mansabdars maintained a stipulated number of sawars with horses and equipment. 'Sawar' rank indicated the number of cavalrymen he was required to maintain.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":349,"n":375,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Jaimal and Patta were the warriors who were given charge of defending the fort of ______","o":{"a":"Raisen","b":"Ajmer","c":"Udaipur","d":"Chittor"},"a":"d","s":"Chittor. Siege of Chittorgarh (1567 - 1568) - It was a military expedition of the Mughal Empire under Akbar against the Mewar kingdom that commenced in 1567 during which the Mughals successfully captured the fort of Chittorgarh.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":350,"n":376,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Tansen, the music legend, was a courtier in which of the following courts before coming to Akbar ?","o":{"a":"Rewa","b":"Benaras","c":"Gandhara","d":"Maghad"},"a":"a","s":"Rewa. Tansen was initially the court singer of King Ram Chand. His original name was RamaTanu Pandey. Tansen was the title given to him by Raja Vikramjit of Gwalior.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":351,"n":377,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"During the reign of Akbar, _________ was the land that has lain fallow for three or four years.","o":{"a":"Parauti","b":"Polaj","c":"Chachar","d":"Banjar"},"a":"c","s":"Chachar. Parauti - The land kept out of cultivation temporarily in order to recoup its lost fertility. Polaj - The ideal and best type of land throughout the empire and it was cultivated always and was never allowed to lie fallow.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":352,"n":378,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Which of the following cities was the first planned city of the Mughals?","o":{"a":"Mirzapur","b":"Fatehpur Sikri","c":"Lucknow","d":"Lahore"},"a":"b","s":"Fatehpur Sikri. It is located near Agra in Uttar Pradesh. It was built by the Mughal Emperor Akbar in the 16th century. Fatehpur Sikri was built during 1571 and 1585. Fatehpur Sikri was declared as UNESCO World Heritage Sites in 1986.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":353,"n":379,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"When was the Battle of Tukaroi fought where Akbar defeated the Sultanate of Bengal and Bihar?","o":{"a":"1564","b":"1582","c":"1575","d":"1591"},"a":"c","s":"1575. The Battle of Tukaroi (Battle of Bajhaura/ Moghalmari) - Fought at Balasore (Odisha) in which Akbar (Mughal Emperor) defeated Daud Khan Karrani (Ruler of Bengal). The battle led to the Treaty of Katak in which Daud Khan Karrani ceded the whole of Bengal and Bihar, retaining only Odisha.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":354,"n":380,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"_________ administration became the model followed by the great emperor Akbar when he consolidated the Mughal Empire.","o":{"a":"Sher Shah Suri's","b":"Alauddin Khalji's","c":"Muhammad Tughluq's","d":"Genghis Khan's"},"a":"a","s":"Sher Shah Suri's. Although the Suri dynasty ruled for only a short period i.e. 15 years, it introduced an administration that borrowed elements from Alauddin Khilji and made them more efficient.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":355,"n":381,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Ain-i Akbari, a document on life of Mughal ruler Akbar does not contain information about _________","o":{"a":"Revenues","b":"Ancestors","c":"Army","d":"Administration"},"a":"b","s":"Ancestors. Abul Fazl (Akbar's court historian) wrote a three-volume history of Akbar's reign, titled Akbar Nama. The first volume (Akbar's ancestors), second volume (events of Akbar's reign) and The third (the Ain-i Akbari). It deals with Akbar's administration, household, army, the revenues and geography of his empire. Ain-i Akbari is divided into five volumes .","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":356,"n":382,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Ajmer became the suba headquarters under the ______.","o":{"a":"Pallavas","b":"Cholas","c":"Mughals","d":"Delhi Sultanate"},"a":"c","s":"Mughals. Ajmer Subah - one of the original 12 subahs of Mughal Empire (1526 - 1858). It was established during Akbar's administrative reforms of 1572-1580. The last Mughal - Bahadur Shah Zafar.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":357,"n":383,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Who among the following is said to have devised many Raagas or melodies in Hindustani music?","o":{"a":"Tansen","b":"Raja Beerbal","c":"Todar Mal","d":"Raja Man Singh"},"a":"a","s":"Tansen (Ramtanu Pandey Ragas - Multani, Bhairavi and Todi, Night raga (Darbari Kanhra), Morning raga (Mian Ki Todi), Mid-day raga (Mian ki Sarang), Seasonal raga (Mian ki Malhar).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":358,"n":384,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"In which year was Kashmir annexed to the Mughal Empire by Akbar? Graduate Level 01/08/2022 (Shift - 4)","o":{"a":"1586","b":"1590","c":"1592","d":"1580"},"a":"a","s":"1586. Military Conquests of Akbar (Mughal Emperor, 1556-1605) : Malwa (conquered in 1562), Gondwana region of central India (1564), Gujarat (1573), Mewar (1576), both Bihar and Bengal (1576).","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":359,"n":385,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"The revenue system of the Mughal period during Akbar's reign was called ______.","o":{"a":"Iqta","b":"Zabti","c":"Ghari","d":"Charai"},"a":"b","s":"Zabti System (Dahsala System), a land revenue system, was introduced by Mughal emperor Akbar's finance minister Raja Todarmal in 1580-82. Under this system, average produce of ten years was derived. In the Iqta system (introduced by Iltutmish) the lands of the Delhi Sultanate were divided into several large and small tracts called Iqta and assigned these Iqtas to the Sultan's soldiers, officers and nobles. Allaudin Khilji imposed taxes called Ghari and Charai (tax on cattle).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":360,"n":386,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"As described in 'Ain-i-Akbari' by Abul Fazl-i-Allami, 'gaz' (unit of measuring length) was divided into equal parts called ______.","o":{"a":"tassuj","b":"liksha","c":"rajahkan","d":"angul"},"a":"a","s":"tassuj . Akbarnama is divided into three books - The first book dealt with Akbar's ancestors. The second recorded the events of Akbar's reign. The third is the Ain-i Akbari.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":361,"n":387,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Military commanders in Akbar's empire were called '______'.","o":{"a":"faujdars","b":"bakhshis","c":"kotwals","d":"diwans"},"a":"a","s":"'Faujdars'. Kotwals often controlled the fort of a major town or an area of smaller towns on behalf of another ruler.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":362,"n":388,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Who among the following issued Mahzarnama in 1579 ?","o":{"a":"Humayun","b":"Jahangir","c":"Shah Jahan","d":"Akbar"},"a":"d","s":"Akbar issued Mahzarnama in 1579. It is a declaration that gives authority to change the religious laws of Islam.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":363,"n":389,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"Which Mughal emperor built the city called 'Makhsudabad' later popularly known as Murshidabad?","o":{"a":"Bahadur Shah Zafar","b":"Humayun","c":"Akbar","d":"Shah Jahan"},"a":"c","s":"Akbar. Murshidabad was named by Nawab Murshid Qulī Khan. Akbar (reign - 1556 - 1605) - Constructed Fatehpur Sikri (made it his capital in 1569), Started a new religion Din-i Ilahi (1582), His biography titled Akbarnama (Wrote by Abul Fazl).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":364,"n":390,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Akbar","q":"In the context of Mughal administration, which of the following is the most appropriate definition of 'Abwab'?","o":{"a":"A clerk in the government treasury","b":"A court jester","c":"A tax levied on the lands over and above the original rent","d":"A reward for good governance"},"a":"c","s":"A tax levied on the lands over and above the original rent in Mughal India all temporary and circumstantial taxes and impositions levied by the government over and above regular taxes were called \"abwab\".","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":365,"n":391,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Jahangir","q":"Who succeeded in securing trading concessions for the East India Company from Mughal Emperor Jahangir?","o":{"a":"John Mildenhall","b":"Captain Hawkins","c":"Captain Best","d":"Thomas Roe"},"a":"d","s":"Thomas Roe was an English diplomat sent by the East India Company in 1615. John Mildenhall was the first British explorer to make an overland journey to India. Captain William Hawkins was an English sea captain who visited the court of Jahangir in 1608.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":366,"n":392,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Jahangir","q":"Sisodiya Rajput ruler of Mewar, Amar Singh accepted Mughal services during the reign of ________.","o":{"a":"Akbar","b":"Jahangir","c":"Aurangzeb","d":"Babur"},"a":"b","s":"Jahangir. Amar singh was the eldest son and successor of Maharana Pratap. Akbar (1556-1605). Aurangzeb belonged to the aristocratic Timurid dynasty, and held administrative and military posts under his father Shah Jahan (1628 - 1658).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":367,"n":393,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Jahangir","q":"Who among the following wrote the biography of Jahangir known as Iqbalnama-i Jahangiri?","o":{"a":"Mutamid Khan","b":"Inayat Khan","c":"Abdul Rahim Khan","d":"Abul Fazl"},"a":"a","s":"Mutamid Khan. He was in the court of Mughal Emperor Jahangir (4 th Mughal ruler, 1605 to 1627). 'Iqbalnama-i Jahangiri' book was written in Persian and it covers the first 19 years of Jahangir's reign. Inayat Khan wrote\" Shajahanama\". Abdul Rahim Khan translated \"Babarnama\" to the Persian language.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":368,"n":394,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Jahangir","q":"'Tuzuk- i -Jahangiri' was originally written in which of the following languages?","o":{"a":"Pali","b":"Arabic","c":"Persian","d":"Sanskrit"},"a":"c","s":"Persian. Jahangir (Nur - ud -Din Muhammad Salim) - Wrote his autobiography Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri (Jahangirnama).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":369,"n":395,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Jahangir","q":"Sarai Nurmahal is a centrally protected monument of India. It is situated in which state of India?","o":{"a":"Uttar Pradesh","b":"West Bengal","c":"Punjab","d":"Himachal Pradesh"},"a":"c","s":"Jalandhar (Punjab). Shalimar Bagh, the Begum Shahi Mosque, and the Bachcha Taj are the monuments built by the Jahangir (Fourth Mughal emperor).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":370,"n":396,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Jahangir","q":"Mehrunnisa married Emperor Jahangir in ______.","o":{"a":"1620","b":"1602","c":"1611","d":"1603"},"a":"c","s":"In 1611 , Jahangir married Mehrunnisa who was known as Nur Jahan (Light of World). She encouraged Persian art and culture in the court.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":371,"n":397,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"Who among the following has written the 'Badshah Nama'?","o":{"a":"Babur","b":"Abdul Hamid Lahori","c":"Inayat Khan","d":"Abul Fazl"},"a":"b","s":"Abdul Hamid Lahori - A historian and traveler who later became a court historian of Shah Jahan. \"Badshah Nama\" (Padshahnama) is the official history of Shah Jahan. This book described the life and activities of Shah Jahan during the first twenty years of his reign. \"Akbarnama\" and \"Ain-I-Akbari\" were written by Abul Fazl.","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":372,"n":398,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"The mausoleum of the Taj Mahal was designed by _________________.","o":{"a":"Bhaktiyar Lahori","b":"Mohammad Isha","c":"Shah Jahan","d":"Ustad Ahmad Lahori"},"a":"d","s":"Ustad Ahmad Lahori was the chief Mughal architect during the reign of Shah Jahan. He was responsible for the construction of the Taj Mahal in Agra and the Red fort in Delhi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":373,"n":399,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"The Taj Mahal was built by:","o":{"a":"Humayun","b":"Jahangir","c":"Akbar","d":"Shah Jahan"},"a":"d","s":"Shah Jahan (1627-1658 AD): He was the fifth Mughal ruler after Babur (1526-1530), Humayun (1530-1540 and 1555-1556), Akbar (1556-1605) and Jahangir (1605-1627). Monuments constructed by Shah Jahan: Red Fort (Delhi), Jama Masjid (Delhi), Moti Masjid (Lahore).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":374,"n":400,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"The art of decoration, called Pietra dura became popular during whose reign?","o":{"a":"Jahangir","b":"Sher Shah Suri","c":"Shah Jahan","d":"Akbar"},"a":"c","s":"Shah Jahan. Pietra dura - An inlay technique using cut and fitted, highly polished colored stones to create images. Some other buildings and monuments by Shah Jahan:- Red Fort (Delhi), Jama Masjid (Delhi), and Shalimar Bagh (Lahore).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":375,"n":401,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"Who among the following Mughal emperors built Sheesh Mahal in Agra?","o":{"a":"Shah Jahan","b":"Humayun","c":"Babur","d":"Akbar"},"a":"a","s":"Shah Jahan. Buildings built by Shah Jahan - Taj Mahal, Jama Masjid in Delhi, Red Fort, Moti Masjid.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":376,"n":402,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"Which among the following was the son of the Mughal emperor Shahjahan?","o":{"a":"Humayun","b":"Jahangir","c":"Aurangzeb","d":"Sikandar Lodhi"},"a":"c","s":"Aurangzeb. He was the third son of Shajahan from his wife Mumtaz Mahal. He is widely considered as the last effective ruler of the Mughal Dynasty.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":377,"n":403,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"Ahmadnagar was finally annexed by the Mughals in_______","o":{"a":"1627","b":"1632","c":"1630","d":"1635"},"a":"b","s":"In 1632, during the period of Shah Jahan, Ahmednagar was merged into the Mughal empire. Malik Ahmed was the founder of Ahmednagar (Maharashtra), who established the Nizam Shahi dynasty.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":378,"n":404,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"Who among the following had written the book 'Char Chaman', during the reign of Shah Jahan, describing the Mughal nobility?","o":{"a":"Chandrabhan Brahman","b":"Gulbadan Begum","c":"Muhammad Waris","d":"Abu'l Dazl"},"a":"a","s":"Chandrabhan Brahman . He was the highly valued minister in the court of Shah Jahan and is credited with writing the first collection of Urdu poetry.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":379,"n":405,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Shah Jahan","q":"Who among the following had been given the title of 'Sultan Buland Iqbal' by Shah Jahan?","o":{"a":"Shuja","b":"Murad","c":"Dara","d":"Aurangzeb"},"a":"c","s":"Dara Shikoh (Eldest Son of Shah Jahan). Sultan Buland Iqbal (Prince of High Fortune) - Title given on 10 September 1642. His works - Majma -ul-Bahrain (Mingling of Two Oceans), Sirr-i-Akbar (Great Mystery). He was executed in 1659 by Aurangzeb in the war of succession.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":380,"n":406,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Aurangzeb","q":"Who among the following was the successor of Mughal emperor Shahjahan to the throne ?","o":{"a":"Murad Bakhsh","b":"Dara Shikoh","c":"Aurangzeb","d":"Shah Shuja"},"a":"c","s":"Aurangzeb (1658-1707) was the last of the powerful Mughal rulers. Mughal Emperors: Babur (1526-1530), Humayun (1530-1540, 1555-1556), Akbar (1556-1605), Jahangir (1605-1627), Shah Jahan (1627-1658). Mughals were descendants of Genghis Khan (mother side) and Timur (Father side). In 1856, Governor-General Lord Canning decided that Bahadur Shah Zafar would be the last Mughal king.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":381,"n":407,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Aurangzeb","q":"Under whose reign was the maximum number of books in Persian on classical Indian Music written?","o":{"a":"Shah Jahan","b":"Jahangir","c":"Akbar","d":"Aurangzeb"},"a":"d","s":"Aurangzeb (1658 - 1707) . He reimposed Jizya and Pilgrim tax in 1679 and ended the celebration of Navroz. He banned music, singing and dancing in the Royal court but not the playing of musical instruments.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":382,"n":408,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Aurangzeb","q":"Which Mughal Emperor has attempted to vandalise the Kailash Temple at Ellora?","o":{"a":"Aurangzeb","b":"Humayun","c":"Shah Jahan","d":"Babur"},"a":"a","s":"Aurangzeb { Alamgir, original name Mu\u0000I al-Din Mu\u0000ammad ( 1658 to 1707)} - He stopped music and alcohol in the court and also the activities of 'jharokha' (public audience).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":383,"n":409,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Aurangzeb","q":"Who among the following Mughal emperors assumed the title Alamgir, World Conqueror ?","o":{"a":"Akbar","b":"Aurangzeb","c":"Babur","d":"Shah Jahan"},"a":"b","s":"Aurangzeb (1658-1707 (sixth Mughal Emperor). He was also known as Zinda Pir (living saint), Fakir, Darbesh. He gave the French permission to build a factory in Surat in 1667. He banned singing, dancing and playing musical instruments, and Kalma writings on coins.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":384,"n":410,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Aurangzeb","q":"Bijapur was annexed by Aurangzeb in which year?","o":{"a":"1689","b":"1686","c":"1687","d":"1683"},"a":"b","s":"1686. Aurangzeb invaded Bijapur under Sikandar Adil Shah and annexed it. This brought an end to the Adil Shahi dynasty. The Deccan Sultanates were the five different Muslim-ruled dynasties of the medieval period that ruled in Golconda (Quli Qutub - ul- Shah), Bijapur (Yusuf Adil Shah), Bidar (Qasim Barid), Ahmednagar (Malik Ahmad Nizam Shah), and Berar (Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk). Bijapur (Gol Gumbaz, 1656 - the mausoleum of Muhammad Adil Shah).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":385,"n":411,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Aurangzeb","q":"Who among the following was defeated by Aurangzeb in the Battle of Deorai?","o":{"a":"Shah Jahan","b":"Shah Shuja","c":"Dara Shikoh","d":"Murad Baksh"},"a":"c","s":"Dara Shikoh was defeated by Aurangzeb in the Battle of Deorai(1659). Aurangzeb is known for being the emperor of India from 1658 to 1707.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":386,"n":412,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Aurangzeb","q":"Who is the author of the book 'Tarikh-i Dilkusha'?","o":{"a":"Panini","b":"Bhimsen","c":"Sushruta","d":"Charaka"},"a":"b","s":"Bhimsen . Bhimsen, as a historian, is of the reign of Aurangzeb and is remarkable for his ability to think independently and record these thoughts on paper.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":387,"n":413,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Sikh Guru","q":"After the death of Shri Guru Gobind Singh, the Sikhs revolted against the ______ under the leadership of Banda Bahadur.","o":{"a":"Gurkhas","b":"Mughals","c":"British","d":"Marathas"},"a":"b","s":"Mughals. He is famous for his creation of the Khalsa (Pure) , the military brotherhood of the Sikhs. Patna Sahib city was founded by Maharaja Ranjit Singh. Dasam granth was written by Guru Gobind Singh.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":388,"n":414,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Sikh Guru","q":"Who among the following Sikh spiritual gurus is said to have assisted Khusrau, the rebellious son of Jahangir?","o":{"a":"Guru Angad Dev","b":"Guru Ramdas","c":"Guru Amar Das","d":"Guru Arjun Dev"},"a":"d","s":"Guru Arjan Dev assisted Khusrau, the rebellious son of Jahangir. He compiled the first official edition of the Sikh scripture called the Adi Granth, which later expanded into the Guru Granth Sahib. He completed the construction of the Golden Temple in Amritsar.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":389,"n":415,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Sikh Guru","q":"Who among the following Sikh Gurus laid the foundation of 'Khalsa Panth' ?","o":{"a":"Guru Gobind Singh","b":"Guru Arjan Dev","c":"Guru Teg Bahadur","d":"Guru Nanak Dev"},"a":"a","s":"Guru Gobind Singh laid the foundation of 'Khalsa Panth' on April 13, 1699, situated in Punjab. It is a name given to the religious structure and the community that manages the affairs of the global Khalsa community.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":390,"n":416,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Maratha Empire","q":"Twenty five percent of the land revenue collected by Marathas in the Deccan was __________ .","o":{"a":"Bhoga","b":"Sardeshmukhi","c":"Chauth","d":"Bhaga"},"a":"c","s":"Chauth - It was paid to the Marathas in order to avoid the Maratha raids. It was levied on the lands which were under the nominal Mughal rule. Sardeshmukhi was an additional ten percent levy on lands on which the Marathas claimed hereditary rights. Bhoga (Gupta period) refers to the tax on gifts, flowers, wood, fruits, etc. Bhaga (Mauryan period) - Tax on agricultural land levied at the rate of 1/6th.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":391,"n":417,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Maratha Empire","q":"Baji Rao I or Baji Rao Ballal was the son of ________.","o":{"a":"Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath","b":"Raghunath Rao","c":"Shivaji","d":"Madhav Rao"},"a":"a","s":"Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath (1713- 1720) . He was the first of a line of hereditary Peshwas from the Bhat family. Baji Rao I (1720-40) - Succeeded Balaji Vishwanath. He was the Seventh Peshwa (Prime Minister) of the Maratha Empire and one of the greatest exponents of Guerrilla tactics after Shivaji. Maratha families: Shindes (Gwalior), Holkars (Indore), Gaikwads (Baroda), Bhosles (Nagpur), Peshwas (Poona).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":392,"n":418,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Maratha Empire","q":"Who among the following was a famous ruler of the Maratha dynasty?","o":{"a":"Porus","b":"Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj","c":"Man Singh","d":"Prithviraj Chauhan"},"a":"b","s":"Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj The Maratha Empire formerly existed from 1674 with the coronation of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and ended in 1818 with the defeat of Peshwa Bajirao II at the hands of the British East India Company. Wars fought by Shivaji - Battle of Pratapgad (1659), Battle of Purandar (1665), Battle of Sinhagad (1670), Battle of Sangamner(1679), etc.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":393,"n":419,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Maratha Empire","q":"The Council of Ministers during the time of Shivaji Maharaj was known as:","o":{"a":"Agraharam","b":"Ashtadiggajas","c":"Ashta Pradhan","d":"Navaratnas"},"a":"c","s":"Ashta Pradhan. The coronation of Shivaji Maharaj was held in 1674, at the fort of Raigad in present-day Indian state of Maharashtra.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":394,"n":420,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"What was the dominant source of income in the Vijaynagar Empire?","o":{"a":"Mining","b":"Industrial duty","c":"Land revenue","d":"Import duty"},"a":"c","s":"Land revenue. Vijayanagar empire: Harihara and Bukka are the founders of the Vijayanagar Empire in 1336 A.D. on the southern banks of Tungabhadra River. Hampi was the capital of Vijayanagar. Sources of income - Land Revenue, tributes, and gifts from vassals and feudal chiefs, customs at ports.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":395,"n":421,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"The Vijayanagar kingdom was established by _________ in 1336.","o":{"a":"Venkata II","b":"Devaraya II","c":"Harihara and Bukka","d":"Krishnadevaraya"},"a":"c","s":"Harihara and Bukka ( Sangama dynasty ): They were originally feudatories of the Kakatiyas of Warangal. Bukka-I sent an ambassador to the court of a Chinese king belonging to the Ming dynasty. Vijayanagara Empire: Capital - Hampi (along the river Tungabhadra). Ruled by 4 Dynasties - Sangama, Saluva, Tuluva, and Aravidu. Krishnadevaraya (1509-29 AD) - Ruler of the Tuluva dynasty of Vijayanagar empire. Dev Raya II - He was the ruler of the Sangama dynasty.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":396,"n":422,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"Who founded the Tuluva dynasty of the Vijayanagar Empire?","o":{"a":"Achyut Deva Raya","b":"Deva Raya I","c":"Krishna Deva Raya","d":"Narsimha Deva Raya"},"a":"c","s":"Krishna Deva Raya (1509 -1529).The Ashtadiggajas (a group of eight scholars) were a group of poets in his court . , Wrote Amuktamalyada (Telugu Poem).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":397,"n":423,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"Who among the following Vijaynagar rulers married his daughter to Firuz Shah Bahmani?","o":{"a":"Deva Raya II","b":"Deva Raya I","c":"Krishna Deva Raya","d":"Bukka I"},"a":"b","s":"Dev Raya I. In 1406 -07, Firuz Shah the ruler of the Bahmani Sultanate, defeated Deva Raya-I, the ruler of the Vijayanagara Empire. Dynasty and Ruler: Sangama Dynasty - Harihara I and Bukka ; Saluva dynasty - Narasimha Deva Raya, Thimma Bhupala, Narasimha Raya II. Tuluva dynasty- Krishnadeva Raya.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":398,"n":424,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"The founders of Vijayanagara Empire were the feudatories of which of the following dynasties?","o":{"a":"Hoysala","b":"Pandya","c":"Kakatiya","d":"Chola"},"a":"c","s":"Kakatiya. Vijayanagar Empire: Founders - Harihara and Bukka. Established - 1336. Vijayanagara Empire ruled by 4 dynasties: Sangama dynasty, Saluva dynasty, Tuluva dynasty, Aravidu dynasty. Greatest ruler of Vijayanagar Empire - Krishna Deva Raya (Tuluva Dynasty). Last ruler of Vijayanagar Empire - Aliya Ramarai.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":399,"n":425,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"The origin of 'Carnatic music' is related to which of the following Dynasties ?","o":{"a":"Bahamani","b":"Chola","c":"Pandya","d":"Vijayanagar"},"a":"d","s":"Vijayanagar. Carnatic Music: Purandara Dasa - A poet-composer of Vijayanagar is the 'Father of Carnatic Music'.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":400,"n":426,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"Vijayanagara emperor Krishnadeva Raya founded a suburban township near Vijayanagara called Nagalapuram after his __________..","o":{"a":"father","b":"teacher","c":"sister","d":"mother"},"a":"d","s":"Mother. Vijayanagara Empire (City of victory) : Founded in 1336 AD by Harihara and Bukka of the Sangama dynasty. Capital - Hampi (along the river Tungabhadra). Krishnadevaraya : Ruler of the Tuluva dynasty of Vijayanagar empire (1509-29 AD), Book - \"Amuktamalyada\".","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":401,"n":427,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"The local communities of merchants were known as _________ in the Vijayanagara empire.","o":{"a":"Rachavarus","b":"Narapattis","c":"Kudirai Chettis","d":"Gajapattis"},"a":"c","s":"Kudirai Chettis (Horse merchants). Rachavarus (Rajulu) - Associated with the ruling dynasty (rulers and generals were actually Sudras). Narapati (Rayas) - Lord of Men. Gajapati - lord of elephants.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":402,"n":428,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"The 'Pattanulkars' migrated from the Gujarat region to the Vijayanagar state. They Were ___________ .","o":{"a":"goldsmiths","b":"cotton traders","c":"silk weavers","d":"diamond makers"},"a":"c","s":"silk weavers. Important terms related to Medieval history :- Bargir - Cavalrymen, Din - Religion, Gomashta - Commercial agent, Shiqdar - Head of Administrative unit (Shiqq), Subhas - Provinces, Zawabit - Secular law.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 3rd shift"},{"id":403,"n":429,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"The Amara-Nayakas were in the Vijayanagara Empire.","o":{"a":"forced laborers","b":"finance officers","c":"ordinary peasants","d":"military commanders"},"a":"d","s":"Military commanders. They were given territories to govern by the Raya and used to collect taxes and other dues from the peasants, traders and craftspersons of the area. Gramas (villages), Gauda (village headman), Sthalas (sub districts), Nadus (districts), {Mandalams or rajyas (provinces)}, Land tax (sist).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 1st shift"},{"id":404,"n":430,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"While historians use the term Vijayanagara Empire and contemporaries of this empire described it as the _______.","o":{"a":"Gajapati Samarajya","b":"Karnataka Samrajyamu","c":"Andhra Samarajyu","d":"Sthalapati Samrajya"},"a":"b","s":"Karnataka Samrajyamu Vijayanagara Empire (1336 -1646) - Established by Harihara and Bukka on the banks of river Tungabhadra. Ruled by 4 Dynasties - Sangama (1336-1485), Saluva (1485-1505), Tuluva (1505-1570), Aravidu (1570-1646). Foreign Visitors - Nicholo de conti (Devaraya I), Abdul Razzaq (Devaraya II), Domingo Paes and Barbosa (Krishnadevaraya), Nuniz (Achyuta Devaraya).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 3rd shift"},{"id":405,"n":431,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"Raichur doab, the land between Krishna and Tungabhadra was a reason of conflict between the kings of Vijayanagar and _________ .","o":{"a":"Malwa","b":"Golconda","c":"Bahamani","d":"Bengal"},"a":"c","s":"Bahamani . The Bahmani -Vijayanagar conflict started during the period of HariHara Raya I. Both kingdoms wanted to control the fertile and rich Raichur doab lying between rivers Krishna and Tungabhadra.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 4"},{"id":406,"n":432,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"Which battle led to the destruction of the Vijayanagara empire?","o":{"a":"Battle of Raichur","b":"Battle of Haldighati","c":"Battle of Talikota","d":"Battle of Arcot"},"a":"c","s":"Battle of Talikota (Rakshasi-Tangadi) - It was fought on 23 January 1565 between the Vijayanagara Empire and an alliance of the Deccan sultanates. Aliya Rama Raya (King of the Vijaynagar Empire) was defeated and killed in the battle, which eventually led to the political collapse of the Vijayanagara Empire. The Battle of Raichur - Fought between the Vijayanagara Empire and the Sultanate of Bijapur in 1520.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":407,"n":433,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"Provida style of architecture belongs to which empire?","o":{"a":"Vijayanagar Empire","b":"Gupta Empire","c":"Mauryan Empire","d":"Bahmani Empire"},"a":"a","s":"Vijayanagar Empire ( Founded by Harihara and Bukka in 1336 ). Temples : Virupaksha temple (Hampi), The Vittala temple, Someshwara (Kolar city), Hazara Rama (Hampi), etc. Architectural Wonders of Vijayanagara: Queen's Bath, Lotus Mahal, Elephant stables, Royal platform, Water tanks.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":408,"n":434,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Vijaynagar Empire","q":"The Anantraj Sagar Tank was built by the __________rulers.","o":{"a":"Vijayanagara","b":"Maratha","c":"Pala","d":"Chola"},"a":"a","s":"The Vijayanagara empire built the Anantraj Sagar water storage tank using a 1.37 km long dam on the Maldevi river. Maldevi river runs through the Nellore district in the Indian state of Andhra Pradesh and empties into the Pennar river. Vijayanagara Empire was established in 1336 by the brothers Harihara I and Bukka Raya I of the Sangama dynasty.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":409,"n":435,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Which Peshwa signed the treaty of Bassein with the East India Company?","o":{"a":"Raghunath Rao","b":"Narayan Rao","c":"Baji Rao II","d":"Madhav Rao"},"a":"c","s":"Baji Rao II . The Treaty of Bassein (1802): It was signed between Lord Wellesley and Baji Rao II. Baji Rao II was restored as Peshwa and accepted all the terms of the Subsidiary Alliance system. Other Treaty: Treaty of Surat (1775) was signed between Raghunath Rao and the British. The Treaty of Salbai (1782) was signed between the Maratha Empire and the British East India Company.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":410,"n":436,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Which king of Punjab signed a tripartite treaty with Shah Shujah and the English Company ?","o":{"a":"Ranjit Singh","b":"Lal Singh","c":"Kharak Singh","d":"Dileep Singh"},"a":"a","s":"Ranjit Singh. The Tripartite Treaty was signed by Shah Shujah, Ranjit Singh and Lord Auckland (Governor -General of India) in 1838. Lahore treaty (In 1846) - A peace treaty marking the end of the First Anglo-Sikh War. It was concluded between the East India Company and Maharaja Duleep Singh Bahadur.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":411,"n":437,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"In 1850, Kashmir was sold under which of the following treaties to the Dogra dynasty of Jammu ?","o":{"a":"Treaty of Allahabad","b":"Treaty of Sugauli","c":"Treaty of Amritsar","d":"Treaty of Purandar"},"a":"c","s":"Treaty of Amritsar - It was executed by the British East India Company and Raja Gulab Singh of Jammu after the First Anglo-Sikh War. Treaty of Allahabad : Signed in - 1765 between the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II and Lord Clive of the British East India Company. Treaty of Sugauli - signed between Nepal and the East India Company in 1815 and ratified by 4 March 1816. Treaty of Purandar - Signed between Jai Singh I and Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj in 1665.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":412,"n":438,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"French possessions in India were restored after the Battle of Wandiwash by the Treaty of _____________signed in the year 1763 between Britain and France.","o":{"a":"Purandar","b":"Paris","c":"Tordesillas","d":"Surat"},"a":"b","s":"Paris. The Battle of Wandiwash was fought in Vandavasi, Tamil Nadu between the French and the British in 1760. This battle was part of the Third Carnatic War. The British defeated the French. Battle of Plassey - This battle was fought between Siraj -ud-Daula of Bengal and the British East India Company in 1757 at Palashi, Bengal.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":413,"n":439,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"The seven years of war in the 18th century was fought between Britain and ________.","o":{"a":"Germany","b":"Portugal","c":"France","d":"Spain"},"a":"c","s":"France. The Seven Years' War, which took place between 1756-1763, was a global conflict that spanned five continents, though it was known in America as the 'French and Indian War'. It solidified Britain's stance as the most dominant European country in the world. Reason behind the Seven Years War - Both England and France wanted territories in North America.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":414,"n":440,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Who was the Iranian ruler who attacked the city of Delhi and took away an immense amount of wealth in 1739 ?","o":{"a":"Ahmad Shah Qajar","b":"Nadir shah","c":"Ahmad Shah Abdali","d":"Abbas |"},"a":"b","s":"Nadir Shah. Battle of Karnal (1739) : Fought between - Nadir Shah and Muhammad Shah. Victory - Nadir Shah easily outnumbered the Mughal forces and the Mughal emperor surrendered. Nadir Shah invaded India four times between 1748 and 1767. Nadir Shah took the famous Kohinoor diamond from India and Shah Jahan's jewel studded peacock from the throne.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":415,"n":441,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Which of the following was the first major and decisive victory for the East India Company on the soil of India?","o":{"a":"Battle of Sindhuli","b":"First Anglo-Maratha War","c":"Battle of Plassey","d":"Battle of Tiruvannamalai"},"a":"c","s":"Battle of Plassey (1757 This battle was fought between the British East India Company led by Robert Clive and the Nawab of Bengal Siraj-ud-daula and his French allies. The Battle of Sindhuli was part of the unification of Nepal led by Prithvi Narayan Shah, King of Gorkha (later King of Nepal). First Anglo-Maratha War (1775 - 1782) - Fought between the British East India Company and the Maratha Empire. Battle of Tiruvannamalai (1767) - Fought between the forces of the East India Company and the Kingdom of Mysore, led by Hyder Ali.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":416,"n":442,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Which of the following Anglo-Maratha wars resulted in merging the Peshwas' territories with the Bombay Presidency?","o":{"a":"Fourth","b":"Third","c":"Second","d":"First"},"a":"b","s":"Third (1817-1819). Anglo -Maratha war (The Great Maratha War) - The War was fought over territorial disputes, political dominance, and control over resources. First (1775-1782) - The Marathas won, Second (1803-1805) - The British won. Warren Hastings - The first Governor - General of Bengal (1773-1785).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":417,"n":443,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Mir Qasim name is associated with which of the following battles of India?","o":{"a":"Battle of Kirkee","b":"Battle of Buxar","c":"Battle of Plassey","d":"Battle of Lahore"},"a":"b","s":"Battle of Buxar (1764) : It was fought between the forces of the British East India Company (led by Hector Munro) and the combined army of an alliance of Shuja-ud-Daulah, Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II and Mir Qasim. The Treaty of Allahabad (1765) was signed after this Battle.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":418,"n":444,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"The second Anglo-Mysore War came to an end by signing which of the following treaties?","o":{"a":"The Treaty of Arcot","b":"The Treaty of Madras","c":"The Treaty of Wandiwash","d":"The Treaty of Mangalore"},"a":"d","s":"The Treaty of Mangalore (1784). The second Anglo-Mysore War (1780 - 84) was fought by Hyder Ali, the Nizam and the Marathas against the British. Anglo-Mysore Wars : First (1767 - 69) - Between Hyder Ali and the British, concluded with the Treaty of Madras. Third (1790 - 92) - The British, the Nizam and the Marathas fought together against Tipu Sultan, concluded with the Treaty of Seringapatam. Fourth (1798 - 99) - Between Arthur Wellesley and Tipu Sultan (Died).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":419,"n":445,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"The Battle of Karnal is known for the invasion of which of the following aggressors against India?","o":{"a":"Nadir Shah","b":"Nasir Khan","c":"Ahmad Shah Abdali","d":"Shah Shuja"},"a":"a","s":"Nadir Shah. The Battle of Karnal - Fought between Nadir Shah (Persian Empire of the Afsharid dynasty) and Muhammad Shah (Mughal Emperor) in 1739. Nadir Shah emerged victorious in the battle. He took the famous Koh - i - Noor diamond and Shah Jahan's jewel-studded Peacock Throne. He was the founder of the Afsharid dynasty of Iran.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":420,"n":446,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Who was the Mughal emperor during the third battle of Panipat?","o":{"a":"Akbar II","b":"Shahalam II","c":"Bahadur Shah I","d":"Bahadurshah Jafar"},"a":"b","s":"Shahalam II (Ali Gauhar) - Son of Alamgir II. Third Battle of Panipat (14 January, 1761): Between Maratha Empires and Ahmad Shah Abdali. (Victory - Ahmad Shah Abdali).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":421,"n":447,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"The Battle of Khatoli fought was in 1517 between the kingdom of Mewar under Rana Sanga and ______.","o":{"a":"Sikander Lodi","b":"Babur","c":"Ibrahim Lodi","d":"Mahmud Lodi"},"a":"c","s":"Ibrahim Lodhi. The Lodi dynasty (1451 - 1526, an Afghan dynasty was the fifth and final dynasty of the Delhi Sultanate.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":422,"n":448,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"In which of these battles the involvement of the Mughal army was not there?","o":{"a":"Battle of Haldighati","b":"Battle of Buxar","c":"Second Battle of Panipat","d":"Battle of Aliwal"},"a":"d","s":"Battle of Aliwal (1846) - British (led by Sir Harry Smith) defeated Sikh forces (led by Ranjodh Singh Majithia). Other battles of the First Anglo-Sikh War (1845-1846) - Battle of Mudki, Ferozeshah, Buddewal, Sobraon. First war concluded with the Treaty of Lahore and Bhairowal in 1846.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":423,"n":449,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Which of the given statements is correct? I. When Alivardi Khan died in 1756, Siraj-ud-daulah became the nawab of Bengal. II. In 1757, Robert Clive led the company's army against Siraj-ud-daulah at Plassey.","o":{"a":"Only II","b":"Neither I nor II","c":"Only I","d":"Both I and II"},"a":"d","s":"Both I and II. Battle of Plassey (23 June 1757) - Bengal (led by Siraj ud-Daulah - last independent Nawab of Bengal) annexed by the British East India Company (led by Robert Clive - First British Governor of the Bengal Presidency).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":424,"n":450,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"In the 18th century, the British fought ______ wars with the Mysore rulers.","o":{"a":"Two","b":"Six","c":"Three","d":"Four"},"a":"d","s":"Four wars were fought between British and Mysore rulers: First Anglo-Mysore War (1767-69), Second Anglo-Mysore War (1780-84), Third Anglo-Mysore War (1790-92), and Fourth Anglo-Mysore War (1799).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":425,"n":451,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"In which year did Tipu Sultan die defending his capital Seringapatam?","o":{"a":"1799","b":"1802","c":"1792","d":"1794"},"a":"a","s":"1799 . He is known as Tiger of Mysore. Tipu Sultan was inspired by the Ideals of the French Revolution. Daria Daulat Bagh is known as Tipu Sultan's Summer Palace. He was finally defeated at the hands of Lord Wellesley.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":426,"n":452,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Who emerged victorious in 'The Siege of Arcot' in 1751 AD ? SSC MTS 05/10/21 (Afternoon)","o":{"a":"French","b":"Dutch","c":"Portuguese","d":"British"},"a":"d","s":"British. The Siege of Arcot was the battle between British and combined forces of French and Nawab of Arcot. Robert Clive captured the Arcot after the battle. The war ended with the Treaty of Pondicherry in 1754 which made Mohammad Ali Khan Walajah as Nawab of Arcot.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":427,"n":453,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"In which year was the Treaty of Versailles signed?","o":{"a":"1921","b":"1919","c":"1933","d":"1940"},"a":"b","s":"1919 . The defeated Germany after the First World War signed the Treaty of Versailles on 28 June 1919.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":428,"n":454,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Which of the following regions was annexed by the British East India Company through the Treaty of Yandabo (1826) ?","o":{"a":"Assam","b":"Kashmir","c":"Odisha","d":"Ladakh"},"a":"a","s":"Assam . Treaty was signed between Myanmar and the United Kingdom.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":429,"n":455,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"In 1818, the Holkar Dynasty signed the Treaty of Mandsaur with the ______.","o":{"a":"Danish","b":"Dutch","c":"British","d":"French"},"a":"c","s":"British. After Malhar Rao II was defeated in the battle of Mahidpur (1817) the treaty of Mandsaur was signed.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":430,"n":456,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Which of the following battles did Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah fight, defeating the forces under Henry Lawrence?","o":{"a":"Battle of Kintoor","b":"Battle of Saragarhi","c":"Battle of Chinhat","d":"Battle of Najafgarh"},"a":"c","s":"Battle of Chinhat. In the Battle of Chinhat, Barkat Ahmad was declared Chief Army Officer of the rebels. The Battle of Chinhat was fought on the morning of 30 June 1857, between British forces and Indian rebels.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":431,"n":457,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"In the Battle of Swally (1612) the British fought against the ______.","o":{"a":"Danish","b":"Portuguese","c":"French","d":"Dutch"},"a":"b","s":"Portuguese. The battle took place on 29-30 November 1612 off the coast of Suvali, a village near Surat city. This relatively small naval battle is historically important as it marked the beginning of the end of Portugal's commercial monopoly over India and the beginning of the ascent of the English East India Company's presence in India.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":432,"n":458,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"The Battle of Bedara was fought between the British Army and the Dutch Army in:","o":{"a":"1760","b":"1759","c":"1772","d":"1764"},"a":"b","s":"25 November 1759, during the Seven Years' War between a force of British troops mainly of the British East India Company and a force of the Dutch East India Company.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":433,"n":459,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"The British annexed Punjab after defeating the Sikhs in the Anglo-Sikh War in the year ______.","o":{"a":"1835","b":"1839","c":"1849","d":"1845"},"a":"c","s":"1849. This war resulted in the fall of the Sikh Empire and The East India Company annexed Punjab and it became part of North West Frontier Province.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":434,"n":460,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Wars and Treaties","q":"Who signed the treaty of Alinagar with the British?","o":{"a":"Mir Qasim","b":"Siraj-ud-Daula","c":"Alivardi Khan","d":"Mir Jafar"},"a":"b","s":"Siraj-ud-Daula . The Treaty of Alinagar was signed on 9 February 1757 between Robert Clive of the British East India Company and the Nawab of Bengal, Mirza Muhammad Siraj Ud Daula. Based on the terms of the accord, the Nawab would recognize all the provisions of Mughal Emperor Farrukhsiyar farman of 1717.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":435,"n":461,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"After the breakup of the Bahmani kingdom, how many independent states arose?","o":{"a":"3","b":"6","c":"4","d":"5"},"a":"d","s":"5. The Bahmani Empire (Established - 1347 A.D.) was founded by Ala-ud-Din Hasan Bahman Shah. It was located in the Deccan region. First Capital - Gulbarga (1347 to 1425) and After that Bidar. After the death of Muhammad Shah-III (ruler) in 1482, the Kingdom was disintegrated into five kingdoms: Bijapur, Ahmednagar, Bera, Golconda and Bidar.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":436,"n":462,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following Bahmani sultans transferred his capital from Gulbarga to Bidar?","o":{"a":"Ahmad Shah","b":"Humayun","c":"Mujahid Shah","d":"Firuz Shah"},"a":"a","s":"Ahmad Shah (1422-1435 Bahmani sultanate was established by Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah (Zafar Khan) in 1347. Bahmani kingdom comprises the parts of the current day's Karnataka, Maharashtra, and Andhra Pradesh. Rulers of the Bahmani Kingdom: Muhammad Shah-I (1358-1377), Muhammad Shah-ll (1378-1397), Feroz Shah Bahmani (1397-1422), Mahmud Gawan.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":437,"n":463,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which Rajput princess belonged to the Bhakti tradition of Medieval India, whose songs were devoted to Lord Krishna ?","o":{"a":"Muddupalani","b":"Akka Mahadevi","c":"Mirabai","d":"Lal Ded"},"a":"c","s":"Mirabai was the Rajput princess of Kudki (Now present in Pali district of Rajasthan) who lived in the 16th century. She was married to Bhoj Raj, crown prince of Mewar. Sant Ravidas (Raidas) was her teacher. Other Bhakti saints: Lal Ded (Lalleshwari) - A Kashmiri mystic of Kashmir Shaivism, who created a style of mystic poetry called vatsun or Vakhs. Akkamahadevi - A 12th-century bhakti saint of Lingayat Shaivism sect who belonged to the southern region of Karnataka.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":438,"n":464,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Europeans entered India with a mindset of trading only. This streak was initiated by which of the following European nations ?","o":{"a":"Dutch","b":"French","c":"British","d":"Portuguese"},"a":"d","s":"Portuguese - They were the first Europeans to come to India by Sea routes in 1498, led by Vasco da Gama at Calicut (Kerala). European nations arrived in India after Portuguese: Dutch, English, Danish, and French. The Portuguese were also the last to leave India in 1961.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":439,"n":465,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the teacher of the famous musician Tansen?","o":{"a":"Ravidas","b":"Kalidas","c":"Surdas","d":"Swami Haridas"},"a":"d","s":"Swami Haridas. Miyan Tansen - Akbar considered him as a Navaratnas (nine jewels), and gave him the title Mian. His books on music are Sri Ganesh Stotra and Sangita Sara. Ravidas - He was a North Indian mystic poet of the bhakti movement. Kalidas - His Sanskrit literary compositions are Abhijnana Sakuntalam, Malavikagnimitram. Surdas - He was a contemporary of Akbar; his books are Sur Sagar, Sur-Saravali.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":440,"n":466,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following did not come into existence as a result of the disintegration of the Bahmani Empire during the 16th century?","o":{"a":"Ahmednagar","b":"Golconda","c":"Bidar","d":"Gaur"},"a":"d","s":"Gaur kingdom was situated in Sylhet region of West Bengal. The Bahmani Empire was founded by Ala-ud-Din Hasan Bahman Shah. It was located in the Deccan region. First Capital - Daulatabad/Devagiri and Second Capital - Gulbarga. After the death of Muhammad Shah-III (ruler) in 1482, the Kingdom was disintegrated into five kingdoms: Bijapur, Ahmednagar, Berar, Golconda and Bidar.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":441,"n":467,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who succeeded Alivardi Khan as Nawab of Bengal ?","o":{"a":"Shaukat Jang","b":"Siraj-ud-Daulah","c":"Mir Kasim","d":"Mir Jaffar"},"a":"b","s":"Siraj-ud-Daulah (Reign: 1756 -1757) - Last independent Nawab of Bengal. Alivardi Khan - Nawab of Bengal from 1740 to 1756. He is known for his victory during the battle of Burdwan against Maratha Empire during the Maratha invasion of Bengal. Mir Qasim - Nawab of Bengal (1760-1763).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":442,"n":468,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Select the INCORRECT pair of a famous minaret and its construction year in India.","o":{"a":"Shaheed Minar-1828 CE","b":"Char Minar-1664 CE","c":"Chand Minar-1435 CE","d":"Jhulta Minar-1452 CE"},"a":"b","s":"Char Minar-1664 CE. It was built by Sultan Mohammed Quli Qutb Shah in 1591. It was built in honour of his wife, Bhagmati in Hyderabad. Shaheed Minar: It was built in the memory of Major-general Sir David Ochterlony, commander of the British East India Company. Chand Minar: It was built by King Ala-ud-din Bahmani in Daulatabad, Maharashtra. Jhulta Minar: It is a surviving part of Sidi Bashir Mosque in Ahmedabad.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":443,"n":469,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Ajmer Sharif is the shrine of which of the following Sufi saints?","o":{"a":"Mohammad Ghori","b":"Mohammad Bin Tughlaq","c":"Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti","d":"Bakhtiyar Khilji"},"a":"c","s":"Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti was an extremely pious saint who devoted himself to providing relief to the poor. He reached Ajmer, via Lahore, and made it his home from 1192 till his death in 1236 AD. Ajmer Sharif Dargah : Situated in - Rajasthan. Built by - Humayun during the rule of the Mughal Empire .","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":444,"n":470,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What did the term 'bhoga' refer to during the medieval period?","o":{"a":"Revenue assignments","b":"Donations to temples","c":"A festival","d":"Religious function"},"a":"a","s":"Revenue Assignment. Other Type of Tax : Kara - It was a generic term used for taxes. Bhaga - it was King's customary share of produce normally amounting to 1/6th of the total produce. Hiranya - Tax on special produce taken in cash. Chat - Security Tax. Prataya -Toll Tax.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":445,"n":471,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Joseph Francois Dupleix was the Governor General of which of the following East India companies in India?","o":{"a":"Dutch","b":"French","c":"English","d":"Portuguese"},"a":"b","s":"French (Last European to arrive in India). Governor-General Dupleix was appointed in 1742. French East India Company : Established in - 1664. The First French Factory - Surat (1668), Second Factory - Masulipatam (1669), Headquarters - Pondicherry. Europeans in India - Portuguese (1498), Dutch (1602), British (1608), Danes (1616).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":446,"n":472,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Rajaram was the leader of which of the following revolts against the Mughlas?","o":{"a":"Satnami","b":"Bundela","c":"Jat","d":"Munda"},"a":"c","s":"Jat revolt (Mathura). In 1669 CE against Aurangzeb. Satnami revolt (1672) - The Satnamis were a sect comprising peasants, artisans and untouchables who rebelled against the Aurangzeb in Narnaul (Haryana). Bundela Rebellion (1842) - Jawahar Singh Bundela of Chandrapur and Madhukar Shah of Narhat were the leaders of the revolt. There was an upsurge in the Sagar-Narmada territory against British tyranny. Munda Revolt (1899 -1900) is a tribal rebellion. Birsa Munda led this movement south of Ranchi.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":447,"n":473,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who composed 'Shahnama\" ?","o":{"a":"Abdulla","b":"Alberuni","c":"Ferdowsi","d":"Utbi"},"a":"c","s":"Ferdowsi ( Court poet of Mahmud of Ghazni) - A Persian poet and the author of Shahnameh (\"Book of Kings''), which is one of the world's longest epic poems created by a single poet, and the greatest epic of Persian-speaking countries. Books and Authors : Astadhyayi (Panini), Mahabhasya (Patanjali), Nagananda (Harshavardhana), Mrichhakatikam (Sudraka), Gitagovinda (Jayadev).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":448,"n":474,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Malik Ambar, who resisted Mughals in Deccan, was an able administrator of which state?","o":{"a":"Bidar","b":"Bijapur","c":"Golconda","d":"Ahmednagar"},"a":"d","s":"Ahmednagar. Malik Ambar was an African-origin statesman and military leader who served as the regent of the Nizam Shahi dynasty in the Deccan, of Ahmednagar (now part of Maharashtra). He changed the capital from Paranda to Junnar and founded a new city, Khadki which was later on changed to Aurangabad by the Emperor Aurangzeb. He is especially famous for the Neher water system of the city.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":449,"n":475,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Imadshahi family was known for the foundation of which state?","o":{"a":"Berar","b":"Bidar","c":"Ahmednagar","d":"Golkonda"},"a":"a","s":"Berar (Maharashtra). Imad Shahi Dynasty : Established by - Fathullah Imad Ul Mulk (1490). He fortified Gavilgad and Narnala. Berar Sultanate was annexed by Sultan Murtaza Shah of Ahmednagar Kingdom in 1574. Capital - Ellichpur. Bahamani sultanate broke up into five states.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":450,"n":476,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In the 15 th century, a text called _____ is ascribed popularly to Lochan Kavi.","o":{"a":"Hridayprakash","b":"Sangeet Darpan","c":"Anup Vilas","d":"Raag Tarangini"},"a":"d","s":"Raag Tarangini - Initiated an alternative system of the division of rags, 'the Thaat system'. Lochan Kavi (Lochan Pan\u0000it) belonged to the Mithila region (Bihar). \"Sangeet Darpan\" was written by Damodar Pandit. \"Anup Rag Vilas\" is written by Kumar Gandharva.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":451,"n":477,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who founded the independent State of Bengal?","o":{"a":"Alivardi khan","b":"Murshid Quli Khan","c":"Sarafraz Khan","d":"Shuja-ud-Din"},"a":"b","s":"Murshid Quli Khan (Zamin Ali Quli) was born as Surya Narayan Mishra; the first Nawab of Bengal (1717 to 1727). He built the Katra Masjid (1723-1724) where he was buried after his death.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":452,"n":478,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the Maharana of Mewar and composed a book on music called 'Sangeet Raj' ?","o":{"a":"Maharana Sanga","b":"Maharana Pratap","c":"Maharana Kumbha","d":"Maharana Udai Singh"},"a":"c","s":"Maharana Kumbha (1433-1468 AD) belonged to the Sisodia clan of Rajputs. Titles of Maharana Kumbha : Abhinav Bhattacharya, Rajguru, Daanguru, Halguru, Rano Raso.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":453,"n":479,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the founder of the Bahmani Sultanate, who took the title of Bahman Shah after his accession to the throne?","o":{"a":"Muhammad Shah","b":"Ghiyas ud din Tahmatan Shah","c":"Daud Shah","d":"Alauddin Hasan"},"a":"d","s":"Alauddin Hasan (Hasan Gangu): Reign - 1347 to 1358. He established the kingdom after revolting against Muhammad Bin Tughlaq . He was succeeded by his son Muhammed Shah I (who defeated Bukka I of Vijaynagar). Feroz Shah Bahmani (1397-1422 A.D.) - defeated Deva Raya I, emperor of Vijayanagara Empire, and married his daughter.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":454,"n":480,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Name the Bhakti Saint from South India who was initially a Jaina and a minister in the court of a Chalukya king in the twelfth century.","o":{"a":"Karaikkal Ammaiyar","b":"Basavanna","c":"Eknath","d":"Tallapaka Annamacharya"},"a":"b","s":"Basavanna - Founder of Lingayatism; Prime minister of Kalachurya king Bijjala (1157-1167); Founded the Anubhava Mantapa. Karaikkal Ammaiyar (5th century AD) - One of the three women amongst the 63 Nayanars. Eknath (1533-1599) - Devotee of the Hindu deity Vitthal and is a major figure of the Warkari movement. Tallapaka Annamacharya (1408-1503) - Devotee of Venkateswara, a form of Lord Vishnu and composed songs called Sankirtana.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":455,"n":481,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The eleventh century structure of Bhojshala in the state of Madhya Pradesh was constructed under the patronage of which dynasty? Graduate Level 27/06/2023 ( Shift - 4)","o":{"a":"Chandela","b":"Paramara","c":"Nanda","d":"Gurjara-Pratihara"},"a":"b","s":"Paramara. 'Bhojshala' is an ancient and the only temple of Mata Saraswati. It is located in Dhar district of Madhya Pradesh. The Paramara dynasty (Rajput) ruled Malwa and the adjacent regions in west-central India from the 9th century to the 14th century. Founder - Upendra or Krishnaraja.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":456,"n":482,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Initially who among the following were subordinate to the Chalukyas of Karnataka? Graduate Level 28/06/2023 ( Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Rashtrakutas","b":"Satavahanas","c":"Pratiharas","d":"Palas"},"a":"a","s":"Rashtrakutas : Capital - Manyakheta. Founder - Dantidurga, he defeated Chalukya King Kirtivarman II. Chalukya dynasty: founder - Pulakeshin I. Satavahana dynasty: founder - Simuka . Pala dynasty - founder - Gopal I.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":457,"n":483,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which of the following places is the Tomb of Ahmad Shah Al Wali Bahmani built ? Graduate Level 30/06/2023 ( Shift - 1)","o":{"a":"Fatehpur Sikri","b":"Agra","c":"Ashtoor","d":"Ajmer"},"a":"c","s":"Ashtoor. Ahmed Shah Al Wali Bahamani ( 1422 to 1436, Bahmani Sultanate ) - Ashtoor is an important center of Indo-Saracenic monuments. Twelve imposing mausoleums are located at Ashtur east of Bidar. Major tombs in Ashtoor: Tomb of Ahmad Shah's Queen, Tomb of Sultan Allauddin Shah II, Tomb of Sultan Humayun, Tomb of Malika-i-Jahan.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":458,"n":484,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Hoysaleshvara temple was built with which of the following stones by a Hoysala king in 1150?","o":{"a":"Dark Schist Stone","b":"White Marble","c":"Sandstone","d":"Grey Basalt"},"a":"a","s":"Dark Schist Stone. The temples were known for its pillared hall and stellate plan. Capital of Hoysala - Dorasamudra (Dwarasamudra). Founder - Sala. Famous poets: Harihar, Rahavanka and Janna . Jain temple of Dilwara in Mount Abu (Rajasthan) made of white marble. The temples of Khajuraho (Madhya Pradesh) were built entirely of sandstone.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 1"},{"id":459,"n":485,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Buland Darwaza at Fatehpur Sikri is a gateway to which of the following mosques?","o":{"a":"Moti Masjid","b":"Jama Masjid","c":"Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra","d":"Jamali Kamali"},"a":"b","s":"Jama Masjid (Great Mosque). The Buland Darwaza (Victory Gate, Highest gateway in the world) at Fatehpur Sikri (World Heritage site, 1986) was built by Mughal emperor Akbar (reigned 1556-1605) to commemorate his victory over Gujarat .","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 2"},{"id":460,"n":486,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Bahmani Monuments at Gulbarga, Karnataka represent _________ style of architecture.","o":{"a":"Mameluke","b":"Sikh","c":"Deccan","d":"Nagara"},"a":"c","s":"Deccan. The Deccan style of architecture is a fusion of Persian and Indian architectural styles. It is mainly inspired by styles from the Delhi Sultanate and later Mughal architecture. Example: Vatapi, Aihole, Pattadakal, etc. The Nagara style of architecture - Kandariya Mahadev Temple (Madhya Pradesh), Sun temple (Konark), Sun temple at Modhera (Gujarat) and Ossian temple (Gujarat). The Sikh style of architecture - Keshgarh Sahib and the Harmandir Sahib (Golden Temple).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":461,"n":487,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Udaipur was founded by _________ after the fall of Chittor to Akbar in 1568.","o":{"a":"Sawai Jai Singh","b":"Raja Bharmal","c":"Man singh","d":"Rana Udai Singh"},"a":"d","s":"Rana Udai Singh. Udaipur is known as 'Venice of the East' and the city of lakes.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":462,"n":488,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which architectural style of India was distinguished by the use of overhanging balconies and arched cornices?","o":{"a":"Rajput","b":"Sikh","c":"Nagar","d":"Mughal"},"a":"a","s":"Rajput. The Rajput fort 'Hawa Mahal' (Jaipur) is an example of Rajput Architecture. Sikh Style - The Harimandir Sahib (Golden Temple). Nagara Style - Sun Temple (Modhera), Sun Temple (Konark), Kandariya Mahadeva (Madhya Pradesh). Mughal Style - Taj Mahal, Humayun's Tomb, Agra Fort.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":463,"n":489,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following built the Hawa Mahal in Jaipur?","o":{"a":"Maharaja Narinder Singh","b":"Krishnarajendra Wadiyar IV","c":"Maharaja Sawai Pratap Singh","d":"Raja Man Singh - I"},"a":"c","s":"Maharaja Sawai Pratap Singh . Hawa Mahal - Its five story outside is likened to a honeycomb with its 953 little windows called Jharokhas.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":464,"n":490,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Ananga Pala was a ruler of which dynasty ?","o":{"a":"Rajput Dynasty","b":"Gurjara-Pratihara Dynasty","c":"Pallava Dynasty","d":"Khilji Dynasty"},"a":"a","s":"Rajput Dynasty - It was founded by Simharaj, who is famously known as the founder of the city of Ajmer. Ananga Pala - He was a king of the Tomara dynasty, who ruled over areas of Delhi in India.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":465,"n":491,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Ahom Kingdom in India was located in the _______.","o":{"a":"Chota Nagpur plateau","b":"Vindhayas","c":"Brahmaputra valley","d":"Coastal Odisha"},"a":"c","s":"Brahmaputra valley Chaolung Sukapha founded the Ahom kingdom (13th century) that ruled Assam for six centuries. It was annexed by British India in 1826 with the signing of the Treaty of Yandaboo. Lachit Borphukan (military general of Ahom kingdom) defeated mughal forces in the Battle of Saraighat 1671.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":466,"n":492,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What did the medieval term \"Raqs\" mean ?","o":{"a":"Dancing","b":"Singing","c":"Chanting","d":"Ritual"},"a":"a","s":"Dancing. Medieval Terms and Meaning: Amils (Revenue), Bargir (Cavalrymen), Daam (Coin), Dhimmis (Non-Muslims people), Din (Religion), Diwan (Function of finance), Wilayat (The spiritual territory of a Sufi).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":467,"n":493,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Islamic style of architecture is also known as __________ style.","o":{"a":"Hegira","b":"Yashmak","c":"Alim","d":"Mehrab"},"a":"d","s":"Mehrab - It indicates the direction of Mecca (Qibla), which Muslims face when praying. Islamic architecture includes use of specific ornamental forms, including mathematically complicated, elaborate geometric patterns, floral motifs like the arabesque, and elaborate calligraphic inscriptions. Other Terms : Hegira - Muhammad's journey from Mecca to Medina in 622 AD. Yashmak - A piece of material covering most of the face, worn by some Muslim women. Alim - A person who became a scholar in the Islamic tradition.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":468,"n":494,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Bhakti saints, Nayanars were devotees of which of the following gods?","o":{"a":"Murugan","b":"Shiva","c":"Vishnu","d":"Indra"},"a":"b","s":"Shiva. The Nayanars were a group of 63 saints devoted to Lord Shiva who lived during the 6th to 8th centuries CE. The Alwars were Tamil poet-saints of South India who espoused devotion to Lord Vishnu.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":469,"n":495,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which among the following forts was principal capital of Qutb Shahi dynasty in Hyderabad?","o":{"a":"Chiktan Fort","b":"Rajgad Fort","c":"Red Fort","d":"Golconda Fort"},"a":"d","s":"Golconda Fort - It was built by the Kakatiya Dynasty. It was the principal capital of the Qutb Shahi dynasty in Hyderabad. Rajgad Fort - Pune ( Maharashtra). Chhatrapati Shivaji renovated this fort and made it his capital in 1674 AD. Chiktan Fort - Kargil (Ladakh).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":470,"n":496,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following books was written by the Roman writer Pliny the elder?","o":{"a":"Indica","b":"Natural History","c":"Geographica","d":"Periplus of the Erythraean Sea"},"a":"b","s":"Natural History. Important Books and Authors: \"Gathasaptashati\" - Hala, \"Indica\" - Megasthenes, \"Geographica\" - Strabo, \"Periplus of the Erythraean Sea:\" - William H Schoff, \"Kathasaritsagara\" - Somadeva, \"Mudrarakshasa\" - Vishakhadatta, \"Rajtarangini\" - Kalhana.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":471,"n":497,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following cities became the capital of Bengal in 1704 ?","o":{"a":"Howrah","b":"Murshidabad","c":"Kharagpur","d":"Kolkata"},"a":"b","s":"Murshidabad. Murshid Quli Khan (Zamin Ali Quli) transferred his capital to the then Mukhsusabad later came to be known after Murshid Quli Khan as Murshidabad in 1704 A.D. Murshidabad (West Bengal) on the banks of the Bhagirathi became the center of silk. Murshid Quli Khan (1717-1727) was the first Nawab of Bengal.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":472,"n":498,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following statement is correct with respect to the Prashastis and land grants? I. Prashastis were composed by learned Brahmanas. II. Kings often rewarded Brahmanas by grants of land which were recorded on copper plates.","o":{"a":"Only I","b":"Both I and II","c":"Only II","d":"Neither I nor II"},"a":"b","s":"Both I and II. Prashastis (metal plates) were written by Brahmans in Sanskrit or any regional language. They have been written in the 1 st millennium and onwards in the form of prose or poetry. Example: Allahabad Pillar Inscription Sanskrit poem (by Harishena in praise of King Samudragupta). Agrahara ( Agraharam, Ghatoka, Boya) refers to the land, village, or fields that were given to Brahmans.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":473,"n":499,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Nagabhata was the king of which of the following dynasty?","o":{"a":"Kakatiya","b":"Vardhana","c":"Pratihara","d":"Reddy"},"a":"c","s":"Pratihara dynasty Nagabhata ruled from 730 - 760 CE. Capital of Pratihara dynasty: first Ujjain then Kannauj. Kakatiya dynasty {Founder: Rudradeva- I}. Vardhana dynasty {founder: Pushyabhuti}. Reddi kingdom {Prolaya Vema Reddi}.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":474,"n":500,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The craftspersons of Bidar were so famed for their inlay work in ______.","o":{"a":"Gold and Bronze","b":"Bronze and Silver","c":"Gold and Copper","d":"Copper and Silver"},"a":"d","s":"Copper and Silver. Work of Bidar (Karnataka) craftsperson called BIdri. Bidar was the capital of Bahmani Kingdom in the 14th century. Bidar fort was built by Ahmad Shah Wali Bahman. Bidar is also considered one of the holiest places for Sikh pilgrimage.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":475,"n":501,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In 1342, one of the nobles, Haji Ilyas Khan united Bengal and became its ruler under the title of __________and laid the foundation of the Ilyas Shah dynasty.","o":{"a":"Shams-ud-din Iliyas Shah","b":"Ghiyasuddin Azam","c":"Sultan Abu al-Mujahid","d":"Sultan Sayf ad-Din"},"a":"a","s":"Shams-ud-din Ilyas Shah {(founder of Bengal Sultanate and Ilyas Shahi dynasty (First dynasty of Bengal)}. Dynasties of Bengal Sultanate - Ilyas Shahi (1342-1415), Ganesha (1414 -1435), Restorer of Ilyas Dynasty (Nasiruddin Mahmud Shah-I, 1435 -1487), Habshi rule (Shahzada Barbak, 1487-1494) and Hussain Shahi dynasty (Alauddin Hussain Shah - 1494-1519).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":476,"n":502,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which Bahmani ruler granted the title of 'Chief of the Merchants' or Malikut-Tujjar to Mahmud Gawan?","o":{"a":"Humayun Shah","b":"Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah","c":"Muhammmad Shah","d":"Feroz Shah"},"a":"a","s":"Humayun Shah (1458 - 1461) Ala-ud-Din Bahman Shah founded Bahmani Kingdom in 1347. Gulbarga Fort in Karnataka was built by him. Mohammed Shah I was the 2nd ruler of the Bahmani Sultanate. Tajuddin Feroz Shah (Bahmani KIngdom) is famous for conquering Raichur Daob from Vijayanagar Kingdoms.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":477,"n":503,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the architect of the famous Mysore Palace in Karnataka? Graduate Level 03/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Henry Irwin","b":"William Emerson","c":"Frederick William Stevens","d":"Edwin Lutyens"},"a":"a","s":"Henry Irwin . Mysore Palace (also known as Amba Villas) is a historical palace at Mysore in Karnataka. It was the official residence of the Wadiyar dynasty. Mysore Palace was constructed in Indo-Saracenic style architecture.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":478,"n":504,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Vimana in the Dravidian style of temple architecture is usually in which shape? Higher Secondary 01/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Semi-Circular","b":"Cylindrical","c":"Pyramidal","d":"Rectangular"},"a":"c","s":"Pyramidal. Gopuram (entrance gateway in the center of the front wall). Antarala (vestibular tunnel), garbhagriha (the innermost sanctuary of a Hindu and Jain temple). Famous Dravidian Temples - Rajarajeswara or Brihadeshwara temple (Rajaraja Chola at Thanjavur), Gangaikondacholapuram temple (Rajendra I), Annamalaiyar Temple (Tiruvannamalai, Tamil Nadu), Meenakshi Temple (Tamil Nadu), Airavatesvara Temple (Tamil Nadu) etc.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":479,"n":505,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Most Mughal buildings are constructed using which building material? Higher Secondary 05/08/2022 (Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Grey sandstone","b":"Yellow sandstone","c":"Red sandstone","d":"Granite"},"a":"c","s":"Red sandstone. Features of Mughal Architecture: a mix of Indian, Persian, and Turkish architectural styles, building materials (red sandstone and white marble), Charbagh gardens, arabesque, geometric patterns on pillars and walls, large entrances, and magnificent calligraphy.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":480,"n":506,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Consider the following statements about a fort: 1. It was originally known as Mankal. 2. It was built on a hilltop in 1143. In the above mentioned sentences, forts is being talked about?","o":{"a":"Chitradurg Fort","b":"Daulatabad Fort","c":"Fort Aguada","d":"Golconda Fort"},"a":"d","s":"Golconda fort (Hyderabad; Built by the Qutb shahi dynasty). Chitradurga fort (Karnataka; built by the Chalukyas and Hoysalas, later the Nayakas of Chitradurga of the Vijayanagar Empire. Daulatabad fort (Maharashtra; built by Yadava dynasty), and Aguada Fort (Goa; built by Portuguese).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":481,"n":507,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The temples of Khajuraho are a magnificent example of the ______ of the temple.","o":{"a":"Vesara style","b":"Nagara style","c":"Eastern style","d":"Dravidian style"},"a":"b","s":"Nagara style. Basically, there are 3 kinds of temple architecture: Nagara Style, Dravida Style, and Vesara Style. Examples of Nagara-style temples in India are- Khajuraho temples in MP, Kandariya Mahadev Temple in Madhya Pradesh, Sun temple at Konark, Sun temple at Modhera, Gujarat, and Ossian temple in Gujarat, etc.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":482,"n":508,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following is a famous example of a temple built in complete Dravidian style?","o":{"a":"Konark Sun temple, Konark","b":"Kandariya Mahadeo temple at Khajuraho","c":"Kamakhya temple at Guwahati","d":"Kailasa temple at Ellora"},"a":"d","s":"Kailash Temple at Ellora was built by Krishna I of the Rashtrakuta Dynasty. King Narasimhadeva I built Konark temple. Kandariya Mahadeo temple was built by Vidyadhara - a powerful Chandela king. Kamakhya temple was built by the Mlechchha dynasty. Rebuilt by Koch King Nara Narayan and Ahom kings.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":483,"n":509,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The ancient Neemrana Baori is located in which of the following districts of Rajasthan?","o":{"a":"Ajmer","b":"Alwar","c":"Bikaner","d":"Jaipur"},"a":"b","s":"Alwar . Famous Baori in India - Chand Baori (Rajasthan), Agrasen Ki Baoli (New Delhi), Rani ki Vav (Gujarat), Pushkarani (Karnataka), Baoli Ghaus Ali Shah (Haryana), Mata Bhavani ni Vav (Gujarat), Rajon ki Baoli (New Delhi), Surya Kund (Gujarat).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":484,"n":510,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the given statements related to the temples of India is correct? I. The Nagara style of architecture is also known as the North Indian style II. The Dravidian style of architecture is also known as the South Indian style.","o":{"a":"Neither I nor II","b":"Both I and II","c":"Only II","d":"Only I"},"a":"b","s":"Both I and II . Different styles of Temple architecture in India Nagara or the north Indian temple style → Rekha-prasada/Latina, Phamsana, and Valabhi; Dravida Or South Indian Temple Style Kuta or Caturasra, Shala or Ayatasra, Gaja-prishta or vrittayata, Vritta, and Ashtasra.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":485,"n":511,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Tipu Sultan became the ruler of Mysore in ______.","o":{"a":"1765","b":"1782","c":"1775","d":"1762"},"a":"b","s":"December 1782. Tipu Sultan (Tiger of Mysore) succeeded his father Hyder Ali as the Ruler of Mysore. The Mysore Palace was the residence of the Wodeyar Dynasty.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":486,"n":512,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was given the title of 'Uttarapatha Swamin' ?","o":{"a":"Mahipala","b":"Devapala","c":"Dharmapala","d":"Gopala"},"a":"c","s":"Dharmapala assumed the title of Uttarapatha Swamin , Parama Bhattaraka, Paramesvara, and Maharajadhiraja. He was the successor of Gopala (founder of the Pala empire). He was a pious Buddhist. He founded Vikramshila University in Bhagalpur, Bihar.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":487,"n":513,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following was an integral part of the Indo-Islamic form of architecture?","o":{"a":"dome","b":"Shutter windows","c":"Tall and steep slopes","d":"Square roofs"},"a":"a","s":"Dome (large hemispherical roof), arch, and Muqarnas vault. Styles of Indo-Islamic Architecture: The Imperial Style (Delhi sultanate); The Provincial styles (Mandu, Gujarat, Bengal, and Jaunpur); The Mughal Style (Delhi, Agra, and Lahore); The Deccani style (Bijapur and Golconda).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":488,"n":514,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the ruler of Mysore in 1761 ?","o":{"a":"Baji Rao","b":"Murshid Quli Jafar Khan","c":"Hyder Ali","d":"Nizam-ul-Mulk"},"a":"c","s":"Hyder Ali . In 1769, he defeated the British forces several times and reached the Madras walls. In the second Anglo-Mysore War, he died in 1782 and was succeeded by his son Tipu.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":489,"n":515,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What was the capital of the Satavahana Dynasty (60 BC-225 AD)?","o":{"a":"Pratishthana","b":"Vidisha","c":"Patliputra","d":"Vanji"},"a":"a","s":"Pratishthana. Satavahanas (Simuka was the founder) succeeded the Mauryas in Deccan and in Central India. They had two capitals one was Pratishthana (modern Paithan) on the Godavari in Aurangabad district and the second one was Amravati .","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":490,"n":516,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Mughal emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar died in______.","o":{"a":"1857","b":"1858","c":"1862","d":"1859"},"a":"c","s":"1862. Bahadur Shah Zafar was a titular Emperor, as the Mughal Empire existed in name only and his authority was limited only to the walled city of Old Delhi (Shahjahanabad). Following his involvement in the Indian Mutiny of 1857, the British exiled him to Rangoon in British-controlled Burma in 1858, after convicting him on several charges.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":491,"n":517,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In 1590, the Govind Deva temple of Vrindavan, near Mathura was constructed from ______ sandstone.","o":{"a":"yellow","b":"black","c":"white","d":"Red"},"a":"d","s":"Red. The Govind Deva temple was built by the king of Jaipur, Man Singh, in 1590 in Vrindavan in Uttar Pradesh.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":492,"n":518,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The book 'Man Kautuhal' was compiled by which of the following kings of Medieval India?","o":{"a":"Raja Mansingh Tomar","b":"Krishna II","c":"Parantaka Viranarayana","d":"Rajashekhara Varman"},"a":"a","s":"Raja Mansingh Tomar. He was the ruler of Gwalior. He was a patron of the Dhrupad genre from Hindustani Classical Music. He built Gwalior fort (Gujari Mahal and Man Mandir Palace). The founder of the Tomar dynasty in India was Anangapala I.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":493,"n":519,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"King Lalitaditya Muktapida ruled over ______.","o":{"a":"Gujarat","b":"Sikkim","c":"Kerala","d":"Kashmir"},"a":"d","s":"Kashmir . A\u0000mad Shah I was the first independent sultan of Gujarat , who founded Ahmedabad (1411).The Chogyal (king who governs with righteousness) Palden Namgyal Thodup was crushed in 1975 and Sikkim merged with India as its 22nd state. King Mahabali ruled over Kerala.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":494,"n":520,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"During the rule of which dynasty were Nalanda and Vikramashila universities founded?","o":{"a":"The Palas","b":"The Senas","c":"The Pratihara","d":"The Rashtrakutas"},"a":"a","s":"The Palas. Kumargupta founded Nalanda University in the 5th century A.D. Vikramashila was established by the Pala emperor Dharmapala .","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":495,"n":521,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Chandannagar was established as a French colony in 1673, obtaining permission from______ the then Nawab of Bengal, to establish a trading post on the right bank of the river Hooghly.","o":{"a":"Mansur Ali Khan","b":"Murshid Quli Khan","c":"Ibrahim Khan","d":"Mir Zafar"},"a":"c","s":"Ibrahim Khan . French Colonies In India- Pondicherry, Karaikal, Yanam (Andhra Pradesh) on the Coromandel Coast, Mahe on the Malabar Coast and Chandernagor in Bengal.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":496,"n":522,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Hafiz (Hafez) was a ______ century poet whose collection of poems is known as 'Diwan' or 'Diwan-e-Hafiz'.","o":{"a":"14th","b":"15th","c":"16th","d":"17th"},"a":"a","s":"14th-century '. Hafez was an Iranian poet. His collection of poems 'Diwan' is in Persian language. Most of the parts of 'Diwan' are Ghazals.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":497,"n":523,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In the context of Sufi traditions, what is the meaning of the word 'sama'?","o":{"a":"recitation of sacred songs","b":"Magical feat","c":"disciple","d":"Ashram"},"a":"a","s":"recitation of sacred songs Sufism ( tasawwuf) is a mystic sect of Islam that emphasizes introspection and spiritual closeness with God. They believe in inner purity rather than external conduct and rituals.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":498,"n":524,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"______ was one of the first Indian rulers to lead an armed rebellion against the British East India Company in 1824 against the implementation of the Doctrine of Lapse.","o":{"a":"Kittur Chennamma","b":"Titumir","c":"Jayi Rajaguru","d":"Pazhassi Raja"},"a":"a","s":"Kittur Chennamma was one of the first Indian rulers to lead an armed rebellion against the British East India Company in 1824 against the implementation of the Doctrine of Lapse.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":499,"n":525,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following cities became the capital of Bahamani sultanate immediately after its foundation?","o":{"a":"Devagiri","b":"Daulatabad","c":"A\u0000sanabad","d":"Berar"},"a":"c","s":"Ahsanabad. Bahamani Sultanate (Sunni Muslim empire that ruled the Deccan) - Founded in 1347 by ʿAlaʾ al-Din Bahman Shah. Between 1490 and 1518 dissolved into the five successor powers of Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda, Berar, and Bidar.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":500,"n":526,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Vikramaditya VI, whose biography was composed by his court poet Bilhana, was a ruler of ______ dynasty.","o":{"a":"Chalukya","b":"Ganga","c":"Rashtrakuta","d":"Pallava"},"a":"a","s":"Chalukya. Vikramaditya VI, whose biography was composed by his court poet Bilhana, was a ruler of Chalukya dynasty. The Chalukya dynasty was established by Pulakeshin I in 543 AD.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":501,"n":527,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"'Rang Ghar', one of Asia's oldest amphitheatres, was built by the king of the ___dynasty.","o":{"a":"Sen","b":"Ahom","c":"Mughal","d":"Gupta"},"a":"b","s":"Ahom Dynasty. Rang Ghar, constructed by the Ahom King Swargadeo Promotta Singha.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":502,"n":528,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the founder of the Bhakti sect named 'Pushtimarg'?","o":{"a":"Vallabhacharya","b":"Kabir","c":"Shankaracharya","d":"Ramanuja"},"a":"a","s":"Vallabhacharya founded the Krishna-centered PushtiMarg sect of Vaishnavism in the Braj region of India, and the philosophy of Shuddha advaita (Pure Nondualism). Pushtimarg is a form of Krishna worship for adoration.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":503,"n":529,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the founder of the Suryavamsi Gajapati dynasty that ruled parts of Orissa and Andhra Pradesh in the 15th century?","o":{"a":"Bhanu Deva","b":"Vasu Deva","c":"Surendra Deva","d":"Kapilendra Deva"},"a":"d","s":"Kapilendra Deva was the founder of the Suryavamsi Gajapati dynasty that ruled parts of Orissa and Andhra Pradesh in the 15th century (1434-66 CE).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":504,"n":530,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which city was the capital of the French Colony in India?","o":{"a":"Cochin","b":"Tuticorin","c":"Pondicherry","d":"Panjim"},"a":"c","s":"Pondicherry . Five French colonies are Pondicherry, Karaikal in Tamil Nadu and Yanaon in Andhra Pradesh on the Coromandel Coast, Mahe in Kerala on the Malabar Coast and Chandernagore in West Bengal.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":505,"n":531,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the Nizam of Hyderabad in 1947?","o":{"a":"Mir Mahbub Ali Khan","b":"Osman Ali","c":"Akbar Ali Khan","d":"Nasir Jung"},"a":"b","s":"Osman Ali . He was the last Nizam (ruler) of the Princely State of Hyderabad. He ruled the Kingdom of Hyderabad between 1911 and 1948.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":506,"n":532,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following is the meaning of 'Pishtaq' in the context of medieval Indo-Islamic Architecture?","o":{"a":"Dome","b":"Tall gateway","c":"True arch","d":"Water tank"},"a":"b","s":"tall gateway. Pishtaq is a Persian/Farsi word that means 'front of arch'. It is a rectangular frame around an arched opening of a building like mosques and palaces.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":507,"n":533,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Prophet Muhammad founded the faith of Islam in the ______ century.","o":{"a":"sixth","b":"eighth","c":"seventh","d":"ninth"},"a":"c","s":"Seventh century. Islam is the 2nd largest religion in the world. Largest religion - Christianity.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":508,"n":534,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was appointed as the curator of ancient monuments in 1880?","o":{"a":"Alfred Foucher","b":"HH Cole","c":"John Marshall","d":"Rajendra Lal Mitra"},"a":"b","s":"HH Cole . He was a British civil servant and inventor who facilitated many innovations in commerce and Education.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":509,"n":535,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which of the following dynasties made Kanauj (Kanyakubja) its capital city?","o":{"a":"Pala dynasty","b":"Pratihara dynasty","c":"Sena dynasty","d":"Chola dynasty"},"a":"b","s":"Pratihara dynasty was an imperial power during the Late Classical period on the Indian subcontinent that ruled much of Northern India from the mid-8th to the 11th century. They ruled first at Ujjain and later at Kannauj.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":510,"n":536,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which part of India did the Nizams of Asaf Jahi dynasty rule?","o":{"a":"Western kingdoms","b":"Eastern states","c":"Deccan provinces","d":"Northern India"},"a":"c","s":"Deccan provinces. The Asaf Jahi was a Muslim dynasty that ruled the Kingdom of Hyderabad. It was founded by Nizam al-Mulk who was intermittently viceroy of the Deccan (peninsular India) under the Mughal emperors from 1713 to 1721 and who resumed the post again under the title A\u0000af Jah in 1724.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":511,"n":537,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was a Portuguese writer who wrote about the trade and society in South India?","o":{"a":"Niccolao Manucci","b":"Francois Bernier","c":"Jean-Baptiste Tavernier","d":"Duarte Barbosa"},"a":"d","s":"Duarte Barbosa was the Portuguese writer who visited India during (1500-1516) and wrote about the trade and society in South India in his manuscript, 'The Book of Duarte Barbosa'.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":512,"n":538,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following foreign travellers is NOT correctly matched with their respective country they hail from?","o":{"a":"Marco Polo - Italy","b":"Peter Mundy - China","c":"Ibn Battuta - Morocco","d":"Al-Biruni - Uzbekistan"},"a":"b","s":"Peter Mundy (Britain) was a seventeenth-century British merchant trader, traveller and writer. He was the first Briton to record, in his Itinerarium Mundi, tasting Chaa (tea) in China and travelled extensively in Asia, Russia and Europe.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":513,"n":539,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the first Portuguese viceroy of India?","o":{"a":"Alfonso de Albuquerque","b":"Duarte de Menezes","c":"Francisco de Almeida","d":"Lopo Soares de Albergaria"},"a":"c","s":"Francisco de Almeida was the first Portuguese viceroy of India in 1505. He was given the credit of Portuguese possession in India. His policy was to maintain a strong naval army over the Indian ocean.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":514,"n":540,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following did NOT belong to the Rajput kingdom of Marwar?","o":{"a":"Rao Jodha","b":"Rao Chanda","c":"Rana Kumbha","d":"Maldeva"},"a":"c","s":"Rana Kumbha was associated with the Mewar Kingdom. He belonged to the Sisodia clan. He Built Vijaya Stambha (dedicated to lord Vishnu) within Chittor Fort in 1448 to commemorate his victory over the army of Malwa led by Mahmud Khalji in the Battle of Sarangpur.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":515,"n":541,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Kalinjar Fort, which was strategically important during medieval times, is located in:","o":{"a":"Uttar Pradesh","b":"Rajasthan","c":"Punjab","d":"Sind"},"a":"a","s":"Uttar Pradesh (Banda district). Kalinjar Fort - Built by the Chandelas. Main attraction of Kalinjar is the Neelkanth Temple built by Chandela ruler Paramaditya Dev.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":516,"n":542,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which year Sanchi was discovered after being abandoned for nearly 600 years?","o":{"a":"1818","b":"1816","c":"1820","d":"1814"},"a":"a","s":"1818 . It was discovered in 1818 by General Taylor. Sanchi is a Buddhist complex, famous for its Great Stupa, on a hilltop at Sanchi Town in Raisen District of the State of Madhya Pradesh, India.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":517,"n":543,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the Governor General of Bengal during the 3rd Anglo-Mysore war?","o":{"a":"John Macpherson","b":"2nd Earl Cornwallis","c":"John Shore","d":"Warren Hastings"},"a":"b","s":"2nd Earl Cornwallis. The resultant war lasted three years and was a resounding defeat for Mysore.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":518,"n":544,"sec":"Medieval History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which year did the Portuguese capture Goa?","o":{"a":"1510 AD","b":"1540 AD","c":"1610 AD","d":"1475 AD"},"a":"a","s":"1510 AD. Portuguese Governor Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa (became the capital of the Portuguese) from the Adil Shahis. The Portuguese were the first European to come (1498) and the last one to leave India (1961).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":519,"n":545,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"Who among the following leaders led the Revolt of 1857 in Bihar?","o":{"a":"Rajendra Prasad","b":"Devi Singh","c":"Rajkumar Shukla","d":"Kunwar Singh"},"a":"d","s":"Kunwar Singh. He belonged to a family of the Ujjainiya clan of the Parmar Rajputs of Jagdishpur (Bhojpur). The Revolt of 1857 is known by several names: the Sepoy Mutiny, the Indian Mutiny, the Great Rebellion, and the First War of Independence (by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":520,"n":546,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"From which place did the Revolt of 1857 start?","o":{"a":"Agra","b":"Meerut","c":"Kanpur","d":"Delhi"},"a":"b","s":"Meerut (10 May 1857). The Revolt of 1857 - Also called Sepoy Mutiny (By the British Historians), First War of Independence (by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar). Lord Canning was the British Governor general during the revolt.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":521,"n":547,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"Nana Sahib, a rebel at Kanpur was the son of which of the following Peshwas?","o":{"a":"Baji Rao I","b":"Balaji Baji Rao","c":"Baji Rao II","d":"Balaji Vishwanath"},"a":"c","s":"Bajirao II (10 January 1775 - 28 January 1851) was the last Peshwa of the Maratha Empire. Leaders and Places of the revolt of 1857: Kunwar singh - Bihar, Rani Lakshmibai - Jhansi, Begum Hazrat Mahal (wife of the deposed Nawab of Awadh, Wajid Ali Shah) - Lucknow, Bahadur shah Zafar - Delhi, Tatya tope and Nana saheb - Kanpur, Khan Bahadur Khan Rohilla - Bareilly.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":522,"n":548,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"Khan Bahadur led the revolt of 1857 from:","o":{"a":"Delhi","b":"Kanpur","c":"Lucknow","d":"Bareilly"},"a":"d","s":"Bareilly. Bahadur Shah II, the last Mughal Emperor led the revolt from Delhi. Nana Sahib led the revolt from Kanpur, he was the adopted son of Peshwa Baji Rao II.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":523,"n":549,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"Which Mughal ruler contributed to the revolt of 1857?","o":{"a":"Shahalam II","b":"Bahadur shah I","c":"Alamgir II","d":"Bahadur shah II"},"a":"d","s":"Bahadur Shah II (Bahadur Shah Zafar). He was the twentieth and last Mughal Emperor of India . In 1857 Bahadur Shah II was deposed by the British and exiled to Rangoon. Revolt of 1857 (the Sepoy Mutiny) is called the First War of Independence by V.D. Savarkar. Lord Canning was the Governor-General during the revolt.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 4th shift"},{"id":524,"n":550,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"The sepoys of Meerut arrived at the Gates of Red Fort to meet the Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah on _____________.","o":{"a":"11 March, 1857","b":"10 March, 1857","c":"10 May, 1857","d":"11 May, 1857"},"a":"d","s":"11 May, 1857. The Indian Mutiny began from Meerut on 10 May 1857.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":525,"n":551,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"Mangal Pandey was hanged to death for attacking British officers in Barrackpore on ______.","o":{"a":"21 March 1857","b":"23 March 1857","c":"31 March 1857","d":"29 March 1857"},"a":"d","s":"29 March 1857. Mangal Pandey was a Sepoy in the 34th Bengal Native Infantry regiment of the British East India Company.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":526,"n":552,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolt of 1857","q":"Whose proclamation of November 1, 1858 declared that thereafter India would be governed by and in the name of the British Monarch through a Secretary of State?","o":{"a":"King Louis'","b":"King Philips'","c":"Queen Victoria's","d":"Queen Elizabeth's"},"a":"c","s":"Queen Victoria's proclamation of November 1, 1858.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":527,"n":553,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Governor General) List-II (Period) A. Warren Hasting 1. 1772-1785 B. Lord Cornwallis 2. 1786-1793 C. Sir John Shore 3. 1793-1798 D. Lord Wellesley 4. 1798-1805","o":{"a":"A - 4, B - 3, C - 2, D - 1","b":"A - 2, B - 1, C - 4, D - 3","c":"A - 1, B - 2, C - 3, D - 4","d":"A - 2, B - 1, C - 3, D - 4"},"a":"c","s":"A - 1, B - 2, C - 3, D - 4 The first Governor of Bengal - Robert Clive. The first Governor General of Bengal in 1773 - Warren Hastings (1772-1785). The first Governor General of India - William Bentinck. The first Viceroy of India - Lord Canning.","ex":"SSC CHSL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":528,"n":554,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Identify the INCORRECTLY matched pair(s) of the British Governor-Generals of India and the events with which they are associated. i) Lord Dalhousie - Revolt of 1857 ii) Lord Auckland - First Afghan War iii) Lord Canning - Wood's Despatch 1854","o":{"a":"Only (ii) and (iii)","b":"Only (ii)","c":"Only (i) and (iii)","d":"Only (i)"},"a":"c","s":"Only (i) and (iii). Governor general and the related events: Lord Dalhousie (1848-1856) - Doctrine of Lapse policy, Wood's Despatch 1854. Lord Cornwallis (1786 - 1793) - Introduction of the Charter Act of 1793, the Third Anglo-Mysore War began and ended with the Treaty of Seringapatam. Warren Hastings (1772 -1785) - Introduction of the Regulating Act of 1773 by the British Parliament, First Anglo-Maratha War began (ended with the Treaty of Salbai). Lord Wellesley (1798-1805) - The Second Anglo-Maratha War began and ended with multiple treaties.","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":529,"n":555,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who was the Governor-General when the first railway line was laid ?","o":{"a":"Lord Cornwallis","b":"Warren Hastings","c":"Lord William Bentinck","d":"Lord Dalhousie"},"a":"d","s":"Lord Dalhousie (Governor -General of India from 1848 to 1856). He was also known as the father of Indian Railways. The first railway line in India was laid in 1853 from Bombay (now Mumbai) to Thane, a distance of 21 miles (34 km) and was operated by the Great Indian Peninsula Railway Company. Warren Hastings (1772-1785) - First Governor-General of Bengal. Lord Cornwallis (Governor-General, 1786-93) - First to bring into existence and organize civil services. Lord William Bentinck (1828-1835) - First Governor-general of India","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":530,"n":556,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Which of the following Governor Generals, under the British rule in India, professionalized, bureaucratised and Europeanised the administration of East India Company?","o":{"a":"Warren Hastings","b":"Lord Canning","c":"Lord Dalhousie","d":"Lord Cornwallis"},"a":"d","s":"Lord Cornwallis : He introduced the Permanent settlement of Bengal (1793). Third Mysore War (1790-92) and Treaty of Seringapatam (1792) took place during his time period. Warren Hastings - First Governor General of Bengal in 1773.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":531,"n":557,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"During British rule in India, which of the following Governor Generals devised the 'Doctrine of Lapse', a policy of annexation ?","o":{"a":"Lord Dalhousie","b":"Warren Hastings","c":"Lord Cornwallis","d":"Lord Canning"},"a":"a","s":"Lord Dalhousie was the Governor-General from 1848 to 1856. The doctrine declared that if an Indian ruler died without a male heir his kingdom would \"lapse\", that is, become part of Company territory. States annexed under Doctrine of lapse: Satara (1848), Sambalpur (1850), Udaipur (1852), Nagpur (1853), Jhansi (1854) and Awadh (1856).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":532,"n":558,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who among the following was the viceroy of India at the time of India's independence from colonial rule on 15 August 1947 ?","o":{"a":"Lord Wavell","b":"Lord Willingdon","c":"Lord Mountbatten","d":"Lord Linlithgow"},"a":"c","s":"Lord Mountbatten (1947-1948): Events During his Regime - Redcliff commission (1947). Lord Wavell (1944-1947) - C. Rajagopalachari's CR Formula (1944), Cabinet Mission (1946). Lord Willingdon (1931-1936) - Communal Award (1932), Third Round Table Conference, Poona Pact (1932). Lord Linlithgow (1936-1944) - Tripuri Crisis and formation of Forward Bloc (1939), Cripps Mission (1942), Quit India Movement (1942).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":533,"n":559,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"English as the official language of India for higher education was incorporated in the resolution formulation by _______.","o":{"a":"Lord Hastings","b":"Lord Wellesley","c":"Lord Macaulay","d":"Lord Dalhousie"},"a":"c","s":"Lord Macaulay - Considered to be the founder of the British education system in India. He was also the chairman of the first Law Commission of pre - independent India.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":534,"n":560,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"The Vernacular Press Act was enacted during the regime of which viceroy of India?","o":{"a":"Lord Dalhousie","b":"Lord Hastings","c":"Lord Ripon","d":"Lord Lytton"},"a":"d","s":"Lord Lytton (1876-1880 Vernacular Press Act (The gagging Act) 1878 - Enacted to control the vernacular press and effectively punish and repress seditious writing.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":535,"n":561,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who among the following signed a Tripartite treaty on behalf of the British Government to interfere in the affairs of Afghanistan?","o":{"a":"Lord Mayo","b":"Lord Auckland","c":"Lord Wellesly","d":"Lord Canning"},"a":"b","s":"Lord Auckland (1836 - 42).Tripartite treaty (1838): It was signed between Governor-General Lord Auckland (British), Maharaja Ranjit Singh (Sikhs), and Shah Shuja (who had been deposed from the Afghan throne in 1809 and had been living since then as a British pensioner at Ludhiana). Lord Mayo (1869 - 1872) - He ordered the first census of India in 1871. Lord Wellesly (1798 - 1805) - Subsidiary Alliance came in 1798. Lord Canning (1856 - 62) - First Viceroy of India.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":536,"n":562,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Which of the following Commissions was appointed by Lord Curzon to enquire into the working of the police administration?","o":{"a":"Kitchener Commission","b":"Hunter Commission","c":"Frazer Commission","d":"MacDonnell Commission"},"a":"c","s":"Frazer Commission (The Indian Police Commission of 1902-03 ) - Established by the British. Headed by - Sir Andrew Frazer and Lord Curzon. Hunter Commission of 1882 was presided over by Sir William Hunter and was appointed by Lord Ripon to examine the country's educational situation at the time and make recommendations for reforms. The Hunter Commission of 1919 (the Disorders Inquiry Committee) was constituted to examine and scrutinize the horrific slaughter at the Jallianwala Bagh. McDonnell Commission - Following famine of 1899 -1900, Lord Curzon appointed a famine commission led by MacDonnell.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":537,"n":563,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who among the following was the Governor General of India when the Permanent Settlement was introduced in Bengal for revenue collection by the East India Company ?","o":{"a":"Lord Cornwallis","b":"Lord Wellesly","c":"Lord Dalhousie","d":"Lord Canning"},"a":"a","s":"Lord Cornwallis. Provision of Permanent Settlement - The Zamindars' proprietorship would stay as long as he paid the fixed revenue at the said date to the government. Other States where Permanent Settlement Implemented - Bihar, Odisha. Other Settlements Related to Agriculture - Ryotwari System (Sir Thomas Munro), Mahalwari System (Holt Mackenzie).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":538,"n":564,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who among the following has been accused of committing the judicial murder of Raja Nand Kumar?","o":{"a":"Lord Wellesly","b":"Lord Dalhousie","c":"Lord Cornwallis","d":"Warren Hastings"},"a":"d","s":"Warren Hastings. Raja Nand Kumar (Nuncomar) was an Indian tax collector and He was the first Indian to be executed by hanging. Lord Wellesley - Established the Subsidiary Alliance system that brought the princely kingdoms under British rule. Lord Dalhousie - Introduced Railway, Electric Telegraph, Postal Service. Lord Cornwallis - Zamindari System.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":539,"n":565,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"____________ announced the bifurcation of the British Indian Empire into a secular India and Islamic Pakistan.","o":{"a":"Lord Ripon","b":"Lord Dalhousie","c":"Lord Louis Mountbatten","d":"Lord Curzon"},"a":"c","s":"Lord Louis Mountbatten (last Viceroy of India) . Lord Curzon - Viceroy (1899 - 1905): He announced the partition of Bengal Presidency In July 1905.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":540,"n":566,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Under whom was CP Ilbert a law member of the council?","o":{"a":"Lord Lytton","b":"Lord Cornwallis","c":"Lord Ripon","d":"Lord Curzon"},"a":"c","s":"Lord Ripon (1880 - 1884) - He repealed the Vernacular Press Act of 1878 which was Introduced by Lord Lytton . CP Ilbert introduced the IIbert Bill that would have granted the right of Indian judges to judge Europeans in court.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":541,"n":567,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Elphinstone was the Governor of which province of India during 1819-27 ?","o":{"a":"North West Frontier province","b":"Madras","c":"Bengal","d":"Bombay"},"a":"d","s":"Bombay. Elphinstone: Known for the compilation of the Elphinstone code (a uniform scheme of criminal law). In 1808 he was sent to negotiate an alliance with the Afghan ruler Shah Shoja to prevent a Napoleonic advance upon India.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":542,"n":568,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who designed the administrative code to separate revenue administration from judicial administration?","o":{"a":"Lord Lytton","b":"Lord Cornwallis","c":"Lord Auckland","d":"Warren Hastings"},"a":"b","s":"Lord Cornwallis. He introduced Permanent settlement in 1793 in Bengal. He is known as 'the Father of civil service in India', Governor general of Bengal (1786-93).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":543,"n":569,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"The Viceroy who took keen interest in the restoration and protection of historical monuments was .","o":{"a":"Lord Ellenborough","b":"Lord Auckland","c":"Lord Lytton","d":"Lord Curzon"},"a":"d","s":"Lord Curzon (1899-1905) : Passed the Ancient Monuments Preservation Act in 1904. Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) - Founded in 1861 by Alexander Cunningham; Headquarters: New Delhi. Lord Ellenborough ( 1842-1844 ) - Annexation Of Sindh (1843). Lord Auckland (1836 - 1842) - The first Anglo-Afghan war occurred during his reign. Lord Lytton (1876-1880) - The Great Indian Famine of 1876-1878.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 1st shift"},{"id":544,"n":570,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"For the political education of the people at the local level, who brought the resolution on local self-government?","o":{"a":"Lord Ripon","b":"Lord Mayo","c":"Lord Canning","d":"Lord Lytton"},"a":"a","s":"Lord Ripon (Father of local self Government, 1880-1884) - Hunter Commission on Education (1882). Lord Mayo (1869 -1872) - Decentralization of finances; Mayo College in Ajmer for educating children of the aristocratic families. Lord Canning (1856-1862) - First Viceroy of India; Abolished doctrine of lapse; East India Company was abolished and powers were transferred to the Crown. Lord Lytton (1876 -1880) - Second Afghan war (1878-1880); Arms Act (1878); The Vernacular Press Act (1878); Tax on Cotton was abolished for the British traders.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 3rd shift"},{"id":545,"n":571,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"The policy of dual system of government of Bengal was the brainchild of which Governor-General?","o":{"a":"Lord Hastings","b":"Lord Clive","c":"Lord Lytton","d":"Lord Dalhousie"},"a":"b","s":"Lord Clive (First governor of Bengal in 1765). Dual system - The administration of Bengal was divided into two parts : Diwani - The right to collect revenue was given to the company, and Nizamat - Administrative rights were given to Nawab. Warren Hastings abolished this system in Bengal in 1772. Lord Hastings (Governor - General of India, 1813 - 1823) - He introduced a new 'paramountcy' policy.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":546,"n":572,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who among the following Governor Generals of Bengal outlawed the practice of Sati?","o":{"a":"Charles Canning","b":"Charles Cornwallis","c":"Warren Hastings","d":"William Bentinck"},"a":"d","s":"William Bentinck. He was the first Governor General of India from 1828 to 1835. Lord Cornwallis - Permanent Settlement, Mint in Calcutta. Lord Canning - Governor general during the revolt of 1857.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":547,"n":573,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Governor general Lord Dalhousie described the Kingdom of _______\"as a cherry that will drop into our mouth one day\".","o":{"a":"Ahom","b":"Mysore","c":"Awadh","d":"Maratha"},"a":"c","s":"Awadh. Lord Dalhousie said this in 1851. Awadh Kingdom was formally annexed to the British Empire in 1856. The doctrine declared that if an Indian ruler died without a male heir his kingdom would \"lapse\" , that is, become part of Company territory. One kingdom after another was annexed simply by applying this doctrine:- Satara (1848), Sambalpur (1850), Udaipur (1852), Nagpur (1853) and Jhansi (1854).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":548,"n":574,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Under ________ (Governor General from 1813 to 1823) a new policy of \"paramountcy\" was initiated. Now the Company claimed that its authority was paramount or supreme, hence its power was greater than that of Indian states.","o":{"a":"Lord Amherst","b":"Lord Cornwallis","c":"Lord Hastings","d":"Lord Auckland"},"a":"c","s":"Lord Hastings ( Anglo-Nepal War took place between 1814 and 1816 ). Lord Amherst was Governor-General of India from 1823 to 1828.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":549,"n":575,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"\"A History of British India\" is a work published by ______.","o":{"a":"Lord Canning","b":"Lord Dalhousie","c":"James Mill","d":"Warren Hastings"},"a":"c","s":"James Mill in 1817, a Scottish economist and political philosopher, published a massive three-volume work \"The History of British India\". In his book history was divided into three periods: Hindu, Muslim, and British.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":550,"n":576,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Subsidiary alliance treaty was imposed on Awadh in ______.","o":{"a":"1802","b":"1803","c":"1801","d":"1804"},"a":"c","s":"1801. Subsidiary alliance, drafted by Lord Wellesley (Governor general of India , 1798 to1805) was a treaty between the British East India Company and the Indian princely states that gave the English dominion over the Indian kingdoms. Indian states annexed under Subsidiary alliance: Hyderabad (1798), Mysore (1799), Tanjore (1799), Peshwa (1802), ScIndia (1803), and Gaekwad (1803).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":551,"n":577,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Based on Macaulay's Minute, the English Education Act was introduced in ______.","o":{"a":"1875","b":"1835","c":"1855","d":"1815"},"a":"b","s":"1835 by Lord William Bentinck . Lord Macaulay presented his education policy titled Minute on Indian Education in 1835. Lord Macaulay was India's First Law member of the Governor-General's Council.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":552,"n":578,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"In 1856, Governor-General ______ decided that Bahadur Shah Zafar would be the last Mughal king and after his death none of his descendants would be recognised as kings - they would just be called princes.","o":{"a":"Canning","b":"Cornwallis","c":"Hastings","d":"Munro"},"a":"a","s":"Lord Canning (1856-1862) - (Last Governor-General and first viceroy of India) - introduced Indian Penal Code (IPC), withdrew Doctrine of Lapse.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":553,"n":579,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Who among the following was the Viceroy of India during the formation of the Indian National Congress?","o":{"a":"Lord Lytton","b":"Lord Ripon","c":"Lord Dufferin","d":"Lord Mayo"},"a":"c","s":"Lord Dufferin (1884-1888 Indian National Congress (INC) was formed by A.O Hume under the presidency of W.C. Benerjee in 1885. The First session of INC was held in December 1885 at Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College in Bombay, attended by 72 delegates. 1 st woman president of INC - Annie Besant (1917).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":554,"n":580,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Which of the following Viceroys of British India took the first step to separate provincial finance from central finance and introduced financial dece-ntralisation in India in 1870? Graduate Level 03/08/2022 (Shift - 3 )","o":{"a":"Lord Chelmsford","b":"Lord Northbrook","c":"Lord Mayo","d":"Lord Dufferin"},"a":"c","s":"Lord Mayo's Resolution of 1870 on Financial Decentralisation visualised the development of local self-government institutions.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":555,"n":581,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"_________ introduced the daroga system, replacing zamindari thanedars under the direct control of the district head and on its payroll. Graduate Level 05/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Lord Minto I","b":"Lord Cornwallis","c":"Lord Hastings","d":"Lord Wellesley"},"a":"b","s":"Lord Cornwallis - Cornwallis Code (1793), Father of Civil Services in India, Third Anglo-Mysore War (1789 - 1792) - (Lord Cornwallis and Tipu sultan), Permanent Settlement (1793) in Bengal.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":556,"n":582,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Which of the following statements is correct? I. Robert Clive had come to Madras from England in 1743 at the age of 18. II. After the battle of Buxar, the East India Company appointed Residents in Indian states.","o":{"a":"Only I","b":"Neither I nor II","c":"Only II","d":"Both I and II"},"a":"d","s":"Both I and II. Robert Clive was a soldier and first British administrator of Bengal, who was one of the creators of British power in India.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":557,"n":583,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Which of the following British officers issued the infamous 'crawling order' whereby Indians had to crawl on all fours to pass an alley?","o":{"a":"General Dyer","b":"Warren Hastings","c":"Lord Irwin","d":"Lord Curzon"},"a":"a","s":"General Dyer . He was responsible for the Jallianwala Bagh massacre in Amritsar.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":558,"n":584,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Governors and Viceroys","q":"Which of the following Viceroys of India addressed the Bengal famine of 1943 by ordering the army to distribute relief supplies to the starving rural Bengalis?","o":{"a":"Lord Wavell","b":"Lord Mountbatten","c":"Lord Willington","d":"Lord Linlithgow"},"a":"d","s":"Lord Linlithgow was the viceroy of India during the Bengal famine 1943. It is believed that the Bengal famine of 1943 which is estimated to have killed up to three million people was not caused by drought but instead was a result of a \"complete policy failure\" of the then-British Prime Minister Winston Churchill.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":559,"n":585,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Match the following Acts/Policies with the respective years in which they were put into place. Acts/Policies Year A. Pitt's India Act 1. 1784 B. Permanent Settlement of 2. 1793 Bengal C. Introduction of the 3. 1798 Subsidiary Alliance System D. Abolition of Sati 4. 1829","o":{"a":"A - 4, B - 3, C - 2, D - 1","b":"A - 2, B - 1, C - 4, D - 3","c":"A - 2, B - 1, C - 3, D- 4","d":"A - 1, B - 2, C - 3, D - 4"},"a":"d","s":"Major acts/policies with respective year during British rule : Regulating Act (1773), Pitt's India Act (1784), The act of good governance (1858), The Prevention of seditious meetings Act (1907), The Explosives Substances Act (1908), Rowlatt Act (1919), Permanent settlement (1793), Cornwallis Code (1793).","ex":"SSC CHSL Tier II","y":2024,"sh":null},{"id":560,"n":586,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which of the following Acts is also known as Morley-Minto Reforms?","o":{"a":"Indian Councils Act, 1909","b":"Government of India Act, 1935","c":"Government of India Act, 1919","d":"Indian Councils Act, 1892"},"a":"a","s":"Indian Councils Act, 1909 Features of the Act: Morley-Minto Reforms (Lord Morley was the then Secretary of State for India and Lord Minto was the then Viceroy of India). It introduced separate electorates on the basis of religion. It was instituted to placate the Moderates (Congress). Lord Minto - Father of the Communal Electorate in India. Satyendra P Sinha was appointed the first Indian member of the Viceroy's Executive Council.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":561,"n":587,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The Provincial elections were held for the first time in _______ under the Government of India Act, 1935.","o":{"a":"1935","b":"1937","c":"1936","d":"1938"},"a":"b","s":"1937, Indian provincial elections: Elections were held in eleven provinces - Madras, Central Provinces, Bihar, Orissa, the United Provinces, the Bombay Presidency, Assam, the North-West Frontier Province, Bengal, Punjab and Sind. The Indian National Congress (INC) won the majority of seats in the provincial elections, and formed governments in seven provinces. Government of India Act, 1935 : It provided for the establishment of all Indian federations consisting of provinces and princely states as units. A feature of the Act - Bicameral legislature.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":562,"n":588,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which of the following Acts, enacted by the British Parliament, provided for the establishment of a Law Commission for the consolidation and codification of Indian Laws?","o":{"a":"Charter Act, 1833","b":"Indian Council Act, 1909","c":"Government of India Act, 1919","d":"Charter Act, 1813"},"a":"a","s":"Charter Act, 1833. Indian Council Act, 1909 (Morley-Minto Reforms) - The legislative councils at the Centre and the provinces increased in size from 16 to 60 Members. Government of India Act, 1919 discarded the 'dyarchy' system at the provincial level and allowed for the emergence of popularly elected provincial legislatures. The Charter Act, 1813 - It ended the trade monopoly of the East India Company in India except for trade in tea and trade with China. It asserted the sovereignty of the British Crown over the Indian territories held by the Company.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":563,"n":589,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Under the Regulating Act of 1773, the King-in-Council created a Supreme Court in which of the following places?","o":{"a":"Madras","b":"Bombay","c":"Calcutta","d":"Delhi"},"a":"c","s":"Calcutta. Regulating Act 1773 - It was passed by the British Parliament to control the territories of the East India Company majorly in Bengal. The act provided for the appointment of a Governor-General along with four Councillors in the Presidency of Fort William (Calcutta), jointly called the Governor-General in Council. As per this, Warren Hastings was appointed as the Governor-General of the Presidency of Fort William (Bengal).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":564,"n":590,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which of the following terms indicates a system/arrangement implemented in Bengal by Lord Cornwallis, under which zamindars were granted legal ownership of land for receiving a certain revenue by a certain date?","o":{"a":"Talukdari system","b":"Rayatwari system","c":"Mahalwari system","d":"permanent settlement"},"a":"d","s":"Permanent settlement - It is also known as the Zamindari System. It was introduced by the East India Company headed by the Governor -General Lord Cornwallis in 1793, and implemented in Bengal, Bihar and Odisha. Mahalwari system - Holt Mackenzie (1822). Ryotwari system - Alexander Read and Thomas Munro (1820).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":565,"n":591,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The Arms Act was passed in _____ that prevented Indians from keeping arms without an appropriate license.","o":{"a":"1878","b":"1897","c":"1912","d":"1909"},"a":"a","s":"1878. The Arms Act was enacted to regulate the manufacture and sale of guns in the country. Only Europeans were allowed to carry arms without a licence. It was enacted during the tenure of Viceroy Lytton. Vernacular Press Act (1878) - Proposed by Lord Lytton and this act allowed the government to confiscate the assets of newspapers including their printing presses if the newspapers published anything that was found \"objectionable\".","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":566,"n":592,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In which year were the Arms Act and Vernacular Press Act passed?","o":{"a":"1878","b":"1879","c":"1877","d":"1876"},"a":"a","s":"1878. The Vernacular Press Act - The Act allowed the government to confiscate the assets of newspapers including their printing presses if the newspapers published anything that was found \"objectionable\". It was repealed by Lord Ripon in 1881. The Arms Act - It restricts Indian access to firearms by asking them to procure licenses whereas the British and Anglo-Indians are exempted from it. Vernacular Press Act and Arms Act, both enacted by Viceroy Lord Lytton .","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":567,"n":593,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"When was the Defence of India Act enacted?","o":{"a":"1920","b":"1918","c":"1915","d":"1921"},"a":"c","s":"1915. The Defence of India Act (1915) - It was an emergency law that gave the British government in India wide powers to suppress dissent and maintain public order during World War I. It was passed by the then Governor General of India Lord Hardinge.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":568,"n":594,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Under which Act was the rule of British Crown established over India?","o":{"a":"Government of India Act 1833","b":"Charter Act of 1813","c":"Regulating Act of 1773","d":"Government of India Act 1858"},"a":"d","s":"Government of India Act 1858. Provisions - East India Company was liquidated. The Court of Directors and the Board of Control were scrapped. The Viceroy was to be assisted with an Executive Council. Made India a direct British colony. Abolished the dual government of the Pitt's India Act. Ended the doctrine of lapse. First viceroy of India - Lord Canning","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":569,"n":595,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which Act declared that British rule in India would end by 30 June 1948?","o":{"a":"Act of 1858","b":"Act of 1947","c":"Act of 1892","d":"Act of 1961"},"a":"b","s":"Act of 1947. The Indian Independence Act 1947 was an Act of Parliament of the United Kingdom that granted independence to British India. The Act was passed on July 18, 1947, and came into effect on August 15, 1947. The Act divided British India into two independent dominions, India and Pakistan.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":570,"n":596,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In India which Act divided the powers between the Central and units in terms of three lists?","o":{"a":"1862","b":"1919","c":"1909","d":"1935"},"a":"d","s":"1935. Government of India Act, 1935 : Contain three lists, Federal List (for Centre containing 59 items), Provincial List (for provinces containing 54 items) and the Concurrent List (for both containing 36 items). The seventh schedule (Article 246) deals with the division of powers between the union and the states.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":571,"n":597,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"From which places did Lord Cornwallis start the permanent settlement system?","o":{"a":"Bengal and Bihar","b":"Madras and Bihar","c":"Bombay and Bihar","d":"Bengal and Madras"},"a":"a","s":"Bengal and Bihar. Provision of Permanent Settlement (Introduced in 1793 AD) - The owners of the land were known as landlords or Zamindars, Hereditary rights of succession of the lands under them were granted to them, The sum which the landlords had to pay was fixed, The Zamindars could sell or transfer the land as they wished. Other Settlements Related to Agriculture - Ryotwari System (Sir Thomas Munro, 1820), Mahalwari System (Holt Mackenzie, 1822).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":572,"n":598,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In which of the following years did the East India Company abolish the diarchy system of Bengal?","o":{"a":"1772","b":"1765","c":"1757","d":"1779"},"a":"a","s":"1772 . The system of Dual Government: Introduced in Bengal by Robert Clive of British East India Company. It lasted from 1765 to 1772. Under this system, the administration of Bengal was divided into Nizamat and Diwani. The Diwani was carried out by the company and the Nizamat by the Nizam.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":573,"n":599,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Ryotwari system of revenue collection in India, introduced by the British, was based on the_______.","o":{"a":"Smith's theory of rent","b":"Ricardian theory of rent","c":"Malthusian theory of rent","d":"Marx's theory of rent"},"a":"b","s":"Ricardian theory of rent - The land of higher fertility gets higher rent than less fertile land. Ryotwari System - The peasants or cultivators were regarded as the owners of the land. The cultivators had to pay annual taxes directly to the government. Introduced by Thomas Munro and Alexander Read in 1820 in the Madras and Bombay presidency. Mahalwari system - The land revenue was collected from the farmers by the village headmen on behalf of the whole village. Introduced by - Holt Mackenzie in 1822 in areas of Uttar Pradesh.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":574,"n":600,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"When was the Secretary of State for India made responsible for the Government of British India by bringing about changes in the Home Government?","o":{"a":"1858","b":"1833","c":"1857","d":"1813"},"a":"a","s":"1858. Charter Act 1858 - The term Governor General changed to Viceroy for the British crown. The first viceroy was Lord Canning. The act ended 'The Doctrine of Lapse'. It ended the Dual government scheme initiated due to Pitt's India Act. Charter Act of 1833 - It made the Governor-General of Bengal as the Governor-General of India. Lord William Bentinck - The first governor -general of India.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":575,"n":601,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The rule of the English East India company was ended in British India under the Government of India Act, _______.","o":{"a":"1858","b":"1857","c":"1859","d":"1856"},"a":"a","s":"1858. Government of India Act 1858 (Good governance Act) - India became a direct British colony through the passage of this act; A viceroy would be appointed who would serve as the representative of the British crown (First Viceroy - Lord Canning).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":576,"n":602,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The Cabinet Mission came to India in _________ to discuss the ultimate transfer of power.","o":{"a":"1946","b":"1945","c":"1943","d":"1942"},"a":"a","s":"1946. Cabinet Mission launched by the British government to discuss plans for the transfer of power from the British Raj to Indian leadership. Members: Lord Patrick Lawrence (Secretary of State for India), Stafford Cripps (President of the Board of Trade), A.V. Alexander (First Lord of the Admiralty).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":577,"n":603,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"With which of the following Government of India Acts did the Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms introduce Dyarchy in the provinces?","o":{"a":"Government of India Act, 1945","b":"Government of India Act, 1935","c":"Government of India Act, 1923","d":"Government of India Act, 1919"},"a":"d","s":"Government of India Act, 1919. Montague was the secretary of state for India and Chelmsford was the viceroy of India. This act introduced Dyarchy in provinces by dividing the provincial subjects into transferred and reserved, bicameralism. It provided for the establishment of a Public Service Commission which was established in 1926. Minto- Morley Reforms (Indian Council Act 1909).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 3rd shift"},{"id":578,"n":604,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"When was a comprehensive despatch on education called Wood's despatch on education sent?","o":{"a":"1833","b":"1854","c":"1858","d":"1813"},"a":"b","s":"1854. Charles Wood (the President of the Board of Control) sent a despatch to Lord Dalhousie (Governor-General of India). It suggested introducing vernacular languages in the primary schools of India, high schools use anglo-vernacular mediums and that English should be the medium for college-level education. Hence, the Wood's Despatch is considered as 'Magna-Carta' of English Education in India. Wood's despatch led to the establishment of the first three universities in 1857, at Calcutta, Bombay and Madras. Important Educational commissions during British rule in India - Hunter Commission (1882), Raleigh Commission (1902) , Sadler Commission (1917), Hartog Commission (1929).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":579,"n":605,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In 1942, the British government sent the___________mission to India.","o":{"a":"Cripps","b":"Linlithgow","c":"Cabinet","d":"Lansdowne"},"a":"a","s":"Cripps. It was sent to India to secure Indian cooperation for the British war efforts. It was headed by Sir Richard Stafford Cripps. Gandhi called it 'A post-dated cheque drawn on a failing bank'.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":580,"n":606,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The question consists of two statements, namely, Assertion (A) and Reason (R). Use them to choose the correct alternative. Assertion (A): During the British rule, India's exports exceeded the imports leading to surplus of balance of trade. Reason (R): The surplus of balance of trade was used for growth and development of India.","o":{"a":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","b":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","c":"A is false but R is true","d":"A is true and R is false"},"a":"d","s":"British Economic policies - Mercantile development (1757-1813) - Revenue collected from Bengal was used to finance exports to England. Industrial phase (1813-1858) - One-way trade flooded the Indian market with cheap, machine-made imports from newly industrialized Britain and Indian traders lost foreign markets. Financial phase (1860 onwards) - Converted India into a market for British manufacturers while still being a supplier of foodstuffs and raw materials.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":581,"n":607,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which among the following Acts introduced the Diarchy, i.e., rule of two which means executive councillors and popular ministers?","o":{"a":"Charter Act, 1813","b":"Government of India Act, 1858","c":"Montague-Chelmsford Reform (Government of India Act), 1919","d":"Indian Council Act, 1861"},"a":"c","s":"Montague - Chelmsford Reform (Government of India Act), 1919. Indian Council Act 1909 - It is also known as Minto Morley reforms. This act introduced communal representations for the Muslims.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":582,"n":608,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which among the following Acts established the dual system of control by the British government and the East India Company in India?","o":{"a":"Pitt's India Act, 1784","b":"Montague-Chelmsford Reform (Government of India Act), 1919","c":"Charter Act, 1793","d":"Charter Act, 1813"},"a":"a","s":"Pitt's India Act (1784) : passed in the British Parliament to rectify the defects of the Regulating Act 1773.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":583,"n":609,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The result of the English Education Act of 1835 was to make ________ as a medium of instruction for ___________ education.","o":{"a":"vernacular, higher","b":"English, primary","c":"English, higher","d":"vernacular, primary"},"a":"c","s":"English, Higher. English Education Act (1835) was proposed by William Bentinck on the Advice of Macaulay. Reforms by William Bentick : The abolition of Sati, the suppression of female infanticide and Thuggee, the abolition of lawlessness, and the abolition of human sacrifices.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":584,"n":610,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which among the following Acts discarded the 'dyarchy' system at the provincial level and allowed for the emergence of popularly elected provincial legislatures?","o":{"a":"Charter Act, 1793","b":"Montague-Chelmsford Reform (Government of India Act), 1919","c":"(Government of India Act), 1935","d":"Pitt's India Act, 1784"},"a":"c","s":"Government of India Act, 1935. The act proposed constitutional proposals and reforms and had many features which included the bicameral legislature, provincial autonomy, separate communal electorate and the creation of an all India federation.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":585,"n":611,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"As a result of which act did Queen Victoria, who was the monarch of Britain, also became the sovereign of British territories in India, with the title \"Empress of India\" in 1858?","o":{"a":"Indian Councils Act","b":"Government Of India Act","c":"Charter Act","d":"Regulating Act"},"a":"b","s":"Government Of India Act (1858): known as 'Act of Good governance'. Abolished the Dual government scheme initiated due to Pitt's India act. Ended controversial 'Doctrine of Lapse'. The powers of the Company's Court of Directors - transferred to the Secretary of State for India. The Viceroy and the governors of the various presidencies - appointed by the Crown.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":586,"n":612,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The Charter Act of 1813 extended the East India Company's rule in India to another _________ years.","o":{"a":"15","b":"5","c":"20","d":"10"},"a":"c","s":"20. The Charter Act of 1813 (The East Indian Company Act 1813) - Features: It ended the monopoly of the East India Company over commercial trade except trade with China and trade in tea with India. The company's dividend was fixed at 10.5% per annum. The Act gave more powers to the courts in India over European British subjects.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":587,"n":613,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which of the following reforms led to the enactment of the Government of India Act of 1919?","o":{"a":"Montagu- Chelmsford reforms","b":"Anti Slavery reforms","c":"Morley-Minto reforms","d":"Young Bengal movement"},"a":"a","s":"Montagu - Chelmsford reforms. The purpose of this act was to ensure the representation of Indians in the administration of their country. At that time, Montagu - Secretary of State for India, Lord Chelmsford - Viceroy of India.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":588,"n":614,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The objective of Simon Commission was to________.","o":{"a":"decide India's Political future","b":"decide India's Religious future","c":"decide India's Educational future","d":"decide India's economical future"},"a":"a","s":"Simon Commission - It was appointed by the British Government in 1927 under the chairmanship of John Simon (other members were Pathick Lawrence and Clement Atlee) to review the workings of Government of India (GOI) Act, 1919 and suggest measures.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":589,"n":615,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which act of the British Parliament was also known as East India Company Act 1793?","o":{"a":"The Government of India Act, 1793","b":"The Charter Act, 1793","c":"The Regulating Act, 1793","d":"The Indian Councils Act, 1793"},"a":"b","s":"The Charter Act, 1793. The act was passed by the British Parliament to renew the charter of the East India Company to trade within India for next 20 years. It was passed during the tenure of Sir John Shore (1793 -1798).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":590,"n":616,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"When was the Champaran Agrarian Law passed by the British Government?","o":{"a":"1918","b":"1919","c":"1917","d":"1916"},"a":"a","s":"1918. Mahatma Gandhi had launched his historic champaran movement in 1917 to abolish the forcible cultivation of indigo on the land of farmers. This bill was introduced by W. Maude . After the bill had passed the land revenue was reduced by 20-26%.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":591,"n":617,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which act was passed by the British Parliament to control the territories of the East India Company majorly in Bengal in the year 1773?","o":{"a":"Charter Act","b":"Regulating Act","c":"Indian Councils Act","d":"Government Of India Act"},"a":"b","s":"Regulating Act 1773 - This Act was passed by the British Parliament and Governor General Warren Hastings and brought forth the basic principles of central administration in India. Indian Councils Act 1861 - It changed the composition of the Governor General's Council for executive and legislative purposes.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":592,"n":618,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In __________, the British government enacted a legislation banning the use of printed cotton textiles - chintz - in England. Interestingly, this Act was known as the Calico Act.","o":{"a":"1720","b":"1700","c":"1740","d":"1730"},"a":"a","s":"1720. The cotton textiles which the Portuguese took back to Europe, along with the spices, came to be called \"calico\". Calico Act , Reason - Textile makers in England were Unable to compete with Indian textiles. Highest cotton producing state (Gujarat).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":593,"n":619,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In which year was a law passed in India to make conversion to Christianity easier?","o":{"a":"1845","b":"1850","c":"1830","d":"1840"},"a":"b","s":"1850. This law allowed Indians who had converted to Christianity to inherit the property of his ancestors. The Charter Act of 1813 allowed Chirstian missionaries to enter India and Preach Christianity. This Act asserted the Crown's sovereignty over British possessions in India.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":594,"n":620,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which act was passed by the British Government of India to introduce provincial autonomy?","o":{"a":"Indian Council Act of 1892","b":"Government of India Act of 1935","c":"Charter Act of 1853","d":"Indian Independence Act of 1947"},"a":"b","s":"Government of India Act 1935. It provided for the establishment of an All India Federation consisting of provinces and princely states as units. It provided for the establishment of a Reserve Bank of India to control the regulation of currency and credits of the country. It is also the largest source of the Indian Constitution which formed after independence.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":595,"n":621,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"_______ is an Act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom that partitioned British India into two new independent dominions of India and Pakistan in 1947.","o":{"a":"Rowlatt Act","b":"Indian Independence Act","c":"Government of India Act","d":"Pitt's India Act"},"a":"b","s":"Indian Independence Act The Indian Independence Act 1947 is an Act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom that partitioned British India into the two new independent dominions of India and Pakistan.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":596,"n":622,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The Pitts India Act of___________ distinguished between the commercial and political functions of the British East India Company.","o":{"a":"1818","b":"1792","c":"1784","d":"1802"},"a":"c","s":"Pitt's India Act, 1784 established the dual system of control by the British government and the East India Company. The Company became a subordinate department of the State and its territories in India were termed ' British possessions '. In this act members of the Executive Council were reduced to 3, and the Governor-General served as its commander in chief.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":597,"n":623,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which act allows the government to confiscate the assets of newspaper in case of publishing anything \"objectionable\" ?","o":{"a":"Ilbert Act, 1883","b":"Vernacular Press Act, 1878","c":"Media Act, 1883","d":"Newspaper Act, 1855"},"a":"b","s":"Vernacular Press Act, 1878 Proposed by Lord Lytton (1876-80). Lord Ripon (1880-84) repealed the Vernacular Act in 1881. Samachar Darpan (1818) was the first vernacular newspaper of India published by Carey and Marshman from Serampore during the period of Lord Hastings.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":598,"n":624,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The bill which provide for the trial of British or European person by Indians magistrates was_________","o":{"a":"Hastings Bill","b":"Dalhousie Bill","c":"Ilbert Bill","d":"George Bill"},"a":"c","s":"Ilbert Bill was introduced in 1883 by Lord Ripon. It allowed senior Indian magistrates to preside over cases involving British subjects in India. The bill was enacted by the Indian Legislative Council on Jan. 25, 1884. It was written by Sir Courtenay Pergine Ilbert.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":599,"n":625,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which of the following Indian states was the first to sign a subsidiary alliance with the British?","o":{"a":"Hyderabad","b":"Cochin","c":"Travancore","d":"Maratha"},"a":"a","s":"Hyderabad. In this system, an Indian ruler had to maintain British troops in his state, either by giving some of his territories or by paying for the maintenance of the troops. It was first introduced by the French East India Company Governor Joseph Francois Dupleix, Later used by Lord Wellesley, Governor-General of India (1798 to 1805).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":600,"n":626,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"What is the original name of the Empress of India Act and when was it instituted? Higher Secondary 04/08/2022 (Shift - 3 )","o":{"a":"The Royalty Act of 1876","b":"The Royal Titles Act of 1876","c":"The Royal Titles Act of 1877","d":"The Titles Act of 1875"},"a":"b","s":"The Royal Titles Act 1876 was an Act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom which officially recognized Queen Victoria (and subsequent monarchs) as \"Empress of India\" . This act was introduced by Benjamin Disraeli. It was repealed on 22 June 1948.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":601,"n":627,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which of the following Acts of 1947 regulates the Indian labor law with regard to trade unions as well as individual workmen?","o":{"a":"Indian Councils Act","b":"Charter Act","c":"Rowlatt Act","d":"Industrial Disputes Act"},"a":"d","s":"Industrial disputes Act 1947 The Rowlatt Act 1919 authorized the arrest, for 2 years without trial, of any person suspected of terrorism living in British India. Indian Council acts (1861, 1892, and 1909). Charter Acts (1793, 1813, 1833, and 1853).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":602,"n":628,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In which year was the Child Marriage Restraint Act passed in India?","o":{"a":"1929","b":"1931","c":"1935","d":"1933"},"a":"a","s":"On 28 September 1929 , in the Imperial Legislative Council of India, fixed the age of marriage for girls at 14 years and boys at 18 years. In 1949, after India's independence, it was amended to 15 for girls, and in 1978 to 18 for girls and 21 for boys.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":603,"n":629,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which Act passed by British Government of India abolished the Council of India?","o":{"a":"Indian Independence Act of 1947","b":"India Council Act of 1892","c":"Government of India Act of 1935","d":"Charter Act of 1853"},"a":"c","s":"Government of India Act 1935 were as follows: Abolition of provincial dyarchy and introduction of dyarchy at centre. Abolition of Indian Council and introduction of an advisory body in its place. Provision for an All India Federation with British India territories and princely states.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":604,"n":630,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which Act was passed in 1929 prohibiting marriage of girls below 14 and boys below 18 years of age?","o":{"a":"Pitt's India Act","b":"Sharda Act","c":"Vernacular Press Act","d":"Rowlatt Act"},"a":"b","s":"Sharda act . The act fixed the marriageable age for girls at 14 years and 18 years for boys. It was sponsor by Harbilas Sarda. Pitt's India act (1784), Rowlatt Act (1919), Vernacular press act (1878).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":605,"n":631,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In which year through the Indian Official Secrets Act was freedom of press curbed?","o":{"a":"1919","b":"1921","c":"1904","d":"1909"},"a":"c","s":"1904. It was enacted during the time of Lord Curzon, Viceroy of India from 1899 to 1905 curbed the freedom of Press. Later this act was replaced by the Official Secrets act 1923.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":606,"n":632,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In 1877, which of the following viceroys organized a durbar to acknowledge Queen Victoria as the Empress of India?","o":{"a":"Lord Lytton","b":"Lord Ripon","c":"Lord Minto","d":"Lord Dufferin"},"a":"a","s":"Lord Lytton organized a durbar to acknowledge Queen Victoria as the Empress of India. This was the culmination of the transfer of control of British India from the East India Company to the Crown.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":607,"n":633,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"The capital of the British Indian Empire was transferred from Calcutta to Delhi in:","o":{"a":"1911","b":"1905","c":"1907","d":"1909"},"a":"a","s":"1911 . The British government believed that ruling India from Delhi was easier and more convenient than from Calcutta. Therefore, Delhi was officially announced as the capital of British Raj by Emperor George V, on December 12, 1911.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":608,"n":634,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"By which British statute were Indian representatives, for the first time, allocated seats for election in Indian Legislative Councils?","o":{"a":"Government of India Act, 1833","b":"Indian Councils Act, 1892","c":"Government of India Act, 1915","d":"Indian Councils Act, 1861"},"a":"b","s":"Indian Councils Act, 1892 The act made a limited and indirect provision for the use of election in filling up some of the nonofficial seats both in the Central and provincial legislative councils.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":609,"n":635,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which law was imposed in India during the command of General Dyer?","o":{"a":"Preventive detention","b":"Discrimination law","c":"Simon law","d":"Martial law"},"a":"d","s":"Martial law . General Dyer enacted an Act on April 13, 1919, called martial law stating that no more than 2 people can form a group and meet at a place.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":610,"n":636,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In order to curb the East India Company traders' unrestrained commercial activities and to bring about some order in territories under the Company control, the British Parliament enacted a series of laws among which the ______ stood first.","o":{"a":"Charter Act of 1813","b":"Government of India Act 1833","c":"Regulating Act of 1780","d":"Regulating Act of 1773"},"a":"d","s":"Regulating Act of 1773. It provided for the establishment of the Supreme Court at Calcutta (1774) and changed the post of Governor of Bengal to \"Governor-General of Bengal\". Warren Hastings was made the first Governor -General of Bengal.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":611,"n":637,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"In which year was the office of the first Accountant General established in India?","o":{"a":"1858","b":"1845","c":"1890","d":"1867"},"a":"a","s":"In 1858 (the year the British took over administrative control of India from the East India Company) the office of the first Accountant General was established in India. In 1860 Sir Edward Drummond was appointed as the first Auditor-General.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":612,"n":638,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Which Committee among the following was formed in Bombay in March 1919?","o":{"a":"Hunter Committee","b":"Khilafat Committee","c":"Sargent Committee","d":"Sapru Committee"},"a":"b","s":"Khilafat Committee . Formed to defend the powers of the Ottoman Caliph which was restricted by the Treaty of Sevres after World War I. Leaders - Mohammed Ali, Shaukat Ali, Maulana Azad, Hakim Ajmal Khan and Hasrat Mohani.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":613,"n":639,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Who was the Prime Minister of Britain/UK when the British Parliament passed an independence bill for India in July 1947 ?","o":{"a":"Clement Attlee","b":"Neville Chamberlain","c":"Ramsay Macdonald","d":"Winston Churchill"},"a":"a","s":"Clement Attlee","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":614,"n":640,"sec":"Modern History","st":"British Acts and Policies","q":"Who among the following was NOT a minister of Cabinet Mission Plan, 1946 ?","o":{"a":"Sir Stafford Cripps","b":"Lord Wavell","c":"Sir Pethick Lawrence","d":"Alexander"},"a":"b","s":"Lord Wavell . The members of the Cabinet Mission were Lord Penthick Lawrence, Secretary of State for India, Sir Stafford Cripps, President of the Board of Trade, and A.V Alexander, First Lord of Admiralty.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":615,"n":641,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Swadeshi Movements","q":"What was the Swadeshi Movement called in the state of Andhra Pradesh?","o":{"a":"Gadar Movement","b":"Quit India Movement","c":"Khilafat Movement","d":"Vande Mataram Movement"},"a":"d","s":"Vande Mataram Movement The Swadeshi Movement started in August 1905 from Calcutta Town Hall against Lord Curzon's decision of dividing the province of Bengal. The partition led to protest meetings in Bengal under which the pledge to boycott foreign goods was first taken. Leaders of the movement - Poona and Bombay (Lokmanya Tilak), Punjab (Lala Lajpat Rai and Ajit Singh), Delhi (Syed Haider Raza), Madras (Chidambaram Pillai).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":616,"n":642,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Swadeshi Movements","q":"Lord Curzon declared the partition of _________ in 1905.","o":{"a":"Central Provinces","b":"Punjab","c":"United Provinces","d":"Bengal"},"a":"d","s":"Bengal. Partition of Bengal: Announcement - by Viceroy Curzon. The Swadeshi Movement was declared on August 7, 1905 in Calcutta Town Hall, Bengal. 16 October was celebrated as 'National Mourning Day' throughout Bengal. It was cancelled by Lord Hardinge in 1911.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":617,"n":643,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Swadeshi Movements","q":"What is the official reason given by Britishers after partition of Bengal in 1905?","o":{"a":"Easy Movement of army","b":"To check on Extremist activities","c":"Economic Progress","d":"Administrative Convenience"},"a":"d","s":"Administrative Convenience The real reason for the partition was to divide and weaken the growing nationalist movement in Bengal. After Partition Swadeshi movement gained even more momentum, and the British were forced to revoke the partition in 1911.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":618,"n":644,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Swadeshi Movements","q":"Before the Partition of Bengal in 1905, Bengal was the biggest province of British India. It consisted of parts of which of the following states?","o":{"a":"Uttar Pradesh and Orissa","b":"Uttar Pradesh and Bihar","c":"Bihar and Orissa","d":"Bihar and Chhattisgarh"},"a":"c","s":"Bihar and Orissa. Partition of Bengal in 1905 {Lord Curzon announced the partition of Bengal on 20th July 1905, reorganization separated the largely Muslim eastern areas from the largely Hindu western areas}.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":619,"n":645,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Swadeshi Movements","q":"At which of the following places was the formal proclamation of the Swadeshi movement made on 7 August 1905?","o":{"a":"Wellington Square, Calcutta","b":"Calcutta Town Hall","c":"Kanai Dhar Lane, Calcutta","d":"Bengal National College, Calcutta"},"a":"b","s":"Calcutta Town Hall. The Swadeshi movement was a direct fallout of the decision of the British India government to partition Bengal. The features of the Swadeshi movement : encourage the use of indigenous goods, boycott foreign goods. The partition of Bengal was annulled in 1911 by Lord Hardinge to curb the revolutionaries.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":620,"n":646,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Swadeshi Movements","q":"Which of the following parties supported the move for partition of Bengal?","o":{"a":"Gadar Party","b":"Forward Bloc","c":"All India Muslim League","d":"Communist Party of India"},"a":"c","s":"All India Muslim League supported the move for partition of Bengal. Founders of All India Muslim League are Muhammad Ali Jinnah, Aga Khan III, Khwaja Salimullah, Hakim Ajmal Khan, Waqar-ul-Mulk. It was founded on 30 December 1906.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":621,"n":647,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Swadeshi Movements","q":"Moved by the Swadeshi Movement, ______ painted his famous image of Bharat Mata portraying her as an ascetic figure.","o":{"a":"Satyendranath Tagore","b":"Abanindranath Tagore","c":"Jyotirindranath Tagore","d":"Dwijendranath Tagore"},"a":"b","s":"Abanindranath Tagore painted his famous image of Bharat Mata portraying her as an ascetic figure in 1905. The painting is depicted as a saffron-clad divine woman, holding a book, sheaves of paddy, a piece of white cloth, and a rosary in her four hands.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":622,"n":648,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Salt Satyagraha was associated with the:","o":{"a":"Civil Disobedience Movement","b":"Quit India Movement","c":"Champaran Satyagraha","d":"Kheda Movement"},"a":"a","s":"Civil Disobedience Movement (1930) - Salt Satyagraha (12 March 1930 - 6 April 1930) - Started from Sabarmati Ashram to Dandi Beach. A distance of about 375 km was covered for breaking the salt law.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":623,"n":649,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Vallabhbhai Patel and ________ helped Gandhi in Kheda by organising his tour of the villages and urging the peasants to stand firm against the government.","o":{"a":"Motilal Nehru","b":"Mohammad Ali Jinnah","c":"Indulal Yajnik","d":"Jamunalal Bajaj"},"a":"c","s":"Indulal Yajnik. Mahatma Gandhi returned to India on 9 January 1915.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":624,"n":650,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following designed a campaign that came to be known as the 'Rowlatt Satyagraha'?","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Feroze Shah Mehta","c":"Mahadev Govind Ranade","d":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak"},"a":"a","s":"Mahatma Gandhi. Rowlatt Satyagraha (1919): A nationwide satyagraha against the proposed Rowlatt Act (1919). Rowlatt Act was based on the Sedition Committee. It gave the government enormous powers to repress political activities, and allowed detention of political prisoners without trial for two years. In Rowlatt Satyagraha Gandhiji tried to utilize the home rule league.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":625,"n":651,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"On whose advice did Gandhi spend a year traveling around British India to know the people of India?","o":{"a":"Krishna Shankar Pandya","b":"Mohammad Ali Jinnah","c":"Shrimad Rajchandra","d":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale"},"a":"d","s":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale: He was a senior leader of the Indian National Congress and the founder of the Servants of India Society (1905).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":626,"n":652,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Which of the following events occurred in 1919?","o":{"a":"Renewal of Charter Act","b":"Representation of the People Act was passed","c":"Suppression of Fraud and Dacoity Act passed","d":"Satyagraha against Rowlatt Act"},"a":"d","s":"Satyagraha against Rowlatt Act. Events in 1919: Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13 April), Start of Khilafat movement in India. Other events and their years: Charter Acts - 1793, 1813, 1833, 1853. The Representation of the People Act - 1950. Thuggee and Dacoity Suppression Act - 1836 .","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":627,"n":653,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In which year was Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi born?","o":{"a":"1659","b":"1869","c":"1889","d":"1879"},"a":"b","s":"1869. Mahatma Gandhi: Born on 2nd October (observed as International Day of Non-violence). He came back to India on 9th January (celebrated as 'NRI day' or 'Pravasi Bhartiya Divas' since 2003) 1915 from South Africa. Some Satyagraha movements by Gandhiji : Champaran satyagraha (1917), Kheda satyagraha (1918), Ahmedabad Mill Strike (1918).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":628,"n":654,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who was the founder of Harijan Sevak Sangh founded in 1932?","o":{"a":"Jawaharlal Nehru","b":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Jyotiba Phule","d":"BR Ambedkar"},"a":"b","s":"Mahatma Gandhi Organisation Founded by MK Gandhi: Majdoor Mahajan (1916) in Ahmedabad. It was related to the labour movement or textile movement in Gujarat. All-India Spinners Association (1925), All-India Village Industries Association (1934).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":629,"n":655,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following leaders led the Bardoli Satyagraha?","o":{"a":"Lala Lajpat Rai","b":"Motilal Nehru","c":"C Rajagopalachari","d":"Vallabhbhai Patel"},"a":"d","s":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel (The Iron Man of India) - First Home Minister and Deputy Prime Minister of India. Bardoli satyagraha (1928) - It was against the increased taxation of farmers by the colonial government.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":630,"n":656,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"The famous scientist, Albert Einstein was inspired by whose idea on non-violence?","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Vardhaman Mahavira","c":"Gautam Buddha","d":"B.R. Ambedkar"},"a":"a","s":"Mahatma Gandhi. His Ideas - Truth and nonviolence, Satyagraha, Sarvodaya (Progress of All), Swaraj (self-rule), Trusteeship (Wealthy people to look after the welfare of the people).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":631,"n":657,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"'Hind Swaraj' or 'Indian Home Rule' is written by which of the following freedom fighters?","o":{"a":"Dadabhai Naoroji","b":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak","d":"Annie Besant"},"a":"b","s":"Mahatma Gandhi. In \"Hind Swaraj\" he expressed his views on Swaraj, modern civilization, mechanisation, among other matters. Books: \"My experiment with truth\", \"The Words of Gandhi\", \"Non-Violent Resistance\". Dadabhai Naoroji: \"Poverty and Un-British Rule in India\" . Bal Gangadhar Tilak: \"Geeta Rahasya\", \"The Arctic Home in the Vedas\". Annie Besant: \"Thought Forms\", \"Occult Chemistry\".","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":632,"n":658,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following signed the Poona pact with Mahatma Gandhi?","o":{"a":"Ali Brothers","b":"Chittaranjan Das","c":"Jyotiba Phule","d":"Bhimrao Ambedkar"},"a":"d","s":"Bhimrao Ambedkar. Poona Pact (24 september 1932) was signed in Yerwada central Jail in Pune during the time of Lord Willington. It provides the depressed classes with the reservation of the electoral seats in British India Parliament.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":633,"n":659,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following gave the famous 'Do or Die' slogan during Quit India Movement?","o":{"a":"Abul Kalam Azad","b":"Jawaharlal Nehru","c":"Subhash Chandra Bose","d":"Mahatma Gandhi"},"a":"d","s":"Mahatma Gandhi. Other slogans of famous Indian leaders : Jawaharlal Nehru ('Aaram haraam hai'), Bhagat Singh ('Inquilab Zindabad'), Lal Bahadur Shastri ('Jai Jawan, Jai Kisaan'), Subhas Chandra Bose ('Give me blood and I will give you freedom'), Bal Gangadhar Tilak ('Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it'), Lala Lajpat Rai ('Simon, Go Back').","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":634,"n":660,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Primarily under whose persuasion did Mahatma Gandhi visit Champaran to understand the problems of peasants?","o":{"a":"Jaiprakash Narayan","b":"Maulana Mazharul Haque","c":"Vallabhbhai Patel","d":"Rajkumar Shukla"},"a":"d","s":"Rajkumar Shukla Champaran Satyagrah (1917) : Gandhiji was requested to look into the problems of the farmers in the context of indigo planters of Champaran in Bihar. The European planters were forcing the peasants to grow indigo on 3/20 part of the total land (called the Tinkathia system).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":635,"n":661,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following returned his/her medal of Kaisar-i-Hind in 1920 ? Higher Secondary 27/06/2023 ( Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"S Subramania iyer","b":"Sarojini Naidu","c":"Rabindranath Tagore","d":"Mahatma Gandhi"},"a":"d","s":"Mahatma Gandhi Kaiser - e - Hind title was given to Mahatma Gandhi by Governor-General of India Lord Hardinge in 1915 for his services during the Boer War (1899 - 1902). Gandhi returned his title in response to the Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13th April 1919). Rabindranath Tagore returned his Knighthood title in response to the massacre.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":636,"n":662,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In 1895, Mahatma Gandhi, along with other Indians has established _____ to fight against racial discrimination.","o":{"a":"Dev Samaj","b":"Brahmo Samaj","c":"Natal Congress","d":"Sabarmati Ashram"},"a":"c","s":"Natal Congress :- A political organisation founded in the South Africa province of Natal, then ruled by Britishers. It later served an important role in opposing apartheid.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":637,"n":663,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In 1918, Mahatma Gandhi successfully led a strike of ________.","o":{"a":"Army Soldiers","b":"Dalits","c":"Mill workers","d":"Traders"},"a":"c","s":"Mill workers. Ahmedabad Mill Strike (1918) - This is considered the first hunger strike led by Gandhi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":638,"n":664,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In which Indian movement, were the issues faced by indigo planters, first brought to light in 1917?","o":{"a":"Champaran Satyagraha","b":"Kheda Satyagraha","c":"Ahmedabad Mill Strike","d":"Non-Cooperation Movement"},"a":"a","s":"Champaran Satyagraha (1917). The first satyagraha movement led by Mahatma Gandhi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":639,"n":665,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Mahatma Gandhi called off the Non-cooperation Movement in February 1922 after which incident?","o":{"a":"Bomb Blast in central assembly","b":"Jallianwala Bagh Massacre","c":"Kakori train robbery","d":"Chauri Chaura Incident"},"a":"d","s":"Chauri Chaura Incident. On 4th February 1922, an angry mob set fire to the police station at Chauri Chaura. Gandhi called off the movement on 12th February 1922.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":640,"n":666,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In Kheda, Gujarat, Patidar peasants organised non-violent campaigns against which of the following demand of the British?","o":{"a":"Low labour rates","b":"Long working hours","c":"High land revenue","d":"Low price for crops"},"a":"c","s":"High land revenue. Kheda Satyagraha of 1918 was a satyagraha movement in the Kheda district of Gujarat in India organized by Mahatma Gandhi during the period of the British Raj. The peasant-Patidar community of Kheda, who refused to accept a 23 percent tax increase imposed on them despite a devastating crop failure and an outbreak of plague and cholera, served as the focal point of this satyagraha.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":641,"n":667,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"When did depressed classes get the reserved seats in provincial and central legislative councils, but they were to be voted in by the General electorate?","o":{"a":"September 1930","b":"November 1932","c":"November 1930","d":"September 1932"},"a":"d","s":"September 1932. Poona Pact - Agreement between Mahatma Gandhi and Dr. B. R. Ambedkar - Seats reserved for the depressed classes were increased from 71 to 147 in provincial legislatures and 18 percent of the total in the Central Legislature. Dr. B.R. Ambedkar - Established All India Depressed Classes Association in 1930 - Rao Bahadur M. C. Rajah (First President). All India Depressed Classes League (1935) - Jagjivan Ram.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":642,"n":668,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"When was the Champaran Agrarian Act passed?","o":{"a":"1920","b":"1922","c":"1918","d":"1924"},"a":"c","s":"1918. Champaran Agrarian bill was introduced by W. Maude in the Legislative Council on 29th November 1917. Mahatma Gandhi was convinced by Raj Kumar Shukla to visit Champaran in 1917 to abolish the forcible cultivation of Indigo.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":643,"n":669,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Gandhiji, aged ______, arrived in India in 1915 from South Africa.","o":{"a":"52","b":"46","c":"28","d":"34"},"a":"b","s":"46. Gandhiji (born on 2nd October 1869 in Porbandar, Gujarat) went to South Africa in 1893 as a lawyer, settling first in Durban before moving to Johannesburg. In South Africa (1893 -1915), he had successfully fought the racist regime with a novel method of mass agitation, which he called satyagraha.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":644,"n":670,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Which of the following Indian freedom fighter said that \"Be the change you wish to see in the world\" ?","o":{"a":"Bipin Chandra Pal","b":"Mohammad Ali Jinnah","c":"Surendranath Banerjee","d":"Mahatma Gandhi"},"a":"d","s":"Mahatma Gandhi. His other Famous Quotes: \"A man is but the product of his thoughts. What he thinks, he becomes.\", \"Nobody can hurt me without my permission.\"","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":645,"n":671,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In the ____________, Sarojini Naidu persuaded Mahatma Gandhi to allow women to join the movement.","o":{"a":"Quit India Movement","b":"Champaran Satyagraha","c":"Salt Satyagraha","d":"Non-Cooperation Movement"},"a":"c","s":"Salt Satyagraha. It was a mass civil disobedience movement initiated by Mahatma Gandhi against the salt tax imposed by the British government in India. He led a large group of people from Sabarmati Ashram on 12th March 1930 till Dandi, a coastal village in Gujarat, to break the salt law by producing salt from seawater.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":646,"n":672,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In which of the following years, did Subhas Chandra Bose refer to Mahatma Gandhi as the \"Father of the Nation\"?","o":{"a":"1942","b":"1944","c":"1939","d":"1941"},"a":"b","s":"1944. Subhas Chandra Bose called Mahatma Gandhi the 'Father of the Nation' in a radio address from Singapore on 6 July 1944.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":647,"n":673,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"The All-India Khilafat Conference in November 1919 took place at:","o":{"a":"Lucknow","b":"Delhi","c":"Bombay","d":"Lahore"},"a":"b","s":"Delhi. In early 1919, the All India Khilafat Committee was formed under the leadership of the Ali brothers (Maulana Muhammad Ali and Shaukat Ali), Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, Ajmal Khan and Hasrat Mohani, to force the British Government to change its attitude to Turkey.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":648,"n":674,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following called Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose a patriot of patriots? Graduate Level 03/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Jawaharlal Nehru","b":"Chittaranjan Das","c":"Mahatma Gandhi","d":"Pattabhi Sitaramayya"},"a":"c","s":"Mahatma Gandhi Rabindranath Tagore called Subhash Chandra Bose a 'Desh Nayak'. The famous slogan given by Subhas Chandra Bose: \"Dilli Chalo, Jai Hind\", \"Give me blood, I will give freedom\". Subhas Chandra Bose Autobiography: \"The Indian Struggle\".","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":649,"n":675,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following persuaded Mahatma Gandhi to allow women to join the Salt Satyagraha movement?","o":{"a":"Kamaladevi Chattopadhyay","b":"Annie Besant","c":"Ashalata Sen","d":"Ambabai"},"a":"a","s":"Kamaladevi Chattopadhyay The Salt Satyagraha was a mass civil disobedience movement initiated by Mahatma Gandhi against the salt tax imposed by the British government in India. It was started by Sabarmati Ashram on 12th March 1930 and was ended by Gandhi- Irwin pact in 1931.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":650,"n":676,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Which of the following events occurred as an after-effect of the Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13th April 1919) ?","o":{"a":"Hunger strike to settle disputes between the mill owners and the workers","b":"Individual princes could sign a separate agreement with the British","c":"Hunter Commission was appointed by the British Government","d":"Popularisation of Charkha and Khadi and Jail Bharo by Congress volunteers."},"a":"c","s":"Hunter Commission was appointed by the British Government. The Hunter Commission came to India in 1919 under the leadership of William Hunter to investigate the Jallianwala Bagh massacre. 1600 Indian civilians were killed in the Jallianwala Bagh massacre, for which General Dyer was responsible. Another Hunter Commission was on Indian Education, prepared in 1882 by William Wilson Hunter.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":651,"n":677,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following was a leader of the Khilafat Movement?","o":{"a":"Bhagat Singh","b":"Kasturba Gandhi","c":"Maulana Abul Kalam Azad","d":"Jyotiba Phule"},"a":"c","s":"Abul Kalam Azad. Its aim was to put pressure on the British government to keep the Ottoman Sultan's rule as Caliph of Islam after the Ottoman Empire disintegrated at the end of the war.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":652,"n":678,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In which year did Mahatma Gandhi first introduce a nationwide satyagraha in India?","o":{"a":"1915","b":"1921","c":"1919","d":"1916"},"a":"c","s":"1919. The first nationwide Satyagraha was launched in 1919 by Mahatma Gandhi and other leaders to end the Rowlatt Act passed by the Imperial Legislative Council, which gave them the power to arrest any person without trial.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":653,"n":679,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Which of the following is NOT a reason for slowing down the Non-cooperation Movement?","o":{"a":"Due to the absence of alternative Indian institution, boycott of British Institutions posed a problem.","b":"Leaders of the movement shifted their focus towards domestic work thus created a void.","c":"Many lawyers resigned but then absence of income for a longer period of time compelled them to rejoin.","d":"Khadi cloth was often more expensive than mass-produced mill cloth and poor people could not afford to buy it."},"a":"b","s":"Mahatma Gandhi started the Non- Cooperation Movement on 4 September 1920. Lord Chelmsford was the viceroy of British India during the Non-Cooperation Movement. The Chauri -Chaura incident was the driver of subsequent withdrawal of the Non -Cooperation Movement. Bhagwan Ahir was the pioneer of the Chauri Chaura incident. Singaravelu Chettiar saw the non-cooperation movement as capitalist autocracy.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":654,"n":680,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"In which of the following years was the civil disobedience campaign completely Ceased?","o":{"a":"1917","b":"1934","c":"1923","d":"1943"},"a":"b","s":"1934 . It began with the famous Dandi March of Gandhiji, on 12 March 1930.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":655,"n":681,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following freedom fighters hoisted the Indian flag at the Gowalia Tank Maidan in Mumbai during the Quit India Movement?","o":{"a":"Aruna Asaf Ali","b":"Sarojini Naidu","c":"Tara Rani Srivastava","d":"Matangini Hazra"},"a":"a","s":"Aruna Asaf Ali . Aruna Asaf Ali popularly known as the 'Grand Old Lady' of the Independence Movement.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":656,"n":682,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following was the only martyr of the Quit India phase of the freedom movement who was hanged?","o":{"a":"Potti Sreeramulu","b":"Senapati Bapat","c":"Kushal Konwar","d":"Kanaiyalal Maneklal Munshi"},"a":"c","s":"Kushal Konwar Facts of Quit India Movement - The Quit India Resolution was passed by the Congress Working Committee on 8 August 1942 in Bombay. 'Quit India' or 'Bharat Chodo 'slogan was given in this movement . Gandhiji gave the slogan to the people - 'Do or die'; It is also known as the India August Movement or August Kranti.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":657,"n":683,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Which Newspaper was founded by Mahatma Gandhi in South Africa in 1903?","o":{"a":"Young India","b":"Indian Opinion","c":"Swaraj Hind","d":"Navajivan"},"a":"b","s":"Indian Opinion The Indian Opinion was an important newspaper established in 1903 in Durban, South Africa, by Mahatma Gandhi and M. H. Nazar. It was a primary communication organ of the Natal Indian Congress.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":658,"n":684,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"Who among the following was one of the leaders who was instrumental in convincing Mahatma Gandhi about the need to start a non-cooperation movement in support of The Khilafat?","o":{"a":"Shaukat Ali","b":"Maulana Abul Kalam Azad","c":"Abdul Ghaffar Khan","d":"Mohammad Ali Jinnah"},"a":"a","s":"Shaukat Ali","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":659,"n":685,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"C Rajagopalachari led the Salt Satyagraha in which of the following states ?","o":{"a":"Gujarat","b":"Maharashtra","c":"Tamil Nadu","d":"Rajasthan"},"a":"c","s":"Tamil Nadu Salt March (Dandi Satyagraha, 12th March 1930 to 6th April, 1930 ) was an act of nonviolent civil disobedience led by Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi. Other leaders - K. Kelappan (Malabar), Gopabandhu Chaudhary (Orissa).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":660,"n":686,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Gandhian Era","q":"_ _____ was a mentor to both Mahatma Gandhi and Mohammed Ali Jinnah.","o":{"a":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","b":"Rabindranath Tagore","c":"Lala Lajpat Rai","d":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak"},"a":"a","s":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":661,"n":687,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"Where was the first jute mill set up in India?","o":{"a":"Mumbai","b":"Rishra","c":"Chennai","d":"Haryana"},"a":"b","s":"Rishra ( West Bengal, 1855 First Jute mill (Acland Mill) - Established by British entrepreneur George Acland and Bengali financier Babu By Sumber Sen in Bengal Presidency. Central Research Institute for Jute and Allied Fibers is located in West Bengal. National jute Board formed by National Jute board Act, 2008 under the Ministry of Textile. In 1854, the first modern cotton mill was established in Mumbai by Cowaszee Nanabhoy Davar.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":662,"n":688,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"Which of the following modern industries were in operation in India during the British Rule ? a. Cotton b. Jute c. Iron and Steel","o":{"a":"All of a, b and c","b":"Both a and b","c":"Only a","d":"Only c"},"a":"a","s":"All of a, b and c. British rulers never permitted modernization nor did they encourage the growth of industries. The unbalanced and lopsided growth structures were a legacy of British rule in India. The first iron and steel mill was established in 1907, in Jamshedpur by Tata Iron and Steel Company.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":663,"n":689,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"In the 18 th century, the development of cotton industries in Britain resulted in _________ .","o":{"a":"Increase in demand of Indian textile in Britain","b":"Decline of textiles production in India","c":"Increase in demand of Indian textile in India","d":"Decline of British textiles industries in India"},"a":"b","s":"Reason for decline: Export of textiles to England became more and more difficult because the British Government imposed high duties on Indian textiles. Thorp Mill (1764) - The first water-powered cotton mill in the world was constructed at Royton, Lancashire (England). The first cotton textile mill (India) - Established at Fort Glastor near Kolkata (1818).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":664,"n":690,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"In 1600, the East India company acquired a charter from_________.","o":{"a":"Queen Elizabeth I","b":"King Edward VI","c":"King Henry IV","d":"Queen Victoria"},"a":"a","s":"Queen Elizabeth I. By the Charter (1600 A.D), they got the sole right to trade with the East, without competition from other British traders. The Company's ships first arrived in India, at the port of Surat, in 1608.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":665,"n":691,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"The first English factory was set up on the banks of the river _______ in 1651.","o":{"a":"Son","b":"Yamuna","c":"Ganga","d":"Hugli"},"a":"d","s":"Hugli. First English factory (Temporary) in India - Masulipattam (1611). First English factory (Permanent) in India - Surat (1613). First factory of other European Companies in India: Dutch - Masulipatnam (Andhra Pradesh) in 1605, Portuguese - Calicut (Kerala) in 1500, French - Surat (Gujarat) in 1668.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":666,"n":692,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"Which of the following pairs is correct regarding the East India Company army? I. Sawar - Men on horses Il. Musket - A heavy gun used by infantry soldiers","o":{"a":"Only l","b":"Only ll","c":"Both l and lI","d":"Neither I nor Il"},"a":"c","s":"Both l and II","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":667,"n":693,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"The British East India Company captured Pondicherry (Puducherry) from the French in the year ______.","o":{"a":"1761","b":"1699","c":"1674","d":"1738"},"a":"a","s":"1761 . Pondicherry got its freedom from the French on 1st November 1954. The Union Territory of Puducherry comprises the erstwhile French colonies viz. Puducherry, Karaikal, Mahe and Yanam.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":668,"n":694,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"William Hawkins met Emperor Jahangir as a representative of the ______ East India Company.","o":{"a":"Portuguese","b":"Dutch","c":"French","d":"English"},"a":"d","s":"English. East India Company founded on 31st December 1600. The Dutch established their first factory in Masulipatnam in 1605. Vasco da Gama, discoverer of the sea route to India (1498), established the first Portuguese factory (trading station). The first French factory was founded by Francois Caron in 1668 in Surat.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":669,"n":695,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Expansion of British Rule","q":"Which monument was built in 1924 to welcome King George V and Queen Mary?","o":{"a":"Gateway of India","b":"Bombay Castle","c":"Victoria Memorial","d":"Chattri Brighton"},"a":"a","s":"Gateway of India . The Gateway of India is an Arch-Monument built in the early 20th century in the city of Mumbai.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":670,"n":696,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"The 'Servants of India Society' was the brainchild of which of the following leaders ?","o":{"a":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","b":"BR Ambedkar","c":"Lala Lajpat Rai","d":"Chittaranjan Das"},"a":"a","s":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale founded - Ranade Institute of Economics (1908). He started an English weekly newspaper, The Hitavada (The people's paper). Servants of India Society was founded in 1905 . Chitranjan Das is popularly known as Deshbandhu. B.R. Ambedkar - All India Scheduled Caste Federation and the Independent Labour Party. Lala Lajpat Rai - Punjab National bank.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":671,"n":697,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following freedom fighter lawyers from Bengal is known as Deshbandhu?","o":{"a":"Chittaranjan Das","b":"Durga Mohan Das","c":"Bipin Chandra Pal","d":"Satish Ranjan Das"},"a":"a","s":"Chittaranjan Das. He co-founded the Swaraj Party (in January 1923). Famous Personalities and their Nicknames: Lal, Bal, Pal - Lala Lajpat Rai, Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Bipin Chandra Pal. Man of Iron - Vallabh bhai Patel. Sher-e-Kashmir - Sheikh Abdullah.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":672,"n":698,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"'Mitra Mela' was a revolutionary organisation founded by:","o":{"a":"Bhupendra Nath Bose","b":"Khudiram Bose","c":"Vinayak Savarkar","d":"Sisir Kumar Bose"},"a":"c","s":"Vinayak Savarkar. Mitra Mela was founded in the year 1899 in Nashik. He and his brother Ganesh Damodar established the society (later renamed Abhinav Bharat Society or the Young India Society). It believed in the concept of Armed rebellion to overthrow the British.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":673,"n":699,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"On 8th April 1929, who among the following two revolutionists threw a bomb in the Central Legislative Assembly?","o":{"a":"Bhagat Singh and Rajguru","b":"Bhagat Singh and Ram Prasad Bismil","c":"Bhagat Singh and Batukeshawar Dutt","d":"Bhagat Singh and Sukhdev"},"a":"c","s":"Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt. They threw bomb against the passage of the Public Safety Bill and the Trade Disputes Bill which would reduce the civil liberties of citizens. They were members of the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association. On 23rd March, 1931 Bhagat Singh was hanged in the Lahore jail at the age of 23.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":674,"n":700,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who is regarded as the 'Mother of Indian Revolution' ?","o":{"a":"Annie Besant","b":"Usha Mehta","c":"Madame Cama","d":"Kasturba Gandhi"},"a":"c","s":"Madame Cama : She is famous for hosting the precursor to the Indian National Flag on foreign soil (Stuttgart in Germany).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":675,"n":701,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"The Koya movement in Malkangiri, during 1880, (now in Odisha) was led by:","o":{"a":"Ratna Naik","b":"Tama Dora","c":"Rindo Majhi","d":"Dayanidhi Dharua"},"a":"b","s":"Tama Dora. Koya revolt - It took place in the eastern Godavari track (modern Andhra Pradesh) in (1803 - 1862). After Tomma Sora's death, Raja Anantayyar organized another revolt in 1886. Other major tribal revolts : Paharias Rebellion (1778), Chuar Uprising (1776), Kol Mutiny (1831), Ho and Munda Uprisings (1820-37), The Santhal Rebellions (1855-56).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":676,"n":702,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Which great leader died due to his injuries in the Anti-Simon Commission Protest of 1928?","o":{"a":"Lala Lajpat Rai","b":"Lala Hardayal","c":"Bhagat Singh","d":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak"},"a":"a","s":"Lala Lajpat Rai ( Freedom fighters). He was also called 'Punjab Kesari' and 'Lion of Punjab'. Bal Gangadhar Tilak - Founded the Home Rule League in 1916. Simon Commission - Indian Statutory Commission, group of seven Members, not a single Indian member in it, Headed by Sir John Simon. Simon Commission came to India in 1928.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":677,"n":703,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"By what name did Vinayak Savarkar form a secret organization of revolutionaries in the year 1904 ?","o":{"a":"Hindustan Republican Association","b":"Naujawan Bharat Sabha","c":"Abhinav Bharat","d":"Forward Block"},"a":"c","s":"Abhinav Bharat. Vinayak Damodar Savarkar - Hindu nationalist political ideology of Hindutva and established Free India Society at India House in London. He wrote \"The History of the War of Indian Independence\".","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":678,"n":704,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Sohan Singh Bhakna and Har Dayal were primarily associated with which of the following organisations?","o":{"a":"Ghadar Party","b":"Kisan Sabha","c":"Anushilan Samiti","d":"Yugantar"},"a":"a","s":"Ghadar Party: Founded in San Francisco (USA) on 15 July 1913. It publishes a weekly newspaper 'the Ghadar'. Kisan Sabha (Akhil Bhartiya Kisan Sabha): Formed in 1936 at Lucknow, headed by Swami Sahajanand Saraswati. Anushilan Samiti Formed by Satish Chandra Basu in 1902, influenced by Bankim Chandra Chatterjee's \"Anandamath\". Yugantar - Founded by Barindra Kumar Ghosh in Calcutta.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":679,"n":705,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"In 1908, ___________ and Prafulla Chaki threw a bomb at a carriage that was occupied, they believed, by Kingsford, the unpopular judge of Muzaffarpur.","o":{"a":"Bhagat Singh","b":"Khudiram Bose","c":"Rajguru","d":"Jatindra Nath Bose"},"a":"b","s":"Khudiram Bose. He was hanged on 11th August 1908. Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev were hanged on 23 March 1931 at Lahore Central jail. Ram Prasad Bismil was hanged in 1927 at Gorakhpur Jail. Chandra Shekhar Azad shot himself and died on 27 February 1931 at Alfred Park (Now Azad Park) in Prayagraj.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":680,"n":706,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following, along with her close associates, started the secret congress radio during the Quit India Movement?","o":{"a":"Bhikaiji Cama","b":"Lakshmi Sehgal","c":"Sarojini Naidu","d":"Usha Mehta"},"a":"d","s":"Usha Mehta. Awards - Padma Vibhushan (1998). Bhikaiji Cama - First person to hoist the Indian flag at foreign land (Germany) in 1907. Sarojini Naidu (Nightingale of India) - First Indian woman President of the Indian National Congress and Governor of state (Uttar Pradesh) after independence. Annie Besant - First woman President of Indian national Congress. Sucheta Kriplani - First woman Chief Minister (Uttar Pradesh) of Independent India.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":681,"n":707,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Hindustan Republican Association took to socialist ideas, under whose leadership, in 1928? Graduate Level 30/06/2023 ( Shift - 1)","o":{"a":"Ram Prasad Bismil","b":"Rajguru","c":"Bhagat Singh","d":"Chandra Sekhar Azad"},"a":"d","s":"Chandra Sekhar Azad Hindustan Socialist Republican Association (HSRA) : Founded by Ram Prasad Bismil in 1928 by a group of Indian revolutionaries including Ashfaqulla Khan, Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev Thapar and Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee at Feroz Shah Kotla (New Delhi) . Bhagat Singh: \"Principal Secretary\" of HSRA in 1928.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":682,"n":708,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"The statement- \"We shall die to awaken the nation\" was made by.","o":{"a":"Bagha Jatin","b":"Pingali Venkayya","c":"Bir Tikendrajit Singh","d":"Surya Sen"},"a":"a","s":"Bagha Jatin (Jatindranath Mukherjee) - Born on 7th December 1879 in Kayagram (Nadia district) present-day Bangladesh. He acquired the moniker 'Bagha Jatin' when he single-handedly killed a Bengal tiger in 1906. Bir Tikendrajit Singh - He was a prince of the independent Kingdom of Manipur.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":683,"n":709,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Which freedom fighter was involved in the Kakori Train Action in 1925, for which the British government condemned him to death?","o":{"a":"Ram Prasad Bismil","b":"Lala Hardayal","c":"Vinayak Damodar Savarkar","d":"Mahatma Gandhi"},"a":"a","s":"Ram Prasad Bismil. Kakori Train Action - It was a train robbery (part of Kakori Conspiracy) that took place on 9 August 1925, near Lucknow. It was organised by the Hindustan Republican Association (HRA). Ram Prasad Bismil, Ashfaqulla Khan, and Roshan Singh were hanged on 19 December 1927. Rajendra Nath Lahiri hanged on December 17, 1927.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":684,"n":710,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following led the Chittagong Armory Raid or the Chittagong Rebellion?","o":{"a":"Surya Sen","b":"Vinayak Damodar Savarkar","c":"Rabindranath Tagore","d":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel"},"a":"a","s":"Surya Sen (Master Da) . He led the Chittagong Armory Raid in 1930. He was a revolutionary who was inspired by the 1916 Easter Rising in Ireland.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":685,"n":711,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"The Revolutionary Socialist Party was established in ________.","o":{"a":"1936","b":"1925","c":"1940","d":"1929"},"a":"c","s":"19 March 1940 by Tridib Chaudhuri. It has its roots in the Bengali liberation movement Anushilan Samiti and the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association . The first general secretary of the party was Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":686,"n":712,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who said the following words? \"Sarfaroshi ki tamanna ab hamare dil me hai, dekhna hai zor kitna baazu-e-qaatil mein hai\"","o":{"a":"Ramprasad Bismil","b":"Bhagat Singh","c":"Ras Bihari Bose","d":"Subhash Chandra Bose"},"a":"a","s":"Ramprasad Bismil (poet, writer, freedom fighter) - He participated in the Mainpuri Conspiracy of 1918, and the Kakori Conspiracy of 1925. Subhash Chandra Bose - \"Jai Hind\" & \"Tum Mujhe Khoon Do, Mai Tumhe Azadi Dunga\". Mahatma Gandhi - \"Karo ya Maro\". Muhammad Iqbal - \"Sare Jahan Se Achha Hindustan Hamara\". Bankim Chandra Chatterjee - \"Vande Mataram\". Pandit Madan Mohan Malviya - \"Satyameva Jayate\".","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":687,"n":713,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Which of the following revolts was led by Kanhu and Sidhu?","o":{"a":"Bheel Revolt","b":"Khasi Revolt","c":"Munda Revolt","d":"Santhal Revolt"},"a":"d","s":"Santhal Revolt (1855-56), {during Lord Dalhousie (1848-1856) tenure} the native rebellion in present-day Jharkhand in eastern India against both the British colonial authority and the corrupt upper caste zamindari system. The Bheel Revolt of 1818, one of the first uprisings undertaken by a tribal group in the country.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":688,"n":714,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Which of the following revolutionaries stated during his trial that \"he did not wish to glorify the cult of the bomb and pistol but wanted a revolution in society\"?","o":{"a":"Bhagat Singh","b":"Surya Sen","c":"Sachindra Nath Sanyal","d":"Barindra Kumar Ghosh"},"a":"a","s":"Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt threw a bomb in the Central Legislative Assembly on 8 April 1929. Surya Kumar Sen was hanged on 12 January 1934 by the British for the Chittagong Armoury Raid (1930). Sachindra Nath Sanyal was co-founder of the Hindustan Republican Association (HRA) in 1924. Barindra Kumar Ghosh (younger brother of Sri Aurobindo) published the Bengali weekly Jugantar patrika (1906).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":689,"n":715,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Abhinav Bharat Society was a secret society of revolutionaries. It was established in______","o":{"a":"Bombay Presidency","b":"United Provinces","c":"Punjab Province","d":"Calcutta Presidency"},"a":"a","s":"Bombay Presidency. Abhinav Bharat Society (Young India Society) was founded by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar and his brother Ganesh Damodar Savarkar in 1904. Savarkar was imprisoned in the Cellular Jail (Andaman Islands) in 1910. His books are 'The Indian war of independence, 1857', 'Essentials of Hindutva'.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":690,"n":716,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following revolutionaries was associated with Swadesh Bandhav Samiti?","o":{"a":"Chandrasekhar Azad","b":"Bhagat Singh","c":"Rajguru","d":"Ashwini Kumar Dutta"},"a":"d","s":"Ashwini Kumar Dutta founded the Swadesh Bandhab Samiti on 6 th August 1905 in Barisal district (Bangladesh). Weekly paper: Barisal Hitaishi. He was responsible for the creation of the Cooperative Hindustan Bank in 1908. Hindustan Socialist Republican Association (Chandrasekhar Azad, Ashfaqulla Khan, Bhagat Singh, Rajguru).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":691,"n":717,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Which of the following revolutionary groups' members assassinated Assistant Superintendent of Police John Saunders in 1927?","o":{"a":"Hindustan Sewa Dal","b":"Hindustan Republican Association","c":"Abhinav Bharat Secret Society","d":"Anushilan Samiti"},"a":"b","s":"Hindustan Republican Association (HRA).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":692,"n":718,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following organized the armed rebellion against the British Raj in 1879 in Maharashtra?","o":{"a":"Vasudev Balwant Phadke","b":"Birsa Munda","c":"Buddhu Bhagat","d":"Tikendrajit Singh"},"a":"a","s":"Vasudev Balwant Phadke - Known as the First Revolutionary and also the 'Father of militant nationalism in India.' Birsa Munda also known as Dharti Aaba (Father of Earth), belonged to the Munda tribe in the Chotanagpur Plateau area.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":693,"n":719,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"The Communist Party of India was founded at ________ in October 1920.","o":{"a":"Calcutta","b":"Britain","c":"Lahore","d":"Tashkent"},"a":"d","s":"Tashkent. M.N. Roy (Manabendra Nath Roy), Abani Mukherji, founded the Communist Party of India (CPI) in 1920 in Tashkent (now the capital of Uzbekistan). The Communist Party of India was officially formed (1925) in Kanpur. Its founders included M. N. Roy, his wife Evelyn Trent, Abani Mukherji, and M. P. T. Acharya (Mandayam Parthasarathi Tirumal Acharya).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":694,"n":720,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"The Anushilan Samiti was active in which part of the Indian subcontinent in the 20th century?","o":{"a":"Madras","b":"Bengal","c":"Punjab","d":"Bombay"},"a":"b","s":"Bengal. Satish Chandra Basu established Anushilan Samiti in 1902.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":695,"n":721,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"The Koraput Revolution occurred in _________ during the Quit India Movement. Graduate Level 02/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Orissa","b":"the United Provinces","c":"Bihar","d":"Bengal"},"a":"a","s":"Odisha. Koraput revolution {On August 25, 1942, 19 people died on the spot in police firing at Paparandi in Nabarangpur (Odisha). More than a thousand were jailed in Koraput district (Odisha). Veer Lakhan Nayak (Gandhi of Odisha) was hanged}. Quit India movement (8 August 1942 - 1944) - led by Mahatma Gandhi.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":696,"n":722,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Which of the following statements about Bharat Naujawan Sabha is INCORRECT? Graduate Level 05/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"It was founded in Punjab","b":"Bhagat Singh became its founding secretary","c":"It was established in 1927","d":"It started political work among the youth, peasants, and workers"},"a":"c","s":"Bharat Naujawan Sabha was founded in Lahore (1926) by Bhagat Singh.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":697,"n":723,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following led of the famous Indigo Revolt in Bengal?","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Bishnu Charan Biswas and Digambar Biswas","c":"Vasudev Balwant Phadke","d":"Madari Pasi"},"a":"b","s":"Digambar Biswas and Bishnu Biswas of Nadia district Bengal led Indigo revolt. This Revolt began as a nonviolent strike in March 1859, as the ryots of a village in Bengal's Nadia district all agreed to refuse to grow any more indigo.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":698,"n":724,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Which of the following revolutionaries was arrested by the British as an accused in the Alipore Bomb Conspiracy Case?","o":{"a":"Kanailal Dutta","b":"Rajendra Lahiri","c":"Roshan Singh","d":"Ashfaq Ullah Khan"},"a":"a","s":"Kanailal Dutta was among the 33 revolutionaries who got arrested in the Emperor vs Aurobindo Ghosh (Alipore Bomb Case). The Alipore Bomb case was a historic trial in which the British Government tried to implicate Sri Aurobindo in various revolutionary activities.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":699,"n":725,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"What was the age in years of the revolutionary Bhagat Singh when he was hanged till death?","o":{"a":"20","b":"23","c":"30","d":"26"},"a":"b","s":"23. On March 23, 1931, Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev were hanged for the killing of British officer JP Saunders. In 1926, Bhagat Singh founded the 'Naujawan Bharat Sabha (Youth Society of India) and joined the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":700,"n":726,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"At which of the following jails was Khudiram Bose sent to the gallows?","o":{"a":"Chittagong","b":"Barishal","c":"Muzaffarpur","d":"Alipore"},"a":"c","s":"Muzaffarpur. Khudiram Bose was arrested for trying to assassinate British judge Douglas Kingsford, he was executed on August 11, 1908, in Muzaffarpur jail in Bihar.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":701,"n":727,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following persons was associated with the Chittagong Armoury Raid?","o":{"a":"Vishnu Sharan Dublish","b":"Sachindra Nath Bakshi","c":"Manmath Nath Gupta","d":"Ardhendu Dastidar"},"a":"d","s":"Ardhendu Dastidar . Surya Sen led the Chittagong armory attack on April 18, 1930. Surya Sen and Tarakeswar Dastidar were found guilty and hanged in the year 1934.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":702,"n":728,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Pandit Madan Mohan Malviya is credited with popularising the slogan '______'.","o":{"a":"Vande Mataram","b":"Inquilab Zindabad","c":"Jai Jawan Jai Kisan","d":"Satyameva Jayate"},"a":"d","s":"'Satyameva Jayate' Malaviya also served on the Imperial Legislative Council (1909-20). Vande Matram was written by Bankim Chandra Chatterjee. The famous slogan Inquilab Zindabad was given by Maulana Hasrat Mohani. Jai Jawan Jai Kisan was the slogan given by Lal Bahadur Shastri.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":703,"n":729,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following killed Michael O'Dwyer, the British officer responsible for the Jallianwala Bagh massacre?","o":{"a":"Ram Prasad Bismil","b":"Udham Singh","c":"Chandrashekhar Azad","d":"Bhagat Singh"},"a":"b","s":"Udham Singh. O'Dwyer, was shot dead at a joint meeting of the East India Association and the Central Asian Society in Caxton Hall, in retaliation for the massacre in Jallianwala Bagh Amritsar.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"evening"},{"id":704,"n":730,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following Indian revolutionaries conceived an armed insurrection against the British in cooperation with Germany?","o":{"a":"Prafulla Chaki","b":"Ashfaqulla Khan","c":"Khudiram Bose","d":"Bagha Jatin"},"a":"d","s":"Bagha Jatin . The epithet 'Bagha Jatin' was earned by young Jatindranath Mukherjee in 1906 when he fought with a Royal Bengal tiger all alone for three hours and killed it using a dagger.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":705,"n":731,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following assassinated Sir William Hutt Curzon Wyllie in London?","o":{"a":"Surya Sen","b":"Madan Lal Dhingra","c":"Sukhdev Thapar","d":"Khudiram Bose"},"a":"b","s":"Madan Lal Dhingra was an Indian revolutionary independence activist. While studying in England, he assassinated Sir William Hutt Curzon Wyllie, a British official, cited as one of the first acts of revolution in the Indian independence movement in the 20th century.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":706,"n":732,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Veer Surendra Sai was a freedom fighter from:","o":{"a":"Nagaland","b":"Sikkim","c":"Telangana","d":"Odisha"},"a":"d","s":"Odisha . Veer Surendra Sai started his revolt against British rule long before 1857 and continued his guerrilla war against the British authority from 1857 till 1864 long after 1857 the struggle led by the Rani of Jhansi, Tantia Tope, Kunwar Singh and others had subsided.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":707,"n":733,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who was the first general secretary of the All India Farmer's Congress?","o":{"a":"Acharya Narendra Dev","b":"Swami Sahjanand Saraswati","c":"Sardar Vallabhai Patel","d":"N.G. Ranga"},"a":"d","s":"NG Ranga . He was a freedom fighter from Andhra Pradesh and also received Padma Vibhusan from the President of India for his contributions to the peasants' movement.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":708,"n":734,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Who among the following was a leader who led a revolt against British rule from North East India?","o":{"a":"Pritilata Waddedar","b":"Rani Gaidinliu","c":"Durgawati Devi","d":"Matangini Hazra"},"a":"b","s":"Rani Gaidinliu was a Naga spiritual and political leader who led a revolt against British rule in India. At the age of 13, she joined the Heraka religious movement established by her cousin Haipou Jadonang in Manipur.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":709,"n":735,"sec":"Modern History","st":"The Revolutionaries","q":"Identify the revolutionary from West Bengal who participated in the freedom movement.","o":{"a":"Khudiram Bose","b":"Balakrishnan Nair","c":"Puli Thevar","d":"Mukunda Kakati"},"a":"a","s":"Khudiram Bose was among the youngest martyrs of India's freedom struggle against the British. At just 15, he had joined Anushilan Samiti and took part in several revolutionary activities against the British Raj.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":710,"n":736,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Which of the following Satyagrahas was started by Dr B R Ambedkar in 1927?","o":{"a":"Nasik Temple Satyagraha","b":"Dharasana Satyagraha","c":"Mulshi Satyagraha","d":"Mahad Satyagraha"},"a":"d","s":"Mahad Satyagraha: It is also known as the Chavdar Tank Satyagraha, begun in Mahad (Raigad), Maharashtra. It centered around the Chavdar Tank, a public water source that was reserved exclusively for upper castes. In 1923, The Bombay Legislative Council passed a resolution that people from the depressed classes should be allowed to use places which were built and maintained by the government.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":711,"n":737,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"'Home Rule League' of Annie Besant was centred at which of the following places in India?","o":{"a":"Bombay","b":"Banaras","c":"Calcutta","d":"Madras"},"a":"d","s":"Madras. Annie Besant (British socialist) - Founded Home rule league in India and she was first woman president of Indian National Congress (1917, Calcutta session). Lala Lajpat Rai founded \"The Indian Home Rule League of America\" in New York in 1917. Balgangadhar Tilak founded the first home rule league at the Bombay provincial congress at Belgaum in April 1916.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":712,"n":738,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Who among the following leaders founded the women's wing of Congress in 1940 ?","o":{"a":"Aruna Asaf Ali","b":"Sucheta Kriplani","c":"Lakshmi Sangal","d":"Sarojini Naidu"},"a":"b","s":"Sucheta Kriplani (First female Chief Minister of Uttar Pradesh from 1963 - 67). She sang Vande Mataram on 14 th August 1947 in the opening session of Parliament. Aruna Asaf Ali hoisting the Indian National flag at the Gowalia Tank Maidan, Bombay. Sarojini Naidu (first female Governor of Uttar Pradesh) convinced Mahatma Gandhi to permit women to participate in the Dandi March.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":713,"n":739,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"On 15 August 1947, the first prime minister of India, Jawaharlal Nehru, raised the Indian National Flag above the ____________ of Red Fort in Delhi.","o":{"a":"Delhi Gate","b":"Kashmiri Gate","c":"Ajmeri Gate","d":"Lahori Gate"},"a":"d","s":"Lahori Gate. The National Flag of India was adopted on 22 July 1947, Designed by Pingali Venkayya. The President of India unfurls the Indian flag on Republic Day (January 26) at Kartavya Path (formerly called Rajpath) in New Delhi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":714,"n":740,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Who moved the resolution proposing that the National Flag of India be a 'horizontal tricolor of saffron, white and dark green in equal proportion '?","o":{"a":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","b":"Jawaharlal Nehru","c":"Mahatma Gandhi","d":"Dr Rajendra Prasad"},"a":"b","s":"Jawaharlal Nehru. The Tricolour was adopted as our national flag at a Congress Committee meeting in Karachi in 1931. The design of the National Flag was adopted by the Constituent Assembly of India on 22 July 1947. The version of the flag closest to the current one came into existence in 1923. It was designed by Pingali Venkayya and had saffron, white and green stripes with the spinning wheel placed in the white section.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":715,"n":741,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Who among the following was the founder of 'Tiger Legion' or 'Free India Legion'?","o":{"a":"Vinayak Damodar Savarkar","b":"Subhas Chandra Bose","c":"Sohan Singh Bhakhna","d":"Lala Hardayal"},"a":"b","s":"Subhas Chandra Bose - founder of 'Azad Hind Fauj'. 'Tiger Legion' was formed in 1941 with the help of the German Government to fight against the British.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":716,"n":742,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Pathans in North-West India organised the society of Khudai Khidmatgars, popularly known as Red Shirt, under whose leadership?","o":{"a":"Muhammad Ali","b":"MA Ansari","c":"Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan","d":"Hasrat Mohani"},"a":"c","s":"Khan Abdul Gaffar Khan (frontier Gandhi/Sarhadi Gandhi/ Badshah Khan/ Bacha Khan) - In 1921 he was elected president of a district Khilafat committee. Khudai Khidmatgar movement was founded in 1929-30. Award: Bharat Ratna (1987), He was the first non-Indian to receive this honour.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":717,"n":743,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"The authorities of which country denied permission to land the ship Komagata Maru, carrying Indians?","o":{"a":"New Zealand","b":"America","c":"Australia","d":"Canada"},"a":"d","s":"Canada. The Komagata Maru was a coal-transport steamship that had been converted into a passenger ship by Hong Kong-based businessman Gurdit Singh. It set off from Hong Kong in April 1914, reaching Vancouver's harbour a month later with 376 people on board, most of them Sikhs. The steamship eventually ended up back in India on 29th Sep 1914 at Budge Budge, Calcutta.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":718,"n":744,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"The origin of Directive Principles of State Policy can be traced to which of the following?","o":{"a":"Gandhi Irwin Pact","b":"Poona Pact","c":"Second Round Table Conference","d":"Karachi Resolution"},"a":"d","s":"Karachi Resolution (1931 The Karachi Session of Congress in 1931 was presided by Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel. Gandhi Irwin Pact - 5 March 1931. It marked the end of civil disobedience.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":719,"n":745,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Chittaranjan Das along with _________set up the Swaraj Party in 1923 to contest the elections.","o":{"a":"Vallabhbhai Patel","b":"Rajendra Prasad","c":"Motilal Nehru","d":"Jawahar Lal Nehru"},"a":"c","s":"Motilal Nehru. The Swaraj Party:- The two most important leaders were Chittaranjan Das, who was its president and Motilal Nehru, who was its secretary. Main Leaders : Vithalbhai J. Patel, Dr. Ansari, M. N. Roy, Muzaffar Ahmed, B. R. Ambedkar, P. C. Joshi, Motilal Nehru and C. R. Das.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":720,"n":746,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Which of the famous personalities belonged to the extremist phase of modern history?","o":{"a":"P.M. Mehta","b":"A.O. Hume","c":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","d":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak"},"a":"d","s":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak. He is also known as Lokmanya Tilak. Mahatma Gandhi had called him \"The Maker of Modern India\". The Congress split into two parts - Moderates and Extremists in 1907 at the Surat Session. Moderate leaders - Madan Mohan Malviya, M.G.Ranade, Gopal Krishna Gokhale, Dadabhai Naoroji, Surendranath Banerjee. Extremist leaders - Lala Lajpat Rai, Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal, Aurobindo Ghosh, Rajnarayan Bose, A K Dutt, V.O.C. Pillai.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":721,"n":747,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Who among the following freedom fighters was one of the founders of the Fergusson College in Pune in 1885 ?","o":{"a":"Subhash Chandra Bose","b":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak","c":"Sardar Vallabh Bhai Patel","d":"Lala Lajpat Rai"},"a":"b","s":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak. Other Co-founding members of Fergusson College :- Vaman Shriram Apte, Vishnushashtri Chiplunkar, Mahadeo Ballal Namjoshi and Gopal Krishna Agarkar. Subhas Chandra Bose Founded the 'Forward Bloc ' in 1939. Lala Lajpat Rai founded \"The Indian Home Rule League of America\" in New York in 1917.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":722,"n":748,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Motilal Nehru, C.R. Das gave up their legal practices during which Movement?","o":{"a":"Self-respect Movement","b":"Quit India Movement","c":"Non-Cooperation Movement","d":"Civil Disobedience Movement"},"a":"c","s":"Non-Cooperation Movement (1920 - 1922) . It was organized by Mahatma Gandhi to obtain self governance. Many lawyers gave up their practices such as Motilal Nehru, C.Rajagopalachari, Saifuddin Kitchlew, Vallabhbhai Patel, Asaf Ali, T. Prakasam and Rajendra Prasad. Self-respect Movement (1925) - started by E.V. Ramaswamy Naicker.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":723,"n":749,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"When was Alluri Sitarama Raju arrested ending a two-year armed tribal struggle?","o":{"a":"May 1924","b":"January 1921","c":"April 1919","d":"December 1929"},"a":"a","s":"May 1924. He was publicly executed in Koyyuru on May 7 after being tied to a tree and shot. His final resting place (tomb) is at Krishna Devi Peta, 111 km from Visakhapatnam. In August 1922, he launched the Rampa Rebellion (Manyam Rebellion) against the British. During this rebellion, he was helped by Bobbili Dora. The Prime Minister has unveiled a bronze statue of Alluri Sitharama Raju in Andhra Pradesh to celebrate 125th birth anniversary on 4th July 2022.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":724,"n":750,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"On learning about the Jallianwala Bagh massacre, _________ expressed the pain and anger of the country by renouncing his knighthood.","o":{"a":"Dadabhai Naoroji","b":"Badruddin Tyabji","c":"Rabindranath Tagore","d":"Surendranath Banerji"},"a":"c","s":"Rabindranath Tagore. The Jallianwala Bagh massacre (the Amritsar massacre) (13th April 1919, Baisakhi day).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":725,"n":751,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"In which year, the Koya rebellion took place in the eastern Godavari tract?","o":{"a":"1882-1883","b":"1874-1875","c":"1879-1880","d":"1887-1888"},"a":"c","s":"1879-1880. The Koya Revolts rose under Tomma Sora during 1879-80. Other important revolts are Kol Mutiny (1831), Ho and Munda Uprisings (1820-1837), The Santhal Rebellion (1855-56), Ramosi Uprising (1822- 1829), etc.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":726,"n":752,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"In which of the following regions did Baba Ramchandra mainly lead the peasant struggle during colonial rule?","o":{"a":"Awadh","b":"Mysore","c":"Jodhpur","d":"Hyderabad"},"a":"a","s":"Awadh . He led a peasant movement in Awadh against Talukdars and Landlords.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":727,"n":753,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"At which of the following places did C. Rajagopalachari break the salt law in the year 1930 ?","o":{"a":"Vedaranyam","b":"Tiruchi","c":"Tirupati","d":"Madurai"},"a":"a","s":"Vedaranyam C. Rajagopalachari broke the salt law in 1930 at Vedaranyam, a small coastal town in the then Tanjore District. The first Indian governor of free India was C. Rajagopalachari.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":728,"n":754,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"What was the name of the women's regiment in the Indian National Army founded by Subhash Chandra Bose?","o":{"a":"Rani of Jhansi Regiment","b":"Rani Ahilyabai Regiment","c":"Rani Padmawati Regiment","d":"Rani of Didda Regiment"},"a":"a","s":"Rani of Jhansi Regiment was the name of the women's regiment in the Indian National Army founded by Subhash Chandra Bose.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":729,"n":755,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Which maharaja of Manipur signed the Instrument of Accession with the Government of India?","o":{"a":"Kulachandra Singh","b":"Bodhchandra Singh","c":"Nara Singh","d":"Gambhir Singh"},"a":"b","s":"Bodhachandra Singh , signed the Instrument of Accession with the Indian government on the assurance that the internal autonomy of Manipur would be maintained. 'Instrument of Accession' which meant that their state agreed to become a part of the Union of India.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":730,"n":756,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"Who among the following was one of the founders of the Congress Socialist Party?","o":{"a":"C Natarajan Annadurai","b":"SK Patil","c":"Atulya Ghosh","d":"Ram Manohar Lohia"},"a":"d","s":"Ram Manohar Lohia Congress Socialist Party (Socialist caucus within the Indian National Congress) - Founded in 1934. Other founders - Jayaprakash Narayan and Acharya Narendra Dev.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":731,"n":757,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"In which city was the Jhanda Satyagraha or Flag Satyagraha of 1923 held?","o":{"a":"Bombay","b":"Nagpur","c":"Calcutta","d":"Ahmedabad"},"a":"b","s":"Nagpur . Flag Satyagraha is a part of civil disobedience during the Indian independence movement.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":732,"n":758,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Struggle for Independence","q":"_______was executed at Lahore in November 1915 for his role in the Ghadar Conspiracy in February 1915.","o":{"a":"Kartar Singh Sarabha","b":"Tantia Mama","c":"Maganbhai Patel","d":"Vijay Singh Pathik"},"a":"a","s":"Kartar Singh Sarabha Ghadar Conspiracy (1915) - Plan to initiate a pan-India mutiny in the British Indian Army during World War by the Ghadar Party (United States), the Berlin Committee (Germany), the Indian revolutionary underground in British India and the German Foreign Office.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":733,"n":759,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct about Prarthana Samaj? A. Prarthana Samaj or 'Prayer Society' in Sanskrit, was a movement for religious and social reforms. B. Prarthana Samaj was founded in 1863 by Dadoba Pandurang and his brother Atmaram Pandurang.","o":{"a":"Neither A nor B","b":"Only A","c":"Both A and B","d":"Only B"},"a":"b","s":"Only A. The Prarthana Samaj was established in Bombay by Dr. Atma Ram Pandurang in 1867. Aim - To remove caste restriction, abolish child marriage, encourage women education and promotes widow remarriages. Members - R.C. Bhandarkar and Justice Mahadev Govind Ranade.","ex":"SSC CHSL Tier II","y":2024,"sh":null},{"id":734,"n":760,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct about the servants of India society ? A. The 'Servants of India Society' was founded by Gopal Krishna Gokhale at Fergusson hill on 12 June 1905 in Pune district of Maharashtra. B. The Society focused on nation- building activities such as education, social welfare , upliftment of the downtrodden and betterment of the under -privileged sections of society.","o":{"a":"Only A","b":"Both A and B","c":"Only B","d":"Neither A nor B"},"a":"b","s":"Both A and B. Mahatma Gandhi considers Gopal krishna Gokhele his mentor in politics. Some Socio religious organizations and their founders : Servants of People Society (1921) - Lala Lajpat Rai, Theosophical Society (1875) - Madame H P Blavatsky and Col H S Olcott, Brahmo Samaj (1828) - Raja Ram Mohan Roy, Prarthana Samaj (1867) - Atmaram Pandurang.","ex":"SSC CHSL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":735,"n":761,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Sree Narayana Guru considered the differences based on caste and religion as meaningless and advocated the principle of 'One Caste, one Religion and one God' for all. He belonged to which of the following organisations?","o":{"a":"Veda Samaj","b":"Sree Narayana Dharma Paripalana Yogam","c":"Brahmo Samaj","d":"Arya Samaj"},"a":"b","s":"Sree Narayana Dharma Paripalana Yogam. Sree Narayana Guru: In 1888, he built a temple dedicated to Lord Shiva at Aruvippuram which was against the caste-based restrictions of the time. Other organisation and their founders - Veda Samaj (1864): Keshab Chandra Sen and K. Sridharalu Naidu. Brahmo Samaj (1828): Ram Mohun Roy. Arya Samaj (1875): Swami Dayanand Saraswati.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":736,"n":762,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Chembeti Sridharalu Naidu was a social reformer and was associated with which of the following organisations ?","o":{"a":"Veda Samaj","b":"Arya Samaj","c":"Brahmo Samaj","d":"Paramahansa Mandali"},"a":"a","s":"Veda Samaj. Organizations and Social reformers- Brahmo Samaj (Raja Ram Mohan Roy), Prarthana Samaj (Atmaram Pandurang), Arya Samaj (Dayanand Saraswati), Theosophical Movement (Madame Blavatsky and Colonel Olcott), Ramakrishna Mission (Swami Vivekananda), Satyashodhak Samaj (Jyotirao Govindrao Phule).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":737,"n":763,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following is related to a purificatory ceremony called shuddhi for those Hindus who had been converted to other religions like Islam and Christianity?","o":{"a":"Veda Samaj","b":"Ramakrishna Mission","c":"Brahmo Samaj","d":"Arya Samaj"},"a":"d","s":"Arya Samaj:- Formation - 10 April 1875 (Bombay). Founder - Dayananda Saraswati. Purpose - Educational, Religious studies, Spirituality, Social Reforms.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":738,"n":764,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following pioneered the movement in the support of widow remarriage and girls' education in Andhra region?","o":{"a":"Kandukuri Veeresalingam","b":"Chembeti Sridharalu Naidu","c":"Sree Narayana Guru","d":"C Rajagopalachari"},"a":"a","s":"Kandukuri Veeresalingam (1848-1919) was a social reformer and writer from Andhra Pradesh. He is considered as the father of the Telugu Renaissance movement. He also fought against child marriage and the dowry system.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":739,"n":765,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following was established by Jyotirao Govindrao Phule in 1873 to fight against idolatry and the caste system?","o":{"a":"Young Bengal Movement","b":"Satyashodhak Samaj","c":"Shree Narayan Guru Dharma Paripalana (SNDP) Movement","d":"Arya Samaj"},"a":"b","s":"Satyashodhak Samaj was established in Pune district of Maharashtra. Other Reform movements and their founders : Young Bengal Movement - Henry Vivian Derozio; Shree Narayan Guru Dharma Paripalana (SNDP) Movement (1903) - Dr. Padmanabhan Palpu; Arya Samaj (1875) - Swami Dayanand Saraswati; Tattvabodhini sabha (1839) - Debendranath Tagore. Amitya sabha (1814) - Raja Rammohan Roy.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":740,"n":766,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following organizations was founded in Poona, Satara and other towns of Maharashtra that worked against Brahmanical orthodoxy and tried to reform Hinduism?","o":{"a":"Brahmo Samaj","b":"Paramahansa Mandali","c":"Ramakrishna Mission","d":"Arya Samaj"},"a":"b","s":"Paramahansa Mandali - It was a secret socio-religious group, established in 1849, in Bombay and was closely related to Manav Dharma Sabha which was founded in 1844 in Surat.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":741,"n":767,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following was NOT associated with 'Prarthana Sabha'?","o":{"a":"RG Bhandarkar","b":"Govind Ranade","c":"Motilal Nehru","d":"Atmaram Pandurang"},"a":"c","s":"Motilal Nehru. Prarthana Sabha : Founder - Atmaram Pandurang in 1867. Objective - Rational worship and social reform. The Four point social agenda of Prarthana Samaj were: Disapproval of caste system, Women education, Widow remarriage, Raising the age of marriage for both males and females.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":742,"n":768,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Landholders Society was founded to look after the interests of which class?","o":{"a":"Traders","b":"Labour","c":"Farmers","d":"Landlords"},"a":"d","s":"Landlords. Landholders' Society also known as the Zamindari Association is considered to be the first political association of modern India. It was formally launched in Calcutta in March 1838. Raja Radhakant dev, Dwarkanath tagore, Prasanna Kumar Tagore, Rajkamal Sen and Bhabani Charan Mitra were its founding members.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":743,"n":769,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"When was the Atmiya Sabha founded to attack the evils within Hinduism ?","o":{"a":"1839","b":"1814","c":"1828","d":"1845"},"a":"b","s":"1814. Atmiya Sabha was started by Raja Ram Mohan Roy (father of Indian renaissance). Aim - To campaign against idolatry, caste rigidities, meaningless rituals and other social ills.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":744,"n":770,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following movements was founded in Bengal ?","o":{"a":"Ramakrishna Mission","b":"Arya Samaj","c":"Satyashodhak Samaj","d":"Prarthana Samaj"},"a":"a","s":"Ramakrishna Mission. The society was founded near Calcutta (now Kolkata) by Vivekananda in 1897. Other movements and their Propounders : Arya Samaj (Swami Dayananda Saraswati in 1875), Satyashodhak Samaj (Jyotiba Phule in 1873), Prarthana Samaj (Atmaram Pandurang in 1867).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":745,"n":771,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following institutes was founded by MG Ranade and GV Joshi?","o":{"a":"Abhinav Bharat","b":"The Indian National Congress","c":"The Bombay Presidency Association","d":"Poona Sarvajanik Sabha"},"a":"d","s":"Poona Sarvajanik Sabha (1870). Important Organization and their founders : Abhinav Bharat organization founded by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar in 1904. Indian National Congress : Established in 1885, by Sir Allan Octavian Hume. The Bombay Presidency Association was founded in 1885 in response to Lytton's reactionary policies and the Ilbert Bill controversy.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":746,"n":772,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who started the temple entry movement in 1927?","o":{"a":"Mahadev Govind Ranade","b":"Bhimrao Ambedkar","c":"Mahatma Gandhi","d":"Annie Besant"},"a":"b","s":"Bhimrao Ambedkar. It was a movement to allow lower caste people to enter temples. Dalits Movements led by Bhimrao Ambedkar: The second biggest movement for the entry of untouchables in the famous Kalaram temple of Nashik in 1930. Mahad Satyagraha (1927) was a movement for the Dalits to get the right to drink water from the tank.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":747,"n":773,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Visva Bharati a famous educational institute was founded in 1921 by:","o":{"a":"Rabindranath Tagore","b":"Vinoba Bhave","c":"Mother Teresa","d":"Swami Chinmayananda"},"a":"a","s":"Rabindranath Tagore. He received the Noble Prize in Literature for \"Geetanjali\" in 1913. His Notable works: ''Ghare Baire'', ''Gora'', ''Gitabitan'', ''The Post Office''. He wrote India's national anthem \"Jana Gana Mana'' and Bangladesh's national anthem \"Amar Shonar Bangla''. Visva-Bharati University: It is located in Shantiniketan, West Bengal and became the world's first \"living heritage university\".","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":748,"n":774,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who established Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan to promote education and culture?","o":{"a":"Dr. Kanhaiyalal Maneklal Munshi","b":"Dr. Atmaram Pandurang","c":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","d":"Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar"},"a":"a","s":"Dr. Kanhaiyalal Maneklal Munshi. Bhartiya Vidya Bhawan - A charitable public trust founded on November 7, 1938.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":749,"n":775,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who had founded the first Women's University in India ?","o":{"a":"Dhondo Keshav Karve","b":"Ashutosh Mukherjee","c":"William Hunter","d":"Sayed Ahmed Khan"},"a":"a","s":"Dhondo Keshav Karve (Maharishi Karve)- He founded the first women university (SNDT Women's University) in 1916 in India. Awards - Bharat Ratna (1958), Padma Vibhushan (1955). The Widow Remarriage Association was founded by Vishnu Shastri Pandit.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":750,"n":776,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following reformers initially set up a school for girls in Calcutta?","o":{"a":"Narayana Guru","b":"Mahatma Phule","c":"Swami Dayanand Saraswati","d":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar"},"a":"d","s":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar His Works : Widow remarriage, women education, introduction of punctuation mark in Bengali language and writing book Barnaparichay, protests against child marriage.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":751,"n":777,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who received the title Pandita from the faculty of Calcutta University for her knowledge of Sanskrit?","o":{"a":"Rokaya Sakhawat Hossain","b":"Ramabai Sarasvati","c":"Sarojini Naidu","d":"Rash Sundari Devi"},"a":"b","s":"Ramabai Sarasvati was an Indian social reformer, a pioneer in the education and emancipation of women in India. She was the first woman to be accorded the titles of Pandita. She was one of the 10 women delegates of the Congress session of 1889. In the late 1890s, she founded Mukti Mission at Kedgaon village, and established Sharada Sadan (Home of Wisdom), a school for widows, in Mumbai. In 1919, the king of England bestowed on her the Kaiser-I - Hind award.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":752,"n":778,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following associations was formed in Maharashtra to get social justice for the weaker sections of society","o":{"a":"Theosophical Society","b":"Arya Samaj","c":"Satya Shodhak Samaj","d":"Dharma Paripalana Yogam"},"a":"c","s":"Satya Shodhak Samaj - Jyotiba Phule established in 1873 in Pune (Maharashtra). It was a reformational society that promoted education, increased social rights, justice and political access in the deprived sections.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":753,"n":779,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who is known as the founder of the Widow Remarriage Association (1861)?","o":{"a":"Dr. Atma Ram Pandurang","b":"RG Bhandarkar","c":"Dwarkanath Tagore","d":"Mahadev Govind Ranade"},"a":"d","s":"Mahadev Govind Ranade Widow Remarriage Association - Started by Vishnu Shastri Pandit in 1856. Aim - Promote widow remarriage and campaigned against Child Marriage. Karsondas Mulji - He started the 'Satya Prakash' in Gujarati in 1852 to promote the remarriage of widows. Organization founded by Mahadev Govind Ranade - Poona Sarvajanik Sabha (1870), Vaktrutvottejak Sabha (1875).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":754,"n":780,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"In which language was the work of Raja Ram Mohan Roy, 'Gift to Monotheists' originally written ?","o":{"a":"Arabic","b":"Persian","c":"Bengali","d":"Sanskrit"},"a":"b","s":"Persian. Raja Ram Mohan Roy: Father of Modern India's Renaissance. Given the title 'Raja' by Akbar II (Mughal Emperor). He founded Atmiya Sabha in Calcutta. Brahmo Samaj in 1828. He also launched a movement for the abolition of Sati through his journal Sambad Kaumudi (first half of 19th century). He established Mirat - ul - Akhbar (first Persian journal).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":755,"n":781,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Narayan Guru, a social reformer from Ezhava caste and who propagated an anti-caste movement, was born in which of the following states of India?","o":{"a":"Karnataka","b":"Kerala","c":"Andhra Pradesh","d":"Tamil Nadu"},"a":"b","s":"Kerala. Shri Narayan Guru: He led a reform movement against the injustice in the caste-ridden society of Kerala in order to promote spiritual enlightenment and social equality. His famous quote: \"One caste, one religion and one god for all\".","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":756,"n":782,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who founded the East India Association in London?","o":{"a":"Justice Ranade","b":"Ananda Mohan Bose","c":"AO Hume","d":"Dadabhai Naoroji"},"a":"d","s":"Dadabhai Naoroji (Grand Old Man of India). He was the President of the Indian National Congress from 1886 to 1887, 1893 to 1894 and 1906 to 1907. His book - \"Poverty and Un-British Rule in India\". East India Association : Formed in - 1866, Main goal - To raise awareness among British people about the conditions in India and to generate popular support for Indian welfare.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":757,"n":783,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following started the Shivaji Festival to generate nationalism among the common people?","o":{"a":"Aurobindo Ghosh","b":"Surendranath Banerjee","c":"Lokmanya Tilak","d":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale"},"a":"c","s":"Lokmanya Tilak - started Ganeshotsav and Shivaji Utsav (Started at Fort Raigad) in 1894 for national awakening.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":758,"n":784,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following founded the Indian Association along with Anand Mohan Bose?","o":{"a":"Dada Bhai Naoroji","b":"Gopal krishna Gokhale","c":"Pheroze Shah Mehta","d":"Surendranath Banerjee"},"a":"d","s":"Surendranath Banerjee Indian Association of calcutta (1876), Aimed to unify Indian people on a common political programme and create a strong public opinion on political questions. Political Organization (till 1885) : Landholders Society (1838), Bengal British India Society (1843), The British Indian Association of Calcutta (1851), The East India Association (Dadabhai Naoroji, 1866), India League (Sisir Kumar Ghose in 1875), The Bombay Presidency Association (Pherozeshah Mehta, K.T. Telang, Badruddin Tyabji, 1885).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":759,"n":785,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who is credited with setting up of Brahmo Sabha, for religious reforms, in 1828, which was later called Brahma Samaj?","o":{"a":"Rabindranath Tagore","b":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy","c":"Keshub Chandra Sen","d":"Devendra Nath Tagore"},"a":"b","s":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy (Father of Modern India's Renaissance): Given the title 'Raja' by Akbar II (Mughal Emperor). Socio-Religious Movements in India - Young Bengal Movement (Henry Vivian Derozio), Arya Samaj (Dayanand Saraswati), Prayer Society (Atmaram pandurang), Theosophical Society (Madame Blavatsky and Karnal Alcott), Ramakrishna Mission (Swami Vivekananda), Aligarh Movement (Syed Ahmed Khan).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":760,"n":786,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following organized Sarvajanik Ganesh Utsav to inculcate national sentiments among the masses?","o":{"a":"Lokmanya Tilak","b":"Bipin Chandra Pal","c":"Aurobindo Ghosh","d":"Lala Lajpat Rai"},"a":"a","s":"Lokmanya Tilak (Bal Gangadhar Tilak) : Tilak launched the Indian Home Rule League in April 1916 at Belgaum (Annie Besant launched the Home Rule League in September 1916 at Madras). His famous declaration \"Swaraj is my birthright, and I shall have it\". The British Government termed him as the \"Father of Indian Unrest\".","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":761,"n":787,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"'The Deccan Sabha' was founded by which of the following nationalists?","o":{"a":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","b":"Pherozeshah Mehta","c":"Surendranath Banerjee","d":"Dadabhai Naoroji"},"a":"a","s":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale: Political mentor of Mahatma Gandhi and Founder of Servants of India Society (1905). He became president of Indian National Congress (INC) in Benaras session (1905).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":762,"n":788,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who founded the Arya Samaj and pleaded for the right of individuals to interpret Vedas and free themselves from the tyranny of priests?","o":{"a":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy","b":"RG Bhandarkar","c":"Swami Dayananda Saraswati","d":"MG Ranade"},"a":"c","s":"Swami Dayanand Saraswati Atmiya Sabha (1815), The Calcutta Unitarian Association (1821), Brahmo Sabha (1828) - Founded by Raja Ram Mohan Roy. Prathana Sabha - It was founded by Atmaram Pandurang on 31 March 1867 when Keshav Chandra Sen visited Maharashtra.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":763,"n":789,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Mohammedan Literary Society founded in Calcutta in 1863 was founded by:","o":{"a":"Nawab Abdul Latif","b":"Mirza Ghulam Ahmad","c":"Sayyid Ahmad","d":"Syed Ahmad Khan"},"a":"a","s":"Nawab Abdul Latif (Father of muslim renaissance in Bengal). Lord Lytton gave him The title of \"Nawab''. Lord Dufferin gave him the title of Nawab Bahadur. Mohammedan Literary Society was a literary society of Muslims in British India.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":764,"n":790,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Rast-Goftar, a newspaper, propagated the messages of socio -religious reforms for which of the following societies?","o":{"a":"Hindu","b":"Muslim","c":"Sikh","d":"Parsi"},"a":"d","s":"Parsi. Rast Goftar (\"The Truth Teller\") was an Anglo-Gujarati paper operating in Bombay that was started in 1854 by Dadabhai Naoroji and Kharshedji Cama and championed social reform among Parsis in Western India. Publishers and their Newspaper : James Augustus Hicky - \" Bengal Gazette\", Pt. Jugal Kishore Shukla - \"Udant Martand\", Raja Ram Mohan Roy - \"Samvad Kaumudi\" and \"Mirat-ul-Akbar\", Madhusudan Ray - \"Hindoo Patriot\", Devendra Nath Tagore - \"Indian Mirror\".","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":765,"n":791,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following was the disciple of Ramakrishna Paramahansa?","o":{"a":"Swami Dayanand","b":"Swami Sahajnanada","c":"Swami Vivekananda","d":"Swami Shraddhanand"},"a":"c","s":"Swami Vivekananda (1863-1902) - He was the founder of Ramakrishna Mission (1897), Vedanta Society of New York (1894). Ramakrishna Paramahamsa (19th century saint) - Disciple of Ramakrishna - Swami Sadananda, Swami Brahmananda, Swami Shivananda, Swami Akhandananda. Swami Sahajanand was the disciple of Mahatma Gandhi. Swami Shraddhanand was the disciple of Swami Dayanand.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":766,"n":792,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following devoted his life for the upliftment of widows and established the Widow Remarriage Association in 1893?","o":{"a":"DK Karve","b":"Annie Besant","c":"KC Sen","d":"BM Malabari"},"a":"a","s":"D K Karve. Karve's autobiographical works: Atmawrutta (1928) in Marathi, and Looking Back (1936) in English. Awards - Bharat Ratna (1958), Padma Vibhushan (1955). The first Widow Remarriage Association, in 1850's, was started by Vishnu Shastri Pandit.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":767,"n":793,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who was the first social reformer to view modern education as a vehicle for the spread of modern ideas in the country?","o":{"a":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy","b":"DK Karve","c":"Savitribai Phule","d":"Swami Vivekananda"},"a":"a","s":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy ( Father of modern India), Organizations founded : Brahmo Samaj (1828, Kolkata), Atmiya Sabha (1815, Kolkata). He was given the title of Raja by Akbar II, the Mughal emperor. He campaigned against caste system, untouchability, superstition and use of intoxicants. Books : \"The Precepts of Jesus: The Guide to Peace and Happiness\", \"Sati: a writeup of Raja Ram Mohan Roy\".","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":768,"n":794,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following organisations is credited with writing literary pieces for the abolition of Sati Pratha?","o":{"a":"The Harijan Sevak Sangh","b":"The Brahmo Samaj","c":"The Satya Shodhak Samaj","d":"The Bahujan Samaj"},"a":"b","s":"The Brahmo Samaj. Founder - Raja Ram Mohan Roy. Founded - 20 August 1828, Kolkata. Harijan Sevak Sangh (1932) : Founder - Mahatma Gandhi, Headquarters - New Delhi. Satya Shodhak Samaj (1873) : Founder - Jyotirao Phule. Founded in - Pune. Bahujan Samaj : A political party formed by Kanshi Ram on 14 April 1984.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":769,"n":795,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following was the founder of the Aligarh Movement, which was largely responsible for the revival of Muslims in India?","o":{"a":"Mirza Ghulam Ahmad","b":"Muhammad Iqbal","c":"Abdul Gaffar Khan","d":"Sayyid Ahmad Khan"},"a":"d","s":"Sayyid Ahmad Khan. He established the Mohammedan Anglo -Oriental College (also known as Aligarh Muslim University) at Aligarh in 1875. Mirza Ghulam Ahmad - Founder of the Ahmadiyya movement. Muhammad Allama Iqbal wrote the famous song 'Saare jahan se achha'. He is also known for giving birth to the 'idea of Pakistan'.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":770,"n":796,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Ramakrishna Gopal Bhandarkar and Mahadev Govind Ranade carried out their work of religious reforms in Maharashtra through which of the following organisations?","o":{"a":"Prarthana Samaj","b":"Atmiya Sabha","c":"Theosophical Society","d":"Brahmo Samaj"},"a":"a","s":"Prarthana Samaj (1867 Other Socio religious moment and their pioneers: Atmiya Sabha (1815) - Raja Rammohan Roy, Deva Samaj (1887) - Shiv Narayan Agnihotri, Theosophical Society (1875) - Helena Petrovna Blavatsky, Henry Steel Olcott.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":771,"n":797,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following was one of the founders of Rahnumai Mazdayasnan Sabha or Religious Reform Association?","o":{"a":"Keshab Chandra Sen","b":"Dadabhai Naoroji","c":"Muhammad Iqbal","d":"Aatmarang Pandurang"},"a":"b","s":"Dadabhai Naoroji (Grand Old man of India). Founded - East India Association (1866). He presided over the second session of Indian National Congress at Kolkata (1886). Keshab Chandra Sen - He established Indian Reform association (1870), Member of Brahmo Samaj (1828). Atmaram Pandurang: Founded - Prarthana Samaj (1867) with the help of Keshab Chandra Sen.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 4th shift"},{"id":772,"n":798,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"'Back to Vedas' was the slogan of which Reform Movement?","o":{"a":"Dev Samaj","b":"Brahmo Samaj","c":"Prarthana Samaj","d":"Arya Samaj"},"a":"d","s":"Arya Samaj: Founded in 1875 by Swami Dayanand Saraswati. Dev Samaj (Divine Society) - Founded by Pandit Shiv Narayan Agnihotri in 1887 at Lahore.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 1st shift"},{"id":773,"n":799,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Karsondas Mulji and Dadoba Pandurang were associated with______, which worked against evils like caste system and encouraged widow remarriage.","o":{"a":"Paramahansa Mandali","b":"Arya samaj","c":"Brahmo Samaj","d":"Ramakrishna Mission"},"a":"a","s":"Paramahansa Mandali: A secret socio-religious group, established in 1849 in Mumbai. Brahmo Samaj: Founder - Raja Ram Mohan Roy. Established in 1828. Aim - to end sati, polygamy, caste system. Ramkrishna Mission: Founder - Swami Vivekananda. Established in 1897. Aim - to spread the teachings of Vedanta.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":774,"n":800,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who founded the Tattvabodhini Sabha that was set up to explore spiritual truth?","o":{"a":"Keshab Chandra Sen","b":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy","c":"Debendranath Tagore","d":"Dayanand Saraswati"},"a":"c","s":"Debendranath Tagore (Father of Rabindranath Tagore) founded the Tattvabodhini Sabha in 1839. He joined the Brahmo Samaj in 1842, which was founded by Raja Rammohan Roy in 1828.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":775,"n":801,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following is NOT associated with Brahmo Samaj? Graduate Level 27/06/2023 ( Shift - 4)","o":{"a":"Atmaram Panduranga","b":"Keshav Chandra Sen","c":"Debendranath Tagore","d":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy"},"a":"a","s":"Atmaram Pandurang founded Prartahana Samaj (1867), Other members - R.C. Bhandarkar and Justice Mahadev Govind Ranade. Objective - Women Education, Widow remarriage and Disapproval of caste system. Brahmo Sabha (1828) : Founder - Raja Ram Mohan Roy, which was later renamed as Brahmo Samaj. Debendranath Tagore : Founded - Tattvabodhini Sabha (in 1839). Keshav Chandra Sen : Founded - Indian Reform Association (1870).","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":776,"n":802,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Veda Samaj of Madras was inspired by the________. Graduate Level 27/06/2023 ( Shift - 4)","o":{"a":"Brahmo Samaj","b":"Arya Samaj","c":"Deoband Movement","d":"Wahabi Movement"},"a":"a","s":"Brahmo Samaj (1828 Founded - by Raja Rammohan Roy. It denounced polytheism and opposed idolatry. Ved Samaj (1864) : Founder - Keshab Chandra Sen and K.Sridharalu Naidu. Deobandi Movement (1866) : Founder - Mohammad Qasim Nanotavi and Rashid Ahmed Gangohi. Wahabi Movement (1820) : Founder - Syed Ahmed.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":777,"n":803,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Principal of a Sanskrit College who represented a blend of Indian and Western culture was ________ . Graduate Level 28/06/2023 ( Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy","b":"Keshab Chandra Sen","c":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","d":"Devendranath Tagore"},"a":"c","s":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar (\"Father of Bengali Prose\"). Hindu Widow Remarriage Act of 1856 was enacted with the efforts of Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar. Raja Ram Mohan Roy - Founder of Brahmo Samaj (1828), Atmiya sabha (1815). Devendranath Tagore - founder of Adi Brahmo Samaj (1866).","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":778,"n":804,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Rani Gaidinliu is associated with which of the following movements?","o":{"a":"Heraka","b":"Meira Paibi","c":"Nupi Lan","d":"Nisha Bandh"},"a":"a","s":"Heraka was a socio-religious movement that arose in the 1920s in the Zeliangrong territory. Meira Paibi : This movement was founded in 1977 in Kaching district. Nupi Lan : There were two demonstrations led by women against the colonial authorities in Manipur, British India. In 1904, The second Nupi Lan began in 1939 in response to rice exports during World War II. Nisha Bandh : The women's movement developed in the late 1970s. Its origin is attributed to the Meitei women's activism to fight against alcoholism and drug addiction.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 1"},{"id":779,"n":805,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Some of Swami Dayanand's followers started a network of schools and colleges called D.A.V. What does 'A' stand for in D.A.V.?","o":{"a":"Author","b":"Angel","c":"Anglo","d":"Accept"},"a":"c","s":"Anglo. The acronym DAV translates as 'Dayanand Anglo Vedic', the melange of the Vedas and Modernity. Mahatma Hansraj established the first DAV School at Lahore on June 1, 1886. Other Educational Establishments in India: 1835 - Calcutta Medical College and the Elphinstone College of Bombay. 1857 - University of Bombay, Calcutta, and Madras. 1882 - The University of Punjab. 1887 - The University of Allahabad.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 4"},{"id":780,"n":806,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The ________ was started by the Sikh reformers to purify their religious places by removing the evil social practices that had slowly crept into them.","o":{"a":"Namdhari Movement","b":"Akali Movement","c":"Ghadar Movement","d":"Nirankari Movement"},"a":"b","s":"Akali Movement (Gurdwara Reform Movement).Namdhari Movement (Kuka Movement) - A Sikh reform movement that began in the Punjab region of India (Founded by Baba Ram Singh in 1857). Ghadar Movement: President - Sohan Singh Bhakna, Founded - 15 July 1913, Dissolved - January 1948, Preceded by - Pacific Coast Hindustan Association. Nirankari Movement - It was a social reform movement for Sikhism (founded by Baba Dyal Das in 1851).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":781,"n":807,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Ahmadiyya forms a sect of Islam which originated from India. It was founded by___________in 1889.","o":{"a":"Mir Nisar Ali","b":"Haji Shariatullah","c":"Shah Walliullah","d":"Mirza Ghulam Ahmad"},"a":"d","s":"Mirza Ghulam Ahmad. The Ahmadiyya Movement The movement, like Brahma Samaj, was founded on liberal principles and principles of a universal religion for all humanity. After his death the movement was led by elected successor Hakeem Noor-ud-din. Mir Nisar Ali (Titu mir) - Narkelbaria movement, 1831. Haji Shariatullah - Faraizi movement. Shah Walliullah - Wahabi movement.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":782,"n":808,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following founded the Widow Remarriage Association?","o":{"a":"Raja Rammohan Roy","b":"Pandit Vishnu Shastri","c":"Swami Vivekananda","d":"Keshav Chandra Sen"},"a":"b","s":"Pandit Vishnu Shastri. Raja Ram Mohan Roy: Atmiya-Sabha (1815), Brahmo Samaj (1828). Swami Vivekanand: Ramakrishna Mission (1897), The Vedanta Society of the city of New York (1894).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":783,"n":809,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Lala Lajpat Rai was an active member of which organisation?","o":{"a":"Arya Samaj","b":"Brahmo Samaj","c":"Brahmo Sabha","d":"Prathana Sabha"},"a":"a","s":"Arya Samaj (founded by Swami Dayanand Saraswati in 1875). It promoted the study of the Vedas and the Upanishads, and it advocated for social reform, including the abolition of caste discrimination.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":784,"n":810,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who was the founder of Dharma Sabha ?","o":{"a":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy","b":"Dadoba Pandurang","c":"R.G. Bhandarkar","d":"Radhakanta Deb"},"a":"d","s":"Radhakanta Deb founded it in 1830 in Calcutta. The Dharma Sabha was a conservative Hindu society. It campaigned against the Hindu Widow Remarriage Act, 1856. Chief public organ - Bengali newspaper \"Samachar Chandrika\" {Editor - Bhabani Charan Bandyopadhyay (Secretary of Dharma Sabha)}.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":785,"n":811,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Hindu College was established in Benaras in___________.","o":{"a":"1785","b":"1786","c":"1791","d":"1784"},"a":"c","s":"1791. Jonathan Duncan suggested establishing a Sanskrit college to develop and preserve Sanskrit Vangmaya to demonstrate British support for Indian education. In 1958 The Sanskrit college became a university and in 1974 the name was changed to Sampumanand Sanskrit University. Banaras Hindu University - Founded by Madan Mohan Malviya in 1916.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":786,"n":812,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Annie Besant laid the foundation of the Central Hindu College in______ in 1898 where both Hindu religion and Western scientific subjects were taught.","o":{"a":"Pune","b":"Chennai","c":"Banaras","d":"Kolkata"},"a":"c","s":"Banaras. Annie Besant: A British socialist, educationist and women's rights activist. She came to India in 1893. She set up her All-India Home Rule League in 1916 in Madras and covered a major part of India (including Bombay city). She became the first woman president of the Indian National Congress (INC) in 1917.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":787,"n":813,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"When was the Dev Samaj founded in Lahore by Shiv Narayan Agnihotri?","o":{"a":"1892","b":"1887","c":"1876","d":"1863"},"a":"b","s":"1887. Dev Samaj is a Hindu reform movement that was founded in response to the perceived ills of orthodox Hinduism. Other Famous Reforms: Brahmo Samaj : Founded by Raja Rammohun Roy in 1828. Prarthana Samaj: Founded by Atmaram pandurang and Mahadev Govind Ranade in 1867.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":788,"n":814,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"What was the objective of \"Arya Samaj\" founded by \"Swami Dayanand Saraswati\"?","o":{"a":"To establish autocratic rule","b":"To rise against Britishers","c":"To encourage the practice of barter trade","d":"To reform Hinduism"},"a":"d","s":"To reform Hinduism. Arya Samaj (1875) was founded by Swami Dayanand Saraswati. It also campaigned against social evils such as caste discrimination and untouchability.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":789,"n":815,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Swami Vivekananda gave his famous speech at the Parliament of World Religions in Chicago in _________ .","o":{"a":"1893","b":"1891","c":"1892","d":"1894"},"a":"a","s":"1893 . Swami Vivekananda's childhood name was Narendranath Datta. In 1893, Maharaja Ajit Singh (Khetri State) gave him the title of 'Vivekananda'. He preached the Philosophy 'neo-Vedanta' and was the disciple of Ramakrishna Paramhansa. He established Ramakrishna Mission (1897) (Headquarter - Belur Math). Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose had called Vivekananda the \"Maker of modern India.\"","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":790,"n":816,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which Indian reformer is regarded as the \"Father of Bengali Prose\"?","o":{"a":"Keshab Chandra Sen","b":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","c":"Bankim Chandra Chatterjee","d":"Swami Dayanand Saraswati"},"a":"b","s":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar - He was a social reformer from Bengal. He is credited with reconstructing the Bengali Alphabet. His Book - 'BornoPorichoy'. Due to his consistent effort, the Government of India passed the Widow Remarriage Act in 1856. Swami Dayanand Saraswati (Martin Luther of India) - Founder of the Arya Samaj (1875). Keshab Chandra Sen (with K. Sridharalu Naidu) - Founder of Veda Samaj (1864) in Madras.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":791,"n":817,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Young Bengal Movement was founded by _____ .","o":{"a":"Henry Louis Vivian Derozio","b":"Dayanand Saraswati","c":"Lala Hansraj","d":"Keshav Chandra Sen"},"a":"a","s":"Henry Louis Vivian Derozio had come to Calcutta in 1826 and was appointed in the Hindu College as a teacher of English literature and History. Lala Hansraj (Mahatma Hansraj): He was an educationist born in 1864 in a small town in Bajwara, in the Hoshiarpur district in Punjab. He was immensely influenced by the ideologies of Swami Dayanand Saraswati.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":792,"n":818,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following started the Kuka Movement?","o":{"a":"Thakur Singh Sandhawalia","b":"Baba Dayal Das","c":"Balak Singh","d":"Satguru Ram Singh"},"a":"d","s":"Satguru Ram Singh. The Kukas, a sect within Sikhism, formed with a motive to purify Sikhism. Baba Dayal Das: founded the Nirankari Movement. Balak Singh: An Indian Sikh religious leader who founded the Namdhari sect. Thakur Singh Sandhawalia: Founder of Singh sabha.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":793,"n":819,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who emphasized the worship of God as Nirankar (Formless) in Punjab?","o":{"a":"Maharaja Ranjit Singh","b":"Baba Ram Singh","c":"Baba Dayal Das","d":"Guru Gobind Singh"},"a":"c","s":"Baba Dayal Das - Founder of the Nirankari Movement. He emphasized the worship of god as Nirankar (formless). Baba Ram Singh - The leader of the Kuka movement in Punjab. Maharaja Ranjit Singh (\"Sher-e-Punjab\" or \"Lion of Punjab\") - First Maharaja of the Sikh Empire. Guru Gobind Singh - The tenth and last Sikh Guru.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":794,"n":820,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Ramakrishna Mission stressed the ideal of____________ through social service and of selfless action.","o":{"a":"bhakti","b":"education","c":"salvation","d":"God"},"a":"c","s":"Salvation . This mission was established by Swami Vivekananda (disciple of Swami Ramakrishna Paramahamsa) in 1897. The organization primarily promotes Advaita Vedanta, a Hindu philosophy, as well as four yogic ideals: Jnana, Bhakti, Karma, and Raja yoga. Motto (Atmano Mokshartham Jagaddhitaya Cha). Math (First Math - Baranagar; Second Math - Belur in 1899).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":795,"n":821,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"In which of the following languages did Jyotiba Phule write 'Gulamgiri' in 1873 ?","o":{"a":"English","b":"Marathi","c":"Hindi","d":"Gujarati"},"a":"b","s":"Marathi. Jyotiba Phule was born on 11 April 1827 in Pune, Maharashtra. In 1873, he formed the Satyashodhak Samaj, Maharashtra. Some of his famous books; Gulamgiri (Slavery) and Shetkaryacha Asud (Cultivator's Whipcord) , 'Tritiya Ratna', 'Sarvajanik Satyadharma', etc.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":796,"n":822,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Dhondo Keshav Karve was an eminent Indian who championed the cause of____________.","o":{"a":"allowing child marriages","b":"prohibiting widow remarriages","c":"strengthening the caste norms","d":"spread of women education"},"a":"d","s":"spread of women's education. On the line of promoting women's education, he founded the first women's university in India - SNDT Women's University . The Government of India awarded him the Bharat Ratna (1958) and Padma Vibhushan (1955). His two autobiographies : Atmawrutta (1928) in Marathi, and Looking Back (1936) in English.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":797,"n":823,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The first session of All India Depressed Classes congress was held at __________ in 1930.","o":{"a":"Nagpur","b":"Surat","c":"Delhi","d":"Kanpur"},"a":"a","s":"Nagpur. The All-India Depressed Classes Association (Dr. B. R. Ambedkar, 1930). 'Bahishkrit Hitkarini Sabha (Outcastes Welfare Association)', 1923. Independent Labour Party (ILP) 1936. Dr.B.R.Ambedkar (Bharat Ratna, 1990).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":798,"n":824,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who founded the Arya Mahila Samaj? Graduate Level 01/08/2022 (Shift - 4)","o":{"a":"Sarojini Naidu","b":"Sarla Devi Chaudhurani","c":"Pandita Ramabai","d":"DK Karve"},"a":"c","s":"Pandita Ramabai (women's rights & education activist). She was the first woman to be awarded the titles of Pandita as a Sanskrit scholar and Sarasvati after being examined by the faculty of the University of Calcutta. She was awarded with the Kaiser-i-Hind Medal for community service in 1919 . She was one of the ten women delegates of the Congress session of 1889 .","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":799,"n":825,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"In 1930, _________ led a satyagraha for the rights of untouchables' entry to the Kalaram temple at Nasik. Graduate Level 01/08/2022 ( Shift - 4 )","o":{"a":"MC Rajah","b":"Dr. BR Ambedkar","c":"Madan Mohan Malaviya","d":"Mahatma Gandhi"},"a":"b","s":"Dr B.R. Ambedkar. In 1920, he began the publication of the weekly Mooknayak (Leader of the Silent) in Mumbai with the help of Shahu of Kolhapur. His important writings: 'The Annihilation of Caste', 'Who Were the Shudras', 'The Untouchables', 'Riddles in Hinduism', etc. Poona Pact {signed between Ambedkar and Madan Mohan Malvaiya (on behalf of Mahatma Gandhi) in 1932 (Yerwada jail Pune), to provide reservation to depressed classes}.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":800,"n":826,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following was known as the forerunner of the Brahmo Samaj? Graduate Level 02/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Prarthana Sabha","b":"Atmiya Sabha","c":"Paramhansa Sabha","d":"Hindu Sabha"},"a":"b","s":"Atmiya Sabha. Brahmo Samaj {Raja Ram Mohan Roy founded Brahmo Sabha in 1828, Aim - worship of the eternal God. It split into two in 1866 (Brahmo Samaj of India led by Keshub Chandra Sen and Adi Brahmo Samaj led by Debendranath Tagore), Prominent Leaders: Debendranath Tagore, Keshub Chandra Sen, Pt. Sivnath Shastri, and Rabindranath Tagore}.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":801,"n":827,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Vishnushastri Chiplunkar started a monthly Marathi magazine __________, in 1874 for the cause of social reform. Graduate Level 04/08/2022 (Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Prabhakar","b":"Kesari","c":"Nibandhmala","d":"Pudhari"},"a":"c","s":"Nibandhmala. Gopal Hari Deshmukh is writer of Prabhakar newspaper. Kesari (Marathi newspaper, 1881, Lokmanya Bal Gangadhar Tilak). Pudhari (Marathi daily, 1937). (Newspaper/ Journal - Founder):- Udbodhan, 1899, Swami Vivekananda. Independent, 1919, Motilal Nehru. Tahzib -ul-Akhlaq, 1871, Sir Syed Ahmed Khan.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":802,"n":828,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha was established at ___________. Graduate Level 04/08/2022 ( Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Hyderabad","b":"Bombay","c":"Delhi","d":"Nasik"},"a":"b","s":"Bombay. Socio-Religious Movements/Organisations - Founders:- (Brahmo Samaj, 1828 - Raja Ram Mohan Roy), (Prarthana Samaj, 1867 - Dr Atmaram Panduran), (Arya Samaj, 1875 - Swami Dayanand Saraswati), (Ramakrishna Mission, 1897 - Swami Vivekanand), (Satyasodhak Samaj,1873 - Jyotiba Phule), (Ahmadiyya Movement, 1889 - Mirza Ghulam Ahmed).","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":803,"n":829,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Vidhava Vivaha Uttejaka Mandal (Society for Encouragement of Widow Marriage) was established in __________ in the second half of the nineteenth century. Graduate Level 05/08/2022 (Shift - 3)","o":{"a":"Calcutta Presidency","b":"Bombay Presidency","c":"the Central Provinces","d":"the United Provinces"},"a":"b","s":"Bombay Presidency. It was established by Vishnushastri Pandit in 1866. Hindu Widow Remarriage Act (1856) was passed by Lord Canning with the efforts of Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar. Sati Practice was abolished by William Bentinck in 1829 with the efforts of Raja Ram Mohan Roy.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":804,"n":830,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following founded the Vedanta College in 1825 ? Higher Secondary 05/08/2022 (Shift - 2)","o":{"a":"Swami Dayanand Saraswati","b":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","c":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy","d":"Swami Vivekananda"},"a":"c","s":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy (Father of Modern India) established the Atmiya Sabha (1815) and the Brahmo Sabha (1828). The Mughal Emperor of Delhi, Akbar II, bestowed upon him the title of 'Raja'.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":805,"n":831,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Begums of Bhopal founded a primary school for girls in the early twentieth century at _____.","o":{"a":"Lahore","b":"Bombay","c":"Aligarh","d":"Pune"},"a":"c","s":"Aligarh. The Begums of Bhopal played a vital role, from the early 20th century, in promoting education among Muslim women. They founded a primary school for girls at Aligarh. Nawab Begum was the founding Chancellor of Aligarh Muslim University.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":806,"n":832,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following was one of the founders of the Deccan Education Society (1884)?","o":{"a":"Dadoba Pandurang","b":"V R Shinde","c":"Tulsi Ram","d":"G G Agarkar"},"a":"d","s":"GG Agarkar. Paramhansa Sabha was founded by Dadoba Pandurang. V R Shinde founded the Depressed Class Mission in Mumbai in 1906. Radha Swami Satsang was founded by Tulsi Ram in 1861.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":807,"n":833,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"The Satnami Movement in Central India had a motive to:","o":{"a":"improve social status of leather workers","b":"ban child marriage","c":"open school for girls","d":"improve widow status"},"a":"a","s":"Ghasidas started the Satnami movement in central India and their aim was to establish an equal society. The Satnami sect was founded by a saint named \"Birbhan\" in 1657 in Narnaul in Haryana. In 1672, they rebelled against the mighty Mughal Empire. Aurangzeb crushed the rebellion.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":808,"n":834,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Sayyid Ahmed Khan founded Mohammedan Anglo-Oriental College in _________ in 1875","o":{"a":"Aligarh","b":"Calcutta","c":"Delhi","d":"Lahore"},"a":"a","s":"Aligarh and evolved into the Mohammedan Anglo-Oriental College. He has often been criticized as the father of Two nation theory which led to the formation of two separate nations i.e. India and Pakistan. His writings include- \"The Causes of the Indian Revolt'' (paper), \"Tahzebul Akhlaq\" (magazine), 'Asbab-e-Baghawat-e-Hind' (Booklet), 'Commentary on the Holy Bible' (book), etc.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":809,"n":835,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following organisations was the earliest to be established, as compared with the other three during British rule?","o":{"a":"Brahmo Samaj","b":"Satya Shodhak Samaj","c":"Prarthana Samaj","d":"Paramahansa Mandali"},"a":"a","s":"Brahmo Samaj, Brahmo also spelled Brahma, the theistic movement within Hinduism, founded in Calcutta in 1828 by Raja Ram Mohun Roy.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":810,"n":836,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"______ fought for the rights of the Dalit community.","o":{"a":"Subhash Chandra Bose","b":"Bipin Chandra Pal","c":"Bhimrao Ambedkar","d":"Krishnaji Gopal Karve"},"a":"c","s":"Bhimrao Ambedkar Ambedkar fought for the rights of workers and peasants. In the late 1920s and early 1930s, he had formed his Independent Labour Party.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":811,"n":837,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following wrote the Brahmo Covenant, which was a statement of the creed of the Brahmo Samaj and made a list of the duties and obligations of its members?","o":{"a":"Keshab Chandra Sen","b":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","c":"Debendranath Tagore","d":"Rammohan Roy"},"a":"c","s":"Debendranath Tagore . He was the founder in 1848 of the Brahmo religion, which today is synonymous with Brahmoism. In 1863 he founded Shantiniketan (\"Abode of Peace\").","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":812,"n":838,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following statements about Swami Dayanand Saraswati is INCORRECT?","o":{"a":"His birthplace was Gujarat.","b":"He authored the book 'Satyarth Prakash'.","c":"He was the founder of Arya Samaj.","d":"He was the founder of Brahmo Samaj."},"a":"d","s":"The Brahmo Samaj was founded by Raja Rammohan Roy along with Dwarkanath Tagore and others on August 20, 1828.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":813,"n":839,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following was NOT a Nayanar saint?","o":{"a":"Sundarar","b":"Appar","c":"Sambandar","d":"Andal"},"a":"d","s":"Andal . The Nayanars were a group of 63 poet-saints living in Tamil Nadu during the 6th to 8th centuries CE who were devoted to the Hindu god Shiva. Along with the Alwars, their contemporaries who were devoted to Vishnu, they influenced the Bhakti movement in early medieval South India.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":814,"n":840,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Which of the following is NOT a correct match of Dargah and its respective location?","o":{"a":"Shaikh Nizamuddin Auliya Dargah - Agra","b":"Khwaja Qutbuddin Bakhtiyar Kaki Dargah - Delhi","c":"Haji Ali Dargah - Mumbai","d":"Shaikh Muinuddin Sijzi (chishti) Dargah - Ajmer"},"a":"a","s":"Shaikh Nizamuddin Auliya Dargah is the Dargah of the Sufi saint Khwaja Nizamuddin Auliya. It is located in the Nizamuddin West area of Delhi","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":815,"n":841,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Veteran freedom fighter, social reformer and feminist Savithribai Phule hailed from which of the following states of India?","o":{"a":"Maharashtra","b":"Odisha","c":"Rajasthan","d":"Gujarat"},"a":"a","s":"Maharashtra . Savitibai Phule - She was the first female teacher of India and opened a school for women in 1848, Married to Jyotirao Phule (Founded Satya Shodhak Samaj in 1873 in Pune).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":816,"n":842,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following is known as the 'father of Muslim renaissance' in Bengal?","o":{"a":"Sir Syed Ahmed Khan","b":"Ameer Ali","c":"Nawab Abdul Latif Khan","d":"Nawab Salimullah Khan"},"a":"c","s":"Nawab Abdul Latif Khan . His title, Nawab, was awarded by the British in 1880. He was one of the first Muslim in nineteenth century India to embrace the idea of modernization.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":817,"n":843,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Socio Religious Reforms","q":"Who among the following scholars is associated with the Tibet-home theory of the Aryans?","o":{"a":"Dayanand Saraswati","b":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak","c":"MacDonell","d":"Max Muller"},"a":"a","s":"Swami Dayanand Saraswati in his book Satyarth Prakash propounded that the original abode of the Aryans was Tibet. The reasoning behind this was that due to the extreme cold in Tibet, sun and fire were worshiped and the flora and fauna described in the Rigveda were found in Tibet.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":818,"n":844,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Indian National Congress celebrated the first Independence Day in Colonial India on _____________ .","o":{"a":"2 October 1929","b":"29 December 1929","c":"15 August 1930","d":"26 January 1930"},"a":"d","s":"26 January 1930. The Indian National Congress passed 'Complete Swaraj' in its Lahore session on 19 December 1929. The president of this session was Jawaharlal Nehru. National Symbols of India and adopted date: National Flag of India - Tiranga (22 July 1947), Indian National Calendar - Saka Calendar (22 March 1957), National Song of India - Vande Matram (24 January 1950), National Anthem of India - Jana Gana Mana (24 January 1950).","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":819,"n":845,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Who led the Indian National Congress at its first annual session?","o":{"a":"Surendra Nath Banerjee","b":"Dadabhai Naoroji","c":"WC Banerjee","d":"AO Hume"},"a":"c","s":"WC Banerjee. Important Congress sessions: First Session - held at Bombay in 1885. Second Session - held at Calcutta in 1886, President - Dadabhai Naoroji. Third Session - held at Madras in 1887, President - Syed Badruddin Tyabji (first muslim President). Fourth Session - held at Allahabad in 1888, President - George Yule (first English President). 1896 session (Calcutta) President - Rahimtullah Sayani, National Song 'Vande Mataram' sung for the first time by Rabindranath Tagore.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":820,"n":846,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Who was the first Woman President of Indian National Congress?","o":{"a":"Sarojini Naidu","b":"Asaf Ali","c":"Annie Besant","d":"Usha Mehta"},"a":"c","s":"Annie Besant : First woman who presided over the calcutta session (1917) of Indian National Congress. Sarojini Naidu - first Indian women president of Indian national congress (1925, Kanpur session) and the first woman Governor of the United Provinces in 1947.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":821,"n":847,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Which flag of the Indian National Congress was adopted in 1931?","o":{"a":"A two colour flag with charkha made by cloth","b":"A tricolour flag made by Khadi with a Charkha symbol","c":"One colour flag with charkha symbol","d":"A blue colour flag made by cloth"},"a":"b","s":"Ad - Hoc Flag Committee : Formed in 1947 under Rajendra Prasad. Pingali Venkayya - Designer of National Flag of India (1921). National flag was adopted on July 22, 1947. Ratio of the length to the breadth of the Indian Flag - 3:2. National flag of India was first hoisted on 7th August, 1906 in Kolkata at the Parsee Bagan Square (Green Park). First Indian flag was hoisted on a foreign land by Madame Bhikaji Cama (1907 - Germany).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":822,"n":848,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"In the 1939 Tripuri Congress Presidential Election, Pattabhi Sitaramayya was defeated by:","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Rajendra Prasad","c":"Subhas Chandra Bose","d":"Jawaharlal Nehru"},"a":"c","s":"Subhas Chandra Bose was elected President of the Haripura Congress Session in 1938. He resigned as congress president in April 1939. Dr. Rajendra Prasad replaced Subhas Chandra Bose as the new congress president.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":823,"n":849,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"In which session of All India Congress Committee was the Quit India Resolution accepted?","o":{"a":"Calcutta","b":"Delhi","c":"Madras","d":"Bombay"},"a":"d","s":"Bombay. Quit India Movement - It was launched at the Bombay session of the All India Congress Committee by Mahatma Gandhi on 8 August 1942, during World War II, demanding an end to British rule in India. Yusuf Meher Ali coined the slogan 'Quit India'.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":824,"n":850,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"In which session of the Indian National Congress was the resolution declaring Poorna Swaraj passed?","o":{"a":"Calcutta","b":"Bombay","c":"Madras","d":"Lahore"},"a":"d","s":"Lahore (1929). Pandit Jawahar Lal Nehru presided over the Lahore session of congress and hoisted the tricolor flag of India. Congress Session and President: Lucknow session - Ambica Charan Mazumdar (1916), Tripuri - Subhash Chandra Bose (1939), Gaya - Chittaranjan Das (1922), Kanpur - Sarojini Naidu (1925), Karachi - Vallabhbhai Patel (1931), Ramgarh - Maulana Abul Kalam Azad (1940).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":825,"n":851,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"The second annual session of Indian National Congress was held at which of the following places ?","o":{"a":"Bombay","b":"Delhi","c":"Calcutta","d":"Madras"},"a":"c","s":"Calcutta (1886, Dada Bhai Naoroji) . Indian National Congress: Founded - 28 December 1885 in Bombay by Alan Octavian Hume. First President - Womesh Chandra Banerjee. Third Session (1887, Madras) - Badruddin Tyabji.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":826,"n":852,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Who was the president of the second session of the Indian National Congress?","o":{"a":"Dadabhai Naoroji","b":"Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee","c":"Pherozeshah Mehta","d":"George Yule"},"a":"a","s":"Dadabhai Naroji. Indian National Congress: The Second session - Calcutta (1886), The first session - Bombay (1885). The first president - Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee. First General Secretary - A.O Hume.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":827,"n":853,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Self-Government or 'Swaraj' as the ultimate goal of the Indian National Congress-this declaration was made by Dadabhai Naoroji in which of the following sessions of the INC ?","o":{"a":"Bombay","b":"Calcutta","c":"Madras","d":"Bankipur"},"a":"b","s":"Calcutta. Dadabhai Naoroji (Grand Old Man of India) founded the Rahnumai Mazdayasnan Sabha in 1851 to revive the Zoroastrian religion and formed the London India Society in 1865. He Became a member of the Legislative Council of Mumbai during 1885-88 and President of the Congress Party in 1886 (Calcutta), 1893 (Lahore) and 1906 (Calcutta). He gave the theory of 'drain of wealth' in his book \"Poverty and Un-British Rule in India\".","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":828,"n":854,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"When did the All India Congress formalize the demand for 'Purna Swaraj' or full independence for India?","o":{"a":"At its Karachi Session in 1931","b":"At its Lahore Session in 1929","c":"At its Guwahati Session in 1926","d":"At its Madras Session in 1927"},"a":"b","s":"Lahore Session (1929) - Jawaharlal Nehru was elected as President of the Indian National Congress (1929). The Karachi Session (1931) - Chaired by Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel; Resolution on Fundamental Rights and the National Economic Programme. The Guwahati Session (1926) - Presided by the Srinivasa Iyengar; Resolution to promote the use of Khadi (as a symbol of national unity) and self-reliance. The Madras Session (1927) - Presided by Dr. Ansari; Resolution for the boycott of the Simon Commission.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":829,"n":855,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Which of the following organisations was established in 1885 ?","o":{"a":"Communist Party of India","b":"Muslim League","c":"East India Organization","d":"Indian National Congress"},"a":"d","s":"Indian National Congress The Communist Party of India (M.N. Roy, Abani Mukherji) was founded in Kanpur on 26 December 1925. The All-India Muslim League was a political party established in Dhaka in 1906. The East India Association was founded by Dadabhai Naoroji in 1866.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":830,"n":856,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Which Session of Congress and Muslim League reached an understanding of creating a joint front against the British regime?","o":{"a":"Bombay","b":"Allahabad","c":"Lucknow","d":"Delhi"},"a":"c","s":"Lucknow (1916) - President: A.C. Majumdar; Lucknow Pact signed between Congress (Bal Gangadhar Tilak) and Muslim League (Muhammad Ali Jinnah) to build political consensus; Unity between two factions - Moderates and Extremists of Congress.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":831,"n":857,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Which among the following sessions of Indian National Congress was the first session to be presided by a lady ?","o":{"a":"Calcutta - 1917","b":"Kanpur - 1925","c":"Lahore - 1929","d":"Surat - 1907"},"a":"a","s":"Calcutta - (Annie Besant, 1917) . Sessions of Indian National Congress: Kanpur (1925) - Sarojini Naidu (First Indian woman president), Lahore (1929) - President (Jawaharlal Nehru), Objective - Resolution for 'Poorna Swaraj.' Civil Disobedience movement for complete independence to be launched, 26 January to be observed as 'Independence Day'. Surat 1907 - President (Rash Behari Ghosh).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":832,"n":858,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"In _________ at its Madras session, the Indian National Congress set up an All Parties Conference to draft a Constitution for India.","o":{"a":"1922","b":"1932","c":"1927","d":"1915"},"a":"c","s":"1927. Indian National Congress (INC) session 1927 (Madras ): President - M A Ansari. INC session 1915 (Bombay): President - Satyendra Prasanna Sinha. Importance - Constitution of the Congress was altered to admit the delegates from the extremist section. INC session 1922 (Gaya): President - C R Das. Importance - CR Das and other leaders broke away from INC, Formation of Swaraj Party. INC session 1932 (Delhi): President - Amrit Ranchhorddas Seth.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":833,"n":859,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"The Indian National Congress passed Purna Swaraj resolution in 1929 under the presidency of ___________ .","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Lala Lajpat Rai","c":"Jawaharlal Nehru","d":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel"},"a":"c","s":"Jawaharlal Nehru. The Poorna Swaraj resolution or the Declaration of Independence was passed at the Lahore session of Congress in December 1929. A public declaration was made on 26 January 1930. The resolution was a short 750-word document.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":834,"n":860,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"The split in Indian National Congress occurred in ________ .","o":{"a":"1901","b":"1905","c":"1903","d":"1907"},"a":"d","s":"1907. The Surat Split was the splitting of Indian National Congress into two groups - Moderates and Radicals.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":835,"n":861,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Badruddin Tyabji addressed the congress as president in ______ .","o":{"a":"1887","b":"1888","c":"1885","d":"1886"},"a":"a","s":"1887 (Madras Session Badruddin Tyabji - First muslim and third president of the Indian national congress (INC). Important sessions of Indian national congress and presidents: 1st (1885, Bombay Session) - W.C.Banerjee. 4th (Allahabad Session, 1888) - George Yule (first English).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":836,"n":862,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"The Indian National Congress was established when 72 delegates from all over the country met at Bombay in December _______.","o":{"a":"1890","b":"1858","c":"1885","d":"1872"},"a":"c","s":"1885. Founder : Allan Octavian Hume. First President: W.C. Bannerjee. First meeting - Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College (Bombay). Prominent delegates included Dadabhai Naoroji, Surendranath Banerjee, Badruddin Tyabji, Pherozeshah Mehta, W. C. Bonnerjee, S. Ramaswami Mudaliar, S. Subramania Iyer, and Romesh Chunder Dutt.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":837,"n":863,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Who among the following was the President of the Indian National Congress during the 1915 session?","o":{"a":"Satyendra Prasanna Sinha","b":"Ambica Charan Mazumdar","c":"Bhupendra Nath Bose","d":"Madan Mohan Malaviya"},"a":"a","s":"Satyendra Prasanna Sinha 1885 - Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee, 1886 - Shri Dadabhai Naoroji, 1887 - Badruddin Tyabji, 1890 - Pherozeshah Mehta, 1892 - Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee, 1895 - Surendranath Banerjee, 1905 - Gopal Krishna Gokhale, 1906 - Shri Dadabhai Naoroji, 1907 - Rash Behari Ghosh, 1909 - Madan Mohan Malaviya, 1916 - Ambica Charan Mazumdar, 1917 - Annie Besant, 1919 - Motilal Nehru.","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":838,"n":864,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Sarojini Naidu was elected first Indian woman President of the Indian National Congress at _________.","o":{"a":"Kanpur","b":"Ahmedabad","c":"Delhi","d":"Nagpur"},"a":"a","s":"Kanpur. Sarojini Naidu (Nightingale of India) was the first woman Governor of a State (Uttar Pradesh) in free India. The first woman president of the Indian National Congress was Annie Besant in 1917.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":839,"n":865,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"How many delegates participated in the second session of Indian National Congress?","o":{"a":"434","b":"628","c":"212","d":"190"},"a":"a","s":"434 , Held (December 27-30, 1886, Calcutta), President (Shri Dadabhai Naoroji), Congress decided to set up Provincial Congress Committees across the country. Father of Indian National Congress: Allan Octavian Hume (known for his Safety valve theory).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":840,"n":866,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"In 1929, Indian National Congress demanded complete independence under the presidentship of_____.","o":{"a":"Dadabhai Naoroji","b":"Jawaharlal Nehru","c":"Mahatma Gandhi","d":"Chitta Ranjan Das"},"a":"b","s":"Jawaharlal Nehru. The Indian National Congress, on 19 December 1929, passed the historic 'Purna Swaraj' - (total independence) resolution - at its Lahore session under the presidentship of Jawahar Lal Nehru. The Indian tricolour flag was also hoisted by him on the bank of the Ravi river.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":841,"n":867,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"In which of the following sessions of the Indian National Congress did George Yule become the President in 1888?","o":{"a":"Calcutta","b":"Allahabad","c":"Madras","d":"Bombay"},"a":"b","s":"Allahabad . On 28 December 1885, the Indian National Congress was founded at Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College in Bombay. Founder - A. O Hume.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":842,"n":868,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"In which of the following years did the resolution at the Karachi Session of the Indian National Congress dwell on how Independent India's Constitution should look?","o":{"a":"1931","b":"1928","c":"1946","d":"1945"},"a":"a","s":"1931 In 1928, Motilal Nehru and other Congress leaders drafted a constitution for India.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":843,"n":869,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Who among the following was elected as the President of the Indian National Congress in the year 1938 and 1939?","o":{"a":"Subhash Chandra Bose","b":"Dr. Rajendra Prasad","c":"C Rajagopalachari","d":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel"},"a":"a","s":"Subhash Chandra Bose","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":844,"n":870,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Who among the following presided over the special session of Congress in September 1923 and at the age of 35 became the youngest man to be elected as the President of the Congress?","o":{"a":"Mahadev Govind Ranade","b":"Ram Manohar Lohia","c":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak","d":"Maulana Abul Kalam Azad"},"a":"d","s":"Maulana Abul Kalam Azad He was also a noted writer, poet and journalist. He was a prominent political leader and was elected as Congress President in 1923 and 1940.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":845,"n":871,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"Who was the President of the Indian National Congress when India won its freedom in the year 1947 ?","o":{"a":"Abul Kalam Azad","b":"Vallabhbhai Patel","c":"Jawaharlal Nehru","d":"J. B. Kripalani"},"a":"d","s":"J. B. Kripalani (Acharya Kriplani) was the president of the Indian National Congress during the transfer of power. He was the husband of Sucheta Kripalani, who was the first woman chief minister (Uttar Pradesh).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":846,"n":872,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Its Sessions","q":"The National Anthem of India, 'Jana Gana Mana' was first sung at the ______ Session of the Indian National Congress.","o":{"a":"Calcutta","b":"Lahore","c":"Belgaum","d":"Tripuri"},"a":"a","s":"Calcutta in 1911 Session of the Indian National Congress on 27 December . The national anthem was written by RabindraNath Tagore.This session was headed by Bishan Narayan Dhar.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":847,"n":873,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Muslim league","q":"In which of the following years was the Muslim League formed?","o":{"a":"1909","b":"1906","c":"1912","d":"1915"},"a":"b","s":"1906. Muslim League :- It was a political party established in Dhaka, Bangladesh (30 December 1906) when some well-known Muslim politicians met the Viceroy of British India (Lord Minto). The party demanded a separate Muslim state (Pakistan) in 1940. Founder - Nawab Khwaja Salimullah. Muhammad Ali Jinnah joined the muslim League in 1913, he called for 'Direct Action Day' on 16th August 1946.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":848,"n":874,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Muslim league","q":"The Lucknow Pact was signed in ____________ by Congress and the Muslim League to work in alliance for representative government.","o":{"a":"1916","b":"1918","c":"1920","d":"1914"},"a":"a","s":"1916. INC session Lucknow (1916) - President: A.C. Majumdar. Lucknow Pact was signed between Congress (Bal Gangadhar Tilak) and Muslim League (Muhammad Ali Jinnah) to build political consensus.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":849,"n":875,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Muslim league","q":"In which of the following years was Muslim League founded ?","o":{"a":"1907","b":"1905","c":"1909","d":"1906"},"a":"d","s":"1906. All India Muslim League (AIML): India's first Muslim political party. Founded at Dacca (Bangladesh). Founders - Khwaja Salimullah, Vikar-ul-Mulk, Syed Amir Ali, Syed Nabiullah, Khan Bahadur Ghulam, Mustafa Chowdhury.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":850,"n":876,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Muslim league","q":"In __________ the Muslim League had moved a resolution demanding \"Independent States\" for Muslims in the north - western and eastern areas of the country","o":{"a":"1940","b":"1942","c":"1946","d":"1935"},"a":"a","s":"1940. All-India Muslim League established in Dhaka in 1906. Founder: Nawab Khwaja Salimullah. First Honorary President: Aga Khan III. Headquarters: Lucknow. 1st Session: Karachi (1907).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":851,"n":877,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Muslim league","q":"Muslim League observed ______ as 'Direct Action Day'.","o":{"a":"16 August 1946","b":"12 August 1946","c":"10 August 1946","d":"14 August 1946"},"a":"a","s":"16 August 1946 Muhammad Ali Jinnah declared 16 August as 'Direct Action Day' and called for Muslims all over the country to 'suspend all business'. Direct Action Day, also known as the 1946 Calcutta Killings.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":852,"n":878,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who designed India Gate, also called the All-India War Memorial?","o":{"a":"Charles Corey","b":"John Tailor","c":"Fredrik Forsyth","d":"Edwin Lutyens"},"a":"d","s":"Edwin Lutyens (an English architect). Other buildings designed by him: Rashtrapati Bhavan, India Gate, The Cenotaph Whitehall (London). The Old Parliament of India was also designed by Herbert Baker and Lutyens. The foundation stone of India Gate was laid by His Royal Highness, the Duke of Connaught in 1921.","ex":"SSC Stenographer","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":853,"n":879,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was/were the founder(s) of the Theosophical Society?","o":{"a":"Annie Besant","b":"Madam Bhikaji Cama","c":"Charles W Leadbeater and Emily Lutyens","d":"H P Blavatsky and Colonel Olcott"},"a":"d","s":"H P Blavatsky and Colonel Olcott. The Theosophical Society was founded in 1875 (New York), to propagate theosophy. In 1882, the headquarters of the Society were established in Adyar, near Madras (now Chennai) . In 1889, Annie Besant joined the Theosophical Society.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":854,"n":880,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the political guru of Subhash Chandra Bose?","o":{"a":"M.G. Ranade","b":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Jawaharlal Nehru","d":"Chittaranjan Das"},"a":"d","s":"Chittaranjan Das (Deshbandhu) . He was the founding member of Swaraj Party (1923). Gopal Krishan Gokahlae was the Political Guru of Mahatma Gandhi.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":855,"n":881,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The name of the Brahmin priest who later came to be known as Ramakrishna Paramahansa is:","o":{"a":"Gadadhar Chattopadhyaya","b":"Dayanand Saraswati","c":"Shree Narayana Guru","d":"Narendra Nath Datta"},"a":"a","s":"Gadadhar Chattopadhyay: Ramakrishna mission :- Founded by Swami Vivekananda (Narendra Nath Datta) on May 1, 1897 at Belur Math (West Bengal).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":856,"n":882,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who is referred to as 'Frontier Gandhi'?","o":{"a":"Rajendra Prasad","b":"Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan","c":"Ashfaqullah Khan","d":"Rajkumar Shukla"},"a":"b","s":"Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan (also known as Bachcha Khan, Badshah Khan, or Sarhadi Gandhi). He founded the Red Shirt movement or \"Khudai Khitmatgar\"in 1930. It was a non-violent protest against the Britishers. Leaders and Nickname: Rajendra Prasad (Desh Ratna), Ashfaqulla Khan (Hasrat), Madan Mohan Malaviya (Mahamana), Gopal Hari Deshmukh (Lokhitawadi), Chittaranjan Das (Deshbandhu), Dadabhai Naoroji (Grand old man of India).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":857,"n":883,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the first person to discuss the poverty line in pre-independence India?","o":{"a":"Dadabhai Naoroji","b":"Mahalanobis","c":"Dr. BR Ambedkar","d":"RK Shanmukham Chetty"},"a":"a","s":"Dadabhai Naoroji. RK Shanmukham Chetty - Finance Minister of India when the country's first budget was tabled in Parliament on 26 November 1947. Dr. BR Ambedkar - Father of Indian Constitution.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":858,"n":884,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"When did the Drafting Committee publish the first draft of the Indian Constitution?","o":{"a":"June 1948","b":"May 1948","c":"January 1948","d":"February 1948"},"a":"d","s":"February 1948. Drafting committee : Formed - 29 August 1947, Chairman - Dr. BR Ambedkar Total member - 7. Indian Constitution : Adopted on - 26 november 1949, Came into force on - 26 January 1950. It took 2 years, 11 months, 18 days to prepare the Indian Constitution.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":859,"n":885,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Moplahs, or Muslim peasants, created a powerful anti-zamindar movement in:","o":{"a":"Tamil Nadu","b":"Bengal","c":"Kerala","d":"Uttar Pradesh"},"a":"c","s":"Kerala. Moplah Revolt (1921 - Against the British and Upper caste Hindu Landlords in Kerala's Malabar region. Led by - Ali Musliyar and Variamkunnath Kunjahammed Haji.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":860,"n":886,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The All India Forward Bloc was founded by__________in 1939 after resigning from the Congress Presidentship","o":{"a":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak","b":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Jawaharlal Nehru","d":"Subhash Chandra Bose"},"a":"d","s":"Subhash Chandra Bose. All India Forward Bloc was a Political Party formed on May 3, 1939 in Makur Unnao, Uttar Pradesh. Its formation was announced in Calcutta.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd shift"},{"id":861,"n":887,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Where was the first All India Kisan Sabha formed?","o":{"a":"Lucknow","b":"Lahore","c":"Amritsar","d":"Delhi"},"a":"a","s":"Lucknow (1936) - By Swami Sahajanand Saraswati (First President), NG Ranga was General secretary. It is the farmer's wing of the communist party of India. Swami Sahajanand Saraswati started the Kisan Sabha movement from Bihar and founded Bihar Provincial Kisan Sabha (1929).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"4th shift"},{"id":862,"n":888,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the first nationalist to give the theory of the drain of wealth from India to England ?","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"RC Dutt","c":"SN Banerjee","d":"Dadabhai Naoroji"},"a":"d","s":"Dadabhai Naoroji. The first attempt to calculate the national income of India was made by Dadabhai Naoroji in 1867 - 68, who estimated per capita income to be ₹ 20.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":863,"n":889,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the first Indian to qualify for Covenanted service?","o":{"a":"Subhash Chandra Bose","b":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","c":"Satyendra Nath Tagore","d":"Surendra Nath Banerjee"},"a":"c","s":"Satyendra Nath Tagore Qualified in 1863. Indian Services Act, 1861 reserved certain positions for covenanted civil servants. It was enacted in 1861 during Lord Canning's Viceroyalty. Charles Cornwallis is known as 'the father of Civil services in India'.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"2nd shift"},{"id":864,"n":890,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Hindu Mahasabha was founded as a response to muslim fundamentalism in which Year?","o":{"a":"1917","b":"1915","c":"1925","d":"1910"},"a":"b","s":"1915. Hindu Mahasabha (Founder - Madan Mohan Malaviya) First All India Hindu Mahasabha Conference - Haridwar (1915). In 1930, it emerged as a distinct party under the leadership of Vinayak Damodar Savarkar. Prominent Leaders - Lala Lajpat Rai, Navin Chandra Rai, Radha Mohan Das Agarwal, Chandroji Angre, Mahant Avaidyanath.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":" 3rd shift"},{"id":865,"n":891,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following wrote 'Anand Math' from where India's National song 'Vande Mataram' has been taken?","o":{"a":"Abanindranath Tagore","b":"Rabindranath Tagore","c":"Mohammad Iqbal","d":"Bankim Chandra Chatterjee"},"a":"d","s":"Bankim Chandra Chatterjee 'Anandamath' is a book about the Sannyasi Rebellion in the late 18th century. His other books: \"Durgesh Nandini\", \"Kapalkundala\", \"Devi Chaudhurani\", \"Bishabriksha\".","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2023,"sh":"1st shift"},{"id":866,"n":892,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following said that \"English education has enslaved us\" ? Graduate Level 30/06/2023 ( Shift - 1)","o":{"a":"Raja Rammohan Roy","b":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Muhammad Ali Jinnah","d":"Muhammad Iqbal"},"a":"b","s":"Mahatma Gandhi Muhammad Iqbal - He wrote the famous song 'Saare jahan se achha', also known for giving birth to the 'idea of Pakistan'. Muhammad Ali Jinnah was a barrister, politician and the founder of Pakistan. Raja Ram Mohan Roy was a religious and social reformer, known as the 'Father of Modern India' or 'Father of the Bengal Renaissance'.","ex":null,"y":null,"sh":null},{"id":867,"n":893,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In 1921-22, farmers of the Malabar seacoast of Kerala undertook a great uprising, which is known as the _______ .","o":{"a":"Munda uprising","b":"Moplah uprising","c":"Santhal uprising","d":"Kol uprising"},"a":"b","s":"Moplah uprising - Led by Variyamkunnath Kunjahammed Haji, against the British and the Hindu landlords; Wagon tragedy - Moplah prisoners died of suffocation during transportation in a closed freight wagon.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 2"},{"id":868,"n":894,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following founded the 'Forward Bloc'?","o":{"a":"Motilal Nehru","b":"Dadabhai Naoroji","c":"Subhas Chandra Bose","d":"Surendranath Banerjee"},"a":"c","s":"Subhas Chandra Bose. It was formed in 1939 in Bengal. Slogans - 'Delhi Chalo', 'Tum Mujhe Khoon Do Main Tumhe Azadi Dunga'. Azad Hind Fauj or the India National Army (INA) was first established by Mohan Singh in 1942 and it was revived by Subhas Chandra Bose on 21 October 1943.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 1"},{"id":869,"n":895,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following social reformers of British India is known as Lokhitwadi ?","o":{"a":"Balshastri Jambhekar","b":"Daboda Pandurang","c":"Gopal Ganesh Agarkar","d":"Gopal Hari Deshmukh"},"a":"d","s":"Gopal Hari Deshmukh Balshastri Jambhekar ( Father of Marathi Journalism) - He started the very first newspaper, 'Darpan', and revolutionized the journalism system in Maharashtra.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 4"},{"id":870,"n":896,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following founded the Asiatic Society in 1784 in Calcutta?","o":{"a":"Jonathan Duncan","b":"William Jones","c":"Warren Hastings","d":"Charles Eyre"},"a":"b","s":"William Jones. Asiatic Society - It was started with the goal of promoting oriental studies. Jonathan Duncan - He started Sanskrit College (1791) at Varanasi to study Hindu Law and Philosophy. He was the Governor of Bombay.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 3"},{"id":871,"n":897,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Social Service League was founded in Bombay by:","o":{"a":"Manabendra Nath Roy","b":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","c":"Narayan Malhar Joshi","d":"Mahadev Govind Ranade"},"a":"c","s":"Narayan Malhar Joshi - He was an Indian trade union leader, Founded social service league (1911). He started the All India Trade Union Congress (1920). He was the general secretary of AITUC. Manabendra Nath Roy - leader of India's communists until the independence of India in 1947.","ex":"Matric Level","y":2023,"sh":"Shift - 3"},{"id":872,"n":898,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"When the British rule ended in India in the year 1947 the literacy rate was just ______.","o":{"a":"18 percent","b":"22 percent","c":"20 percent","d":"12 percent"},"a":"d","s":"12 percent. The population census, a Union subject under Article 246 of the Indian Constitution, conducted its first census of Independent India in 1951, listed as serial number 69 in the seventh schedule . This census revealed the population of the country to be 36, 10, 88, 090.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":873,"n":899,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What was the title given to the late Sarojini Naidu?","o":{"a":"Nightingale of India","b":"Kite of India","c":"Sparrow of India","d":"Hornbill of India"},"a":"a","s":"Nightingale of India (Bharat Kokila). This title was given to Sarojini Naidu by Mahatma Gandhi. She was the first Indian woman to become president (Kanpur Session ,1925) of the Indian National Congress and served as the first governor (1947 - 1949) of the United Provinces. Notable Works - The Golden Threshold, The Broken Wing.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":874,"n":900,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Deccan Riots of 1875 took place in ______ .","o":{"a":"Himachal Pradesh","b":"Bihar","c":"West Bengal","d":"Maharashtra"},"a":"d","s":"Maharashtra. Deccan Riots: Began at - Supa village (Poona) in 1875. The farmers' main motive was to destroy the account books of the moneylenders and they also socially boycotted the moneylenders. The movement was supported by the Poona Sarvajanik Sabha (co-founded by M G Ranade).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":875,"n":901,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Editor of a Marathi newspaper \"Kesari\" was .","o":{"a":"Bipin Chandra Pal","b":"Aurobindo Ghosh","c":"Lala Lajpat Rai","d":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak"},"a":"d","s":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak. Kesari is a Marathi newspaper that was founded on 4 January 1881 and Maratha newspaper in English (Weekly).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":876,"n":902,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What happened in Bengal, India, in 1770 that resulted in the death of one-third of the population?","o":{"a":"Massacre","b":"Famine","c":"Slavery","d":"Chemical explosion"},"a":"b","s":"Famine. 1770 Bengal Famine - It affected Bengal and Bihar. Relief - Attempts to prevent export and hoard or monopolize grain; 15,000 was spent on the import of food grains.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":877,"n":903,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which freedom fighter was also the first woman to become the Governor of Uttar Pradesh State?","o":{"a":"Raj Kumari Amrit Kaur","b":"Rani Lakshmi Bai","c":"Sarojini Naidu","d":"Kasturba Gandhi"},"a":"c","s":"Sarojini Naidu","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":878,"n":904,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Tribal leader Birsa was associated with which tribe?","o":{"a":"Munda","b":"Bhil","c":"Gond","d":"Bhutia"},"a":"a","s":"Munda. Birsa Munda was a tribal reformer, religious leader, and freedom fighter. 15 November - Birsa Munda's birth anniversary (Jharkhand carved out of Bihar on 15th November 2000). Important tribal movements: Chuar Uprising, Kol Mutiny (1831), The Santhal Rebellion (1855-56), Bhil Revolts, Ramosi Risings, Khasi Uprising.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":879,"n":905,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Dr. B.R Ambedkar was associated with which of the following communities?","o":{"a":"Mahar","b":"Berar","c":"Santal","d":"Sangoi"},"a":"a","s":"Mahar. It is listed as the Scheduled Caste in India. Dr. B.R Ambedkar - Architect of Indian Constitution. He started an Association for the welfare of the depressed classes in 1924 and Bahishkrit Bharat newspaper in 1927. He led Mahad Satyagraha or Chavdar Tale Satyagraha in 1927. Awards : Bharat Ratna (1990).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":880,"n":906,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which freedom fighter of British India is widely regarded as the \"father of the two-nation theory\" ?","o":{"a":"Syed Ahmed Khan","b":"Muhammad Ali Jinnah","c":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak","d":"Jawaharlal Nehru"},"a":"a","s":"Syed Ahmed Khan was the founder of Aligarh Muslim University and a forward looking Islamist reformer. Mohammed Ali Jinnah - first governor-general (1947-48) of Pakistan.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2023,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":881,"n":907,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who founded Trinamool Congress in 1998 and became its Chairperson, after separating from the Indian National Congress?","o":{"a":"Jayaram Jayalalithaa","b":"Kanimozhi","c":"Supriya Sule","d":"Mamata Banerjee"},"a":"d","s":"Mamata Banerjee (Founder and first chairperson). Symbol of the Trinamool Congress (TMC) - Jora Ghas Phul. Slogan of TMC - Ma Mati Manush.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":882,"n":908,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was a Roman Catholic missionary who worked in Bengal for the majority of his/her life?","o":{"a":"Saint Bernadette","b":"David of Basra","c":"Mother Teresa","d":"Columba"},"a":"c","s":"Mother Teresa ( from Macedonia ) - Nobel Peace Prize (1979), founded the Missionaries of Charity. Saint Bernadette - French saint whose visions led to the founding of the Marian shrine of Lourdes.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"3rd Shift"},{"id":883,"n":909,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"When did Indigo revolt, a peasant movement, start in India?","o":{"a":"1857","b":"1861","c":"1859","d":"1889"},"a":"c","s":"1859 (March). The Indigo Rebellion (Neel Bidroh) started in Bengal and was a revolt by the farmers against British planters who had forced them to grow indigo under terms that were greatly unfavorable to the farmers.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2023,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":884,"n":910,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What was the distance (around in km) covered by the first-ever train between Bombay and Thane in 1853?","o":{"a":"38","b":"25","c":"34","d":"51"},"a":"c","s":"34. The first passenger train between Mumbai and Thane started on 16 April 1853. Three locomotives named Sultan, Sindh, and Sahib pulled the carriage train. Lord Dalhousie : Father of Indian Railways.","ex":"SSC CGL Tier II","y":2023,"sh":null},{"id":885,"n":911,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The Paris Indian Society was an Indian nationalist organisation founded in ______.","o":{"a":"1907","b":"1905","c":"1909","d":"1900"},"a":"b","s":"1905 at Paris under the patronage of Madam Bhikaji Cama, Munchershah Burjorji Godrej and S. R. Rana. It was a branch of the Indian Home Rule Society which was established in London in 1905. Bhikaji Cama's important publication is 'Vande Mataram' (1909).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":886,"n":912,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the founder of Visva-Bharati University?","o":{"a":"Madan Mohan Malaviya","b":"Lala Hardayal","c":"Swami Vivekananda","d":"Rabindranath Tagore"},"a":"d","s":"Rabindranath Tagore in 1921. Until independence it was a college, got the status of Central University in 1951 by an act of the Parliament.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"1st Shift"},{"id":887,"n":913,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which year was India House established by Shyamji Verma?","o":{"a":"1905","b":"1899","c":"1902","d":"1909"},"a":"a","s":"1905. Other revolutionaries : V.D. Savarkar, Bhikaji Cama, V.N. Chatterjee, Lala Har Dayal, V.V.S. Aiyar, M.P.T. Acharya, P.M. Bapat, etc. Shyamji Krishna Verma founded the Indian Home Rule Society and The Indian Sociologist (Journal) in London. He became the first President of Bombay Arya Samaj.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"4th Shift"},{"id":888,"n":914,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Identify the freedom fighter who, as a child, hated going to school and found it suffocating and oppressive.","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Jawaharlal Nehru","c":"Jyotiba Phule","d":"Rabindranath Tagore"},"a":"d","s":"Rabindranath Tagore Rabindranath Tagore: Santiniketan (1901), Nobel Prize in Literature (1913, Gitanjali), composed Jana Gana Mana (India's National Anthem) and Amar Shonar Bangla (Bangladesh's National Anthem). Other Works: Manasi (1890) [The Ideal One], Sonar Tari (1894) [The Golden Boat], Gitimalya (1914) [Wreath of Songs], and Balaka (1916) [The Flight of Cranes].","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"2nd Shift"},{"id":889,"n":915,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The claim for 'swarajya' or self-government with in the British Empire, on the model of self-governing colonies like Australia and Canada, was put forward from the Congress platform in 1905 by:","o":{"a":"Lala Lajpat Rai","b":"Dadabhai Naoroji","c":"Bipin Chandra Pal","d":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale"},"a":"d","s":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale (Diamond of India) was Political Guru of Gandhi, Congress President (1905), established Servants of India Society in 1905, English Newspaper (The Hitavada), founded The Ranade Institute of Economics (1905), 'Dharmatma Gokhale' (Writer - Mahatma Gandhi).","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":890,"n":916,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the founder of the Bhoodan Yojana (Land Gift) movement?","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Tarabai Shinde","c":"Atmaram Joshi","d":"Vinoba Bhave"},"a":"d","s":"Acharya Vinoba Bhave in 1951, the Bhoodan Movement (first started in Andhra Pradesh) was initiated at Pochampally village (Telangana) . The Bhoodan movement attempted to persuade wealthy landowners to voluntarily give a percentage of their land to landless people.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":891,"n":917,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The national song of India, 'Vande Mataram', is an excerpt taken from the novel ___________ written by Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay.","o":{"a":"Saraswati Chandra","b":"Durgeshnandini","c":"Anand Math","d":"Bishabriksha"},"a":"c","s":"Anandmath (1882). Vande Matram was first sung by Rabindranath Tagore in the 1896 session of the Indian National Congress. Its first two verses were adopted as the National Song of India in October 1937 by the Congress. It was adopted as National song by the Constituent Assembly of India on 24 January 1950. It was composed originally in Sanskrit and Bengali. It was translated in prose by Shri Aurobindo in Karmayogin on 20 November 1909.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":892,"n":918,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The first President of the All India Trade Union Congress was _________.","o":{"a":"C Rajagopalachari","b":"Lala Lajpat Rai","c":"Chandra Shekhar Azad","d":"Motilal Nehru"},"a":"b","s":"Lala Lajpat Rai. All India Trade Union Congress, AITUC (created on October 31, 1920 in Bombay) is the first Central Trade Union of India and the second largest trade union federation in India after the Indian National Trade union Congress. In India, Trade Unions in India are registered under the Trade Union Act (1926). The founding leaders of AITUC were Bal Gangadhar Tilak, N.M.Joshi, B.P.Wadia, Diwan Chamanlall, Lala Lajpat Rai, and Joseph Baptista.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":893,"n":919,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following Indian authors wrote the famous novel 'Gora' during the Nationalist Movement of India?","o":{"a":"Jawaharlal Nehru","b":"Rabindranath Tagore","c":"Bhagat Singh","d":"Mahatma Gandhi"},"a":"b","s":"Rabindranath Tagore Famous poems - 'Gora', 'Gitanjali', 'Chitto Jetha Bhayshunyo', 'Dui Bigha Jomi', etc. Bhagat Singh- 'Why I Am an Atheist', 'Jail Diary and Other Writings', 'The Jail Notebook and Other Writings', 'No Hanging', 'Please Shoot Us', 'Letter to my Father', etc.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":894,"n":920,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following freedom fighters of India revived the Indian National Army 'Azad Hind Fauj' (which was formed in 1942 by Rash Behari Bose and Captain-General Mohan Singh) in the year 1943 ?","o":{"a":"Mahatma Gandhi","b":"Jawaharlal Nehru","c":"Bhagat Singh","d":"Subhas Chandra Bose"},"a":"d","s":"Subhas Chandra Bose Jayanti is celebrated as 'Parakram Diwas' on 23rd January. He founded 'the Forward Bloc' to consolidate the political left and major support base in his home state Bengal. He presided over two congress sessions first at Haripura in 1938 and next in Tripuri in 1939.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":895,"n":921,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following freedom fighters of India was assassinated?","o":{"a":"Jawaharlal Nehru","b":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Nana Sahib","d":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak"},"a":"b","s":"Mahatma Gandhi was assassinated by Nathu Ram Godse on 30 January 1948 at age 78 in the compound of Birla House (now Gandhi Smriti), a large mansion in central New Delhi. His death anniversary is marked as Martyr's Day in India.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":896,"n":922,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was the first Indian to be selected to the British 'House of Commons' on a Liberal Party ticket?","o":{"a":"Chandra Shekhar Azad","b":"Dadabhai Naoroji","c":"Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar","d":"Bhagat Singh"},"a":"b","s":"Dadabhai Naoroji. He was known as the Grand old man of India. He was known as India Gladstone. He was the founder of the East India Association in 1866. The first attempt to calculate National Income of India was made by him. He was the first Asian (Indian) member of British Parliament. He coined the name \"Indian National Congress\" (INC).","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":897,"n":923,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who founded Shantiniketan near Bolpur on 22 December 1901?","o":{"a":"Dr Rajendra Prasad","b":"Rabindranath Tagore","c":"Durgabai Deshmukh","d":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale"},"a":"b","s":"Rabindranath Tagore Visvabharati university was founded by Rabindranath Tagore. Bangadarshan magazine was edited by Rabindranath Tagore.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":898,"n":924,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who used the ancient texts to suggest that widows could remarry?","o":{"a":"Narayan Guru","b":"Pandita Ramabai","c":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","d":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy"},"a":"c","s":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar's efforts, the then Government of India passed the Widow Remarriage Act in 1856. He also raised concern for the abolition of child marriage and polygamy. He supported Bethune to establish the first permanent girls' school in India, the Bethune School.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":899,"n":925,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which freedom fighter of India composed the song, 'Saare Jahan Se Achha' ?","o":{"a":"Jawaharlal Nehru","b":"Jyotiba Phule","c":"Mahatma Gandhi","d":"Muhammad Iqbal"},"a":"d","s":"Mohammad Iqbal. The song is also referred to as 'Taranah-e-Hindi'. The poem was published in the weekly journal lttehad on August 16, 1904.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":900,"n":926,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was popularly known as 'Gurudev' for his expertise in binding together elements of Indian classical music and folk pieces to suit the western tunes entailed with precision along with his expertise in other art domains?","o":{"a":"Rabindranath Tagore","b":"Abanindranath Tagore","c":"Hemanta Mukherjee","d":"Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay"},"a":"a","s":"Rabindranath Tagore . He won the Nobel Prize in literature for \"Gitanjali'' in 1913. He was First Non-European to Win a Nobel Literature Prize. Rabindranath Tagore is mostly renowned as the \"Bard of Bengal''. Some Literary works by him are Chokher bali, The Postmaster, Gora, Kabuliwala (The Fruitseller from Kabul), 1961, Sadhana.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":901,"n":927,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"On 21 October 1943, Subhash Chandra Bose proclaimed the formation of the Provisional Government of Free India in ______.","o":{"a":"Russia","b":"Singapore","c":"Japan","d":"Germany"},"a":"b","s":"Singapore . The All India Forward Bloc was founded by Subhash Chandra Bose. He founded the Azad Hind Government on 21st October 1943.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":902,"n":928,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following Indian freedom fighters was fondly called Gandhi Buri (Old Lady Gandhi) for her dedication towards Gandhian principles?","o":{"a":"Kamla Chaudhary","b":"Leela Roy","c":"Dakshayani Velayudhan","d":"Matangini Hazra"},"a":"d","s":"Matangini Hazra . Matangini Hazra was an Indian revolutionary and social activist who participated in the Indian independence movement.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2022,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":903,"n":929,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Avadh Kisan Sabha was set up in the year ______.","o":{"a":"1918","b":"1920","c":"1921","d":"1924"},"a":"b","s":"1920. Oudh Kisan Sabha (Oudh Farmers Association) was an association formed by Baba Ram Chandra. He was a trade unionist who organized the farmers of Oudh and led the first protest against the landlords.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":904,"n":930,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"India and Pakistan had signed the ______, under which the two countries agreed to sort out differences and disputes bilaterally.","o":{"a":"Calcutta Pact","b":"Shimla Pact","c":"Lucknow Pact","d":"Agra Pact"},"a":"b","s":"Shimla Pact was signed in 1972 by Zulfiqar Ali Bhutto, the President of Pakistan, and Indira Gandhi, the Prime Minister of India.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":905,"n":931,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What was the name of the operation that led to the annexation of the Hyderabad State into India?","o":{"a":"Operation Sunrise","b":"Operation Unity","c":"Operation Polo","d":"Operation Gateway"},"a":"c","s":"Operation Polo - the code name of the Hyderabad \"police action\" in September 1948. It was a military operation in which the Indian Armed Forces invaded the Nizam-ruled princely state, annexing it into the Indian Union.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":906,"n":932,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which one of the following is not one of the writings of Lala Lajpat Rai?","o":{"a":"The Story of My Deportation","b":"Unhappy India","c":"India Wins Freedom","d":"Arya Samaj"},"a":"c","s":"\" India wins freedom\" is the Autobiography of Maulana Abul Kalam Azad.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":907,"n":933,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"With whom is the 'Patharughat Uprising' associated?","o":{"a":"Lawyers' uprising","b":"Soldiers' uprising","c":"Teachers' uprising","d":"Peasants' uprising"},"a":"d","s":"Peasants' uprising. It was against the tax policies of the British in 1894. Patharughat is a place in Assam and is currently known as Patharighat.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":908,"n":934,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was a freedom fighter, based on whose design the present Indian National Flag is designed?","o":{"a":"Tanguturi Prakasam","b":"Pingali Venkaiah","c":"Badal Gupta","d":"Tirot Sing"},"a":"b","s":"Pingali Venkaiah . The design of the National Flag was adopted by the Constituent Assembly of India on 22 July 1947. The ratio of width of the flag to its length is two to three.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":909,"n":935,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The ______ movement was led by Chaudhary Devi Lal in 1987.","o":{"a":"Sangharsh Yudh","b":"Insaaf Yudh","c":"Maha Yudh","d":"Nyaya Yudh"},"a":"d","s":"Nyaya Yudh .He founded the Lok Dal party.","ex":"SSC MTS","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":910,"n":936,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Chandernagore (Chandannagar) was a ______ colony captured by the British Navy on 23 March 1757.","o":{"a":"French","b":"Dutch","c":"Danish","d":"Portuguese"},"a":"a","s":"French. Battle of Chandernagore (1757) - Between French and British (led by Robert Clive) during the Seven Years' War (1756-1763).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":911,"n":937,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which of the following years did Japan invade India resulting in the Battle of Imphal?","o":{"a":"1944","b":"1901","c":"1862","d":"1899"},"a":"a","s":"1944 . The outcome of the battle of Imphal was a turning point of one of the most gruelling campaigns of the Second World War (1939-45).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":912,"n":938,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which of the following years was the Bombay Reorganization Act passed?","o":{"a":"1980","b":"1960","c":"1956","d":"1975"},"a":"b","s":"1960 . After this act, Bombay State was dissolved and split on linguistic lines into the two states of Gujarat (Gujarati speaking population) and Maharashtra (Marathi speaking population).","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":913,"n":939,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following is one of the founder members of Bhartiya Jana Sangh?","o":{"a":"KM Munshi","b":"Minoo Masani","c":"Baldev Singh","d":"Shyama Prasad Mukherjee"},"a":"d","s":"Shyama Prasad Mukherjee BJS was an Indian right-wing political party that existed from 1951 to 1977 and was the political arm of Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS), a Hindu nationalist volunteer organization.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":914,"n":940,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"A large area of land demarcated as Damin-i-Koh in 1832 was declared as the land of the ______.","o":{"a":"Warlis","b":"Paharias","c":"Murias","d":"Santhals"},"a":"d","s":"Santhals (Tribal who inhabited the forest of Rajmahal hills). Damin-i-Koh - British promised non-interference in the land.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":915,"n":941,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Fort Saint Louis was a ______ fort that stood in Pondicherry on the eastern coast of India.","o":{"a":"French","b":"Danish","c":"Dutch","d":"British"},"a":"a","s":"French. Fort Saint Louis - Built by Francois Martin and Destroyed by the Britishers in 1761. First French factory - Surat, Gujrat (1668). Forts of British - Fort William (Calcutta), Fort St. George (Madrass).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":916,"n":942,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"What was the meaning of 'Ryot' in British records in the 18th century?","o":{"a":"Peasants","b":"Weavers","c":"Traders","d":"Jobbers"},"a":"a","s":"Peasants. Ryots were Individual cultivators who had full rights regarding sale, transfer, and leasing of the land as long as they paid the rent under the Ryotwari system (Land revenue paid by the farmers directly to the state).","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":917,"n":943,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the Nawab of Bhopal that ruled from 1868 to 1901?","o":{"a":"Shah Jahan Begum","b":"Begum Sajida Sultan","c":"Sikander Jahan Begum","d":"Sultan Kaikhusrau Jahan Begum"},"a":"a","s":"Shah Jahan Begum. Nawab of Bhopal that ruled - Nawab Sikandar Begum:- 1860-1868, Begum Sultan Shah Jehan:-1844-1860 and 1868-1901. Begum Kaikhusrau Jahan:- 1901-1926, Begum Sajida Sultan:- 1961-1995.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2021,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":918,"n":944,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who among the following was one of the speakers after Jawaharlal Nehru to address the Parliament on the midnight of 15 August 1947 ?","o":{"a":"Rajendra Prasad","b":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","c":"C Rajagopalachari","d":"Sarvepalli Radhakrishnan"},"a":"d","s":"Dr. Sarvepalli Radhakrishan (1st Vice President of India) was one of the three main speakers at the Central Hall of Parliament - other than JL Nehru (Tryst with Destiny Speech) and Chaudhary Khaliquzaman.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":919,"n":945,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"The princely state of Hyderabad was brought under the Indian Union in the year ______.","o":{"a":"1950","b":"1952","c":"1948","d":"1963"},"a":"c","s":"1948 . After the State Reorganisation Act 1956 Hyderabad was merged with Andhra state and later renamed as Andhra Pradesh.","ex":"SSC CPO","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":920,"n":946,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Who was the chairman of the Economic Programme Committee (EPC) that was formed by the All India Congress Committee in 1947?","o":{"a":"Purshottamdas Thakurdas","b":"Jawaharlal Nehru","c":"Dadabhai Naoroji","d":"Subhash Chandra Bose"},"a":"b","s":"Jawaharlal Nehru . The aim of this committee was to make a plan which could balance private and public partnership and urban and rural economies.","ex":"SSC CHSL","y":2020,"sh":"Afternoon"},{"id":921,"n":947,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"In which of the following cities is the tomb of Rani of Jhansi situated?","o":{"a":"Jabalpur","b":"Gwalior","c":"Indore","d":"Ujjain"},"a":"b","s":"Gwalior. The Samadhi of Rani Lakshmi Bai is situated at Phool Bagh. It is to commemorate the great warrior of Jhansi. Rani Lakshmi Bai, fought the battle against the British Raj during the Indian rebellion of 1857.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"},{"id":922,"n":948,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"Which leader gave us the C-R Formula?","o":{"a":"C.Rajagopalachari","b":"Tilak","c":"C.R. Das","d":"Mahatma Gandhi"},"a":"a","s":"Rajagopalachari's formula (or C. R. formula or Rajaji formula) was a proposal formulated by Chakravarti Rajagopalachari to solve the political deadlock between the All India Muslim League and the Indian National Congress on the independence of British India.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Morning"},{"id":923,"n":949,"sec":"Modern History","st":"Miscellaneous","q":"______ was awarded the Nobel Prize in 1913 and a knighthood in 1915.","o":{"a":"Sarojini Naidu","b":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Rabindranath Tagore","d":"Mother Teresa"},"a":"c","s":"Rabindranath Tagore became the first non-European to receive the Nobel Prize for his literary work. He was awarded knighthood in 1915 by King George V.","ex":"SSC CGL","y":2020,"sh":"Evening"}]};
